[Event "Calculate Like a Beast: The Moranda Method"]
[White "Introduction"]
[Black "I. What Is Calculation?"]
[Result ""]
[FEN "rnbqkbnr/pppppppp/8/8/8/8/PPPPPPPP/RNBQKBNR w KQkq - 0 1"]

{ Calculation in chess is defined as the ability to predict a sequence of best moves available in the given position. As opposed to other skills like, for example, anticipation, calculation tends to be extremely concrete and takes the form of variations instead of just general predictions regarding the future course of the game. Fast and accurate calculation skills have always been of high value in the chess world, but in the age of information, they have now become just indispensable for anyone willing to make it to the chess Olympus. This is because we live in the 21st century, when even amateur players know opening theory, basic middlegame plans as well as theoretical endgames quite well. With data being accessible nowadays literally within a blink of an eye, one naturally seeks to prove their superiority by challenging rivals at skills which cannot be learned by heart, but should rather be practiced infinitely. After all, as Derek Sivers once famously stated, ‘if more information was the answer, then we would all be billionaires with perfect abs’ … Depending on the situation, calculation may serve a superordinate or a subordinate role as regards other thinking methods in chess. To speak more precisely, calculation usually overrides general assumptions, which are typical for strategic play. On the other hand, in some cases calculation shall serve merely as a more scientific way of confirming our knowledge-based views on the current position. } 
1.--   

