{ Let us begin discussing this topic deeper by looking at a position which demands nothing else but well-developed calculation skills to be handled correctly.This highly-imbalanced position has arisen as a result of an ultra-sharp and theoretical Sicilian Najdorf. Black is, nominally speaking, a pawn up here, but does not have too many other reasons to celebrate. This is because White's battery along the [d3]-[h7] diagonal is very strong, with ideas like [Ne7xg6] sometimes under inclusion of [Rf1xf7] already hanging in the air. Black is certainly thirsty for counterplay, although there is a risk that it might not arrive in time. What is Black supposed to do then? } Rfd8 $3 { Attack? Defend? Both? It is truly difficult to classify what type of a resource this move represents, except for the fact that it just works, and that at the end of the day, Black may survive after a series of extremely accurate moves. } ( 23...Qe6? 24.Rxf7! Qxf7 25.Nxg6 +- { is what happened in the game, with Black not being premature about his resignation anyhow. } ) ( 23...f5? { surely feels tempting at first, only to get severely punished by } 24.Rxf5! gxf5 25.Nxf5 Rxf5 26.Qxa8 +- { with the back [f5]-rook constituting collateral damage. } ) ( 23...Rad8? { was my idea when giving this puzzle a try. The point behind this move is that I am removing my rook from the attacked [a8]-square just in case, with a timely strike on [d3] for the purpose of reducing White's attacking potential coming into consideration at the same time. What I missed, however, was that after the more or less forced } 24.Nxg6! Qa1+ 25.Kd2 Rxd3+ 26.Qxd3 Qa5+ 27.Ke2 fxg6 28.Rxf8 Bxf8 29.Qd7+ +- { the black monarch would be getting unexpectedly mated within the next couple of moves. } ) 
24.c3  { The best move according to the engine, making room for the king on [c2] in case of need, but also killing off Black's [g7]-bishop, should it become active one day. } ( 24.Nxg6? { would now lose because of } 24...Qa1+ 25.Kd2 Qxf1 26.Ne7+ f5! 27.Nxf5 Rxd3+! 28.cxd3 Qf4 + -+ { , with White's attack petering out to nothing after the trade of queens. Now it becomes clear why 23...[Rfd8] was superior to 23...[Rad8] - there was no rook of Black on [f8] left for the white knight to capture... } ) ( 24.Rf3 { is a move designed to renew the threat of [Ne7xg6] without however dropping the rook to a skewer on [f1]. Now Black's best move would have been the astonishing } 24...Rab8!! { , yielding } 25.Nxg6? { ineffective in view of } 25...Rd4! { , while }  {25.Bf6 would be optimally met by 25...Rxd3! 26.cxd3 Qa1+ 27.Kc2 b4! 28.Qxe5 Rb5= with White being unable to capitalize on his material advantage due to safety concerns regarding his king. Who would have predicted, though, that 24...Rab8!! would not be played for the sake of evading possible attacks from the white queen, but instead as preparation of ...[b5]-[b4]-[b3]+ ... } ) ( { Finally, } 24.Kd2 {(would need to be countered by the prophylactic yes, you read it right!)} 24...Ra7!! { after which White would not be in a position anymore to weaken Black's kingside by means of [Bh4]-[f6] due to his hanging knight on [e7]. Instead, } 25.Nc6 f5 26.Rxf5! { would have allowed him to bail out after the outstanding } 26...Rxd3+! 27.Qxd3 Rc7= { when White would probably need to accept the draw by perpetual check one way or another. In all honesty, however, 24...Ra7!! is not the type of move players would be going for with some strategic concepts in mind, but rather just because it works on a tactical level under the given set of circumstances. } ) Rxd3 $1 { A necessary transposition towards a relatively safe endgame for Black. The following sample lines prove that he would be very close to saving a draw. } 
25.Qxd3  ( 25.Qxa8 f5! { A truly invisible defense against mate on [g8] - Black's queen on [a2] stays on guard despite the distance. White should probably share the point by agreeing to a threefold repetition of moves after } 26.Qc6 e4! 27.Qxg6+ Kh8 28.Nxf5 Qa1+ 29.Kc2 Qa4+= { . } ) Re8  
26.Nd5  { and now after the more or less forced } e4 $1 
27.Nb4  Qxb2+ $1 
28.Kxb2  exd3  
29.Rxf7  g5  
30.Bg3  Kg8  
31.Rc7  Re2+  
32.Kc1  Rxg2  
33.Nxd3  h5  { Black should be able to hold thanks to passed pawns on both wings in conjunction with the activity of his rook. }  

