42.--   

[Event "Calculate Like a Beast: The Moranda Method"]
[White "Introduction"]
[Black "Demchenko, A. vs. Krysa, L., Teplice 2023"]
[Result ""]
[FEN "6k1/1p3q2/2p1p2p/P2nP1pP/1p1PQ1P1/3B4/1P6/6K1 w - - 0 42"]

{ Admittedly, in your own tournament practice you will also inevitably stumble upon positions which can only be solved by means of brute force calculation. These moments are one of the distinguishing factors separating elite players from all the other average Joes in the chess scene. Then again, they are statistically speaking pretty scarce, with the most standard case of using calculation featuring us just calculating short lines here and there as the game progresses, for the purpose of making sure that our strategic ideas are not flawed tactically. In such situations, I like to think of calculation as one of many programs running constantly in the background of a computer operating system, which – from the standpoint of the gravity of the situation, that is whether we happen to be dealing with a critical moment of the game – may constitute a lower or higher load on the processor, just to ensure it does not all of a sudden crash. An excellent illustration of this tendency is to be seen in displays of perfect endgame technique, which } { (and this is probably going to come as a surprise to you) } { is to a considerable degree comprised exactly of calculation. Consider this position. After a tense middlegame, we arrive at an endgame featuring a couple of interesting imbalances. First of all, White has more space. Depending on the circumstances, this can prove to be helpful } { (as it means that his pawns are closer to promotion square) } { or detrimental to his cause with a smaller number of pieces remaining on the board, it becomes difficult to maintain control over the position. More importantly however, we can see a battle of a queen and bishop against the queen and knight duo. The general rule says that the Q+N has to potential to outplay the Q+B as it represents a very tactical, hard to contain team. Still, it is White who is on the move so let us see what his best option is here. } 
42.Qg6+ $1 { An extremely responsible decision. After the exchange of queens there is simply no turning back for White - he will need to make sure that the bishop is going to prevail over the black knight in the long haul. One of the points behind this trade is that, without the queens on board, the bishop becomes a theoretically better piece compared to the knight, provided obviously that there are pawns present on both wings in the given position. } ( { Instead, the game saw } 42.Bc2? Kf8 43.Bb3 { , after which the window of opportunity for White had already closed, with Black being perfectly safe even should the white queen be able to infiltrate the queenside eventually, e.g. } 43...Ke7 44.Bxd5 cxd5 45.Qc2 Kd8 46.Qc5 Qf3= { and White will probably manage to win a pawn on the queenside, but this will certainly not be enough to claim the full point, as a perpetual check on the kingside is already looming. } ) Qxg6  
43.hxg6 $1 { Only this way - the pawn is supposed to absorb the attention of the black monarch for his white counterpart to gain enough time to activate in direction of the queenside. Our primary target is now Black's [b7]-pawn - its elimination is key if we want to turn our own little guy on [a5] into a queen one day. Still, the [g6]-passer is surely vulnerable, with White furthermore required to neutralize all sorts of tricky knight maneuvers of Black. } Ne3  { This move is designed to discourage the white king from approaching the [f2]-square, but concrete calculation proves that losing the [g4]- or [b2]-pawn is a mere nuisance on the path towards victory. } ( 43...Nf4 44.Be4! { A strong concept indeed! Black does not enjoy the luxury of focusing on taking down White's [g6]-pawn after ...[Kg8]-[g7] and ...[Nf4xg6], as this would have jeopardized him to [Be4xc6]! followed by a pawn-march all the way to promotion square. What is even more, after } 44...Ne2+ 45.Kf2 Nxd4 46.Ke3 { it turns out that all the time spent by Black on capturing the [d4]-pawn would have benefited White only who not only brought up his king to [e3] rather quickly, but now additionally enjoys a clearer passage towards [c5] than before. Black can try to establish a fortress on the queenside with something like } 46...Nb5 47.Kd3 Nc7 { , but White deals with it thanks to } 48.Kd4 Na6 49.Bc2 Kg7 50.Bd3 +- { with the ensuing pawn endgames being winning for White no matter how Black proceeds afterwards. } ) 
44.Kf2 $1 Nd1+  ( 44...Nxg4+ { is hopeless, as after the further } 45.Kf3 h5 46.Be4 c5 47.Bxb7 cxd4 48.a6 Nxe5+ 49.Ke4 Nc4 50.a7 Nb6 51.Kxd4 +- { the black knight will not be able to prevent [a7]-[a8=Q] for much longer. } ) 
45.Ke1 $1 { This is imperative, as White's bishop temporarily has to stay along the [f1]-[a6] diagonal in order to prevent Black from eliminating our future [a5]-passer by means of ...[Nb2]-[c4]. } Nxb2  
46.Bf1  { Now sample lines prove that White is always faster than his rival, notwithstanding which approach passive defense or generating a passed pawn of his own on the opposite flank Black resorts to: } Kg7  ( 46...b3 47.Kd2 Na4 48.Bc4 b2 49.Bxe6+ Kg7 50.Kc2 Kxg6 51.Bc8 +- { when the disappearance of Black's [b7]-pawn would also have marked his end. } ) 
47.Kd2  Kxg6  
48.Kc2  Na4  
49.Kb3  Nc3  { The black knight escapes fate, but its jumping back and forth gives the white monarch an additional boost, even if the road towards the [b7]-pawn is going to be longer c5-[d6]-[d7]-[c8] instead of [c5]-[b6] than anticipated. } 
50.Kxb4  Nd5+  
51.Kc5  h5  
52.gxh5+  Kxh5  
53.Kd6  { Oops, the black [e6]-pawn falls as well, meaning that stopping both the [e5] and [a5]-pawns is going to constitute mission impossible for the black knight. } Kg4  
54.Kxe6  Kf3  
55.Kd7  Ke3  
56.e6  Kxd4  
57.Bg2  Nf6+  
58.Kc7 $18  

