{ Finally, there are some positions in chess which barely require any calculation whatsoever, and as such should be played purely intuitively. Intuition is defined as the sum of one’s previous experience and knowledge, and takes the form of a whisper in the back of their head suggesting ideas which ‘look good’ in the given situation. The better player you become, the more accurate feedback will you be receiving from this type of an advisor. Such cases include playing positional endgames with reduced material without any tactical potential left in them, but also some closed middlegames in which abilities like piece coordination or long-term planning happen to be more beneficial. Please have a look at this position and – except for finding a solution to it as White – try to objectively determine how much calculation relative to other skills would it objectively entail, and then proceed to the next trainable, where you can enter the solution. } 
26.--   

[Event "Calculate Like a Beast: The Moranda Method"]
[White "Introduction"]
[Black "Tari, Aryan vs. Carlsen, Magnus, World Cup Baku 2023"]
[Result ""]
[FEN "r4n1r/p3qk2/4p3/3pPpPb/2pP3p/3N3P/P1PB3K/R3QR2 w - - 0 26"]

{ In 2023, Magnus Carlsen won the very last trophy remaining on his career bucket list, that is the World Cup. This granted him the right to participate in the Candidates tournament, which he then famously proceeded to waive in favor of Nijat Abasov. This again led indirectly to Gukesh's victory at the aforementioned event and, at the time of me writing this, him commencing preparation for the World Championship Match against Ding Liren. All this is history, which could just as well have become history } { (pun intended) } { , had Tari made use of the opportunity Caissa presented him with in the second game of his match against the big guy in Baku. } 
26.g6+ $1 { A strategic clearance motif. By playing like this, White could have exposed Black along the dark squares not only on the queenside in particular the [a3]-[f8] diagonal but the kingside as well. As much as White's play on the queenside is going to be mostly positional in nature invasion via the b-file, on the opposite flank he might even start considering generating a minor attacking initiative based on the idea of infiltrating the [g5]/[f6]/[h6] squares with his queen. } ( { Instead, Tari allowed Black to establish a reliable blockade on the kingside after } 26.Nf4?! Ng6 { with a trade of knights followed by a draw offer a couple of moves later. Considering the fact that this result threw White out of the cup, personally I would have probably decided in favor of the speculative } 27.Nxd5!? { instead, when after something like } 27...exd5 28.Rxf5+ Kg8 29.Bb4 Qe8 30.Rf6 Rh7 ⇆ { White would at least be having some fun for a couple more moves. In earnest however, Black should still be objectively fine here thanks to his solid grip over the light squares. } ) ( { One student of mine suggested } 26.Bb4?! Qd8 27.Nc5 { as an alternative, with the idea of 'feeding' Black with the [g5]-pawn, while in the meantime making serious progress on the other wing. Chess is no checkers, however, so Black does not need to accept this gift, instead achieving a double-edged position after the further } 27...Ng6 28.Rb1 Rb8 29.Qd2 Re8 30.Na6 Rb6 31.Ba5 Rxb1 32.Rxb1 Qc8 ⇆ { , with White forced to watch out for ...[Ng6xe5]! counters every time he decides to retreat his own knight back to [c5]. } ) Nxg6  
27.Nc5  { Another nice feature of White's intermezzo as of move 26 is the additional distance the black knight would now need to cover in order to put in question the placement of our knight on [c5]. } Rab8  
28.Qc1 $1 { A lovely multi-purpose move, indeed! This way, White on one hand makes the nasty [Bd2]-[g5]-[f6] possible, while at the same time getting ready to transfer his queen to [a3], for the purpose of connecting the rooks, having the future battle for the b-file in mind. } Rb6  
29.Bg5  Qc7  
30.Bf6  Rh7  { A sad necessity, as the [h6]-square needs to stay under control, but even so, after the further } 
31.Rg1  Ke8  
32.Qa3 $18 { Black would be eventually getting himself overpowered along the b-file as his other rook is unable to come to the rescue via [b7], while the disappearance of the black [b6]-rook would most probably also spell doom for his [e6]-pawn. All in all, calculation is crucial for any chess player, although it does function best when accompanied by other abilities, often strategic in nature. In any case, however, I would like to invite you to continue this journey started by purchasing this course, as the reward awaiting you after its diligent study is not only excellence, but also a certain form of revelation regarding training optimization in the future. What you must know is that the main obstacle on just about anybody’s path towards mastery is usually not the question of ‘what and for how long’, but rather ‘how and why’ … }  

