{ Short story long – as a person with approximately thirty years of playing experience out of which the last ten include coaching others too, I have always been on the lookout for ways for me and my students to climb to new heights. For a while, I used to believe that no matter how I do things, as long as I put the Gladwellian ten thousand hours of work into it, my goals would have surely been achieved. My initial research endeavors as a coach focused on strategy and resulted in the publication of ‘Universal Chess Training’ back in 2020. Its main premise was that 80% of strategic mistakes committed by ourselves in a game of chess can be classified within the following five categories: Anticipation & Prophylaxis, Attack & Defense, Coordination, Statics & Dynamics and playing against Weaknesses. So, if we were able to construct a strategic training program oriented exactly around those five topics, we would eventually be able to claim close to perfect understanding of this area of the game, especially as the remaining 20% of the strategic problems arising on the board would just be deemed too random or unpredictable to even be bothered with. But just as humanity has constant expansion and conquest in their DNA, I too very soon afterwards started wondering whether it would even remotely be possible to create something identical, but designed specifically for calculation training instead. This appeared to be an arduous task at first, especially considering the amount of misconceptions surrounding this topic, spread at times even by high caliber players. Bad training habits inherited from previous coaches never help either, with the final straw breaking the camel’s back being sloppily written puzzle books and courses, frequently nevertheless adored by the chess populace who simply do not seem to know any better. As I do not want to be accused of merely paying lip-service here, before I explain my proprietary method of training chess calculation, let me first guide you through the maze of misleading advice and subpar learning sources which have accumulated over time. Who knows, maybe you are going to discover that your own approach to calculation study might not have been optimal until now either? } 
1.--   

[Event "Calculate Like a Beast: The Moranda Method"]
[White "Introduction"]
[Black "III. What Is Calculation Training NOT Supposed To Look Like?"]
[Result ""]
[FEN "rnbqkbnr/pppppppp/8/8/8/8/PPPPPPPP/RNBQKBNR w KQkq - 0 1"]

{ Since my early childhood, I have repeatedly heard coaches advise their students to solve plenty of tactical puzzles if they wanted to improve their calculation skills. As much as excellent tactical vision is an absolute must when calculating lines, I do need to put a hard stop here already by remarking that calculation absolutely does not equal tactics in chess. Tactics namely infer the ability to recognize certain patterns comprised of moves or short sequences thereof, leading to a more or less immediate gain of material or danger to the enemy king. In practice, tactics are often correlated with calculation, in a sense that the calculated line might feature a tactical shot somewhere along the way, but there are also so many lines which we calculate in every game of ours that have absolutely nothing to do with tactics. So if the relationship between tactics and calculation is to such a degree non-exclusive and fluid, recommending solving tactical puzzles as a method of improving one’s calculation skills could be compared to a doctor prescribing vitamin C as a cure for influenza. } 
1.--   

[Event "Calculate Like a Beast: The Moranda Method"]
