{ Let us now have a look at one example which highlights my point. White had spent the largest part of this game hidden behind a double guard, only to get all of a sudden cocky with the rather unexpected 37.g4?. Such an incoherence should not go unpunished in a perfect world, and Black in fact could have taken advantage of this turn of events right away. } Bd4 $3 ( { In the game Black continued } 37...fxg4? { As much as the winning continuation for Black was far from obvious, it is truly beyond me why would he be capturing on [g4] with his pawn in this position, this way going straight for a more or less hopeless endgame. } 38.f5 Rexe1+! { Entering this tactical skirmish would have been Black's only way to stay in the game. } {( The game saw the inferior 38...g3? when after 39.fxg6 gxf2 40.Rxh6+ Kg7 White could have maximized his advantage by means of 41.Rh7+ Kf8 42.Kxf2 Ra5 43.Nf3 +- , leaving Black struggling to survive due to the strength of his [g6]-passer. )} 39.Bxe1 Qh7! { Encouraging White to go for the tempting } 40.Rxh6! Qxh6 41.Rh1 Qxh1+ 42.Bxh1 { only to retaliate with } 42...Ne5! 43.Bxb7 Nd3 { , although White would have surely kept some winning chances after } 44.Qb6 ⩲ { when his king triumphantly dances away from danger as the queen commences action behind enemy lines. } ) ( { Instead, } 37...Bd4!! { would have been a lovely counter-punch in response to White's recent bid for an initiative. The point behind this still mysterious move is revealed after Black's next. } ) 
38.cxd4  c3 $1 { Death meets the white king at the least expected of places, that is the [f1]-[a6] diagonal. } 
{ For example } 
39.Bf3  cxd2  
40.Qxd2  Rexe1+  
41.Kf2  Rxg1  
42.Rxg1  Rxg1  
43.Kxg1  fxg4 $19 { That was a pretty strike Black could have executed here, but was there any calculation whatsoever involved in the process? I believe not, especially as the only thing Black would need to do in order to seal the deal afterwards would be briefly making sure that White cannot really neutralize the danger towards his king without sustaining heavy material losses. }  

