[White "Introduction"]
[Black "III. What Is Calculation Training NOT Supposed To Look Like? (2)"]
[Result ""]
[FEN "rnbqkbnr/pppppppp/8/8/8/8/PPPPPPPP/RNBQKBNR w KQkq - 0 1"]

{ Another thing I hear all the time in the context of chess calculation, is that there is no better way of excelling at it than by solving endgame studies. During my junior years, there was this one coach who would even teach me and my peers calculation almost exclusively based on such puzzles. We assumed that this is some sort of ‘chess apocryphon’ – secret knowledge that was only to be taught to the future chess elite, until we overheard him mention to his work colleague that since he got access to some sort of encyclopedia of chess compositions, he would never need to bother preparing any training material of his own for the training sessions anymore... So in case you did not know, studies are puzzles artificially constructed by their composers for the purpose of accentuating a particularly beautiful motif or sequence of moves. These are by definition supposed to be aesthetically pleasing, with the final point behind the puzzle representing the solver’s reward for their efforts. As much as I have nothing against using these as one of many elements of your own training regimen, if I were you, I would be definitely making sure that they meet some basic working criteria in the first place. First of all, those studies need to be as practice-oriented as possible, which means that the featured positions could have theoretically arisen in an over-the-board game. Secondly, make sure that they are cherry-picked by a coach or friend, and that their main theme varies between different stages of the calculation process. Finally, be aware of the effects of the dopamine rush solving excessive numbers of studies delivers to your chess mind. In particular, you would prefer to avoid a situation in which you go to a tournament after an intense training session and subconsciously start looking for pretty hidden motifs in otherwise innocuous positions, just because you have been fed with such puzzles all the time right before the event... } 
1.--   

[Event "Calculate Like a Beast: The Moranda Method"]
