{ Evaluate 35.Nce4. } 
35.--   

[Event "Calculate Like a Beast: The Moranda Method"]
[White "Introduction"]
[Black "Puranik, A. vs. Iniyan, P., Pune 2023"]
[Result ""]
[FEN "8/1p3pk1/2pNnn2/p3r2p/8/P1N2QP1/2q2PK1/1R6 w - - 0 35"]

{ As a sidenote, this book is not going to include any studies since it only contains generic examples discovered while doing my own analytical work. But fear not, tournament games can also provide for pure, non-synthetic study-like enjoyment, as demonstrated by the following position. Black seems to be on the path to victory here. He is two pawns up and White is unable to recapture the one on [b7] in view of Black's counter-attack starting with ...[Ne6]-[g5]. This is also why White needed to resort to attacking ideas on the kingside instead. } 
35.Nce4 $1 { Putting immediate pressure against Black's [f6]-knight, with his [f7]-pawn being potentially next on the kill-list. Still, Black is naturally far from defenseless as he can also capture on [e4] with his rook instead. } ( 35.Rxb7? Ng5 { would have been just terrible for White, as now taking on [c6] would have resulted in instant defeat after ...[Nf6]-[g4]. In lieu of that, White could minimize the damage with } 36.Qf4 { , but then after } 36...Qxc3 37.Nf5+ Rxf5 38.Qxf5 Qd2 -+ { his material advantage should secure him the win in the long run. } ) Rxe4  ( { Obviously, } 35...Nxe4?? { would have been losing on the spot, although it is still instructive to witness how exactly White would be finishing the opponent off here: } 36.Qxf7+ Kh6 37.Rxb7 Nxd6 38.Qf6+ Qg6 39.Qh8+ Kg5 40.Qxe5+ Nf5 41.f4+! { The cavalry has arrived! } 41...Nxf4+ 42.Qxf4+ Kf6 43.Ra7 +- { with a technically winning endgame for White. } ) 
36.Rxb7  Ng5  ( 36...Nd8 { would have been surprisingly ineffective on account of } 37.Rb8! { , forcing Black to repeat moves or deviate into something like } 37...Re5 38.Rxd8 Qc5 39.Nb7 Qe7 40.Qxc6= { when from the practical point of view White would need to remain vigilant due to the absence of his forces from the kingside. Objectively speaking, however, his position would nonetheless be viable. } ) 
37.Qf5  { One difference between 35.Rxb7? and the text move is that now White can plant his queen on [f5], thus keeping his attacking prospects on the kingside alive. At the same time, helping the black [g5]-knight stay exactly where it is should suffice for the purpose of keeping the position of Black's king intact. There are two reasonable moves allowing him to achieve this - 37...Qc1 and 37...Qd2. I hope you managed to deal with both of them in advance! } Qc1  ( 37...Qd2 { could be countered by the tactical } 38.Rxf7+! Nxf7 39.Qxf6+! Kxf6 40.Nxe4+ Ke5 41.Nxd2 { with an equal knight endgame, despite the burden of proof that this is the case lying surely with White. Sample line: } 41...Kd5 42.Kh3! { It is mandatory for White to create a passer of his own as quickly as possible. } 42...c5 43.Kh4 c4 44.Nxc4 Kxc4 45.Kxh5 Kb3 46.g4 Kxa3 47.f4! a4 48.f5! { The move order in which White's kingside pawns were supposed to progress was not irrelevant - I do encourage you to check out why on your own. } 48...Kb2 49.g5 a3 50.g6 Nd6 51.g7 Ne8 52.Kg6 Nxg7 53.Kxg7 a2 54.f6 a1=Q 55.f7= { with a simple theoretical draw arising on the board. } ) 
38.Rb1 $1 { Deee-flectiooon! Wherever the black queen goes, the white rook follows - a truly study-like motif! } Qd2  ( { Letting go of the g5-knight results in a draw on the spot: } 38...Qxb1 39.Qxg5+ Kh7! 40.Qxf6 Re1 41.Qxf7+ Kh8 42.Qf8+ Kh7 43.Qf7+= { with a draw by threefold repetition. } ) 
39.Rd1 $1 { The final touch - now Black has no other choice but to transpose into the following endgame. } Qxd1  
40.Qxg5+  Kf8  
41.Qxf6  Qd5  
42.Nxe4  Qxe4+  
43.Kg1  Qd5  
44.Qc3 $10 { Black may indeed be a pawn up, but enjoys only tiny winning chances. He cannot push his [c6]-pawn without dropping the little guy on [a5]. What is more, every attempt to activate his king is going to be met by a barrage of checks coming from the white queen. }  

