{ To be fair, however, all the evil in the chess world cannot be ascribed solely to undereducated or just lazy coaches. Very often, it is going to be the use of poorly written books and courses which is going to be to blame. How can you namely play good chess or give accurate advice as a coach if your own knowledge stems largely from inadequate sources? In view of the rise in popularity of defamation lawsuits among the chess community thanks a lot, Hans, I shall abstain from doing any finger-pointing here, restricting myself merely to indicating the most basic venial sins committed by authors when writing about chess calculation. First of all, it is surprisingly difficult to find a source which – apart from providing puzzles – would care to explain in more detail what calculation actually is and what it is comprised of. Secondly, it is not such a rare occurrence for books to be titled something along the lines of ‘Learn to calculate better’, only for whole chapters inside of it being devoted to topics like e.g. prophylaxis. Do not get me wrong here – using prophylaxis when calculating variations may improve your results for that matter, but as such, prophylaxis does not constitute an inherent element of the calculation process, and is therefore not to be considered as one in such a work. Last but not least, clumsy writing is something that I personally cannot stand. I still deem it unforgivable when the author quotes dozens more or less irrelevant lines or transpositions without any instructive commentary within a single exercise, when all it really took to accentuate the idea behind it was a couple of variations, enriched by extensive verbal commentary. What really grinds my gears, however, is when their sloppiness ‘infects’ the very essence of the puzzle, with the prime example thereof being authors deliberately using puzzles featuring more than one viable solution. And trust me when I say it, there is nothing more annoying and actually even demotivating when solving calculation exercises than the fact that there was more than one road to success available from the start. } 
1.--   

[Event "Calculate Like a Beast: The Moranda Method"]
[White "Introduction"]
[Black "Wagner, Dinara vs. Rudzinska, Michalina, Rhodes 2024"]
[Result ""]
[FEN "r3kn1r/1b2b1p1/pq2pn1p/1pp3N1/2p1PB2/P1N3PB/1PQ2P1P/2KRR3 w kq - 0 16"]

{ 16.e5 or 16.Nxe6? } 
16.--   

[Event "Calculate Like a Beast: The Moranda Method"]
[White "Introduction"]
[Black "Wagner, Dinara vs. Rudzinska, Michalina, Rhodes 2024"]
[Result ""]
[FEN "r3kn1r/1b2b1p1/pq2pn1p/1pp3N1/2p1PB2/P1N3PB/1PQ2P1P/2KRR3 w kq - 0 16"]

{ Taking this into consideration, below I present to you a captivating example, which I however eventually decided not to include in this book, for the sake of providing the chess audience with training material of the absolutely highest quality. I have been working together with Michalina during the years 2022 and 2023. During that time, she managed to win the Polish Female Championships twice in a row in both cases as a complete outsider rating-wise!, thus qualifying for the 2022 Olympiad as well as the 2023 European Teams, not to mention countless other high-profile events. Not too shabby for a previously relatively-unknown player in her early twenties, eh? Despite us needing to part ways for independent reasons, I am very confident that the chess world is going to hear about her more in the near future. But here after the misplayed opening part, Black is already on the ropes. White's pieces are namely locked and loaded for some action in the center, with the only question remaining which target to hit first. Both 16.e5 and 16.Nxe6 look promising, but a coin toss will certainly not help you elect the best continuation! } 
16.Nxe6 $1 { Only like this - White should have eliminated and important defender of the black king for starters. } ( { The game saw } 16.e5? { , when after } 16...hxg5 17.exf6 { Black should have simplified as much as possible by capturing on [h3] and then on [f4], thus significantly reducing White's attacking potential. Instead, she became too greedy and went for } 17...gxf6? { , with the intention of benefiting from a double-attack against White's bishops shortly afterwards. Unfortunately for her, Wagner's tactical vision was impeccable this time, with the powerful counter } 18.Rxe6! Nxe6 19.Qg6+ Kf8 20.Bd6 +- { forcing Black's resignation just a couple of moves later. } ) Nxe6  
17.e5  { The second point behind White's sixteenth move is now revealed - a real feast awaits the white queen once she reaches the weakened [g6]-square. } ( 17.Nd5? { does not make the cut though as after } 17...Bxd5 18.exd5 Nxf4 19.gxf4 Kf8 { Black's strong defenses along the dark squares could only be compromised, but not overcome with } 20.d6! Bxd6 21.Re6 Bxf4+ 22.Kb1 Qc7 23.Rxf6+ gxf6 24.Rd7 ⇆ { when all three results would have still been possible. White will be namely trying to create the black king along the light squares while Black scrambles to reconnect her rooks. } ) Nxf4  
18.gxf4  ( 18.exf6? { would have again been to hasty on account of } 18...Qxf6 19.gxf4 Rd8 □ 20.Re6 Qxf4+ 21.Kb1 Rxd1+ 22.Qxd1 Qd4 ⇆ { after which Black will admittedly keep on suffering due to her exposed king for longer, although objectively speaking the position should remain defensible. } ) O-O  
19.exf6  Bxf6  { When trying to figure out the intricacies of this exercise myself first, I stopped at this moment and concluded that Black should be fine as she managed to bring her king into safety. It turns out, however, that I finished my calculations prematurely as White still has a murderous resource: } 
20.Qg6 $1 { With the idea of either bringing the bishop towards the [b1]-[h7] diagonal or even the absolutely barbaric [Re1]-[e7]!, making use of the pin along the sixth rank. Black can try to postpone this with } Bc8  { but after the insistent } 
21.Bg2 $1 {(I will let you check on your own why both 21.Re7? and 21.Nd5?? are suboptimal)} Bb7  
22.Rd7  Bxg2  
23.Rxg7+  Bxg7  
24.Qxb6 $18 { The position would have been ripe for resignation already. } { An extremely pretty puzzle - I am pretty sure that almost no author would hesitate before putting it into their own book. Except for yours truly, and that being due to a tiny little detail which might have escaped your attention in the course of the solving process. Surprisingly, White could have also obtained a winning position by means of 19.Qg6! , that is, even before taking the black knight on [f6] first. Many readers would not mind, other authors would just mention this briefly or even omit it entirely, but in my personal opinion this circumstance 'dilutes' the point behind the whole puzzle, therefore yielding it imperfect. By the standards of this course, alternative solutions are only going to be allowed in non-essential moments, typically seen after the point of the whole sequence had already been disclosed. }  

[Event "Calculate Like a Beast: The Moranda Method"]
