[Black "III. What Is Calculation Training NOT Supposed To Look Like? (4)"]
[Result ""]
[FEN "rnbqkbnr/pppppppp/8/8/8/8/PPPPPPPP/RNBQKBNR w KQkq - 0 1"]

{ In terms of the singular most misinterpreted rule governing the calculation process, the first thing that comes to my mind is Alexander Kotov’s recommendation in ‘Think like a Grandmaster’ to try calculating every line within a given tree of variations only once. I think I must have heard my students wrongly understand his words at least a couple of times already. As far as I can remember, the work in which he provided this basically undeniable piece of advice, the emphasis was put on the word try instead of the part only once. To rephrase – the point he most probably wanted to make was to avoid going round in circles calculating the same positions over and over again, thus also wasting precious time on the clock. What he sure did not have in mind, though, was universally covering every branch of the calculated line once, notwithstanding its level of complexity. There are namely going to be times during our own careers when we will have to refrain from sticking to such a principle and be guided by the needs of the position themselves. } 
1.--   

[Event "Calculate Like a Beast: The Moranda Method"]
[White "Introduction"]
[Black "Based on Zaragatski, I. vs. Wickenfeld, S., Germany 2024"]
[Result ""]
[FEN "r7/1b1Pq1k1/1p3n1p/pPp2r2/P1B1pp2/R7/Q4PPP/3R1NK1 w - - 0 27"]

{ White to play. } 
27.--   

[Event "Calculate Like a Beast: The Moranda Method"]
[White "Introduction"]
[Black "Based on Zaragatski, I. vs. Wickenfeld, S., Germany 2024"]
[Result ""]
[FEN "r7/1b1Pq1k1/1p3n1p/pPp2r2/P1B1pp2/R7/Q4PPP/3R1NK1 w - - 0 27"]

{ Let me use the following position in order to illustrate a respective exception. Before we speak about this position, I wanted to draw your attention to the fact that some of the examples provided in this course will be preceded by the add-on 'based on'. This usually means that the source game had been modified to a certain degree, for the sake of submitting perfect training material. This could include prolonging some of the games, investigating alternatives available at their critical moments, but also some deeper interventions into their content. So, if you happen to be one of the players involved in a game discussed in this work and do not recognize some of the quoted lines - no worries, this had been done on purpose to ensure the other Readers' optimal training experience. Material is equal here, but just about everything else happens to be extremely imbalanced at the same time. White's [d7]-passer looks strong at the moment, but so do his opponent's connected central pawns. White would certainly appreciate the possibility of bringing his rook into play via [g3] followed by the knight arriving to [e3], but this looks like mission impossible in view of the presence of Black's pawn on [f4]. The only way to make this happen involves softening up the said controller of the [g3]- and [e3]-squares with [g2]-[g3], but this again appears to be just suicidal on account of the ...[e4]-[e3] advance, enabling the deadly ...[Qe7]-[e4] with mate along the light diagonal to come next. Then again, calculation refuses to accept appearances as the ultimate proof for the viability of a given idea... } 
27.g3 $3 { White goes all in! Now there is no turning back - it is either White achieving his positional goals or succumbing to a ruthless attack along the light squares. } ( { Just for the sake of clarity, } 27.Be6? { would have been utterly hopeless because of } 27...Re5 28.Bh3 Rd8 29.Qa1 Rh5 -+ { , with Black getting his hand on the white monarch eventually. } ) e3  ( 27...Rd8? { would have been simply too slow, as after } 28.gxf4 Rxf4 29.Rg3+ Kh8 30.Qb2 +- { he would have ended up being too paralyzed to continue attacking White anyhow. } ) 
28.gxf4  ( 28.Nxe3? fxe3 29.Rxe3 { was my initial idea for White when giving this puzzle a try myself, but the only thing I was able to achieve was a draw by perpetual check after the more or less forced } 29...Re5! 30.Rxe5 Qxe5 31.d8=Q Rxd8 32.Rxd8 Qe4 { . The white monarch is unable to dance away towards safety, e.g. } 33.Kf1 Qh1+ 34.Ke2 Bf3+ 35.Ke3 Qe1+ 36.Be2 Bxe2 37.Qxe2 Ng4+ 38.Kf3 Nxh2+ 39.Ke3 Ng4+= ) Qe4  
29.fxe3  { The last few moves reminded me of the poker scene from the movie 'Casino Royale', where both of the players believed in their respective hands so much that they did not hesitate to bet all of their chips to prove it. And although neither of them was bluffing it was a full house against a straight flush, if my memory serves me right, only one of them could be pronounced the winner... In this case, it turns out that despite all the ghosts of mate looming over the white king, he would have been able to escape unscathed just by running away towards the queenside. } Qh1+  
30.Kf2  Rd8  
31.Ke1  Rxd7  
32.Rxd7+  Nxd7  
33.Rd3  Ne5  
34.Rd8  Nf3+  
35.Ke2  Nd4+  
36.Kd2 $18 { The point I wish to make here is that - upon discovering the line starting with 27.g3 - as White I would have surely spent 90% of my time left on the clock to evaluate it properly. What is more, I would be purposefully calculating in repeat, checking the same lines many times, looking for possible holes that my tactical vision could be missing. And contrary to the principle explained earlier, this behavior would need to be assessed as correct, just because of the mere fact that if White would be right, he would also be winning on the spot... }  

