{ To summarize, after a comprehensive analysis of the entirety of information on calculation training available to the wider public, I have come to the solemn realization that it is probably the most misunderstood area of the game of chess that there is. Many players do not know how to exactly define calculation, others use wrong training methods or at least utilize them ineffectively. Even distinguished authors provide subpar examples quality-wise, while many adepts of the game seem to be perplexed by the sea of information or contradicting advice. In my opinion, the only antidote to this chaos lies in creating a detailed breakdown of the calculation process’ construction, for the purpose of determining the optimal type of exercises helping chess players practice key scenarios in this respect. Just as different stages of the calculation process pose a varying set of problems to solve, making sure that your own training regimen includes these elements is simply crucial. Below you shall find such a categorization, with a concise description of each and every one of those listed elements. More sophisticated problems arising within the given stage of the calculation process shall be discussed in much more detail within the examples featured in the latter part of this course. } 
1.--   

[Event "Calculate Like a Beast: The Moranda Method"]
[White "Introduction"]
[Black "IV. Construction Of The Calculation Process: 1. Vision and Perceptivity"]
[Result ""]
[FEN "rnbqkbnr/pppppppp/8/8/8/8/PPPPPPPP/RNBQKBNR w KQkq - 0 1"]

{ It all starts with vision, that is simply put the ability to spot a move or an idea which deserves attention. As opposed to ‘tactical vision’, which is the ability to recognize tactical patterns, pure vision is to be understood in a much broader manner, that is as a skill allowing us to find a candidate for a strong move, notwithstanding the nature of the reasoning behind it. } 
1.--   

[Event "Calculate Like a Beast: The Moranda Method"]
[White "Introduction"]
[Black "Javakhishvili, L. vs. Batsiashvili, N., Tbilisi 2023"]
[Result ""]
[FEN "r1r1n1k1/4bpp1/6b1/p1Np4/6P1/1P2BP2/P7/2RR1BK1 b - - 0 25"]

{ Black to play. } --   

[Event "Calculate Like a Beast: The Moranda Method"]
[White "Introduction"]
[Black "Javakhishvili, L. vs. Batsiashvili, N., Tbilisi 2023"]
[Result ""]
[FEN "r1r1n1k1/4bpp1/6b1/p1Np4/6P1/1P2BP2/P7/2RR1BK1 b - - 0 25"]

{ Black's position here is unpleasant for sure, as White's perfectly coordinated forced are putting pressure against her queenside, with the weak IQP on [d5] about to fall any moment now. In such situations, when statics speak in favor of the opponent, we are usually advised to seek dynamic countermeasures in order to destabilize the opponent's control over the position. } Bb1 $3 { A bolt from the blue sky! I presented this position a while ago to GM Mateusz Bartel who not only struggled to solve it, but even remarked that 'he could look at this position for many days straight, and the text move would still not have dawned up on him'. Humans usually operate namely in a problem-oriented manner. In this case, if the [d5]-pawn is hanging, let us try and defend it. This seems to be exactly the logic which Batsiashvili was following in the game when playing 25...Nc7 instead. Interestingly, this is also why some other students of mine, when instructed specifically to pay attention to the potential of the said bishop, would be rather trying to quickly transfer it to [f7] instead, obviously in vain. } ( 25...Nc7? { was met by Lela with } 26.Na4 { , when after the further } 26...Ne6 27.Rxd5 Rxc1 28.Bxc1 Rc8 29.Bc4 +- { she had little trouble converting the pawn into a full point. } ) ( { Naturally, another rather straightforward way of defending the problematic pawn would have been } 25...Nf6?! { , but I would not do it if I were you, unless you happen to be a true fan of prolonged, hopeless suffering. Now, after something like } 26.Bb5 Rc7 27.Na4 Rxc1 28.Rxc1 Rb8 29.Bf1 ± { , White would not only be in control over the crucial c-file, but also ready to start approaching the center with her king. In the meantime, the black forces do not look capable of generating any counterplay since their [d5]-passer remains securely blocked. } ) 
26.Rxb1  ( { Trying to keep as many pawns on the queenside as possible with } 26.a3 { would have been met by the stubborn } 26...Ba2! { , when after the further } 27.Rd3 Rab8 28.Rxd5 a4! 29.Nxa4 Rxc1 30.Bxc1 Bxb3= { White would most probably have to use her [a3]-pawn as bait in order to be allowed to restore her coordination without any undue adventures. } ) Bxc5  { Lo and behold, the main point behind 25...Bb1!! is revealed. White's excellent coordination is a matter of the past, as Black has managed to dispose of her useless light-squared bishop in return for the opponent's octopus-knight on [c5]. What is more, White does not have the time to capture on [d5] as her [e3]-bishop is under attack, while taking on [c5] now would have only strengthened the pawn with the arrival of the black rook to [c5]. Finally, Black is ready to equalize completely thanks to the inroads gained into White's camp along the c-file. } 
27.Kf2  Bxe3+  
28.Kxe3  Rc3+  
29.Bd3  Rac8  
30.Kd4  { And, just when it seemed White was about to squeeze out something out of this position after all, Black could answer with } g6 $5 { as after the further } 
31.Kxd5  Rd8+  
32.Ke4  Nf6+  
33.Ke3  a4  
34.bxa4  Ra3 $10 { White would once again be forced to part with both of her queenside pawns just to be able to consolidate her tangled position. }  

[Event "Calculate Like a Beast: The Moranda Method"]
