[Result ""]
[FEN "1r2r1k1/p4pp1/6n1/2pp2q1/7p/Q1P1PP1P/P1bRBBPK/4R3 b - - 0 23"]

{ Black to play. } --   

[Event "Calculate Like a Beast: The Moranda Method"]
[White "Introduction"]
[Black "Seo, Jung Min vs. Sharan, R., Parnu Rapid 2023"]
[Result ""]
[FEN "1r2r1k1/p4pp1/6n1/2pp2q1/7p/Q1P1PP1P/P1bRBBPK/4R3 b - - 0 23"]

{ Perceptivity, on the other hand, means the capacity to discover resources which many other chess players would describe as ‘invisible moves’, that is moves that for some reason tend to escape the attention of human players. It entails the ability to withstand certain limitations of our way thinking pertaining to optics, like for example looking for resources across the board even if the main battle is taking place exclusively on a small part of the board ‘global vision’, as I personally like to call it. In this post-Ragozin middlegame Black's position has started to get a little bit shaky, with a multitude of his pieces bishop on [c2] and pawns a7 and [c5] already hanging. He is not able to defend all of his weaknesses at the same time, so there is only one thing he can do in order to stay in the game - rely on dynamics... } Rxe3 $1 { What are we supposed to do when our position is hanging by a tread? Obviously, sacrifice even more material! But seriously, this move is tactically perfectly justified, as the rook is taboo on account of the ...Qg5-[g3]+ double-attack. But this is exactly what many, even strong players find hard to agree to in their games - applying 'real sacrifices', that is parting with material without the guarantee of recapturing it soon enough. } ( {(Instead, Sharan decided in favor of the equally pretty but not equally effective!)} 23...Nf4? { , as now after the cool } 24.Bf1! Bf5 25.Qxc5 Be6 26.Qxa7 +- { White just helped himself to the free meal and went on to convert the arising material advantage without any unnecessary hiccups along the way. } ) 
24.Rxc2  Nf4 $2 { Although this move turns out to be losing at the end of the day, I simply could not help myself and needed to quote the arising variations for the purpose of highlighting various types of invisible moves seen in chess practice. } ( 24...Rbe8!! {(is the beautiful resource which would have allowed Black to maintain the equilibrium. Admittedly however, not every player would be mentally ready to refrain from going for 24...[Nf4] a threat of mate validating the previous material sacrifice immediately in lieu of an apparently innocuous inclusion of the other rook into play. Upon closer inspection however, it becomes clear though that it is only now that the ...[Ng6]-[f4] begins to be dangerous as with the black rooks doubled along the e-file, securing the [g2]-pawn with [Be2]-[f1] would have cost White material. In other words, the majority of players is going to be uncomfortable with leaving a piece en prise, even for a short period of time. Under these circumstances, White's smartest choice would have probably been to simplify the situation after)} 25.Bxe3 Qg3+ 26.Kh1 Qxe1+ 27.Bg1 Rxe2 28.Rxe2 Qxe2 29.Qxc5 Qxa2 30.Qxa7 Qxa7 31.Bxa7 Nf4= { with a probable draw. } ) 
25.Bf1  Nxg2 $1 { In the ocean of visually attractive opportunities, this one stands out as being most critical. One of the main reasons for this could be that the immediate removal of the knight from [f4] actualizes the threat of ...[Qg5]-[e5]+. Interestingly, now White has only one move which leads to victory, and definitely not one which would stand out as representing the most straightforward continuation... } ( { The alternative } 25...Rxe1 26.Bxe1 Rb1 27.Rc1 Nxg2 28.Rxb1 Nxe1 { looked good for a second there, as Black would this way be threatening eventual mate without the need to fear about the fate of his [e1]-knight. Still, White would be able to consolidate with a short series of foxy consolidating moves, that is } 29.Kh1!! Qg3 30.Qb2!! Qxf3+ 31.Bg2 +- { with Black all of a sudden running out of checks. This way we have unraveled yet another subtype of moves which tend to escape the human eye - quiet moves. } ) 
26.Qxa7 $3 { This was not your very first candidate move when analyzing this position, am I right? Most of my titled students did not even consider this in the first place, at least not without me hinting at the need to broaden the list of ideas considered. A move like this is just so easy to miss as humans tend to take into account only moves that 'fit the narrative' of the position, or at least take place somewhere in the neighborhood of the main arena of action, in this case specifically the kingside. } ( { First of all, } 26.Bxg2 { stumbles upon } 26...Rxe1 27.Bxe1 Rb1! 28.Re2 Rxe1! 29.Rxe1 Qg3+ 30.Kg1 Qxe1+= { , when the white monarch would have not been able to avoid perpetual check. } ) ( { By comparison, } 26.Rxe3 Nxe3 27.Bxe3 Qg3+ 28.Kh1 Qxf3+ 29.Bg2 Qd1+ 30.Bg1 Qxc2 ⇆ { allows White to avert the forced draw for the time being, without simultaneously presenting him with serious winning chances. True, some of Black's queenside pawns are not going to survive for long but the counterplay associated with bringing the rook to the first rank should be enough to ultimately secure a draw for Black. } ) ( { Finally, the engine claims that White's second best attempt would have been the absolutely inhuman } 26.Ra1!? { , when Black would need to carry on playing insanely accurately in order to save his skin: } 26...Nf4!! 27.Qxa7 Rd8! 28.Re1!? Rxf3 29.Qe7 Rxf2+! 30.Rxf2 Qg3+ 31.Kh1 Qxf2 32.Qxd8+ Kh7 33.Qg5 f6! 34.Qg4 Qxe1 35.Qxf4 c4= { , and with the queenside pawns of White probably falling in the long haul and him also remaining with the so-called 'wrong' bishop from the point of view of future attempts to promote his [h3]-pawn, Black would just need to demonstrate moderate accuracy in order to stay safe. } ) Nxe1  ( 26...Rbe8!? {(would have worked like a charm as means of convincing White to either relinquish control over the e-file or letting go of the [g3]-square by taking on [e3] with the bishop in one way or another, was it not for the exquisite)} 27.Ree2 +- { leaving Black no other choice than to transpose into a lost endgame after massive simplification on [e2]. Such short, collinear moves tend to escape our attention as they, at least optically, do not seem to introduce any change to the status quo of the position. As much as this sounds reasonable, in this case the text move happens to be possible for one important reason - the inability of Black to infiltrate via [b1] with his rook anymore. } ) 
27.Qxb8+  Kh7  { Just when it seemed as if Black's sacrificial play was finally about to bear some fruit, White could bring in the cavalry with the most astonishing } 
28.Qb1 $3 { when after the necessary } Qf4+  ( 28...Nxf3+ 29.Kh1 Re1 30.Rc1+ +- { being the other fine tactical point behind this possibility. } ) 
29.Kh1 $1 Qxf3+  
30.Kg1  Nxc2  
31.Qxc2+  Kg8  
32.Bxe3  Qxe3+  
33.Kh1 $16 { this position would have yielded him plenty of winning chances thanks to the greater number of pawns left on the board as well as a higher degree of king safety. Out of all the incredible moves we have just witnessed in this example, I personally believe 28. [Qb1]!! to be the easiest one to miss a distant, backward move across the whole board which defends everything and introduces a discovered check motif on top of that, especially if you consider the fact that it must have constituted White's underlying idea already in advance, when embarking on the similarly abstract idea of consuming a pawn on [a7] two moves earlier... }  

