[FEN "3rrbk1/5p1p/p4np1/1pp3B1/1n2P1b1/1B3NN1/PP3PPP/R3R1K1 b - - 0 19"]

{ This game had generated a heated discussion among a couple of GM friends of mine during an open tournament last summer. The primary reason for this was that White was the driver, and our ride back to the hotel was at least 30 minutes overdue as his opponent was hesitating to resign in a hopeless position far later towards the endgame. Our irritation by this circumstance sparked an additional roast of the aforementioned friend on how could he have allowed himself to land into such a double-edged position in the Berlin Defense against a lower-rated opponent in the first place yes, GMs can be savage at times. In any case, however, after some ten minutes of brief analysis we concluded that the game had indeed been a balanced encounter for most of the time, and then decided to check the correctness of our assumptions with an engine. Now imagine our surprise when learning that in this position } Nd7 $3 { would have granted Black a virtually winning position on the spot. Needless to say, nobody even remotely considered this to be a legitimate candidate move, not only because this move drops an exchange apparently for free, but also due to the optically forced nature of White's last move which was 19.Bg5. } ( { The game as well as our post-mortem analysis featured } 19...Be7?! 20.Ne5! Be6 21.Bxe6 fxe6 22.a3 Nc2 23.Nc6 { , and if only Black had now gone for something like } 23...Kf7 24.Nxe7 Rxe7 25.Bxf6 Kxf6 26.e5+ Kf7 27.Rad1 Rd5 28.Rxd5 exd5 29.Rc1 Nd4 30.Rxc5 Ke6 ⇆ { , he would have most certainly not been worse here based on the activity of his monarch alone. } ) 
20.Bxd8  Rxd8  { Time to evaluate the position. Black is an exchange down, but this seems to be the only inconvenience he is suffering from. White's problems, on the other hand, are just getting started. His bishop is about to be killed off any moment now, with this leading to collateral damage in form of the connection between his rooks being cut by the retreat of said bishop to [d1]. The vacated [c5]-square will most certainly be utilized by Black's [d7]-knight, when both of them would all of a sudden be annoyingly targeting the [d3]-outpost deep in the opponent's camp. What is more, Black's dark-squared bishop looks uncontested here, with the long dark diagonal representing a juicy area of focus, or alternatively, the [c1]-[h6] diagonal, especially in conjunction with the ...[Bg4xf3] & ...[Nd7]-[e5] combo. In the meantime, White's forces appear to be defenseless to stop all this, in particular with his [g3]-knight looking like a mere bystander. Sample lines confirm that White is about to get dominated on all fronts here: } 
21.Ng5  ( 21.Reb1 c4 22.Bd1 { is an elaborate way of maintaining a quantum of coordination suggested by the silicon beast, although after the further } 22...Nd3 23.Be2 Bg7 24.Bxd3 cxd3 25.Nd2 Ne5 -+ { the threat of ...[Ne5]-[c4] simply must prove decisive, eventually based on the removal of the blockader in front of the powerful [d3]-pawn. } ) Ne5  
22.h3  Bd7  
23.f4  { and just when it seemed that not all hope was yet lost for White, Black uncorks } Be7 $1 { when after } 
24.fxe5  Bxg5  
25.Red1  c4  
26.a3  Nd3  
27.Rab1  Bc6  
28.Bc2  Bf4  
29.Ne2  Bxe5 $19 { Black's total control over the position would have been beyond any doubt. By this point of time he would also happen to be only a pawn down already, with the poor fellow on [e4] being next die. }  

[Event "Calculate Like a Beast: The Moranda Method"]
