{ Black to play. } --   

[Event "Calculate Like a Beast: The Moranda Method"]
[White "Introduction"]
[Black "Sadhwani, Raunak vs. Chigaev, Maksim, Biel 2021"]
[Result ""]
[FEN "8/2r1k3/R4p2/Pp2nP1p/1P2p2P/KNr3P1/4R3/8 b - - 0 34"]

{ Once we have already figured out the evaluation of the position, now is the time to create a list of fitting candidate moves. The search for candidates itself will be usually reliant on the so-called ‘CCTV method’ popularized by Judit Polgar, that is considering checks, captures and threats in the first place. One must not forget that, on a conceptual level, there always needs to be a logical link present between the evaluation of the position and the elected candidate moves in the sense that the considered candidate moves should aim to bring into fruition the goals set forth by the evaluation process itself. Black is a pawn down here, but he should not be particularly fearful of the future thanks to the activity of his pieces. One specific idea coming into consideration would be to double up his rooks along the third rank quickly for the purpose of claiming White's knight, or even organizing mating threats against his monarch. In this context, claiming the [g3]-pawn looks like combining business with pleasure. But, as is quite frequently the case in highly tactical situations, logical assumptions stripped of concrete calculation may not suffice to figure out the optimal follow-up. } Rf3 $3 { As we shall witness in a moment from now, this was Black's only winning move. Stopping one square before [g3] is as disappointing as promising your kids a visit to the ice cream parlor after preschool, just to tell them right beforehand that 'actually, bedtime comes early today'. Then again, here Black prevails as the idea mentioned above was supposed to constitute just a prelude to the motif which will only eventually make all the difference. } ( 34...Rxg3? { sure was tempting, but after the further } 35.Ka2 Rcc3 36.Nd4 Ra3+ 37.Kb1 Rg1+ 38.Kc2= { Black was unable to generate any serious threats against the white king afterwards. The main reason for this was that - in order to be successful in a possible attack - Black would furthermore need to include his knight into play via [c4]. This, on the other hand, would have allowed White to organize some vicious counterplay with [Re2xe4]+. } ) ( 34...e3? { would have certainly made sense, but then after something like } 35.Re6+ Kf7 36.a6 Nc4+ 37.Ka2 Rd7 38.Rc6= { Black would not have been able to escort his [e3]-pawn all the way to promotion square, at the very least not without letting go of White's passer on [a6] at the same time. } ) 
35.Ka2  e3  { The point. It is not the king of White that Black needed to be after here. Instead, the threat of hitting the opponent with ...[Rc7]-[c3] was a 'decoy idea' intended to lure his king towards the second rank. Once this happens, with ...[e4]-[e3] and ...[Rf3]-[f2] coming right after, White proves to be unable to put Black's passed pawn to a halt without incurring significant material losses. Sample lines confirm this statement: } 
36.Nd4  ( 36.Re1 Rf2+ 37.Kb1 Rc3 38.Nd4 Rc4 39.Nb3 e2 -+ { , when it becomes apparent that attempts at postponing the movement of Black's passed pawn without the need to part with material would have resulted in landing in a hopelessly passive position. } ) Rf2  
37.Kb3  Rd7  
38.Rxe3  Rxd4  
39.Rb6  Rd1  
40.Rxb5  Kd6 $19 { Some accuracy would surely still be required for Black to convert the extra piece into a full point, but putting his pieces behind White's pawns in conjunction with checks against his exposed king would have sufficed for a win in the long haul. }  

