{ Once we have established the list of candidate moves, then comes the time to separate the wheat from the chaff - reducing their number accordingly usually to two. The first thing to be done in this respect is typically elimination, that is: rejecting any candidate move which fails tactically already at first glance or maybe just does not correspond with the needs of the position after all. A technical remark: although elimination is mostly useful with a larger number of available candidates, for the sake of clarity, every such exercise included in this course will ask you to choose between two options only. } 
1.--   

[Event "Calculate Like a Beast: The Moranda Method"]
[White "Introduction"]
[Black "Ding Liren vs. Nepomniachtchi, Ian, World Championship Match, Astana 2023"]
[Result ""]
[FEN "3r4/p1QP1pk1/1p2p1p1/2p2nP1/2P4q/P1P5/3R1P2/4KB2 w - - 0 32"]

{ 32.Kd1 or 32.Qxd8? } 
32.--   

[Event "Calculate Like a Beast: The Moranda Method"]
[White "Introduction"]
[Black "Ding Liren vs. Nepomniachtchi, Ian, World Championship Match, Astana 2023"]
[Result ""]
[FEN "3r4/p1QP1pk1/1p2p1p1/2p2nP1/2P4q/P1P5/3R1P2/4KB2 w - - 0 32"]

{ We see a famous game here, so I apologize in advance if you already happen to know this example. White uncorked an interesting novelty in the opening stage and obtained serious winning chances later on in the middlegame. Now, however, he is facing a choice between capitalizing on his advantage right away by capturing on [d8] } { (but possibly allowing a perpetual check at the same time) } { , or parrying any such countermeasures of his opponent at the price of prolonging the process } { (without keeping the guarantee of winning either) } { . 32.Kd1 or 32.Qxd8? } 
32.Qxd8 $1 ( { Instead, in the game White went for the prophylactic } 32.Kd1? { when after the further } 32...Qxg5 33.Kc2 { I think Black's best chance to save the game would be to opt in favor of } 33...Qh4! 34.Kb3 Nh6 ⇆ {(when his queen would have always been ready to harass White with ideas like ...[Qh4]-[f4] e4, with the knight about to be transferred towards the defensive square [f7] after the preparatory ...[f7]-[f5] one day.Many armchair experts criticized Ding for not picking up the gauntlet here. True, with the benefit of hindsight or rather - engine assistance the lines look pretty straightforward and logical, but the psychological burden of potentially allowing the opponent to escape with a draw must have taken its toll on him. In his defense, I can confirm what has been emphasized already by many other commentators - the main variation leading to a win was literally spiked with optically dangerous moments which could discourage just about any player in the world from pursuing this idea any further.)} ) Qe4+  
33.Re2  Qb1+  
34.Kd2  Qb2+  
35.Kd3  Qb1+  
36.Rc2 $1 { The first spooky moment for White has just arrived - it is never a pleasant experience to have to allow one's opponent to capture a piece with a check. } Qd1+  ( { In all honesty, however, } 36...Qxf1+ 37.Kd2 Qxf2+ 38.Kc1 Qf4+ 39.Kb1 +- { would have clearly been all bark and no bite from Black's side, as [Qd8]-[f6]+ followed by [d7]-[d8=Q] would be now becoming inevitable. } ) 
37.Ke4 $1 { The die has now been cast. Almost everyone would have been comfortable with sacrificing a small chunk of their material advantage for the sake of consolidating, but how about putting much more than that on the line? } Qxc2+  
38.Bd3  ( 38.Kf3? { , with the idea of evading Black's knight checks eventually on [h3], would have been White's last chance to go wrong in view of the fancy anticipatory move } 38...Kh7!! { . The point behind it is that White is not in time to bring his queen to [f6], as this would drop the [d7]-pawn due to ...[Qc2]-[d1]+. At the same time, by comparison } 39.Qe8 { would not have been as effective as after } 39...Qd1+ 40.Kf4 Qd2+ 41.Kg4 Qd1+ 42.f3 Qd6! { . White would most certainly need to settle for an equal position after something like } 43.Qxf7+ Kh8 44.d8=Q+ Qxd8 45.Qxe6 Kg7= ) Nd6+  
39.Ke5  Qxd3  
40.Qf6+  Kh7  
41.d8=Q  Nxc4+  
42.Kf4  e5+  
43.Kg4 $18 { With this being high-time for Black to throw in the towel, as he is officially going to run out of checks in a moment from now. }  

[Event "Calculate Like a Beast: The Moranda Method"]
[White "Introduction"]
[Black "Giri, Anish vs. So, Wesley, Warsaw Blitz 2023"]
[Result ""]
[FEN "8/5k2/8/6p1/1p4P1/2p4p/2K2P2/5N2 b - - 0 60"]

{ 60...Ke6 or 60...Kf6? } --   

[Event "Calculate Like a Beast: The Moranda Method"]
[White "Introduction"]
[Black "Giri, Anish vs. So, Wesley, Warsaw Blitz 2023"]
[Result ""]
[FEN "8/5k2/8/6p1/1p4P1/2p4p/2K2P2/5N2 b - - 0 60"]

{ As opposed to elimination, comparison proves to be helpful when dealing with two } { (rarely more than two) } { moves that look similar to one another or aim to achieve the same goal in the given position. The difference between such alternatives shall often times be minuscule, so we will need to remember to compare them with one another very strictly, and not just based on our optical preference or even gut feeling. I think that it was absolutely clear for both of the players at this point that it is only Black who can win this position. Black's queenside pawns are successfully absorbing the attention of the white king, with his knight being unable to relieve him of defensive duties in this respect because of the need to keep an eye on the opponent's [h3]-passer as well. In view of this, we could draw an additional conclusion that including Black's monarch into play should be decisive for the final outcome of the game. But does it matter at all where does Black put his king next as long as it makes him more centralized? 60...Ke6 or 60...Kf6? } Kf6 $1 { Only this move turns out to be winning at the end of the day. } ( { In the game, So played } 60...Ke6? {(, which is something that any self-respecting grandmaster would have gone for without hesitation when under time-trouble. The reason for this is that, as opposed to 60...Kf6, the text appears to be more flexible - Black can namely proceed according to plan towards the kingside, but is also ready to switch directions and head towards [c4] in case of need. Giri did not put up a fight and resigned after a couple more moves - I can only imagine his surprise upon learning after the game that)} 61.f4! gxf4 62.Nh2 Kd5 63.Kd3= { , with the white knight reaching [f3] next, would have granted him a fortress-like position and therefore also a draw. Black is surprisingly unable to penetrate via any of the wings even upon the use of pawn-sacrifices, with White's remaining [g4]-pawn serving as a reminder that he is not supposed to get too cocky about his winning attempts either. } ) 
61.f4  ( 61.Nh2 Ke5 62.Nf3+ Kf4 63.Nh2 Ke4 -+ {(is more or less what happened in the game. White landed in a zugzwang, with the only choice left for him to be made is via which square f3, [e3] or [d3] is he going to allow the black monarch to invade.)} ) gxf4  
62.Nh2  Kg5 $1 { Now we see the difference - putting the king to [f6] in the first place made him admittedly less centralized, but also neutralized White's possible defensive by arriving with the king to [g5] before the rival manages to establish a knight on [f3]. } 
{ Now Black wins rather trivially with something like } 
63.Kd3  Kh4  
64.Kc2  f3  
65.Nxf3+  Kxg4  
66.Nh2+  Kg3  
67.Nf1+  Kg2 $19 { White is surely not in time to consume the [b4]-pawn with his knight and come back all the way to [h1] before Black promotes his pawn to a queen. }  

[Event "Calculate Like a Beast: The Moranda Method"]
[White "Introduction"]
[Black "IV. Construction Of The Calculation Process: 4. Forced Lines & Intermediate Moves"]
