[FEN "rnbqkbnr/pppppppp/8/8/8/8/PPPPPPPP/RNBQKBNR w KQkq - 0 1"]

{ Finally, once we have already decided which of the candidate moves appeals to us the most, it is time to start calculating its consequences. Some of such variations can take the form of ‘forced’ ones, meaning that they will constitute longer sequences of only moves. Naturally, forced lines } { (or lines containing forced moves) } { are typically to be prioritized within our calculation process, since it is relatively easier to reach their respective end than in case of non-forcing ones. } 
1.--   

[Event "Calculate Like a Beast: The Moranda Method"]
[White "Introduction"]
[Black "Based on Matviishen, Viktor vs. Raja, H., Sunnyvale 2023"]
[Result ""]
[FEN "3r1rk1/pb3ppp/1p3n2/2q1n3/4P3/P2B2NP/1B1R2P1/3Q1R1K w - - 0 22"]

{ Evaluate 22.Rxf6. } 
22.--   

[Event "Calculate Like a Beast: The Moranda Method"]
[White "Introduction"]
[Black "Based on Matviishen, Viktor vs. Raja, H., Sunnyvale 2023"]
[Result ""]
[FEN "3r1rk1/pb3ppp/1p3n2/2q1n3/4P3/P2B2NP/1B1R2P1/3Q1R1K w - - 0 22"]

{ In this tense situation arising out of a Nimzo Rubinstein/Saemisch, White seems to be locked and loaded for the thematic exchange-sacrifice on [f6]. This way, he would be able to expose the black king along the g-file as well as, in the future, the long dark diagonal. It looks tempting, but only concrete calculation can prove to us whether it works or not. } 
22.Rxf6 $1 gxf6  ( { As much as simplification is almost always a good idea when defending, here } 22...Nxd3? { would have led to trouble for the black king even faster than in the game as after the further } 23.Rxd3 Rxd3 24.Qxd3 gxf6 25.Nf5 Rc8 { White would be sealing the deal already thanks to the most accurate } 26.Qf3! Kf8 27.a4 +- { , when the incoming skewer [Bb2]-[a3] would have proven to be decisive. } ) 
23.Nh5  Rd6  
24.Bxe5  ( 24.Bc4? Rxd2 25.Qxd2 Nxc4 26.Qh6 Qxh5 27.Qxh5 Nxb2 28.Qg4+ Kh8 29.Qf4 { initially looks like a risk-free option to keep on playing for a win, as Black cannot possibly avoid the need to return one of his light pieces due to some clever double-attacks by White. In reality, however, after the further } 29...Rc8 30.Qxf6+ Kg8 31.Qxb2 Bxe4= { it turns out that White will not manage to break through the opponent's defenses. ...[Rc8]-[c2] always needs to be considered, while ...[Be4]-[g6] will provide the black monarch with enough security against checks. } ) fxe5  { We have just reached the first moment when some of my students would be starting to doubt White's cause as a queen check from [g4] would now be met by ...[Rd6]-[g6]. Hopefully you, dear Reader, kept on going! } ( 24...Qxe5? { with the intention of reinforcing the kingside with ...[Qe5]-[g5] would have been the way to go for Black, but fails tactically on account of } 25.Qg4+ Qg5 26.Nxf6+! Rxf6 27.Qxg5+ +- ) 
25.Ba6 $3 { There you go - that is a double-attack and a decoy in one. Capturing on [d2] results in mate in two, so the follow-up keeps on being forced. } Bxa6  ( { Just for the record, } 25...Rfd8 { leads to a winning position for White by virtue of his material advantage after the relatively simple } 26.Qg4+ Kf8 27.Qg7+ Ke7 28.Qg5+ Kd7 29.Rxd6+ Qxd6 30.Bxb7 +- ) 
26.Rxd6  Be2 $1 { The fun continues! Black's bishop is clearly taboo so the knight needs to beat it. } 
27.Nf6+  Kh8  
28.Qd2 $1 { Statistically speaking, about 80% of my pupils were positive at this point of time that this is the end of the line, with Black having no other choice but throwing in the towel in view of the [Qd2]-[h6] threat with mate to come. In my humble opinion, the reason for missing Black's final defensive try lies in the difficulty to find resources which are optically not designed to directly neutralize a given idea, but rather work based on some more or less unexpected coordination-based tendency. } Qf2 $1 { It is not over until Black says it is over! All of a sudden, White needs to stop celebrating for a second and gather their thoughts from anew in order to find the only winning move. } ( 28...Rc8? { would have been relatively less tenacious in view of } 29.Rd8+ Rxd8 30.Qxd8+ Kg7 31.Ne8+ Kf8 32.Nd6+ Kg7 33.Nf5+ Kg6 34.Qd2 +- { . } ) 
29.Nxh7 $1 { Annihilation of the defense at its finest. The remaining sample lines confirm White's win, but also the need for him to remain precise till the very end. } ( { But obviously not } 29.Qh6? { due to } 29...Qf1+ 30.Kh2 Qf4+ 31.Qxf4 exf4= { and in the absence of queens on the board, mate would have been a matter of the past already. } ) Rg8  ( 29...Rc8 30.Rf6! Qg3 31.Rxf7 +- { and fortunately for White, his rival would still be a couple of moves away from landing a single check. } ) 
30.Ng5  Qf1+  
31.Kh2  Qf4+  
32.Qxf4  exf4  
33.Nxf7+  Kg7  
34.Ng5 $18 { With Black about to lose yet another pawn soon, White's victory becomes just a question of easy technique. }  

[Event "Calculate Like a Beast: The Moranda Method"]
[White "Introduction"]
[Black "Based on Gukesh, D. vs. Praggnanandhaa, R., Warsaw Rapid 2024"]
[Result ""]
[FEN "2r2rk1/1bq4p/1p2p3/p2pP1p1/P3np2/BP3PP1/2P4P/R2QRBK1 b - - 0 24"]

{ Evaluate 24...b5. } --   

[Event "Calculate Like a Beast: The Moranda Method"]
[White "Introduction"]
[Black "Based on Gukesh, D. vs. Praggnanandhaa, R., Warsaw Rapid 2024"]
[Result ""]
[FEN "2r2rk1/1bq4p/1p2p3/p2pP1p1/P3np2/BP3PP1/2P4P/R2QRBK1 b - - 0 24"]

{ Sometimes a forced line gets however ‘interrupted’ by a more or less unexpected move, which either changes the nature of the variation back to a more general } { (non-forced) } { one or just leaves us no other choice but to do some additional branching at this point of time – we call them ‘intermediate moves’. These tend to be rather unpleasant as, on one hand, they disturb the natural flow of the calculated line, and on the other, introduce a certain degree of chaos into our calculation process, especially if we had not anticipated their emergence from the very beginning. This position could have arisen on the board in this high-profile encounter between two super-talents } { (and future World Champions?) } { from India. The black rook on [f8] is under attack, but it turns out that Black can just as well ignore this threat, provided that he decides to take advantage of the suspicious situation of the white king after } b5 $1 
25.Bxf8  { If you want to suffer already, make sure at least that you are not doing it for free. Honestly speaking however, other options at this point were even more straightforward for Black than the text. } ( 25.fxe4 Qb6+ 26.Kh1 b4 27.Bc1 dxe4 -+ { is just crushing, as White is unable to defend his king along the long light diagonal or against Black's rampaging central pawns. } ) ( 25.Qd4 b4 26.Bb2 Qf7! 27.fxe4 dxe4 -+ { looks a tad safer for the white monarch at first, with the objective evaluation however still being hopeless for him in view of, among others, the additional ...[Rc8xc2] threat. } ) Qb6+  
26.Kg2  Qf2+  
27.Kh1  Nxg3+ $1 { A necessary inclusion - Black needs to remove everything possibly obstructing his [b7]-bishop, even if it is supposed to be his very own piece! } ( { By comparison, } 27...Rxc2? { admittedly looks scary for White and compelling for Black, but after the further } 28.Re2 Qxf3+ 29.Kg1 Rxe2 30.Qxe2 Qc3 31.Ra2 Kxf8 32.Qh5= { White would be getting away with a draw thanks to some perpetual check ideas on the kingside. } ) 
28.hxg3  d4 $1 
29.Bg2  Rxc2  { Please note the timely fashion in which Black's threats arrive one after the other, granting White no relief at all. } 
30.Rg1  ( { It was already too late for a bailout with } 30.Qxc2 Qxc2 31.Rac1 { , as then after something like } 31...Qg6 32.g4 Kxf8 33.Rc7 d3 -+ { the white rooks would be having a hard time generating counterplay and putting Black's [d3]-passer to a halt at the same time. } ) Qxg3  { Unexpectedly, corridor mate along the h-file starts representing White's most burning issue, with pressure exerted diagonally and horizontally serving a complementary role, that is reducing the number of available defensive resources almost to zero. } 
31.Qxc2  ( 31.Qe1 { would have been met by yet another extraordinary intermezzo in form of } 31...Rf2! { , when after the more or less forced } 32.Qxf2 Qxf2 33.Rgf1 Qh4+ 34.Kg1 Kxf8 35.axb5 Qg3 -+ { , ...[g5]-[g4] would have led to White's resignation any moment. } ) d3 $1 { I hope you have not had enough of those foxy intermediate moves so far! As we shall see shortly, this one was the final touch required for Black to be able to claim the full point in this position. } ( { Incidentally, the natural } 31...Qh4+? { would have been too hasty because of } 32.Bh3 Qxh3+ 33.Qh2 Bxf3+ 34.Rg2 Bxg2+ 35.Kg1 Qc3 36.Qh6! Qxa1+ 37.Kxg2= { , with Black probably needing to settle for a draw by perpetual check considering White's mating threat on [g7]. } ) 
32.Qd2  Qh4+  
33.Bh3  Qxh3+  
34.Qh2  Qxf3+  
35.Rg2  d2 $19 { Now the difference between this and the previous line after 31...[Qh4]+? can be seen in full blaze. Despite being two rooks up, White is paralyzed, and therefore defenseless against pawn-promotion on [d1] followed by the recapture of the white rook on [g2] after a barrage of checks as well. }  

[Event "Calculate Like a Beast: The Moranda Method"]
[White "Introduction"]
[Black "IV. Construction Of The Calculation Process: 5. The Fifth Element - Find The Mistake"]
[Result ""]
[FEN "rnbqkbnr/pppppppp/8/8/8/8/PPPPPPPP/RNBQKBNR w KQkq - 0 1"]

{ Solving puzzles is like democracy – there are drawbacks to it as a training method, but nothing better has been discovered so far instead. What I mean by this specifically is that I cannot imagine anybody becoming a grandmaster without the extensive use of exercises but, at the same time, solving is not perfect as such as the mere fact that you have been given a concrete position to be solved already biases you into knowing that there is something special about it – that it is now the time for you to focus and find a strong move. In a practical game, on the other hand, nobody gives you such a metaphysical pat on the shoulder indicating the moment for you to focus more. In order to neutralize this phenomenon, a sophisticated training method has been created in which – instead of a position from a game – the player receives a fragment thereof featuring a series of five to ten moves, and is supposed to not only locate which of the players had committed a mistake and when, but also to figure out what the supposed improvement was. The point behind this approach is to improve the general awareness level of the given player, but also to develop a sixth sense for critical moments of the game, that is unprecedented situations in which punishing the opponent for their mistakes becomes all of a sudden possible, but only based on perfect play. The first subtype of such exercises is going to involve situations in which one of the players was winning all the time, erred however at a certain point of the game, but his rival did not manage to recognize his window of opportunity. One remark from the technical point of view needs to be made here – if you see that one of the players won the game relatively easily, in order to solve such a puzzle you will logically just need to focus on the moves of the losing side. } 
1.--   

