{ Despite being a pawn down, White is completely winning here. The main reason for this is the weakness of Black's king, with threats along the [b1]-[h7] diagonal about to start hurting him the most once his [e4]-pawn gets eaten up. } 
22.Nxg7  ( { A fairly logical move to begin with. Neutralizing the ...Bg7xc3 threat as well as removing an important defender of the black monarch cannot be possibly bad, although } 22.Nxh6 { also happens to be winning, as indicated by the silicon beast. } ) Kxg7  
23.Bxe4  Bd7  { It must have been a sad necessity for Black to be forced to play this slow defensive move, especially as this gave White the time to rub even more salt into his wounds with } 
24.Qd2  Rae8  ( 24...Nf4 { would have been a brilliant defensive resource for Black, was it not for the trivial } 25.Qxf4 Qxf4 26.Bxf4 Rxf4 27.Rad1 Be8 28.b5 +- { with Black once again paying the piper in material for his previous reckless play. } ) 
25.Qxh6+  Kg8  
26.Bg5 $2 { This move is the problem-child. As far as I remember from our training session, Lukas mentioned that he had seen some winning lines, but wanted to finish the game in style, hence this flashy idea. Let me just say that, as his coach, I did not appreciate that response too much... } ( { The engine claims that any reasonable defense of the f2-pawn, e.g. } 26.Re2 { , would have done the trick, with } 26...Rxe4 { being met by } 27.Ng6! { . Personally however, I am a fan of simplicity, with }  {26.Qg6+ Qxg6 27.Nxg6 Rf6 28.Bd5+ Kg7 29.Rxe8 Bxe8 30.Nh4 +- being my favorite from the practical perspective. } ) Rxe4 $1 { At first glance, this move looks truly mysteriously. Why would Black namely want to eliminate this bishop if White's [f2]-pawn is there for the taking instead? Well, let us find out! } ( { Instead, } 26...Ne5? { was Rodrigue-Lemieux' answer, with White not making use of the relatively simple } 27.Be3 Re6 28.Qg5+ Ng7 29.Bd5 +- { winning an exchange, and thus also eventually the game itself. } ) ( { By comparison to the text, the move order starting with } 26...Qxf2+? 27.Kh1 Rxe4 { would have allowed White to consolidate cleverly after } 28.Qg6+ Ng7 29.Qxe4 g3 30.Re2 +- ) 
27.Rxe4  Qxf2+  
28.Kh1  Ne5 $3 { A lovely tactical trickshot indeed! This way, Black vacates the [c6]-square for his bishop for the purpose of harassing the white king along the [h1]-[a8] diagonal. } ( { Just for the record, } 28...g3? { would have once again been not enough for Black on account of } 29.Qg6+ Ng7 30.hxg3 Qxg3 31.Re3 +- { and the black queen runs out of checks. } ) 
29.Ng6 $7 ( { Pulling the safety break was mandatory here, as one thing White does not want to see happening is } 29.Rxe5?? Rf6 -+ { when ...[Bd7]-[c6]+ arrives with a murderous effect, in case you wondered. With the dust settling already, Black should now be more than glad to accept a draw by threefold repetition after } ) Qf3+  
30.Kg1  Qf2+ $10  

