{ Another scenario in this context includes balanced games in which one of the players committed an almost invisible mistake, which then also went unpunished, with the game eventually escalating into equality. This case is far trickier as there you will usually need to verify the soundness of play of both opponents. To picture this better, if the game fragment is five moves long, it means that you will need to check 9-10 different positions, all this with a high degree of accuracy and without spending excessive amounts of time on the clock either... Try to find the mistake in the following continuation. Then proceed to the next informational to see the solution. } Rfc8  
15.Nb3  a5  
16.Bb5  Ne4  
17.a4  Qg5  
18.Kf1  Qh4  
19.Ra2  Qe7  
20.Ke2  Nxc5  
21.Nxc5  Rxc5  
22.Rc1  Rac8  
23.Rd2 $15  

[Event "Calculate Like a Beast: The Moranda Method"]
[White "Introduction"]
[Black "Basso, Pier Luigi vs. Pichot, Alan, Casablanca 2023"]
[Result ""]
[FEN "r4rk1/pp2qpp1/5n1p/2Pp1b2/3Q4/2P1P3/P2NBPPP/R3K2R b KQ - 0 14"]

{ White is half-a-pawn up here but has very little reason to be overjoyed really. His [c5]-pawn is extremely weak, and will therefore sooner or later fall. This is furthermore going to lead to a situation in which White's remaining [c3]-pawn shall be much easier to attack than the black [d5]-counterpart. Still, this would be probably the smartest way to proceed for White - gaining enough time to castle and then using a solid outpost on [d4] to organize his defenses. } Rfc8  
15.Nb3  a5  
16.Bb5 $2 { Everything was going 'according to plan' until White decided to try for more, that is to prevent ...[a5]-[a4] and at least pretend that Black's attempt to recapture a pawn with ...[Nf6]-[d7] might not immediately work as the white bishop is now eyeing the said [d7]-square. White's 16th move was therefore the one which stood out as being suspicious, but did you also recognize how could Black have punished him for that? } ( { In light of the above, } 16.Bd3 Bxd3 17.Qxd3 a4 18.Nd4 Rxc5 19.O-O Rac8 20.Ne2 Ne4 21.Rab1 ⩱ { would have been my personal recommendation for White. We would have been a little passive out here for sure, but not yet at risk of ending up down on material. } ) a4 $1 { This must have escaped the attention of both of the players, each of them for a different reason probably. White must have assumed that he had just neutralized such a possibility a second ago by bringing his bishop to [b5]. Black, on the other hand, might have not connected the dots that this inclusion is supposed to serve as a tiny, yet meaningful detriment to White's coordination of pieces... } ( { Pichot's manner of handling this situation seemed very much to the point, as after } 16...Ne4? 17.a4 Qg5 18.Kf1 Qh4 19.Ra2 Qe7 20.Ke2 Nxc5 21.Nxc5 Rxc5 22.Rc1 Rac8 23.Rd2 ⩱ {(Black got the pawn back, deprived the white knight of castling rights and even threatened mate once along the way. Still, White remains solid as in the absence of knight on the board, Black is unable to effectively evict the 'brain' of the opponent's defensive setup, that is his queen on [d4]. Black is certainly not worse here and may keep on testing his rival by interchangeably putting pressure against [c3] and other kingside pawns of White, but the most reasonable result at the end of the day should be and also was a draw.)} ) 
17.Bxa4  b6 $1 { There you go! All of a sudden it turns out that the black pawn on [b6] is taboo due to ...[Rc8]-[c4] netting White's bishop on [a4]. Burying his head in the sand does not look like a feasible option either, since the incoming ...[b6xc5] followed by ...[c5]-[c4] would have been capable of cutting the communications between the white queen and bishop just as much as before. To add insult to injury, more tactical methods of defending this position fail similarly, as demonstrated by the sample lines below: } 
18.c6  ( 18.Qf4 { would have been met by } 18...Bc2 19.O-O Bd3! 20.Rfd1 Be2 21.Rd2 Bc4 -+ { with the fate of White's light-squared bishop once again being sealed. } ) Qa3  
19.Bb5  Qxb3 $1 { The fine tactical point behind Black's previous play. Now after something like } 
20.axb3  Rxa1+  
21.Ke2  Rxh1  
22.c7  Bd7  
23.Qxb6  Bxb5+  
24.Qxb5  Ra1 $19 { Black will eventually eliminate White's only hope for counterplay on [c7] and carry on converting his considerable material advantage rather effortlessly. I can imagine you shrugging right now and saying ‘OK, so what? Nothing new under the sun, I know all this already!’ Fair enough, but please be honest – has this knowledge translated over the course of your own chess career into a respective adjustment of your personal calculation training program? In other words – does your daily calculation routine include exercises which help you improve at handling all of the stages of the calculation process discussed previously? If not, I invite you to the next chapter, where I describe the details of my proposed method of training chess calculation. }  

