{ The calculation training method which I use when working with my selected students is comprised of a set of five puzzles, each of them pertaining to a separate element of the calculation process, which are once again: 1. Vision & Perceptivity 2. Evaluation & Candidate Moves 3. Elimination & Comparison 4. Forced Lines & Intermediate Moves 5. Find The Mistake } { (as a separate training method) } { Such a five-puzzle set represents a singular, comprehensive calculation training unit. Dealing with it exhausts in my opinion the need for further calculation exercise on the given training day as it grants you exposure to the entirety of problems which can theoretically appear within the calculation process. You are naturally free to do more than that, but the quality of your training efforts afterwards might suffer as a result. And trust me when I say it – already after a single calculation training unit like this you are simply going to be too worn out to even want to keep on working on calculation on the very same day anymore... You will find twelve such sets in this book, organized subjectively by myself into three chapters } { (four sets each) } { , in accordance with their difficulty level: Chapter 1: Let’s Just Skip The Warm-Up } { (Easy) } { Chapter 2: No Pain = No Gain } { (Intermediate) } { Chapter 3: Train Insane Or Remain The Same } { (Hard) } 
1.--   

[Event "Calculate Like a Beast: The Moranda Method"]
[White "The Moranda Method"]
[Black "⚠️ How To Use And Score The Exercises ⚠️ "]
[Result ""]
[FEN "rnbqkbnr/pppppppp/8/8/8/8/PPPPPPPP/RNBQKBNR w KQkq - 0 1"]

{ Just as it was the case with my second book ‘Supreme Chess Understanding: Statics & Dynamics’, for the sake of making the whole solving process as entertaining as possible, the sets have been prepared in a quiz format, with solutions accompanied by a short explanation regarding the scoring system for the given puzzle, depending on the fine points you may have spotted or missed while giving them a try. On Chessable this means that each puzzle is given as an informational with the starting position and the puzzle prompt followed by a trainable puzzle where you can enter your proposed solution. Correct answers to each puzzle from chapters one, two and three are worth two, three and five points, respectively. All this means that you can earn up to two hundred points altogether. After working through the entirety of the course, in order to learn what is your estimated playing strength in the area of calculation, you should divide the total number of achieved points by two, and refer to the table below: I usually ask my +2200-rated students to solve all five of them in one go, within a prescribed limit of 60 minutes. Specific time-management is up to them, as long as they naturally make it in time. Paradoxically, this makes things even harder than solving one puzzle after the other featuring the same limit each } { (like in this case, an average of 12 minutes per position) } { as it gives the time-management part yet another layer of complexity. There is also a more practical reason for this – from my experience, students have been able to handle the first puzzle relatively quickly, with the ‘Find The Mistake’ one requiring statistically much more diligence. If your rating is lower than this threshold, it is possible to increase the amount of time needed to say 75 minutes } { (2000-2199 bracket) } { or even 90 minutes } { (1800-1999) } { , but please bear in mind that calculation speed matters a lot in a practical game. We certainly do not want to program ourselves into getting into time-trouble in our over-the-board games as critical moments like these occur statistically 2-3 per game, with each one of them supposed to be universally dealt with in 10-20 minutes maximum. And one more thing: although I believe that +1800 players are going to benefit from this course the most, I do encourage players rated 1500-1800 to try and give it a go as well. This is based on the experience of some of my ‘adult improver’ students from within that rating range who routinely score about 50% from similar sets. As they say, shoot for the moon – there is no way you can possibly miss an object with a diameter of about 3475 kilometers... Moving pieces is disallowed. Setting up the position on the board instead of staring at the monitor is surely advisable, but more experienced players should try to solve the puzzles blindfold. The benefits of visualization training are namely not to be underestimated. After all, there is a reason why – when calculating complicated variations – most elite players look at just about everything else at the playing hall than the board itself. The answer to this conundrum is quite straightforward – they are namely usually so far ahead in their calculation efforts already that looking back at the board would have actually disturbed them! Once you are done, make sure to write down your line proposals } { (variations together with ideas) } { and have them compared with mine. The master solutions I provide contain moves and concepts which should have been seen/understood in order to be able to claim that you have solved the given exercise. Just as as a sidenote, despite its construction as a quiz, the course can also be treated as a manual of chess calculation as every puzzle discusses a separate problem pertaining to this area of the game. There are going to be numerous references to chess psychology as well, since calculation is this kind of a skill which is susceptible to various types of fallacies and biases, but also one that tests our own psychological strength to the limit. In order to further cut the learning curve on your side in this respect, the course is going to end with a summary of all the discussed rules and principles governing the calculation process, organized according to themes as well as the level of their complexity. Finally, it is my utmost belief that the material included in this course may also benefit coaches, who would prefer to use these examples for the purpose of organizing more interactive sessions with their own students. If you would also like to train like this but do not have a coach, request a friend’s for help. Ask them to set up the given position on the board as well as to serve as a moderator for the whole discussion that is about to take place afterwards. They are supposed to be obviously acquainted with the intricacies of the given puzzle and steer you in the right direction by asking the right questions or dropping some tiny hints here and there. This approach obviously kills the test-mode grading system on the spot, but nonetheless strongly contributes to increasing the player’s analytical skills instead. } 
1.--   

