{ Material is equal here, but make no mistake - it clearly feels as if it was Black who was under more pressure here. The reason for this is that literally all of White's forces are targeting the poor black king. What apparently makes things even more difficult for him is the inability to contest White's main, undisputed asset, that is the light-squared bishop on [c4]. All signs in the sky seem to be telling us to bail right away, especially as there is a queen trade available at will for Black at the moment. Would that however really be his best option in this situation? } b5 $1 { No, not at all! Actually, Black can even claim a win after this completely unexpected intermezzo on the queenside. This resource represents the manifestation of the phenomenon which I personally like to call 'global vision', which is the ability to search for possibilities across the whole board, even if the main action is taking place exclusively on one part thereof. It is a trademark of world-class players that they stay vigilant and sensitive to moves at which the position does not even hint at the slightest. By definition, global vision will also be largely independent from standard tactical pattern recognition, which circumstance only makes things harder for the untrained eye. } ( { Knowing Dimitrios' solid playing style as well as taking note of the incoming time-trouble, it did not come as surprise to me that he decided to simplify the position with } 30...Qe4+? { when after the further } 31.Qxe4 Nxe4 32.Be3 b5 33.Bxb5 Rc2+ 34.Kh3 Rxa2 35.Bc4 Nf6= { he was able to claim equality with a clear conscience. The problem of modern chess, however, is that 'equality' can be quite far away from a 'draw'. In this case, Black still needed to remain cautious as regards White's bishop-pair, in particular in conjunction with the plan of pushing his g-pawn eventually. The situation remained more or less even for the next thirty moves, until Black missed a tiny little detail in 2 v. 1 endgame with pieces and lost. } ) ( { What is more, after the game it turned out that - provided Black would have been content with a draw - he could have actually helped himself to the white bishop on h6, as after the accurate } 30...gxh6? 31.Qg6+ Kh8! 32.Qxf6+ Qxf6 33.Rxf6 Bc5 34.Rxh6+ Kg7 35.Rh4 Rd7= { , White's additional pawn would have probably proven to be insufficient for a win in view of the existence of opposite-colored bishops on the board. Then again, in such endgames every additional pair of pieces present on the board reduces drawish tendencies to a certain degree. If we add on top of that that White also happens to be in possession of a passed h-pawn, this could lead to the conclusion that obtaining the desired half-a-point by Black would not be a walk in the park at the end of the day. } ) 
31.Be2  { With the benefit of hindsight regarding the consequences of the capture on [b5], keeping his pieces together might look like White's best defensive try. Then again, this retreat allows Black to infiltrate the second rank with a murderous effect. } ( 31.Bxb5 Rc5 32.Qd3 Qd5+! 33.Qxd5 Nxd5 -+ { highlights the point behind Black's thirtieth move perfectly. By luring White's bishop to [b5], he could have achieved a situation in which both of those pieces of White would have ended up hanging on two separate parts of the board. } ) ( 31.Bxg7 { was another option for White, but then after } 31...Qe4+ 32.Qxe4 Nxe4 33.Bd3 Nc5 -+ { the game would have ended in a conceptually similar way, that is: White going down for the account in light of a double-attack. } ) Qb2 $1 
32.Rf2  Rc2  
33.Kf1  Bc5  { Now sample lines convince us that Black will win this in a matter of a couple of precise moves from now: } 
34.Bxg7  ( 34.Qc8+ Bf8 35.Qf5 Qa1+ 36.Kg2 Qxa2 37.Qf3 gxh6 38.Qxf6 Qxb3 -+ { is a bit anti-climatic type of victory, compared at least to the flashy tactical lines we have seen thus far. Fortunately, chess is not ski jumping, so there are no bonus points for style awarded. } ) Rc1+ $1 { An essential intermezzo. The reasoning behind it will be revealed in just two more moves from now. } 
35.Kg2  Bxf2  
36.Bxf6  ( 36.Qg5 Be3! { The cleanest! Now after } 37.Bxf6+ Bxg5 38.Bxb2 Rc2 -+ { Black would, all of a sudden, be a whole rook up. } ) Qxe2  { The point behind 34...[Rc1]+! becomes understandable - the white king needed to go to [g2] instead of [f2] in the process, leaving his [e2]-bishop unattended. This, on the other hand, allowed Black to capture said piece with his queen, effectively also stripping the white counterpart of a check from [g4]. The second best thing for her would be } 
37.Qg5+  { but then after the forced } Kf8  
38.Qg7+  { I would not touch the rook if I were you because of ...[Bf2]-[e3]+, obviously } Ke8  
39.Qg8+  Kd7  
40.Qd8+  Kc6  
41.Qc8+  Kb6 $19 { the black king would be dancing away towards safety against checks towards [a6], with his material advantage constituting soon enough White's cause of death. Your score: One point for discovering 30...b5! as well as the winning 32...[Qd5]+!. The second one belongs to you if you understood that 31.Be2 would have been White's most tenacious try instead, together with at least the general notion of how to win the game afterwards invading the second rank. No points for chickening out with 30...[Qe4]+?. }  

