[FEN "r1b1k2r/pp3ppp/2n5/8/4P2q/PN1p4/1P1K2PP/R1NQ1B1R b kq - 0 16"]

{ We have reached this position when analyzing some sharp Sicilian lines with one of my students. Black had just sacrificed a piece for the sake of exposing the white king in the center. Job well done, it might seem already, but now we need to try and 'force-feed' him our [d3]-pawn, so that the dynamic sequence of ...[Bc8]-[g4] combined with ...[Ra8]-[d8] can follow right thereafter. But the question remains - how specifically are we supposed to coerce White into a capture on [d3]? } Qg5+ $1 { There were four queen checks available for Black in this position, but only one of them led to victory! In each case, we needed to consider two more variables, that is whether White takes the pawn or prefers to run away with his king towards [c3] instead. Thus, eight permutations were to be analyzed altogether, which might sound like a lot of calculation. In reality however, it sufficed to check in the beginning whether the most stubborn [Kd2]-[c3] works under the given set of circumstances. Upon understanding that 16...[Qg5]+! cannot be answered by 17.Kc3, what was left to be checked was merely a fifth permutation, that is 16...[Qg5]+! 17.Kxd3. A beautiful case of logical thinking making paving our way via the maze of variations easier. } ( 16...Qh6+? { would have allowed White to evade the problem entirely by means of } 17.Kc3! { , when after the further } 17...Ne5 18.Nxd3 Bg4 19.Qd2! { the situation of his king would have proven to be surprisingly resilient as he is already offering a favorable trade of queens while making sure in the meantime that nearly his whole army will be able to come to the aid of their leader in case of need, e.g. } 19...Rc8+ 20.Ndc5 Qb6 21.Qd4 Nd7 22.Bc4 Nxc5 23.Nxc5 Rxc5 24.b4= { with White being totally out of danger by now. } ) ( 16...Qf4+? { would have also led to Black selling himself off too cheaply as after } 17.Kc3 Bg4 18.Nxd3 { Black would have to lose time evacuating his queen in the first place. True, the position would have still remained tricky after } 18...Qc7 19.Qc1 Rc8! { , but after the most accurate } 20.Nbc5 Ne5 21.b4 b6 22.Qf4 Nxd3 23.Qxc7 Rxc7 24.Bxd3 bxc5 25.b5 ↑ { White would have safely landed in an endgame which in practice could only be won by him thanks to the 2 v. 1 pawn-majority along the a-b files. } ) ( { Finally, the optically most enchanting } 16...Qf2+? 17.Kc3 Qf6+ 18.Kxd3 Bg4! { would have been neutralized by } 19.Qe1! { when after something like } 19...Qxb2 20.Ra2 Rd8+ 21.Ke3 Qf6 22.Qg3 ⇆ { White would be catching a breath thanks to an attack against the opponent's light-squared bishop as well as the option to retreat towards the kingside with his monarch, should such a necessity occur. } ) 
17.Kxd3  { That is clearly a bad sign for White if helping himself to that poisoned pawn constitutes his best chance for survival. } ( 17.Kc3 Bg4 18.Qxd3 Rc8 19.Qb5 { looked promising for a moment, but Black has } 19...Qe3+ 20.Kc2 a6 -+ { with all the bad things happening to the white king, after the discovered attack along the c-file once the pin along the [a4]-[e8] pin gets released. } ) Bg4  
18.Qe1  Bd1 $3 ( { The point of Black's play which needed to be seen from afar. White's only way out for the king was to run as fast as he can via c2 to b1. Funnily enough the more natural } 18...Rc8? { would have failed at preventing that, so the text move becomes the only option to take this escape route away from White. Now the fact that his king has to stay in the center proves to be fatal for him. Sample lines: } ) 
19.Kc3  ( 19.Qxd1 O-O-O+ 20.Kc2 Rxd1 21.Kxd1 Rd8+ 22.Kc2 { would have been fine for White, provided that he had the time to consolidate his forces. Sadly for him, however, especially } 22...a5 -+ { followed by ...[a5]-[a4] undermining his [b3]-knight would have been immediately disillusioning. } ) Qe5+  
20.Kd3  O-O-O+  
21.Ke3  Rhe8  
22.Qc3  Qxe4+  
23.Kf2  { Just when it seemed that White was about to successfully secure his king on the kingside, Black follows up with } Qf4+  
24.Kg1  Re3  
25.Qc4  Qxc4  
26.Bxc4  Ne5 $19 { Even the absence of queens on the board does not save White anymore in view of the incoming material losses on the queenside. Your score: You deserve one point for understanding why 16...[Qg5]+! was better than the alternatives. The second point is due if you managed to figure out 18...Bd1!! as means of keeping the white king at bay. No points if you did not comprehend the fact that White does not simply have to take the pawn on [d3] for starters. }  

