{ 34.e7 or 34.d7? } 
34.--   

[Event "Calculate Like a Beast: The Moranda Method"]
[White "1.1) Let’s Just Skip The Warm-Up: Puzzle Set #1"]
[Black "Pranav, Anand vs. Van Foreest, Jorden, Menorca 2023"]
[Result ""]
[FEN "5rk1/7p/1p1PP3/1p3pp1/p1nB4/P2r2P1/R6P/2R3K1 w - - 0 34"]

{ Black is half-a-pawn up here, but has certainly a lot of reasons for concern. His main problem is White's pair of connected passed pawns standing on the sixth rank. As it is White to move, there is no way Black is going to be able to handle them without giving up material, in this case his knight or maybe even a rook. From White's point of view, however, the only question which remains to be answered is which one of them to move first. } 
34.d7 $1 { This move is far less intuitive than the alternative, as it does not arrive with a tempo, i.e. attacking any of the opponent's pieces along the way. What is more, the untrained eye may actually conclude that - no matter the move order - there is only a single idea which is worth pursuing by White here, in particular if you take into consideration the optical similarity of both candidate moves. Such 'automatic decisions' within the calculation process should be either avoided altogether, or at least reduced to only legal moves and obviously forced ones. As we shall see in a moment from now, the two candidate moves represented two completely different ideas of following up in this position. } ( 34.e7? { admittedly looked very tempting, but after the further } 34...Rxd4 35.exf8=Q+ Kxf8 36.Rf2 Rd5 ⇆ { the position remained dynamically balanced. Now it is White who is slightly up on material. On the other hand however, the [d6] and [a3]-pawns of his are badly hanging. Especially the possible disappearance of the latter represents an existential threat for White who needed from that point on to seriously reckon with Black's pawn-mass rolling on the queenside. In the game, Pranav had not been able to neutralize Black's powerful [c4]-knight and even ended up losing the thread. I can imagine that Pranav's decision to choose 34.e7? was dictated more by intuition than calculation during the game. As mentioned however already in the introduction to this course, those two skills should optimally go hand in hand supplementing each other. Resorting to intuition exclusively will usually be a good idea only when running out of time on the clock, or maybe while already being deep within a calculated line and therefore unable to carry on checking it any further beyond due to your own human limitations... } ) Rxd4  
35.Rf2 $1 { The point behind White's text move is now revealed. He was not supposed to sacrifice his pawns for material gains, but rather to make sure that one of them does eventually manage to become a queen. For this very purpose, however, he would need to make sure that the defender of the eighth rank Black's [f8]-rook is removed, in this specific case decoyed by means of [Rf2xf5]. } ( { Knowing the right idea in the given position might have still not sufficed for a win in such a dynamic position if White had gone for the similarly-looking but inferior } 35.Rf1? { , as then after } 35...Re4 36.Rxf5 Rd8 37.Raf2 { he would have been unexpectedly countered by } 37...Re1+! 38.Rf1 Rxf1+ 39.Rxf1 Nd6= { with the pawns being effectively put to a halt. } ) Re4  
36.Rxf5  { Now Black may surely refuse White's sacrifice with } Rd8  { but then after the forced } 
37.Rcf1  Rxe6  
38.Rf8+ $1 Rxf8  
39.Rxf8+  Kxf8  
40.d8=Q+  Kf7  
41.Qxg5 $18 { a technically winning position would have arisen on the board. If Black consumes the [a3]-pawn right away, he will drop his own on [h7] almost instantaneously as well, with White later taking the situation on the queenside back under control and following up with a double pawn-march on the kingside. Trying to save the [h7]-pawn would be tantamount to capitulation too as White will simply centralize his queen and eventually still create a passer on the kingside, supported additionally by his king. Your score: One point for resisting the temptation to play 34.e7? automatically, just because it appears to be the most forcing continuation. The second point belongs to you if you managed to figure out the distinct idea behind 34.d7!. }  

