{ Evaluate 25.Qg5. } 
25.--   

[Event "Calculate Like a Beast: The Moranda Method"]
[White "1.1) Let’s Just Skip The Warm-Up: Puzzle Set #1"]
[Black "Srihari, L R. vs. Aryan, C., Qatar 2023"]
[Result ""]
[FEN "1r2r1k1/1b1q1ppp/p7/3n4/2p1pQ2/6P1/PB3PBP/2RR2K1 w - - 0 25"]

25.Qg5  { White's position is worse, that is a given. Two pawns down and with his queen under attack, every player would have started seeking resources enabling them to complicate matters on the board. Under these circumstances, no wonder 25.Qg5 with a mating threat on [g7] represented his best practical chance. } f6 $1 { Picking up the gauntlet - Black lures the white dark-squared bishop to [f6] in hopes of taking advantage of its exposed situation there tactically. } ( { The apparently more solid } 25...f5? { would have jeopardized Black to tremendous pressure along the adjacent diagonals [a1]-[g8] as well as [a2]-[g8] after } 26.Bf1! { with him actually quite lucky to be able to bail out to a draw thanks to } 26...e3 27.Bxc4 exf2+ 28.Kxf2 h6 29.Bxd5+ Bxd5 30.Qxg7+ Qxg7 31.Bxg7 Kxg7 32.Rxd5 Rb2+= { . } ) 
26.Bxf6  h6  { If he wants to stick to his dark-squared bishop, now White needs to place his queen on [g6], thus however walking straight into a pin along the sixth rank after } 
27.Qg6  Re6 $1 { This position should have been anticipated by both of the players in advance. It is namely within the reach of the human mind to be able to calculate short, 3-4 move long variations very accurately, with anything exceeding that threshold starting to be less and less clear, or sometimes even outright opaque. This is also why even longer lines should be calculated in relatively short chunks, optimally accompanied by the option for the stronger side to escape towards a draw, should the position begin to look ugly. If the given line is however not only long but also forced in nature, we will need to try and calculate it from start till the very end by means of brute force, possibly with some intuition-based corrections being taken by us into consideration along the way. } ( { Instead, the game saw } 27...Qf7? { , when after the further } 28.Qxf7+ Kxf7 29.Ba1 Rbc8 30.Rd4 c3 31.Bxe4 Rc5 32.f3 Rec8= { both players agreed a draw, apparently in view of the possibility for White to exchange his way straight into a draw after } 33.Bxd5+ Bxd5 34.Rxc3= { . It is naturally a shame that Aryan did not want to pursue the consequences of the text move any further, but I presume that he simply did not like the position arising after White's follow-up. } ) 
28.Bh3 $1 { This move marks the starting point of yet another chunk of forced moves in this example. } ( 28.Bxe4?! { would not have made the cut on account of } 28...Rxf6 29.Qh7+ Kf7 30.Rxc4 Rd8 31.Rcd4 Qe6 -+ { with Black converting the additional piece immediately after the pin along the d-file gets released. } ) Rxf6  
29.Qxf6  Qxh3  
30.Qd6  ( 30.Qe5 { would have been far less challenging for Black from a practical point of view, as it allows Black to retain his [d5]-knight after } 30...Rd8 31.Qxe4 Kh8! 32.Qxc4 Rd7 33.Qf1 Qh5 ∓ { when the material might indeed be equal, but defending this position as White would have surely been a nightmare considering the weakened light squares around his monarch. } ) Rf8  
31.Rxd5  { The second key moment of this puzzle has just arrived. At first glance, it might seem that Black is now forced to recapture on [d5], resulting in a technically difficult endgame once his [e4]- and [c4]-pawns begin to drop. In reality, however, the potential of Black's light-squared bishop should not be monetized yet, with possible plans based on an attack against the white king capable of breathing new life into this piece. } e3 $1 { Opening up the [h1]-[a8] diagonal as well as the f-file for starters. What comes next is even more brutal as after the queen transfer via [g4] to [f3], White would have to worry about of multitude of threats, not all of which could be parried by rooks alone. } ( { Inverting the move order with } 31...Qg4? { would have been winning on the spot, was it not for the miraculous save of White after } 32.Rb1!! e3 33.Qxf8+! Kxf8 34.Rxb7 Qc8 35.Rb2! c3 36.Rc2 e2 37.Re5= { with the white rooks being just in time to restore coordination and this way fend off any attempts generated by the black queen in the context of pushing his [c3]-passer. } ) 
32.f4 $1 { Keeping the position as blocked as possible would have been White's best chance for survival here. } ( 32.fxe3?? { would be losing quickly because of } 32...Qg4 33.Rb1 Qf3 34.Qe6+ Kh7 35.e4 Qe3+ 36.Kh1 Bc8 -+ { with the bishop heading for [h3] to create a mating net together with the queen on [f3]. } ) ( 32.f3? { would have not been that much better either as then after } 32...Rxf3 33.Qb8+ Rf8 34.Qxb7 e2 35.Rdd1 exd1=Q+ 36.Rxd1 Qe6 -+ { White would be a pawn down and objectively also in trouble on top of that due to his weakened king. } ) Qg4  
33.Qd7 $1 { Simplification is the defender's best friend! Now the likely development of events would have been } Qf3  
34.Qxb7  Qf2+  
35.Kh1  e2  
36.Rdd1 $1 exd1=Q+  
37.Rxd1  Qe2  
38.Qd5+  Kh8  
39.Rc1  Rc8  
40.Qg2  Qe3  
41.Qc2  c3  
42.Kg2  h5 $17 { when despite material equality, White would have been struggling to stay alive. His king is going to be subjected to a series of checks as Black switches his attention between pushing his passed pawn to promotion-square and hunting for an unexpected rook-invasion along the second rank. Your score: You receive one point if you dared to go for 25...f6! in conjunction with 27...Re6!. You may add yourself another point if you spotted 28. [Bh3]!, but still evaluated the position as promising for Black around move 31. No points awarded if you wanted to steer the game towards a draw anywhere along the way. }  

