{ Find the mistake in the following continuation. } 
23.Nxe6  Qxe6  
24.b5  Nce5  
25.Nxe5  Nxe5  
26.Be2  Rxc1  
27.Qxc1  Rc8  
28.Qb2 $10  

[Event "Calculate Like a Beast: The Moranda Method"]
[White "1.1) Let’s Just Skip The Warm-Up: Puzzle Set #1"]
[Black "Erigaisi, Arjun vs. Abdusattorov, Nodirbek, Kolkata 2023"]
[Result ""]
[FEN "2rr2k1/pp2qp1p/2n1b1p1/6N1/1PN1P1n1/P2BP3/1Q4PP/2RR2K1 w - - 0 23"]

{ Black has just sacrificed his e-pawn for the sake of damaging the opponent's central structure. However, as structural transformations tend to affect the capabilities of the pieces more than they do the structure itself, White's position as a whole is also beginning to shake right now. Both his [g5]-knight and the [e3]-pawn are in danger, and this is also why he has no time to lose. } 
23.Nxe6  ( 23.Nf3? { would drop material after } 23...b5! 24.Na5 Nxa5 25.bxa5 Nxe3 26.Rxc8 Bxc8 27.Rc1 a6 -+ { with White's position collapsing not just because his structure would have already been in shambles. } ) Qxe6  
24.b5 $2 { But this already stretches it. A sixth sense for dynamics in the position is admittedly Erigaisi's trademark, but here the position desperately called for some more restrained action. } ( { Instead, White should have consolidated his position with } 24.Be2 { , which is a move with which the white bishop would be prophylactically running away from any attacks on the exposed [d3]-square, offering a trade of rooks for the purpose of reducing the rival's attacking potential, but most importantly also threatening to eliminate the dangerous [g4]-knight of Black at the same time. The engine now claims that after the optimal } 24...Rxd1+ 25.Bxd1 Nf6 26.Ne5 Nxe5 27.Rxc8+ Qxc8 28.Qxe5 Qc1 29.Qxf6 Qxd1+ 30.Kf2 Qd2+= { the game should end in a by perpetual check. } ) Na5 $1 { This resource must have been missed by both of the players. Its justification, which is purely tactical in nature, shall only be revealed to a full extent a move later. } ( { As this was a blitz game, no wonder that Abdusattorov decided to stick to the positionally-appealing } 24...Nce5? { , creating a position of dominance for this knight over the white bishop. In reality, however, after the further } 25.Nxe5 Nxe5 26.Be2 Rxc1 27.Qxc1 Rc8 28.Qb2= { Black's strategic advantage would have sufficed for him merely to claim compensation as he is unable to either seriously endanger White's e-pawns or to invade his camp. Funnily enough, Black's knight currently even finds itself in a so-called 'golden cage', that is a lovely, undisputed outpost stripped however of any useful squares to go to from. } ) 
25.Nxa5  ( { It was too late for White to try and hold his position together with } 25.Qe2?! { as then after } 25...Rxd3! 26.Qxd3 Nxc4 27.Qd7 Nge5 28.Qxb7 Kg7 -+ { the powerful two knights would have granted Black a winning position both from the tactical and strategic point of view. } ) Qb6 $1 { It turns out that Black's last move constituted a decoy designed to enable smothered mate ideas along the [g1]-[a7] diagonal. Certainly not the easiest of motifs to spot, especially if you take into consideration that the path for the black queen towards [b6] was still blocked by his own knight just a moment ago. Now White will need to fight hard for his life. Sample lines: } 
26.Re1  { Probably the most stubborn continuation for White. } ( 26.Nc4?! { would have been losing outrightly on account of } 26...Rxc4! 27.Bxc4 Rxd1+ 28.Rxd1 Qxe3+ 29.Kh1 Nf2+ 30.Qxf2 Qxf2 31.Rf1 Qb2 32.Bxf7+ Kg7 -+ { . } ) ( 26.Qd2?! { would have been similarly hopeless in view of } 26...Nxe3 27.Kh1 Nxd1 28.Rxd1 Qd6 { with White failing to reorganize his forces effectively, e.g. } 29.Qb2 Qf4 30.Qb1 Rc3 31.Nb3 Qe3 32.Nc1 Qxe4 -+ { . } ) Nxe3  ( { But not the hasty } 26...Rxc1?! 27.Qxc1 Rxd3 28.Qc8+ Kg7 29.Qxg4 Qxa5 30.Qg3 Qxb5 31.Qc7 ⩱ { , as endgames involving the presence of exclusively heavy pieces are considered to be difficult to convert due to the still existing dangers for both kings. } ) 
27.Kh1  Rxc1  
28.Qxc1  Rxd3  
29.Nc4  Nxc4  
30.Qxc4  Qf2  
31.Qc1  Rxa3  
32.h3  Rg3  
33.Rg1  b6 $17 { And now, compared to the previous line, Black would not only be a pawn up, but also fully in control at the same time. Depending on White's defensive approach, Black could now oscillate between focusing on taking down the opponent's [b5]-pawn, or maybe just moving his [g6]-pawn all the way to [g4], with the idea of this way conclusively exposing the white monarch. Your score: You get two points only if you managed to spot 24...Na5! followed by 25...Qb6!. }  

[Event "Calculate Like a Beast: The Moranda Method"]
[White "1.2) Let’s Just Skip The Warm-Up: Puzzle Set #2"]
