{ Black to play. } --   

[Event "Calculate Like a Beast: The Moranda Method"]
[White "1.2) Let’s Just Skip The Warm-Up: Puzzle Set #2"]
[Black "Piorun, K. vs. Wojtaszek, R., Warsaw 2023"]
[Result ""]
[FEN "8/3Pk3/2KN2r1/8/5n2/8/8/3R4 b - - 0 76"]

{ Black's position looks critical. White's pawn is just one square away from promotion, with a multitude of methods available for him to make it happen eventually. For this very purpose, he can try to put the king on [c7] or [c8], this way simultaneously also unpinning his own knight. Making use of his rook naturally comes into consideration as well, be it by bringing it to [e8] via the e-file or maybe the eighth rank instead. Luckily for Black however, being in a tough spot not always equals hopelessness. The [d8]-square can be namely taken under control by more than one piece of his. Apart from the king, the arrival of the knight to [e6] or the rook to [g8] could be taken into account as viable resources. Finally, speaking of the rook already, Black may want to keep it along the sixth rank so that it ensures that the white knight stays pinned, and also sometimes allows an interference-based defense in case of a check from [e1]. So, which of the described defensive methods would you like to resort to here? } Nd3 $3 { Hopefully none of the above! It turns out that Black had an incredibly beautiful stalemate motif available up his sleeve. In order for it to materialize on the board however, first he would need to get rid of the 'dispensable' knight. } ( { Instead, surely due to the lack of almost any time on the clock, Black blundered with } 76...Rg8? { and subsequently also surrendered after the further } 77.Re1+ Ne6 78.Ne8 Rg2 79.Rxe6+ Kd8 80.Nf6 +- { , as [Re6]-[e8]+ can only be postponed for a short while now. } ) ( 76...Ne6? { could have been met similarly, that is by } 77.Re1! Rf6 78.Ne8 Rg6 79.Nc7 +- { with the black knight falling. } ) ( 76...Kd8? { would be releasing the pressure against White's [d6]-knight too quickly, thus allowing } 77.Rh1 Ne6 78.Rh8+ Ke7 79.Re8+ Kf6 80.Rxe6+ Kxe6 81.d8=Q +- ) ( { Temporizing with } 76...Rf6? { would have been to no avail either, as then after } 77.Rd4! Kd8 78.Ra4 Ke7 { White would be landing an elegant mate after } 79.d8=Q+! Kxd8 80.Ra8+ Ke7 81.Re8 { . } ) 
77.Rxd3  Kd8  { The moment of truth has arrived. The difficult thing about calculation in such situations is that White can test us in various ways without really ruining whatever he had achieved so far, while as Black we need to adjust and have concrete answers ready to all of the opponent's winning attempts. From the psychological point of view, it can be even harder at times for us to decide in favor of a solution as drastic as this one, as the difference been genius and stupidity can be rather thin. This is also why relying on some of those unorthodox saving methods like stalemate, perpetual check, threefold repetition or establishing a fortress becomes a possibility only after confirming that every other reasonably-looking alternative fails. Fortunately for Black, however, here White is unable to make any further progress, as proven by the following sample variations: } 
78.Rd1  ( 78.Ra3 { or any other removing the rook from the d-file would have been countered by } 78...Rxd6+! 79.Kxd6= { with a draw by stalemate. } ) Re6 $1 { Keeping the rook along the sixth rank is the way, although it does matter which square it goes to. } ( { In particular, } 78...Rh6? { would have failed utterly due to discovered-attack motifs after } 79.Kc5! { when the [d7]-pawn is taboo no matter how Black slices it, e.g. } 79...Rh5+ 80.Kb6 Kxd7 81.Nf5+ Ke6 82.Ng7+ +- ) 
79.Kb7  Re7 $1 { Very resilient - the white pawn is not getting away anywhere. } ( { Again, the hasty } 79...Kxd7?? { would have amounted to a heart-break just before the finish line in view of } 80.Nf5+ Ke8 81.Ng7+ +- ) 
80.Kc6  Re6 $10 { And Black can carry on like this forever. There will come a time that White will have to swallow his pride and seek winning chances by surrendering his pawn for nothing in return and transposing in a rook and knight versus rook endgame. Still, the last time I checked, with the exception of some extremely unfavorable instances, this type of an endgame was easily drawn for the weaker side... Your score: You receive one point for discovering the stalemate idea after 76...Nd3!!. The second point can only be rewarded if you understood that Black can survive this even if he does not take down White's [d7]-pawn at the very first opportunity. }  

