[Event "Calculate Like a Beast: The Moranda Method"]
[White "1.2) Let’s Just Skip The Warm-Up: Puzzle Set #2"]
[Black "Psyk, Radoslaw vs. Gumularz, Szymon, Rzeszow 2023"]
[Result ""]
[FEN "r1q2r1k/1b4pp/1pn2p2/1Nb1pn2/p1P5/P4NP1/1P1BQPBP/3RR1K1 b - - 0 22"]

{ Black could hardly wish for more when emerging out of the opening stage. White's forces are crippled, with only his [b5]-knight working properly by neutralizing the potentially killing ...[Nc6]-[d4] for now. By comparison, both Black's pieces and structure are flawless. His knights and bishops are just waiting to unleash hell in the center, while the [g7]-[f6]-[e5] pawn-chain strongly restricts his rival's play. At the same time however, the lack of action on his side might give the opponent the precious little amount of time required to consolidate his position, for example by means of [Bd2]-[c3] followed by [Nf3]-[d2]-[e4]. This means that Black should strike when the iron is hot, or else he would be at risk of losing his advantage completely. } e4 $1 { The perfect moment to attack is when you are ready and the opponent is not - every moment of hesitation from that point on theoretically increases his chances of survival. The idea behind 22...e4! is to lure the opponent's queen towards the exposed [e4]-square, and then swiftly take advantage of it for the sake of generating an initiative too dynamic to be dealt with. Still, this is a pawn-sacrifice, so Black would need to accurately determine its consequences before going for it in the game. } ( { Instead, the game witnessed the restrained } 22...Re8?! { , apparently played with the intention of first preparing ...[e5]-[e4] before executing it. However, this would have allowed White to save his skin thanks to } 23.Bc3 { when after the further } 23...e4 24.Nfd4 Ncxd4 25.Nxd4 e3 26.Kh1 ⩱ { his position would have not been all rainbows and sunshine, although surely still very far away from losing thanks to a massive number of exchanges about to take place on the board. I have not had the opportunity to speak to Szymon about this game, so I will once again have to play the role of a 'chess-shrink' here. It is namely my belief that he must have seriously considered the winning text move, but was not entirely certain about its consequences. By comparison, his static domination in the starting position was beyond any doubt, so maybe he preferred to stick to safety instead, in hopes that simple means will eventually do the trick too. In practice, however, there always comes a time when we will need to convert a strategic advantage into something more concrete, with calculation serving in such cases as the ultimate verification tool for your execution ideas. In other words, when the time comes, we need to be ready to stop basking in positional control and take action instead, even if the road to victory paved by a more tactical solution involves a bit more anxiety. } ) 
23.Qxe4  Ne5  
24.Qxb7 $5 { I suspect this is what Szymon did not like in his calculations. By sacrificing his queen, White would be drastically transforming the nature of the position, this way even turning from prey into a predator at times. Still, Black would have continued having everything under control had he just calculated this position precisely. } ( { By comparison, the passive } 24.Qe2 { would have just been hopeless on account of } 24...Ng4 25.Rf1 Re8 26.Qd3 Be4 27.Qc3 Qb7 -+ { with White about to lose plenty of material once ...[Ra8]-[d8]-[d3] finally arrives. } ) Bxf2+ $1 { Definitely not anybody's first candidate move in this position if you consider that White's queen is right there for the taking. Then again, once you dive into the sea of lines, you will quickly understand that you want to capture it, but without handing over all the momentum to the opponent after [Nf3xe5]. } ( { The most tempting } 24...Qxb7? { would have been met by the unexpected } 25.Nxe5 Qc8 26.Nd3! { when, despite having only two light pieces and a pawn in return for a queen, the all of a sudden impeccable coordination of his army would have allowed him to survive. Funnily, he should not even insist on desperately recapturing material, instead focusing on purely positional elements. Sample line: } 26...Rd8 27.Nxc5 bxc5 28.Bc3! Rxd1 29.Rxd1 Ne7 30.Re1! Nc6 31.Bd5 ⩱ { , when the engine still claims that Black is a tad better here, although in practice I would not like to be him once ideas like [Re1]-[e6] or in particular [Nb5]-[d6] start working for White. } ) 
25.Kxf2  Nd3+  ( 25...Ng4+?! { would have been terribly sloppy as after } 26.Kg1 {(the smothered mate idea does not work I am going to let you figure out the details yourself, though!, while)} 26...Qxb7 { would have again been unimpressive due to } 27.h3! Ngh6 28.g4 ⩱ { when Black would effectively have to part with his [f5]-knight, thus granting White a third light piece as compensation for the missing queen. } ) 
26.Ke2  Qxc4 $1 { Accuracy till the end would have been an absolute necessity here. By playing like this, Black would not be giving up on the idea of snatching the opponent's queen. It is just that a direct capture on [b7] would have still not worked, so he would need to resort to a so-called 'by-pass' solution, that is using a mating threat as a pretext in order to force White to succumb to a discovered attack, only then resulting in the fall of the white queen. } ( 26...Qxb7? { would have once again allowed White to get away with murder, as after something like } 27.Kxd3 Rfd8+ 28.Kc2 Rac8 29.Kb1! Rxc4 30.Nc3 ⇆ { his slight material disadvantage would have been compensated by the general solidity of his position. } ) 
27.Nc7  Nc5+  
28.Kf2  Nxb7  
29.Nxa8  Rxa8 $19 { White is simply losing in the long run, as he is two pawns down and suffering because of the weakness of his king. Your score: Give yourself the first point for figuring out that 22...e4! is Black's most promising move in the starting position. The second point belongs to you if you noticed White's counter-sacrifice with 24. Qxb7!? and understood on top of that that you would be getting nothing out of the position in case of an immediate capture. No points distributed if you preferred to hold on to your strategic edge and avoided 22...e4! altogether. }  

[Event "Calculate Like a Beast: The Moranda Method"]
