{ 22...Rxh4 or 22...Nxd4? } --   

[Event "Calculate Like a Beast: The Moranda Method"]
[White "1.2) Let’s Just Skip The Warm-Up: Puzzle Set #2"]
[Black "Popovic, D. vs. Zilka, S., Bundesliga 2023"]
[Result ""]
[FEN "1r2k2r/3bnpb1/1q2p1p1/3pP3/2pP1PPB/1nP2N2/R2N1KB1/3Q1R2 b k - 0 22"]

{ This position has arisen out of a childhood pet-line of mine in the Sicilian Defense 1. e4 c5 2.d3!?, which was the circumstance which initially caught my attention as regards this game. Later on, however, as I delved deeper into it, I came across this very interesting position. White enjoys a slight structural edge here - his [e5]-pawn strongly restricts the opponent's [g7]-bishop while the one on [g4] is in charge of Black's [e7]-knight. If you add on top of this the inability of the black monarch to castle as well as his rather lukewarm tries for some queenside counterplay, you will quickly understand that Black's position is slowly becoming critical already. In particular, he does not want to wait for White to complete the transfer of the king [g3], where he would be extremely safe. The only way to prevent this from happening involves a series of sacrifices designed to disintegrate the powerful central pawn-chain of White, that is ...[Nb3xd4] and ...[Rh8]-[h4] in conjunction with ...[g6]-[g5]. In extreme cases, the disappearance of White's [d4]-, [e5]- and [f4]-pawns might prove to be fatal for the white king, but before Black starts day-dreaming, the question needs to be answered - which of the moves is he supposed to start off with? } Nxd4 $1 { This was the most accurate move order for Black, as it rules out any unwanted intermediate moves from White's side. Sometimes the comparison process related to a two or more similarly-looking moves shall pertain strictly to determining the most accurate course of action. However, just to make things clear - in such a case, we are not looking for the move order which is as forcing as possible cuts the majority of intermediate moves, but rather one that does not allow the opponent to throw in a spanner into our works anywhere along the way. } ( { By comparison, } 22...Rxh4? 23.Nxh4 Nxd4 { would have stumbled upon } 24.Nxc4! { , when after the further } 24...dxc4 25.Qxd4 g5 26.Qxb6 Rxb6 27.Nf3 gxf4 28.Ra8+ Nc8 29.Rd1 +- { Black compensation for the missing material would have been close to non-existent. The queens are off the board already and Black's pawns on the fourth rank are going to get targeted any moment. } ) 
23.cxd4  ( { Alternatively, White could try out } 23.Nxd4 Rxh4 24.Kg3 Rh8 25.Qa1 { , although I am not convinced that he would be getting anything more than a balanced game in return for the sacrificed pawn after something like } 25...Nc8 26.Rb1 Qc7 27.Rxb8 Qxb8 28.Ra8 Qc7 ⇆ { . Admittedly, Black would be stripped of castling rights already, but I see no reason why the black king would not be allowed to simply walk over via [f8] to [g7] one day, especially after ...[Bg7]-[f8]-[c5] comes first. } ) Rxh4 $1 { Now things finally go by the numbers for Black. } 
24.Nxh4  Qxd4+  
25.Kg3  g5 $1 
26.fxg5  Qxe5+  
27.Kh3  Qxg5  { Time to summarize all the developments which have taken place on the board so abruptly during the last couple of moves. Black only has three pawns in exchange for the sacrificed rook, but from the positional standpoint his prospects look truly excellent. He enjoys a monopoly over the dark squares thanks to his potent bishop on [g7]. In the meantime, the pawn chain stretching all the way from [f7] to [c4] debilitates the potential of virtually all of White's light pieces. White's forces, on the other hand, appear to be disorganized - his rooks are disconnected and unable to infiltrate the rival's camp for the time being. If he keeps on standing still here, apart from his king eventually getting himself intro trouble along the dark squares, White will furthermore need to reckon with Black going for even more once he manages to progress with his [e6]-pawn all the way to [e4]. Your score: You get one point for figuring out the need to destroy White's pawn-center in the first place. The second point goes to you only if you managed to comprehend the specific difference the 24. Nxc4! intermezzo between the discussed move orders. }  

