{ Evaluate 25...d4. } --   

[Event "Calculate Like a Beast: The Moranda Method"]
[White "1.2) Let’s Just Skip The Warm-Up: Puzzle Set #2"]
[Black "Leenhouts, Koen vs. Van der Hagen, Loek, Vlissingen 2023"]
[Result ""]
[FEN "3r2k1/pb1q1pb1/1p1B2pp/3pP2n/1R4P1/2rB1N1P/P3QP2/3R2K1 b - - 0 25"]

{ This dynamic position has arisen in a Grunfeld game of a student of mine. Koen had just played 25.g4!?, with the rather straightforward intention of helping himself to the black knight stranded on the edge of the board. In moments like these, the importance of accurate calculation grows immensely, as being correct about the evaluation of such a material-winning operation would have been rewarded more or less immediately by a full point in the tournament table. This, on the other hand, also influences the construction of calculation process in the given case - you will need to pay special attention to the resources of the opponent as you definitely want to rule out any unpleasant surprises before finally consuming the material in question. Automatically, the spectrum of your rival's candidate moves will also need to be broadened. Let us try to explain this approach based on the position in discussion. } d4 $1 { A very strong counter indeed, making the battle flare up with renewed vigor. Chopping on [h5] drops the [h3]-pawn as well as the game, while ignoring it does not come into consideration either in view of the nasty ...[Nh5]-[f4]. } ( { Just for the record, } 25...Nf6!? { was also very interesting here, with the point being that White momentarily cannot really help himself to the black knight on [f6] as his [d6]-bishop would have fallen too. The road towards equality would have however been a little bit bumpy for him, as now after something like } 26.Bb5 Bc6 27.Nd4 { he would need to discover } 27...Ne4! { , with the justification that the multi-piece endgame arising after the more or less forced } 28.Nxc6 Rxc6 29.Rb3 Qb7 30.Bxc6 Qxc6 31.Rbd3 Re8! 32.Qf3 Bxe5 33.Bxe5 Rxe5 34.Qf4 Re8 ⇆ { would have remained almost perfectly balanced. Black's [d5]-pawn is taboo for the time being due to the knight-fork on [c3], while no attempt to infiltrate the opponent's camp along the c-file can take place without simultaneously abandoning the possibly required defense of his own monarch. } ) 
26.Rxd4  Qc6 $1 { Now it turns out that 25...d4! was not just meant to be a method of postponing the inevitable losing the knight. In reality, suddenly White is not so safe anymore either, as the only thing separating him from getting mated along the [h1]-[a8] diagonal is merely his hanging [f3]-knight. } 
27.Be4  Nf4  { Well, well, well... turns out that the lifeline of Black's knight was not so short here after all! Still, the game is not over until White says it is over. The reason for this is that he can choose from among at least three reasonably-looking responses, all of which needed to be analyzed and neutralized from afar by Black when going for the text move. } 
28.Qe1  { White's most testing attempt here - over-defending his [e4]-bishop as well as putting a bit more pressure against the black [c3]-rook nicely solidifies the whole setup. Fortunately for Black, there is a remedy available to any tricks his opponent might be up to here. } ( 28.Qd2 { is logical as it aims to give Black an additional headache, that is the attack against his [f4]-knight. Still, Black can handle it coolly with } 28...Nxh3+ 29.Kg2 Rxf3! 30.Bxc6 Bxc6 { when it is White who would need to consider himself lucky to successfully evade that monstrous discovery along [h1]-[a8], e.g. } 31.Rc1 Rxf2+ 32.Kxh3 Rxd2 33.Rxd2 { with an impenetrable fortress being established by Black after the further } 33...Bb7 34.Rc7 Bc8! 35.Kg3 Be6 36.Rdc2 a6= { . } ) ( { If losing his h3-pawn was White's main concern after calculating 28.Qd2, the natural reaction of many players would have been to steer their attention towards } 28.Qf1?! { instead, with the additional benefit of this approach being the possibility to collect the opponent's knight on [f4] after a possible exchange of bishops on [b7]. The problem with this one however is that now after } 28...Qxe4! 29.Rxe4 Bxe4 { the burden of proof that the position is holdable would have once again unexpectedly been with White: } 30.Rd4! Nxh3+! 31.Qxh3 Bxf3 32.Qf1 Rdc8 33.Rd3! Rc1 34.Rxf3 Rxf1+ 35.Kxf1 Rc4 36.Ra3 a5 37.Rb3 Rxg4 38.Rxb6 g5 39.Ra6 a4 ⩱ { , when White would be suffering in an inferior endgame because of the missing pawn as well as the need to keep an eye on Black's runaway pawn on [h6] all the time. } ) Qc8 $3 { If you managed to keep your cool till the very end, you would have been rewarded with the revelation that all of White's apparent threats out here happen to be only paper tigers. The rook is namely constantly taboo due to a knight-fork on [e2], while after a double-capture on [b7] and [f4] White's [f3]-knight ends up as collateral damage too. } ( { But not the tempting game continuation } 28...Nxh3+? 29.Kh2 Qxe4 {(here van der Hagen blundered for the second time with 29...Qc8? instead)} 30.Rxe4 Rxf3 31.Re3 Ng5 { , as after the most accurate } 32.e6! fxe6 33.Bg3 +- { White would have managed to kill off Black's counter-attacking ideas around [f3] without actually the need to sustain any further material casualties whatsoever. } ) 
29.Bxb7  Qxb7  
30.Rxf4  Rxf3  
31.Qe4  Qxe4  
32.Rxe4  Rxh3  
33.Rc1 $10 { Black is a pawn up, but objectively speaking the activity about to be generated by [Rc1]-[c7] would have kept the position dynamically even. Your score: You get the first point if you saw the line allowing you to save your offside knight. Add another point to your stack if you did branching as of move 28 at least two candidate moves! and managed to defend your cause as Black against both of them. }  

