33.Rb4  Nd6  
34.Ra4  Qb6  
35.Bd3 $10  

[Event "Calculate Like a Beast: The Moranda Method"]
[White "1.2) Let’s Just Skip The Warm-Up: Puzzle Set #2"]
[Black "Javakhishvili, L. vs. Dronavalli, Harika, World Cup Baku 2023"]
[Result ""]
[FEN "1r1rn1k1/1p2qp2/p1p3pp/P2p4/3P4/1R2P1P1/2Q2PBP/1R4K1 w - - 0 26"]

{ Black is a pawn up in this position featuring the thematic Karlsbad structure. Then again, the pressure exerted by White along the b-file does not give her any serious prospects of playing for a win. } 
26.Bf1  { What is more, after 26.Bf1, targeting Black's [a6]-pawn, it immediately became clear that White would be restoring the material balance any moment now. At first, Black appeared to be unfazed by this. } Qc7  { This way overdefending her [b8]-rook and simultaneously keeping an eye on the opponent's [a5]-pawn. White was not however ready to leave empty-handed. } 
27.Qb2  { when after the further } Nd6  
28.Bxa6  { White has finally been rewarded with a full pawn. All of those moves have been more or less forced so far, and therefore require barely any commentary. But now a more intense part of the game commences, as we can anticipate the pawns [a5] and [b7] about to be dynamically traded off for one another. This will result in Black sustaining some severe damage to her queenside structure with the [c6]-pawn possibly falling, but also White's bishop feeling a bit stuck on [b7]. The question is which of those elements is going to prove to be more critical here... } Qxa5  ( { Instead, } 28...Ra8 29.Bxb7 Rxa5 30.Qc1 Nc4 { followed by ...[Ra5]-[a7] & ...[Nc4]-[a5] could have given White a hard time, was it not for the freeing } 31.e4 ⇆ {(when Black would have never been in time to carry out the said threat in view of a multitude of her pawns even the little guy on [h6]! hanging at the same time.)} ) 
29.Bxb7  Nc4  
30.Qa1 $2 { This was our malefactor after all. Exchanging pieces increases drawish tendencies in general, but putting the queen to [a1] here specifically introduced some additional, unnecessary tensions between the pieces of both sides, which could have proven to be fatal for White at the end of the day. } ( 30.Qc1 { would have been a much safer option, e.g. } 30...Nd2 31.Ra3 Qc7 32.Qxd2 Rxb7 33.Rc1= {(when the position would have remained objectively speaking equal, but I can imagine myself carrying on playing against Black's slightly weak [c6]-pawn forever in a practical game. I think that the reason why Lela erred here with 30.Qa1? is that this move looked safer than the alternative 30.Qc1, as the latter would at first glance be leaving some room for ...[Nc4]-[d2]. As we know with the benefit of hindsight that the said fork only looked scary at first, we are now able to understand what kind of process could have led her to committing such a mistake. It is a very common mistake for players, when confronted with a problem of choice, to automatically that is, with no or scarce calculation prefer the option which looks less risky. Such an approach should, however, be rejected as one that has very little to do with an empirical science such as chess calculation.)} ) Rd7 $1 { This must have been missed by both of the opponents. By playing like this, Black would be unexpectedly putting White into some sort of a quasi-zuzgwang. If she namely does nothing, Black exchanges queens and helps herself to the undefended bishop. Trading off the queens on [a5] only increases the number of attackers against the said bishop, but how about simply taking on [c6] instead? } ( { In the game, Dronavalli missed her chance for glory and went for the naturally-looking } 30...Qc7? { , hoping that White is not going to be able to neutralize ...[Nc4]-[d2] or ...[Nc4]-[a5]. However, after the further } 31.Ba6 Rxb3 32.Rxb3 Ra8 33.Rb4! Nd6 34.Ra4 Qb6 35.Bd3= { there was nothing left really to play for anymore, so Black took on [a4] and both players agreed a draw right thereafter. } ) 
31.Bxc6  Rxb3  
32.Bxd7  { Just when it seemed as if it was actually White who was about to start playing for a win thanks to her slight material edge, Black would be uncorking } Ra3 $1 { which does not win White's queen in view of } 
33.Rb8+  Kg7  
34.Qc1  { when a skewer from [a1] can always be met by the timely [Rb8]-[b1] comeback, but how does White intend to keep all of his pieces together now in case of the heart-breaking } Qa7 $19 { is a mystery to me. After having witnessed this example for the first time, whenever nowadays I want to purposefully damage the coordination of pieces in my own games just because I think I can justify such a decision by means of concrete calculation, I always remind myself of what could have happened to White in this game, and instantly know that I do not want to ride such a train myself... Your score: Two whole points for spotting 30...Rd7! - you either see it or you do not. }  

