[Event "Calculate Like a Beast: The Moranda Method"]
[White "1.3) Let’s Just Skip The Warm-Up: Puzzle Set #3"]
[Black "Iturrizaga Bonelli, Eduardo vs. Korobov, Anton, World Cup Baku 2023"]
[Result ""]
[FEN "8/3r2kp/1R1n4/p4p2/5K2/1P2PP2/P7/8 b - - 0 44"]

{ Black is a knight up in return for a pawn. This type of advantage usually suffices for a win in a simplified endgame like this one, but here converting this edge will not be so easy for Black after all. There are a couple of reasons for this. First of all, there are not many pawns left on the board anymore. In particular, Black's [a5]-pawn looks like a dead man walking, while the one on [f5] is hanging by a thread since its main defender } { (Black's [d6]-knight) } { is exposed to [Kf4]-[e5]. What is more, their possible disappearance might also lead to a situation in which White's own pawns would have become dangerous passers. Finally, it does not seem feasible for Black to approach the center with his king as this way he would just be walking into a problematic pin along the sixth rank. Still, where is a will, there is a way, and Black had one way of making progress here after all... } Nc8 $1 { This regroupment of the knight was essential. Black transfers the knight to the much more stable [e7]-square, beyond the reach of the white king. The full extent of this winning idea shall be revealed a little later. Instead, for the moment I would like to explain why I think that Korobov might have missed this resource in the first place. In my opinion, this move is a so-called 'backward knight-move', that is a very specific sub-type of invisible moves. There are many examples among chess classics featuring a similar motif and these always raise the eyebrows of commentators. It is quite difficult for me to indicate the root cause of this phenomenon, but if I were to guess, I would say that it goes against human nature to retreat one's pieces. The knight is however a very special, tactical piece, which can almost always bounce back towards a more active outpost in case of need. } ( { In the game Black went for } 44...Kf6?! 45.Ra6 h5 { , which could have allowed White to simplify the position conclusively with } 46.e4 fxe4 47.fxe4 Kg7 48.Rxa5= { when Black's [h5]-pawn would have been next on the kill-list. } ) ( { Some of my students wanted to make } 44...h5?! 45.Kg5 Nf7+ 46.Kxh5 Rd2 { work, with the justification that the white monarch could find himself into a mating under some extreme circumstances, but after the calm and collected } 47.Kh4 Rxa2 48.Kg3= { the days of the remaining black pawns would have again been numbered, especially once the white king reaches [f4]. } ) 
45.Rb5  Ra7 $1 { Only now does the point behind 44...Nc8! become fully clear. Black's [a5]-pawn is out of danger, while its colleague on [f5] can be captured exclusively by the rook beware a knight-fork from [d6]!. Then again, helping himself to the [f5]-pawn in the said manner would have allowed Black to trade off the rooks on the spot, making the whole conversion process all of a sudden childishly easy. Sample lines: } 
46.e4  { Probably White's most stubborn try. } ( 46.Rxf5?! Rf7 47.Rxf7+ Kxf7 48.Ke5 Na7 -+ { is a relatively uncomplicated win for Black as his knight is now going to take care of the opponent's queenside pawns, thus eventually generating a passer of his own out there. The white king would certainly like to come to their rescue, but is chained to the kingside as Black's [h7]-pawn can start moving any day now. } ) fxe4  
47.fxe4  Ne7  
48.a3  a4  
49.b4  { White would surely prefer to exchange as many pawns as possible, but taking on [a4] here would have either led to the loss of his [a3]-pawn, or the necessity to passivize his rook completely after the retreat to the third rank. Still, after something like } h6  
50.Rc5  Ng6+  
51.Ke3  Nf8 $19 { Black would be transferring his knight to [e6] for the purpose of temporarily restricting the opponent's king. In the long run, Black would be trying to have it delegated all the way via [c7] to [b5], resulting in a critical loss of White's [a3]-pawn. It will surely take some time, but with White's attention constantly being absorbed by the [h6]-passer, his fate would have most probably already been sealed. Your score: One point goes to you if you managed to figure out 44...[Nc8]!. Award yourself the second one if you understood the mixed tactical/strategic justification behind it. }  

