20.--   

[Event "Calculate Like a Beast: The Moranda Method"]
[White "1.3) Let’s Just Skip The Warm-Up: Puzzle Set #3"]
[Black "Gusain, H. vs. Gines Esteo, Pedro Antonio, Oviedo 2023"]
[Result ""]
[FEN "r2q1k1r/1b1n1pp1/1p2p3/1P2P2p/p1B1N1Q1/P3bN2/6PP/R4R1K w - - 0 20"]

{ White has just carried out an enterprising pawn-sacrifice on [e3] in this position stemming from the Arkell-Khenkin variation of the Caro-Kann Defense. In return for this, he managed to open up the f-file, thus obtaining serious attacking chances against the black king. If this was not enough, apparently acting like a deer in the headlights, just a move ago Black played the ultra-provocative 19...h5!?!, this way evidently ignoring the growing danger in favor of potentially being able to snatch White's [e4]-knight once the white queen leaves [g4]. Hope you are not going to let him get away with it! } 
20.Qh3 $3 { I know what you are thinking. First I am provoking you into punishing the opponent by entering some tactical slugfest implicitly after a fantastic queen-sacrifice on [e6], and now I am telling you that a quiet queen-retreat to [h3] instead would have deserved a double exclamation mark. Well, even in the most tempting situations you are advised to consider various options, especially in case it turned out that your primary idea does not seem to work at the end of the day. The habit of always taking into account at least two candidate moves/ideas is priceless, as it helps us avoid so-called 'tunnel vision', that is the situation in which we are so enchanted with a given move so much that our chess mind literally refuses to acknowledge the existence of any alternatives. And I do now need to convince what sort of a danger the lack of any branching poses for the correctness of one's calculation process. } ( { In the game, White simply could not resist the urge to play an immortal game with the flashy } 20.Qxe6? { , and even went on to win the game in great technical style, although objectively speaking after the further } 20...fxe6 21.Nfg5+ Nf6! 22.Nxe6+ Ke7 23.exf6+ gxf6 24.Nxd8 { Black could have improved with } 24...Raxd8! { , when after the more or less forcing } 25.Rae1 Bxe4 26.Rxe3 Rd4 27.Rfe1 Rxc4 28.Rxe4+ Rxe4 29.Rxe4+ Kd6 30.Rxa4 Rc8= { , despite being a pawn down, drawish tendencies governing rook endgames would have most probably allowed Black to hold this endgame with accurate play. } ) Bxe4  
21.Nd4 $1 { A literal bolt from the blue sky! It turns out that now all of White's tactical strikes against [f7] and/or [e6] take place with double as much power. Also, the fact that Black's [e3]-bishop is hanging at the moment constitutes a tiny but pleasant bonus for White, who could always recapture material back while still keeping all of his advantages, just in case his attack was supposed to backfire for any reason whatsoever. Who would have thought that wasting one tempo and surrendering a whole piece at the same time could have such great influence on one's attacking potential?! } Bxd4  ( 21...Kg8 { evades nothing, as now after } 22.Rxf7! Qg5 23.Rxd7 Bxd4 24.Bxe6+ Kh7 25.Rxd4 Qxe5 { White would be winning prosaically, that is by means of } 26.Re1 +- { . Any attempts to preserve the bishop would have failed in view of the constant threat of corridor mate on [h5]. } ) 
22.Rxf7+ $1 ( { The position is winning but White still needed to keep on playing accurately, as an equally-attractive follow-up of } 22.Qxe6? { would have been unexpectedly countered by } 22...Bxg2+! 23.Kxg2 Qg5+ 24.Kh1 Qg1+! 25.Rxg1 fxe6 -+ { with Black emerging victorious here due to the extra piece. } ) Kg8  { Consuming the rook would have resulted in an immediate mating hiccup, obviously. } 
23.Bxe6 $1 ( { Once again, surgical precision was called for here as going after material right away with } 23.Rxd7? { would have been premature because of } 23...Bxa1 24.Bxe6+ Kf8 25.Rxd8+ Rxd8 -+ { , when White would have all of a sudden found it impossible to defend his own monarch along the back rank. } ) Rh6  { The least of evils according to the machine, but now after the most direct } 
24.Rxd7+  Kh8  
25.Rxd8+  Rxd8  
26.Rd1 $18 { White would be sooner or later winning thanks to his considerable material advantage. Your score: One point for 20.Qh3!! and 21.Nd4! each. Exceptionally, you may give yourself one point for 20.Qxe6?, but only if you correctly evaluated the line following afterwards as approximately equal. }  

