{ 37.Kg3 or 37.Kg2? } 
37.--   

[Event "Calculate Like a Beast: The Moranda Method"]
[White "1.3) Let’s Just Skip The Warm-Up: Puzzle Set #3"]
[Black "Supi, L. vs. Adhiban, Baskaran, Wijk aan Zee 2023"]
[Result ""]
[FEN "5rk1/1p4p1/4pq1p/p2pP1nP/P5P1/1P1bB2K/1Q3P1N/2R5 w - - 0 37"]

{ Little did White know when expanding on the kingside with his pawns in an Advance French that it would only lead to his king being very badly exposed to Black's counter-attack. At the moment, he needs to decide where to run with his monarch to g3 or [g2], in both cases needing to deal with consecutive checks nonetheless. Under these circumstances, White's goal is clearly survival, with the path towards it being singular and bumpy... } 
37.Kg2 $1 { At first glance, this move looks suicidal, as it literally invites an apparently deadly bishop check from [e4]. Appearances can be deceitful, though, and this catchphrase applies particularly to the comparison process in chess. So many strong chess players handle similar moments purely intuitively, sometimes even claiming that such an approach allows them to find the correct move while spending less time on the clock. In reality, the sharper the position, the less there is room for general assumptions in your thinking process. If you happen to be in time-trouble - fine, just trust your gut feeling. But with sufficient time still left on the clock, there is simply no justification for refraining from using concrete calculation to determine which of the alternatives is better than the other. } ( { In the game, Supi went for } 37.Kg3? Ne4+ 38.Kg2 Qh4 39.Re1 { , which at least optically allowed him to consolidate by eliminating any checks and even enabling the slow improvement of his position, e.g. by chasing away the opponent's bishop on [d3] by means of [Qb2]-[d4] without letting the nasty ...[Bd3]-[e2] happen at the same time. The problem is that he completely missed the stunning } 39...Rf3! { , after which Black converted the position with surgical precision: } 40.Nxf3 Qxg4+ 41.Kh2 Qxh5+ { - Black can take his time before recapturing on [f3] at the end of the day. } 42.Kg2 Qg4+ 43.Kh2 Qxf3 44.Qd4 Be2 { Slowly but steadily, dark clouds are gathering around the white king, with the light squares specifically turning into his graveyard. } 45.Rg1 Qh5+ 46.Kg2 Bg4 47.Kf1 Qh3+ 48.Ke1 Qf3 49.Kf1 h5 -+ { , when White decided to throw in the towel, as he is too paralyzed to be able to deal with mating threats and the [h5]-passer simultaneously. } ) Be4+  ( 37...Qd8 { would have allowed White to dance away from danger in a rather peculiar manner with } 38.Kg3! Ne4+ 39.Kh3! { when after something like } 39...d4 40.Qxd4 Qxd4 41.Bxd4 Nxf2+ 42.Kh4= { the game is still on, but the worst would have certainly been behind him already. } ) 
38.Kg3  { Scary but necessary. Quite unexpectedly, the moment of truth has just arrived, but for Black as a simple retreat would have now allowed White push Black back with [f2]-[f4]. } Qf3+ $1 { There is no turning back for either of the sides now. Black sacrifices his queen, but obtains threats which look so dangerous that they could give a heart-attack to even the resilient of players. } ( 38...Qf7?! { would have shifted the odds in White's favor in view of } 39.f4 Nh7 40.Qc3 { , when Black would desperately need to generate counterplay on the kingside simply not to get outplayed on the opposite flank completely. For example } 40...g6 41.Qxa5 gxh5 42.Qc7 Qe8 { , and now after the most challenging } 43.f5! exf5 44.Bxh6 Qg6 45.Qd6 Qxd6 46.exd6 Rd8 47.gxh5 Nf6 48.Kh4 Rxd6 49.Rg1+ ⩲ { , the material may be equal but Black would now need to head with his king passively towards the h-file in order to avoid an immediate rook-infiltration along the seventh rank by the opponent. } ) 
39.Nxf3  Rxf3+  
40.Kg2  { I can only speculate whether Supi saw this position in his calculations during the game. If so, taking into consideration the element of zeitnot on top of that, I can fully relate to the subconscious decision not to go for this. Then again, after the further } Rxe3+  
41.Kf1  { as much as it might seem anti-climatic to you, the position remains perfectly balanced, as proven by the following couple of lines: } Bd3+  ( 41...Rh3?! { actually would have even presented White with a small edge on account of } 42.Rc3 Nf3 43.Ke2 d4 44.Rc8+ Kh7 45.Rf8! {(aiming to return a small chunk of material in return for the neutralization of the rival's mating threats)} 45...d3+ 46.Ke3 d2 47.Qa1! Bc2! 48.Rxf3 Rxf3+ 49.Kxd2 Bxb3 50.Qd4 ⩲ { , when Black might still be very much within the drawing margin here, but surely needs to aggressively look for ways to maintain a fortress-like position. } ) 
{ Personally, as White I would have agreed a draw by threefold repetition after 42.Kg2, but let us see what would have happened if White tried to carry on playing for a win with } 
42.Kg1  Re4 $1 
43.Rc3  Rxg4+  
44.Kh2  Be4  
45.f3 $1 { That is a must if he wants to invite reinforcements to protect his king. } Bxf3  
46.Qf2  Be4  
47.Rc8+  Kh7  
48.Rf8  d4  
49.Rf4  Rxf4  
50.Qxf4  d3  
51.Kg3  Bf3 $10 { This looks like a far safer version of a fortress compared to the previous line, despite Black having only light pieces left in exchange for the queen. His pieces make a very solid appearance though, supporting the [d3]-pawn on its path towards the promotion-square. White's remaining pawns may possibly as become vulnerable one day as they are all standing on the light squares, thus being subjected to possible attacks by from the black bishop from time to time. Your score: Two points go to you if you chose 37.Kg2! and still assessed 38...[Qf3]+! as safe for White. }  

