[Event "Calculate Like a Beast: The Moranda Method"]
[White "1.3) Let’s Just Skip The Warm-Up: Puzzle Set #3"]
[Black "Based on Ponomariov, Ruslan vs. Vogel, Roven, Dortmund 2023"]
[Result ""]
[FEN "6kr/p2q2pp/b1pb1RN1/7Q/2p5/1P6/P2N1P1P/2K3R1 b - - 0 24"]

{ This crazy position had arisen on the board during a lesson with one of my students. White seems to be making colossal progress. Not only is he, temporarily speaking, a rook up, but also getting dangerously close to mating the opponent's king. During the analysis, it was my belief that Black had no other choice but to eliminate White's knight on [g6] with 24...hxg6. After checking later on, the engine revealed how far away from the truth I was... Apart from the extremely instructive and challenging nature of this puzzle, I decided to include it in this course for the purpose of explaining the nature of forced lines in a little more detail. In the introduction I just briefly mentioned that a given variation is deemed to be 'forced' if it constitutes a longer sequence of only moves. However, what does 'only' mean in this context? Well, first of all, a given move may be the only legal follow-up available in the position, e.g. running away with your king from a double-check. Additionally, we speak about only moves in cases in which it is just obvious to us that there is only one idea available in the position, e.g. eliminating an attacking piece of the opponent a second before it lands mate. Finally, in the game of chess we also distinguish forced lines comprised of only moves, with their 'only' nature not being obvious at all without diving deep into the analysis of the situation. In other words, the difficulty level of calculating a forced line consisting of only legal moves is going to be drastically different from calculating the same type of a line build of only, but at the same time optically non-forcing moves. What is more, depending on your current playing strength, taking the above distinction into consideration, you might not be able say for sure whether you are really dealing with a forced line or not. And if this happens to be the case, does it even make sense for coaches to provide their students with specific advice regarding the treatment of calculating forced lines in their tournament games? Please treat all this as food for thought before we revert back to the example itself... } Ba3+  
25.Kd1  { This is certainly White's only reasonable move. } ( 25.Kc2? { would have been outright wrong as this way the white monarch would be walking straight into } 25...cxb3+ 26.axb3 Bd3+ { , when after something like } 27.Kc3 hxg6 28.Qg4 Qxg4 29.Rxg4 gxf6 30.Kxd3 Kf7 ∓ { the additional pawn would have granted Black excellent winning chances, especially as White's kingside pawns look so vulnerable already. } ) hxg6  { This was unavoidable at the end of the day, as Black would not be able to survive without significantly reducing the rival's attacking potential. } ( { Instead, creating a direct mating threat on d2 with } 25...c3?? { looked awfully tempting, but gets refuted tactically by means of } 26.Ne7+! Qxe7 27.Rxg7+ Qxg7 28.Qe8+ Qf8 29.Rxf8+ Bxf8 30.Ne4 +- { with the black king even getting himself mated soon by the white queen and knight duo. } ) 
26.Qe5 $1 { A multi-purpose move indeed. This way, White prevents ...[c4]-[c3] but also makes sure that his queen remains in close proximity of the opponent's king, especially should the capture on [f6] take place. } ( { As opposed to the text move, } 26.Qa5? { would have not done the trick, as after the further } 26...gxf6 27.Qxa6 c3 28.Qc4+ Kg7 29.Qxc3 Rd8 -+ { the white king would not be able to find a safe haven in the center in the near future. } ) c3 $1 { If Black wants to eat the cookie and have the cookie at the same time, that is to help himself to the white [f6]-rook without getting mated immediately thereafter, this decoy simply needs to happen. } 
27.Qxc3  gxf6  { Now taking on [f6] does not lead to defeat on the spot, but this does not mean that Black's problems had come to an end already. } 
28.Rxg6+  Kh7  
29.Qxf6  Bf8 $1 ( { Again, the only method allowing Black to prevent the murderous Rg6-h6+. Out of curiosity, I encourage you to figure out the consequences of the tricky } 29...Be2+!? { intermezzo, with the intention of luring the white king towards [e2] in order to make the ...[Qd7]-[e8]+ double-attack possible. } ) 
30.Rg5 $1 { It is not over until White says it is over! Now Black for a change needs to reckon with mating threats from [h5] and [g6] simultaneously. There are two reasonably looking antidotes available here for him: the prophylactic 30...[Qe8] and the interference-based 30...[Bg7]. Which one are you going to choose? } Bg7  ( 30...Qe8? { admittedly looked very logical. Apart from gaining control over the problematic [h5]- and [g6]-squares, the black queen would prove to be very helpful here in generating counterplay based on the [e2]-square deep in White's camp. When discussing this resource with the aforementioned student, we initially concluded that after } 31.Qf5+ Kh6 32.Rg3! Be2+ 33.Kc2 Bh5 34.Ne4 Be7 { White would already have to steer the game towards safety with some sort of a perpetual check. What did not dawn up on us at all was that White could now play } 35.Qe5 Qf8 36.f4 +- {(, putting Black virtually into a zugzwang in the middlegame! To our surprise we discovered that Black can barely move his pieces here, while total passivity would be bringing him nowhere on account of the further [f4]-[f5]-[f6] push. General rules of attacking play including all the pieces into play do apply within the calculation process perfectly after all.)} ) 
31.Qg6+  Kg8  { and now White can force out a draw by threefold repetition by means of } 
32.Re5 $1 Kf8  
33.Rf5+  Kg8  
34.Re5 $10 { Your score: One point belongs to you for reaching 26.Qe5!, and you can add the second one to your stack after getting to 29...Bf8!. The line naturally carried on, but were are still in the first chapter of the course, so I do not expect miracles from you... yet... }  

