{ Black is two pawns up here and therefore pretty much already on his way to collect the full point in the tournament table. All he needs to do in order to make it happen is to activate his pieces for the purpose of trading off some of them, thus reducing White's potential to complicate matters on the board conclusively. } Rc8  
32.Nd3  Rc4  { This makes perfect sense - bringing the rook into play with a tempo. } 
33.g3  Rd4 $2 { But this natural follow-up turns out to be quite surprisingly an almost losing mistake. The strategic idea behind it was clear and simple - pinning White's [d3]-knight and thus ensuring that he can inflict less and less damage to Black's position over time. It might have been however the case that Black got a little too complacent while converting his material edge, which resulted in him dropping his tactical guard for a split second. } ( { Black had more than one winning move at his disposal here, whereas considering the course of the game, my preference would go towards the prophylactic } 33...a6 { , when after something like } 34.Nb2 Rb4 35.Red1 Rc7 36.Re2 Rb5 37.Ra1 Nd4 -+ { the days of White's third consecutive pawn on [a5] would have been numbered. } ) 
34.Nh7 $1 { This would have been a most unexpected counter indeed. All of a sudden everything becomes obvious - the knight sacrifice constitutes a decoy, aiming to enable [Re1]-[e8]+ in conjunction with [Rd2]-[c2]+, potentially even threatening mate on the spot! Examples like these should serve as a reminder that even in apparently calm, winning positions we cannot afford to stop calculating and hope that reasonably-looking strategic resources are going to guide us safely past the finish line. In particular, from the psychological point of view, players whose position is objectively winning tend to relax prematurely, assuming that the game is going to 'win itself'. Their opponents however, upon feeling that the noose is tightening around their necks, become extremely motivated to save the game and because of this focus twice as much than before. They start calculating literally all possible lines in search of a singular path towards salvation. In other words, even the biggest of advantages can be squandered when a reckless player faces an inferior, but highly-driven one... } ( { Instead, the game saw the toothless } 34.Ree2? { when after the further } 34...Re7 35.Rxe7 Nxe7 36.Nxf7 Nc6 37.Rd1 Nd5 38.Nf2 Rxd1 39.Nxd1 Nxa5 40.Nxd6 Kc7 -+ { White has been able to recapture one pawn in the process, but still remains lost as Black's connected pawns on the queenside are just about to start rolling fast. } ) Re7  { Black's most resilient try. } ( { After } 34...Nxh7?! 35.Re8+ { Black can certainly still avert mate thanks to } 35...Rd8 { , but then after } 36.Rxd8+ Kc7 37.Rh8 Nf6 38.Rf8 Nh6 39.Rc2+ Kd7 40.Nf4 +- { his position would have remained objectively lost on account of the back-rank invasion of the white rooks. Who would have thought that Black's 33rd move would be so critical just because this way his rook left the previously more or less irrelevant c-file... } ) 
35.Rxe7  Nxe7  
36.Nxf6  Nc6  { And just when it seemed Black was about to recapture material with ...[Nc6]-[e5]b4, or at least make his rival's winning task much more difficult in view of the pin along the d-file, White could uncork the startling } 
37.a6 $1 { with the tactical justification that now after the most logical } Ne5  
38.Rb2 $1 Rxd3  
39.f4  { Black would be well-advised not to allow White's rook to infiltrate along the seventh rank, with } b6  { being however met by the torturous } 
40.Rc2 $16 { leaving Black's [e5]-knight for dessert because of some mating threats along the back-rank, should it decide to move one day. Your score: This one is straightforward - two points for discovering 34.Nh7!. }  

