{ Black is a pawn up here light piece in return for two pawns, but this is exactly where the list of reasons for him to be enthusiastic about the position comes to an end. His light pieces are passive, while the slightly more active queen is clearly far away from laying his hands on the white king, who is also defended securely by a pair of bishops. Temporizing is out of the question, as White will eventually make his queenside pawns roll, with at least one of them getting promoted eventually. Under these circumstances, Black should surely do something... But what?! } Bxh4 $1 { In view of the close to hopeless situation of Black, he should have made use of another unorthodox defensive method, in this specific case a perpetual check. In order however for this to take place, the pawn-shield of the white king needs to be annihilated at all cost. } ( { In the game, Taher went for the ambitious } 32...Nf6? 33.exf6 Bxf6 { with the justification that Black's [h7]-knight was useless no matter what, so why not sacrifice it for the sake of taking down White's [d4]-pawn and this way exposing the white monarch to some checks? Sounds good, but it does not work. After the further } 34.c6 Bxd4 35.Bxd4 Qxd4+ { Black admittedly achieved his goal, but thanks to flawless technique, Laylo was able to disillusion his rival relatively quickly: } 36.Kf1 Qa1+ 37.Kg2 Qb2 38.Qb5 { - Black is gradually running out of checks and threats, but the white c-pawn is starting to run away. } 38...Qc2 39.Kf1 Qc1+ 40.Kf2 d4 41.Qc4 Qe3+ 42.Kf1 d3 43.Bxd3 Qf3+ 44.Ke1 Qxg3+ 45.Kd1 +- { , when Black resigned since there is nothing he can do to prevent the white king from reaching safety on [b3]. } ) ( { In my opinion, } 32...Qa1? { , with the intention of infiltrating the kingside would have been much more testing, even if at the end of the day the position would have still remained winning for White with precise play: } 33.Qb8 Bxh4 34.gxh4 Qh1 35.c6 Qxh4+ 36.Kg2 Qe1 37.Qb5 g5 38.Qd3 gxf4 39.Bd2! { A very important retreat as taking on [f4] would be only inviting more complications on this wing after ...[Nf8]-[g6]. } 39...Qh4 40.c7 Ng5 { and now the only move to maintain the win would have been the highly-abstract } 41.Bc1!! {(. I leave it up to you to try and comprehend the real point behind this move retrograde thinking!, restricting myself merely to indicating what would have happened now after the most tempting)} 41...f3+ { : } 42.Bxf3 Qh3+ 43.Kf2 Qh2+ 44.Bg2 f4 45.c8=Q Ne4+ 46.Kf1 Ng3+ 47.Ke1! Qxg2 48.Bxf4 Qg1+ 49.Kd2 Qf2+ 50.Kc1 Qxf4+ 51.Kb2 +- { and White once again successfully dances away towards safety. } ) 
33.gxh4  g5 $1 
34.hxg5  Nxg5 $1 
35.fxg5  f4 $1 { With nothing else to lose, Black should have thrown everything he had left in the tank to be able to land a barrage of checks against the enemy king. } 
{ After the further } 
36.Bxf4  Qxd4+  { it turns out that White is not in a position to defend both of his bishops while at the same time avoiding further series of checks. The following sample line demonstrates this perfectly: } 
37.Be3  Qh4+  
38.Kf1  Qh3+  
39.Ke1  Qxe3  
40.c6  { White gained a tempo to be utilized for pushing his c-pawn, but now his king becomes vulnerable along the dark squares. } Qc1+  
41.Bd1  Qe3+  
42.Kf1  Qf4+  
43.Ke2  Qc4+  
44.Kd2  { Just when it seemed that Black was slowly running out of ammo, } d4 $1 { would have spelled an end to White's dreams of victory. } 
45.c7  Qc3+  
46.Ke2  Qe3+  
47.Kf1  Qc1  
48.c8=Q  Qxd1+  
49.Kf2  Qd2+ $10 { The white monarch stays trapped within the [h1]-[d4] rectangle. Your score: You receive whole two points for figuring out the 32...Bxh4! to 35...f4! sequence. Exceptionally, you may award yourself one point if you wanted to try out 32...Nf6? or 32...Qa1? in conjunction with the ...[Be7xh4] idea, respectively. }  

