{ Black has allowed the opponent to push him back practically across the whole board in this Advance Caro-Kann. As a result thereof, on the queenside he has been stripped of the thematic ...[c6]-[c5] push, while on the opposite flank he is currently under attack after [f4]-[f5] happened. Taking on [f5] } { (as played by Ezra in the game) } { is to be rejected immediately, as such a structural transformation would only be fueling White's play on the kingside by exposing the black monarch along the g-file. This effectively leaves us with two alternatives: reinforcing the kingside or countering the opponent. Which of these solutions are you going to pick? } f6 $1 { A wise choice, but I do hope that while opting for this move you understood its consequences fully, those including the readiness to part with material for a longer period of time. } ( { The more restrained } 19...Qd8? { attempting to put the white pawn-storm to a halt would have proven to be extremely ineffective when confronted with } 20.f6! gxf6 21.Qe3! { as all of a sudden it would have become clear that Black is defenseless against the capture on [f6], followed by bringing the other white pieces gradually towards the position of the black monarch for attacking purposes, e.g. } 21...fxe5 22.Qxh6 e4 23.g5 Nf5 24.Rxf5! exf5 25.Ra2 +- { with [g5]-[g6] and/or [Ra2]-[g2] coming more or less inevitably already. } ) 
20.fxe6  fxe5  ( { But not } 20...Nxe5? { , as after the further } 21.dxe5 fxe5 22.Rxf8+ Rxf8 23.Qg3 Ng6 24.g5 +- { Black's compensation for the missing piece, if any, would have been at the very best ephemeral. } ) 
21.exd7  Qxd7  { This position should have been anticipated before going for 19...f6!. Black is a piece down in exchange for a pawn, but from the strategic point of view things look very promising. White's [g4]-pawn, which represents the gateway to his king's safety, is hanging. The f-file is going to be conquered by Black in a matter of seconds, forcing the opponent to decide already now about the placement of his king. Will he be safe on the kingside or should he start running towards the other wing as soon as possible? Naturally, if granted a sufficient amount of time, White would have been able to consolidate his material advantage, but how much time is enough and will he be able to handle his problems it in this concrete situation? Such questions are impossible to answer without calculating a couple of longer lines here, and evaluating them correctly. Then again, the human mind is unable to calculate accurately multiple branches within a given variation beyond a certain limit typically 4-5 moves. Does this mean that we need to reject any options which we are unable to confirm conclusively at the board? Absolutely not! In situations like these, the optimal solution seems to be to calculate some representative lines until the whole process starts feeling a little bit too messy or complicated. At that point, a judgment call is needed, that is an intuitive evaluation of the position arising at the end of each calculated line. In other words, in truly complicated positions calculation and intuition should go hand in hand, supplementing each other at moments when one of them fails to work anymore. What is more, in a practical game such variations should not be too long as they will just eat up your time on the clock without providing the guarantee of their precision... } 
{ Now let us see exactly some of those variations which needed to be considered by Black at least to a certain degree when embarking on such an adventurous piece-sacrifice: } 
22.Qg3  ( 22.Rxf8+ Rxf8 23.Qg3 e4 { would have granted Black the excellent [f3]-outpost for his pieces. Now after the necessary } 24.Ra2! Nf3+ 25.Nxf3 Rxf3 26.Qe1! Qxg4+ 27.Rg2 Qh3 28.Rg3! Rxg3+ 29.hxg3 Bd6 30.Bf4 Bxf4 31.gxf4 Qg4+= { a draw by perpetual check would have represented a fair result for both sides. } ) Rxf1+  
23.Kxf1  Rf8+  
24.Ke2  e4  
25.Kd1  ( 25.Nc5 Bxc5 26.bxc5 { would have been met by the foxy } 26...Qf7! { , with White once again having have to agree a draw by threefold repetition after the further } 27.Qxh4 Qf1+ 28.Ke3! Qf4+ 29.Ke2 Qf1+= { . } ) Nf3  
26.Nc5  Qc8 $1 { When handling compensation in return for material, it makes sense to avoid unnecessary exchanges. On top of that, here specifically ...[Be7]-[h4] is the follow-up which we should be eyeing instead of simply eliminating White's [c5]-knight. } 
27.Nc2  Bh4  
28.Qh3  { And just when it seemed White was about to neutralize his opponent's initiative, Black could uncork } Ng1 $1 { with a draw being achieved on a tactical basis. Sample lines: } 
29.Qxh4  ( 29.Qg2 Rf1+! 30.Qxf1 Qxg4+ 31.Kd2 Nf3+ 32.Kd1 Nxd4+ 33.Kd2 Nf3+= { , when White would be lucky that Black is not able to monetize the discovered attack into anything more than a draw. } ) Rf1+  
30.Ne1  Nf3  
31.Qh3  Rxe1+  
32.Kc2  Qf8  
33.Qg3  e3  
34.Nd7  Nxd4+ $1 
35.cxd4  Qxb4 $10 { This time it would be White who would be supposed to ensure himself of a draw by threefold repetition after 36.Qb8+. Your score: You get the first point for deciding in favor of 19...f6!. The second point goes your way if you saw the position arising after 21...Qxd7, evaluated it as double-edged, and calculated a couple of short lines, but without spending more than 20% of the time available per set on it. }  

[Event "Calculate Like a Beast: The Moranda Method"]
[White "1.4) Let’s Just Skip The Warm-Up: Puzzle Set #4"]
[Black "Nicos Doetsch-Thaler vs. Nicholas Wentzlaff, Basel 2023"]
[Result ""]
[FEN "5rk1/1p4pp/1P3q2/3Pb2n/pR1Np3/P3R1PP/1Q3P2/5K2 b - - 0 35"]

{ 35...Bxg3 or 35...[Nxg3]+? } --   

[Event "Calculate Like a Beast: The Moranda Method"]
[White "1.4) Let’s Just Skip The Warm-Up: Puzzle Set #4"]
[Black "Nicos Doetsch-Thaler vs. Nicholas Wentzlaff, Basel 2023"]
[Result ""]
[FEN "5rk1/1p4pp/1P3q2/3Pb2n/pR1Np3/P3R1PP/1Q3P2/5K2 b - - 0 35"]

{ My student sacrificed an exchange a couple of moves earlier, and in return obtained this tactically-potent position. Black is namely locked and loaded for a strike on [g3], with White being additionally bound by a pin along the [a1]-[h8] diagonal. The question remains however - which piece } { (the bishop or the knight) } { should he use here in order to inflict maximum damage? } Nxg3+ $1 { The only way to go, but this move only works in conjunction with a not-so-obvious follow-up. } ( { In the game, Black played } 35...Bxg3? { , apparently causing a panic reaction on White's side in form of } 36.Nf3? { , which leads with best play to equality. Instead, White could have obtained a winning position had he ignored the danger against [f2] by means of } {36.Ne6! when after the further 36...Qxf2+ 37.Qxf2 Rxf2+ he would have once again had to demonstrate limitless courage by walking straight into discovered attack with 38.Ke1! . It turns out that no rook move would have created any serious problems for the white monarch, e.g. 38...Rg2+ 39.Kd1 Nf6 40.Rc4! Nxd5 41.Rc8+ Kf7 42.Rxe4 Nxb6 43.Rf8+ Ke7 44.Rf5 +- . Just for the record, escaping with the king to the right looked much safer indeed, but fails on account of ...[Rf2]-[d2] followed by ...[Bg3]-[f2]+ & ...[Nh5]-[g3]+, effectively forcing White to give back the additional exchange almost at once. I think White must have at least considered the 36.Ne6! alternative, but rejected it intuitively in zeitnot on account of its tactical ugliness. } ) 
36.Ke1  { Staying on the kingside for his Majesty would have resembled seeking shelter against a thunderstorm under a tree, but now White runs into a completely unexpected blow. } Nh1 $3 { After this White's days would have been numbered all the same. No matter what White does next, Black consumes the [f2]-pawn with his knight, with a multiple fork about to take place from [f3] next. Here I can positively confirm that Nicholas strongly took 35...[Nxg3]+! into consideration, but none of us him or me would have continued with 36...Nh1!!, this time because of such a resource being evidently against the rules - a knight on the rim is grim after all, right? } ( { For a second there, } 36...Bd6? 37.fxg3 Bxb4+ 38.axb4 a3! { , with the idea of introducing some critical lack of coordination among White's forces, looked like an interesting alternative to the text, but White manages to defend with perfect play, e.g. } 39.Qc3 Qf1+ 40.Kd2 Rf2+ 41.Re2 a2 42.Qc8+ Rf8 43.Qe6+ Kh8 44.Nc2 a1=Q 45.Nxa1 Qxa1 46.Qxe4 Qb2+ 47.Kd1 Qb3+= { , when agreeing a draw by perpetual check or threefold repetition appears to be the most reasonable thing both of the sides could now do. } ) 
37.Rxe4  Nxf2  
38.Re3  { And now even though the [d3]-outpost seems to belong to the past due to the disappearance of Black's [e4]-pawn, he could still opt for } Nd3+ $1 { leading to mate or huge material losses after the further } 
39.Rxd3  Qf1+  
40.Kd2  Qg2+  
41.Kc3  Rc8+  
42.Rc4  Rxc4+  
43.Kxc4  Qxb2 $19 { My theory regarding this game is that one of the players most probably rejected a winning line because he saw that somewhere along it he needed to play a tactically scary move, while the other missed a decisive idea because it did not dawn up on him that a knight-move to the edge of the board could be so good. Maybe I am totally wrong about the motifs of both rivals here for which I apologize in advance just in case, but one thing is for sure. Some of the biases you will be encountering while trying to evaluate the consequences a given move properly are going to be fully conscious 'I shall not play this move as I do not like the look of it'. On other occasions, however, it will be your subconsciousness which will be playing games on you, which means that your chess mind is going to refuse to view a certain move as a feasible candidate, thus without you even getting the chance to think about it. Your score: You are granted one point for discovering 35...[Nxg3]+! 36.Ke1 Nh1!!. Add yourself another one if you saw and understood the deficiencies of 35...[Bxg3]?. }  

