{ Evalute 23...Qa5. } --   

[Event "Calculate Like a Beast: The Moranda Method"]
[White "1.4) Let’s Just Skip The Warm-Up: Puzzle Set #4"]
[Black "Allahverdiyeva, Ayan vs. Valiyeva, Asnad, Baku 2024"]
[Result ""]
[FEN "3r3r/pp1Pkpp1/q7/2pp1Q1p/5N1P/8/P4PP1/R5K1 b - - 0 23"]
Qa5 $2 
{ Black is whole four pawns up here } { (an exchange and two pawns) } { , but not yet ready to celebrate in view of her king being exposed to multiple checks. On top of that, White's [d7]-pawn is untouchable for the time being on account of Qf5-[e5]-[b8]+, which apparently complicates converting the material advantage even more. Under these circumstances, no wonder that Black decided to use prophylaxis and played 23...Qa5, this way at least temporarily ruling out the murderous [Ra1]-[e1]+. There were at least two good reasons to reject this move though. } { First of all, Valiyeva could have consolidated her edge almost immediately thanks to 23...Kf8! 24.Re1 Qf6 25.Qxd5 Qc6 -+ , when White would be dropping her biggest asset without being able to keep on creating threats against the opponent's monarch for a prolonged period of time. Secondly, and more importantly however, is White's reply after 23...Qa5. Can you find it? } 
24.Rb1 $1 { At first glance, this move makes little sense. In theory at least, White should be desperately targeting the black king and not some rather irrelevant queenside pawn of the opponent. The text move is strong, however, because it nicely anticipates the further course of action. In a moment from now, White will namely be trying to 'convince' the black king to capture on [d7], thus exposing him to a rook-check from [b7] instantly afterwards. Sounds like a plan, right? Well, maybe, but Black was not born yesterday... } ( { Instead, Allahverdiyeva decided in favor of the tempting } 24.Nxd5+? Kf8 25.Rd1 { , but here Black could have once again put herself together successfully with } 25...Rh6 -+ { , introducing the previously dormant rook into play without any more delay. } ) Rxd7  { The most logical response. Taking material and neutralizing an important threat of the opponent can barely ever be a bad idea. What is more, this way Black would be simultaneously parrying the [Qe5]-[b8]+ motif as the black queen is now ready to retreat to [d8] in case of need. } ( { By comparison, } 24...Rh6?! { well demonstrates the dangers connected with the invasion of the white rook along the seventh rank, e.g. } 25.Qe5+ Kxd7 26.Rxb7+ Kc6 27.Qe7 Rhd6 □ 28.Nd3 f6 □ 29.Rxa7 Qb6 □ 30.Nxc5! Kxc5 31.Rc7+ Kb4 □ 32.Rb7= { , when Black would still need to face some practical challenges before being allowed a draw by White. } ) 
25.Nxd5+  Kd8  ( 25...Ke8 26.Qe5+ Kf8 { would have been fully sufficient for a win, was it not for the real idea behind 24. [Rb1]!, that is the absolutely astonishing } 27.Rb6!! { , cutting communications between the black queen and the [d8]-square, thus creating a serious mating threat literally out of nothing! Fortunately for Black, the position would have still remained drawn as after something like } 27...Rxd5 28.Qb8+ Ke7 29.Qxb7+ Rd7 30.Qe4+ Kf8 31.Qa8+ Ke7 32.Qe4+= { it is a threefold repetition. } ) 
26.Nf6 $1 { This too needed to be seen by White already when embarking on 24.Rb1!. It turns out that - due to the fact that the knight is taboo - Black's rook cannot stay on [d7] any longer, thus being forced to let the white rook infiltrate his queenside. } 
{ Black can certainly still try some tricks, but after the most natural } Rd4  
27.Rxb7  Qe1+  
28.Kh2  Qe6  
29.Qxc5  Qxf6  
30.Rb8+  Kd7  
31.Qxa7+  Ke6  
32.Qa6+  Kf5  
33.Qxf6+  Kxf6  
34.Rxh8  Rxh4+  
35.Kg1 $10 { The draw would have been beyond any doubt already. One quick comment before I let you check out your score. I believe that the chances of either of the players locating 24.Rb1! would have been severely increased if they used anticipation as a strategic supplement of their calculation process. As explained in the foreword already, calculation alone rarely allows us to discover the whole spectrum of resources available in the given position. This is especially true for the defender in similar situations, as due to human nature which pushes us into focusing on our own possibilities in the first place, it is essential to look into the ideas of the attacker in a much more comprehensive manner. Your score: You receive the first point if you saw and appreciated 24.Rb1!, with the second point depending entirely on noticing 26.Nf6! later on. }  

