33.Nf6  
{ White has been playing this game very aggressively so far. At a certain point of time, he even sacrificed a piece for an attacking initiative as well as some pawns, just to carry on hunting down the black king. Neither of the sides acted perfectly throughout the encounter, with things eventually escalating into a highly-interesting tactical endgame. After 33.Nf6 is where the really scary part for Black begins. Mate with [Rd7]-[h7] is hanging in the air, but at the same time the [f6]-knight is taboo because of corridor-mate along the seventh and eighth ranks. No wonder that in this apparent catch-22 situation Black simply started checking. } Rc1+  
34.Kh2  Rh1+  
35.Kg3  { But now the music is beginning to stop, as the third consecutive check would, at least at first glance, cost Black a whole knight already. Kudos to you if you managed to determine that things would have not been so simple there after all, but points is what you would be getting instead if you discovered the only real defensive resource. } Be4 $3 { A fantastic geometrical counter! This way, Black 'mechanically' prevents mate on [h7], while simultaneously being ready to continue defending tactically no matter what White's reaction is. The text move was difficult to spot for at least two reasons. First of all, there is an alternative discussed below which looked much more forcing and tempting. More importantly however, when being on the verge of getting mated or losing material, barely any reasonable chess-player is going to pour more oil into the fire by offering even more material to the opponent. } ( { Instead, in the game Black resorted to } 35...Rh3+? { , naively hoping that White is going to help himself to the black knight, thus allowing Black to neutralize his opponent's mating threats with a check after the capture on [f6]. Still, this was one check too far as now after } 36.Kg4! { Black went full berserk with } 36...Rg8+ 37.Nxg8 Rxh4+ 38.Kxh4 Ng6+ 39.Kg3 Nxe5 40.Rxa7 +- { , only to land in a completely lost endgame a couple of moves later. } ) 
36.Rxe4  { The most testing continuation for Black, but also one which reveals the full extent of the saving motif. } ( { As opposed to the text move, } 36.Nxe4 Rg1+ 37.Kh2 Rg4 38.Ree7 Rxh4+= { would have led to a relatively easy draw, while }  {36.Kxf4? could even prove to be troublesome for White on account of 36...Rxf6+ 37.Kg5 Rg6+ 38.Kh5 Bc6 -+ , when White would need to work very hard in order prove that his two pawns as rook-activity constitute enough compensation for the missing piece. } ) Rg1+  
37.Kh2  Rg2+ $1 { The point - this tiny yet decisive intermezzo would have allowed Black to win time to capture White's [f6]-knight without dropping his rook, while at the same time making sure that he will be able to keep an eye on the vulnerable back-rank ...[Rg2]-[g8] being the saving grace. Now after something like } 
38.Kh1  Rxf6  
39.Rxf4  Rxf2  
40.Rxf6  Rxf6  
41.Rxa7  Rf4 $10 { The drawish result would have become inevitable. Your score: Only discovering 35...Be4!! grants you two points in this case. }  

