{ This highly-imbalanced position represents the byproduct of an equally hard-fought Sicilian Najdorf middlegame. Black is a pawn up here } { (rook versus knight and three pawns) } { , but the sharper the situation, the less we should pay attention to static elements within the evaluation process, instead focusing rather on the dynamic/tactical 'here and now'. Black's forces are all targeting the queenside already, with her central pawn-mass making an even more intimidating impression. At the same time, however, White's battery along the g-file cannot be underestimated either, especially as [Bc1]-[h6] could prove to be lethal any moment now. So what is Black advised to play now in your opinion? } Bxb2 $1 { Annihilation of the defense was of essence to begin with as Black desperately needs to include her knight via [c3] into the attack. } ( { Instead, the game saw Black opt for the admittedly tempting } 38...d3? { , only to succumb to a deadly counter-attack after } 39.Bh6! dxc2 40.Bg7+ Kg8 41.Bf8+ +- { with mate on [g8] to come thanks to this lovely little interference-based sacrifice of the bishop. } ) ( { Similarly, } 38...Nc3+? { could be neutralized, provided that White just keeps a cool head: } 39.Ka1! Nd5 40.bxa3 d3 41.Qb2 Qxb2+ 42.Bxb2 c3 43.Bc1 c2 44.Bd2 +- { when Black would have officially run out of ammo already. } ) ( 38...Qb3+? 39.Qxb3 cxb3+ { would have proven to be ineffective when confronted with a simple retreat of the white monarch to [b1], but certainly not the greedy } 40.Kxa3?? { as then after } 40...Nd6 41.Ka4 Nc4 { White would need to return way too much material just in order to be able to stay in the game, e.g. } 42.Bd2 Nxb2+ 43.Kxa5 Nc4+ 44.Ka6 Nxd2 -+ { , when Black's [b3]-pawn would not be promoting yet, but his winning chances being beyond any doubt due to the fact that the white rooks are unable to generate any further active play from this point on. } ) ( 38...Nxf6? { would have been by far the trickiest move to meet as it demands for } 39.f3! { contributing to the additional defense of the sensitive [b2]-pawn along the second rank. Now after something like } 39...Qxf3 40.bxa3 c3 41.Rf2 Qd5+ 42.Ka1 Ng4 43.Qf5! Qb3 44.Bg5! Qxa3+ 45.Ra2 Qd6 46.Qxf7 +- { the noose around the black king's neck would be tightening rapidly, without his white counterpart getting in any more trouble in the process. } ) 
39.Qxb2  ( { In a perfect world } 39.Bxb2? { would have been the most desirable reaction from the point of view of countering the aforementioned follow-up by Black, but it fails to } 39...d3 40.Qc1 Qb3+ 41.Ka1 d2 42.Qb1 c3 -+ { with White literally getting run over on the queenside. } ) ( { Alternatively, } 39.Bh6 Nc3+ 40.Qxc3 Bxc3 41.Bg7+ Kg8= { would have only sufficed for a draw as the thematic } 42.Bf8+?? { would this time be countered by } 42...Qxg2! 43.Rxg2+ Kxf8 -+ { - no battery, no cry! } ) Nc3+  
40.Ka1  
{ Just when it seemed that the onslaught was about to peter out completely, Black could uncork the unexpected } Qb4 $3 { With the threat of ...[Qb4]-[a4]+ forcing White to trade off queens. } 
41.Qxb4  axb4  { This way, however, also opens up the a-file, with ...[Rb8]-[a8]-[a2] all of a sudden becoming deadly. Under these circumstances, White would now need to once again agree a draw by threefold repetition after the necessary } 
42.Bh6  Ra8+  
43.Kb2  Ra2+  
44.Kc1  Ra1+  
45.Kb2  Ra2+ $10 { The star move 40...Qb4!! is a so-called 'collinear' one, meaning that in this case the black queen standing along the b-file did not reach the final [b1]-square, instead stopping somewhere along the way. This type of a move tends to escape the attention of human players as with major pieces of both sides standing on one file, rank or diagonal, the brain tricks us into thinking that mutual exchanges constitute the only available possibility. And, obviously, the deeper into the calculated line a move like this is supposed to happen, the less visible it is from afar... Your score: You receive three points for spotting the 38...Bxb2 39.Qxb2 Nc3+ 40.Ka1 Qb4!! sequence. Exceptionally, I am willing to grant you a single point for choosing 38...Nxf6? instead, but only if you saw 39.f3! and treated this as the least of evils for Black from among other, non-working ideas. }  

