[Event "Calculate Like a Beast: The Moranda Method"]
[White "2.1) No Pain = No Gain: Puzzle Set #1"]
[Black "Luo, Terry vs. Naroditsky, Daniel, Charlotte 2022"]
[Result ""]
[FEN "6k1/Q2nbppp/8/8/1p2PPB1/4B1P1/1P1p1K1P/2r2b2 b - - 0 35"]

{ This position is a result of a complex Catalan middlegame in which Black, somewhere along the way, decided to sacrifice his queen in exchange for some nice momentum in the center. This type of compensation eventually transformed into another advantage, that is a strong passed pawn on [d2]. Simultaneously, the said pawn is taboo in view of the ...[Be7]-[c5]+ double-attack. All this makes his situation enticing, especially as it is his turn to play and can even immediately cash in on his investment by simply promoting. But would this in fact be his best option? } Bh3 $1 { The only winning possibility for Black. The point behind this move is that White's light-squared bishop now gets overloaded in a manner which disallows him from claiming material in particular any of the opponent's pieces standing along the seventh rank in return. Personally, when solving this puzzle I rejected both of the alternatives discussed below, and only managed to discover the text move as a result of searching for an improvement over the latter. The reason why I am mentioning this right now will be explained a bit later on. } ( { The majority of my students saw nothing wrong with the most natural } 35...d1=Q? { , at least not until I asked them about their antidote to } 36.Bxd1 Rxd1 37.Qa8+ Bf8 38.Qa4! { , heavily stretching the black rook's defensive capabilities. In all honesty, Black still has the foxy decoy of } 38...b3! 39.Qxb3 Ra1 { , apparently allowing him to untangle and then continue playing on for a win based on the activation of his knight. In reality however, after } 40.g4 ⇆ { this task would have proven to be difficult to carry out in practice. By playing like this, White would be anticipating the need to transfer his king towards a safe haven on [g3] one day, especially should he be subjected to some checks coming from Black's minor pieces. Material remains equal for the time being, so in the absence of stable outposts for Black's forces, I would be far from claiming a durable edge for him here. } ) ( { In the game, Black went for } 35...Nf6? { , probably with the very same idea as the text, which is to eliminate the defender of the [d1]-square. This move led to a quick victory for Black, but things could have gotten far more complicated for him had White resorted to } 36.Bd1!! { as an attempt to postpone the promotion of Black's passer for as long as possible. This privilege would have cost him a whole piece upfront, but would have allowed to stall the said pawn nearly for good. Now after something like } 36...Rxd1 37.Qxe7 { Black could at will force out a draw by means of perpetual check with ...Nf6-[g4xh2]+, but opting for more after } 37...h5 38.Qd8+ Kh7 39.Kf3 ⇆ { could potentially become an affair involving risks for both sides already. } ) 
36.Qa8+  ( 36.Qxd7? { would have revealed yet another crucial difference between 35...Nf6? and 35...Bh3!, that is the fact that now Black could free his rook with a tempo thanks to } 36...Rf1+ 37.Ke2 Bxg4+ 38.Kxf1 Bxd7 -+ ) Nf8  
37.Bxd2  { Black's main asset got eliminated, but now he may switch his attention to the weak white monarch instead. } Bc5+  
38.Ke2  ( { An attempt to defend the g4-bishop with } 38.Kf3 { does not do the trick because of } 38...Rf1+ 39.Ke2 Rf2+ 40.Kd3 { , and now helping himself to the bishop should be enough for a win in the long haul, but more convincing would be } 40...Bf1+! 41.Kc2 Be3 -+ { with material still being conceded by White, but dark clouds also continuing to gather around his king at the same time. } ) Bxg4+  
39.Kd3  Rd1  { With a winning position for Black, who would just need to establish some more coordination among his pieces before being able to cash in on the full point. Sample line: } 
40.Qa5  Be7  
41.Qc7  Bf6  
42.e5  Ne6 $1 
43.Qb8+  Bd8  
44.Qxb4  g6 $19 { The white monarch is about to take a walk into the wild. The vulnerability of his kingside pawn chain only makes things worse here. This example confirms a truth about chess calculation which many players do not seem to be consciously aware of, which is the fact that it is rarely linear in nature. Most coaches will namely tell you that you need to first evaluate the position. Then comes the time to elect some candidate moves which correspond with the needs of the position. Afterwards, you should use elimination and/or comparison to choose the one which is best, and finally have its viability confirmed by means of calculating its consequences before you execute it. All this is indeed correct, provided however that your initial list of candidate moves indeed includes the best move from the start. Otherwise, you will make it all the way to the end of the process, but leave empty-handed as there is always going to be something wrong about each of the primary candidates. What should we do then? My suggested approach is to gather all the knowledge on the position you have accumulated so far, come back to the candidate move selection process and look for additional alternatives which serve the desired purpose, but do not suffer from the same defects. There is a chance that the position still features a strong idea - it is just that you did not see or appreciate it initially as you were still in the dark to a certain extent regarding the evaluation of the position, and only figuring out why other moves fail to deliver would have allowed you to understand what you should have been really looking for from the start. Your score: Two points for finding 35...Bh3! and additional one for understanding why do the other two alternatives fail. Choosing either of the moves 35...[d1]=Q? or 35...Nf6? grants you a single point, but only if you were simultaneously aware of their respective deficiencies. }  

