[FEN "4Qbk1/R4pp1/3p4/2nP1q2/2P1p3/6PP/3B2BK/3r4 w - - 0 39"]

{ 39.Bf4 or 39.Bg5? } 
39.--   

[Event "Calculate Like a Beast: The Moranda Method"]
[White "2.1) No Pain = No Gain: Puzzle Set #1"]
[Black "Based on Fernandez Guillen, E. vs. Dragnev, Valentin, Formentera 2024"]
[Result ""]
[FEN "4Qbk1/R4pp1/3p4/2nP1q2/2P1p3/6PP/3B2BK/3r4 w - - 0 39"]

{ This position has arisen during an analysis with one of my students. Material is equal, but both sides seem to be locked and loaded to go after each other's throats. For this very purpose, White may consider targeting Black's [f8]-bishop } { (and thus also his king) } { , while Black just cannot wait to arrive with his queen to [f2], intending to land mate on [g1] or maybe even along the second rank soon enough. Still, it is White's turn to move and he seems to have two very tempting alternatives at his disposal - 39.Bf4 and 39.Bg5. Which one of those do you like more though? } 
39.Bg5 $1 { This highly-tactical move happens to be the only winning resource in this position. The apparent point behind it is that it creates an instant mating threat with [Qe8xf8]+. In reality however, its justification is much deeper than just that. } ( { When giving this puzzle a try myself, I really liked } 39.Bf4?! { , as I felt that I was killing two birds with one stone here. This way, the black [d6]-pawn would be beginning to hang very badly, while at the same time the presence of my bishop on [f4] would be preventing any queen infiltration by Black into my camp. Combining business with pleasure cannot be bad, one could say, but in this specific case it fails tactically to } 39...Nd3! 40.Bxe4 Rd2+! { , when White would be quite unexpectedly facing a choice between allowing a draw by threefold repetition or continuing to play for a win after } 41.Bxd2 Qf2+ 42.Bg2 Qxa7 43.Qe3 Nc5= ∞ { with rather meager chances of success in converting his additional pawn due to the solidity of Black's position as well as the indolence of his light-squared bishop. My mistaken preference for 39.Bf4?! in lieu of 39.Bg5! is instructive from the point of view of a typical bias players encounter when comparing candidate moves with one another. I decided in favor of the latter mostly because it was more universal than the alternative. 39. [Bf4]?! namely attacks and defends at the same time, while 39.Bg5! optically created a threat of similar gravity, without however doing anything about ...[Qf5]-[f2] in the meantime. Especially in sharp positions however, the comparison process cannot rely on general assumptions like 'Which of the moves solves more problems of mine?', but should rather be based on precise calculation of the consequences of the respective alternatives. In such situations, generalizations may serve a supplementary role in helping us choose only when specific lines do not provide concrete proof for superiority of one idea over the other. } ) ( { Although this was not an element of the assignment, I guess you might be wondering what would have happened after the natural looking } 39.Ra8?? { . Well, this move would turn out to be a blunder in view of } 39...Qf2 40.Qxf8+ Kh7 41.Qg8+ Kg6 { when despite being a piece up for the time being, White would be defenseless in the long run against the opponent's mating threats. The following sample line highlights this perfectly: } 42.Be3 Qxe3 43.h4 Rd3 44.h5+ Kf6 □ 45.Qd8+ Ke5 46.Qh4 Rd2 -+ { , with Black enjoying the luxury of choice between attacks along the second rank and the march of his [e4]-passer. On top of that, he does not need to worry about the safety of his own king anymore, as he will become virtually untouchable on [d4] any moment now. } ) Qf2  
40.Be3 $1 { Only now can we witness the concept standing behind 39.Bg5! in full blaze. In this manner, White avoids mate on [g1] and tactically wins a tempo on Black by forcing his queen back to [f5], thus effectively gaining an important tempo compared to the starting position. To put it simpler, once the black queen retreats, White will enjoy many more options due to the fact that his dark-squared bishop will longer be hanging on [d2]. } ( { But not the greedy } 40.Qxf8+? { as chess is not checkers, so Black could just evade with } 40...Kh7 { , leaving this time White struggling to survive, as demonstrated by the following variation: } 41.Be3 □ Qxe3 42.h4 □ Qg1+ 43.Kh3 Rd2 44.Rxf7 □ Qxg2+ 45.Kg4 Qe2+ 46.Kh3 Qg2+= { with it being White who should consider himself lucky to have escaped with a draw. } ) Qf5  ( 40...Qxe3 { does not work because of the rather anti-climatic } 41.Qxf7+ Kh7 42.Qh5+ Kg8 43.Qxd1 +- { with Black losing the game based exclusively on material. Funnily enough, this fact escaped the attention of many of my students who were by this point of time fully absorbed by attempts of landing mate on [g7] instead... } ) 
41.h4 $1 { Calm and collected. White's position is so good already that also the direct 41.Ra8 would have yielded him the win, but I personally appreciate the elements of a quasi-zugzwang and prophylaxis the text move introduces. Black is going to be outplayed here in the long run, so if I were him I would be trying to wiggle with } Nd3  
42.Ra8  Ne5  
43.Qxf8+  Kh7  { Even if White could now nullify the rival's counterplay after } 
44.Qh8+  Kg6  
45.h5+ $1 Qxh5+  
46.Qxh5+  Kxh5  
47.Bxe4  Ng4+  
48.Kh3 $18 { With the [e3]-bishop being once again taboo, this time in view of the [Be4]-[f3]+ double-attack. Your score: You get three points for seeing 39.Bg5! Qf2 40.Be3!. No points if you played the text move intending to follow up with 40. [Qxf8]+?. One point goes to you if you understood the problem arising after 39.Bf4?!, without comprehending the essence of 39.Bg5!. }  

