{ Evaluate 14...Nxd4. } --   

[Event "Calculate Like a Beast: The Moranda Method"]
[White "2.1) No Pain = No Gain: Puzzle Set #1"]
[Black "Mueer, Sebastian vs. Prediger, Rupert, Fredersdorf-Vogelsdorf 2022"]
[Result ""]
[FEN "r2q1rk1/1ppbbppp/n1n1p3/pNPpP3/B2P4/P2Q1N2/1P3PPP/R1B2RK1 b - - 0 14"]

{ This position has arisen as a result of a rather unorthodox treatment of the Alekhine Defense by both players. Black is clearly getting squeezed here in the center, and on top of that, some of his pieces most notably the [a6]-knight and the [d7]-bishop are not too dazzling either, too put it mildly. My student } { (who was White) } { had made tremendous progress on the queenside as well, but is now more interested in targeting the opponent's kingside by means of [Ba4]-[c2]. Under these circumstances, would cutting the Gordian knot with 14...Nxd4 grant Black any relief? } Nxd4 $3 { Absolutely! When landing in a statically inferior position, those are usually dynamic means which are capable of bringing us out of such a slump. By playing like this, Black could have turned the tables on his rival by drastically changing the nature of the position. Instead of defending passively, he would have been able to generate plenty of activity based on the knight arriving to [c5], in conjunction with the sudden clumsiness of the white light pieces on the same part of the board. Still, it is only fun if it works, so let us now check the viability of this concept by applying concrete calculation. Fasten your seatbelts ladies though, as this is going to be a long and bumpy ride! } ( { Instead, the game saw } 14...f5? {(which was, at least conceptually, not such a bad idea at all closing the [b1]-[h7] diagonal in advance or at least gaining some play along the f-file in case of an en passant capture. Good intentions are, however, what the road to hell is paved with, and also here after something like)} 15.exf6 Bxf6 16.Bc2 g6 17.Bh6 Rf7 18.Qd2 +- {(new weaknesses the backward [e6]-pawn as well as the square in front of it for White to play against would be appearing on the kingside.)} ) 
15.Qxd4  { White definitely does not want to see a knight-fork happening on [c5] here. } ( 15.Nfxd4? { would have therefore been just hopeless on account of } 15...Nxc5 16.Qc2 Nxa4 17.Qxa4 c5 18.Ne2 Qb6 19.Nec3 d4 -+ { when the misplaced and pinned [b5]-knight would have been White's cause of death at the end of the day. } ) Nxc5  { Time for a preliminary summary. Black sacrificed a piece in exchange for two pawns, but has also managed to create some unpleasant threats at the same time. One such possibility for him would be to take on [b5] followed by a knight-fork on [b3], leading eventually to a win of an exchange we can safely assume that the knight is not going to make it alive out of [a1]. What I personally find even more annoying for White however would be the capture on [a4], accompanied by ...[c7]-[c5] and ...[Qd8]-[b6], analogically to the above quoted sideline. One thing is for sure - White certainly needs to roll up his sleeves and get to work here, simply not to end up getting outplayed out of apparently nothing. } 
16.Nc3 $1 { This move looks a little too cooperative at first glance as it outright allows Black to claim the white rook on [a1]. As it is however going to turn out in a moment from now, White's approach is not going to revolve around material at all. } ( 16.b3? { initially made a good impression as an attempt to defend the light-squared bishop and return some material in a controlled manner. Surprisingly, however, Black could now resort to } 16...Ne4! { instead, when after the further } 17.Bb2 c6 18.Nc3 Nxc3 19.Bxc3 b5 ∓ { the days of the said bishop would have once again been numbered. } ) Bxa4  
17.Nxa4  Nb3  
18.Qg4 $1 { Abruptly switching gears from two to five! Unexpectedly, White proves to his opponent that there are opportunities on the opposite flank worth exploring as well. } ( { Interestingly enough, } 18.Qd1? Nxa1 19.Be3 { , aiming to collect the said knight right away, would have failed utterly due to } 19...d4! 20.Nxd4 c5 21.Nb5 Qxd1 22.Rxd1 Nb3 -+ { when White could resign on the spot with a clear conscience as he cannot even infiltrate the seventh rank effectively with his rook anymore. } ) Kh8 $1 { There is no way Black can carry on fighting in this situation without inserting this defensive move. } ( { The greedy } 18...Nxa1? { would have stumbled upon } 19.Bh6 g6 { when despite it sounding logical to snatch as much material as possible, White's most convincing try for a win would have been } 20.Rxa1 Re8 21.h4 b5 22.Nc3 b4 23.Ne2 +- { with the black monarch about to get slaughtered in a few moves from now by the whole swarm of white pieces. } ) 
19.Rb1  { Just when it seemed White was about to consolidate him material edge by retreating the knight to [c3], Black could uncork } d4 $1 { keeping the said piece at bay for the time being. White's [a4]-knight does admittedly look like a fly trapped in a spider's net, paralyzed by its neurotoxin and waiting to be consumed by means of ...[b7]-[b5]. This is also why he now needs to act fast. } 
20.Rd1  { Striving to eliminate the very essence of his knight's demise, White simultaneously arranges conditions for a peculiar evacuation route. } Qe8 $1 { Yet another last minute change of plans. Black namely had very good reasons to remove his queen from the d-file upfront. } ( { The already thematic } 20...b5? { would now be met most convincingly by } 21.Nc3 { when after the further } 21...dxc3 22.Rxd8 Raxd8 23.Be3 cxb2 24.a4 Nc1 25.g3 +- { any back-rank mating threats of Black would have become a matter of the past already. } ) 
21.Bg5 $1 { I hope you saw this resource from afar! The [a4]-knight is history, so it is a far better idea now to focus on the kingside even more. Maybe the opponent will falter one step before the finish line? } b5 $1 { Not a chance! By coming back to his first idea, Black would have liquidated the position into a perfectly equal endgame. } ( { Inviting the white knight towards g5 with a tempo after } 21...Bxg5? 22.Nxg5 Qxa4 { would have amounted to playing with fire in view of } 23.Qe4! g6 24.Rd3 Nc5 25.Qh4 h5 26.Rxd4 Qc2 27.Rf1 +- { , when despite being a pawn up, Black would have been pretty much defenseless against [g2]-[g4]. } ) 
22.Bxe7  Qxe7  
23.Nxd4  Nxd4  
24.Qxd4  bxa4  
25.Qxa4  Qc5  
26.Qd4  Qxd4  
27.Rxd4 $10 { Black's slightly inferior queenside structure would have canceled itself out with White's exposed [b2] and [e5]-pawns. The length of the forced line featured in this puzzle may seem dazzling, but to me it is more important to highlight another aspect of such variations. The forced nature of a given line is namely independent from the pace of the game itself, that is whether one of players is attacking and the other one defending, respectively. This puzzle, for example, witnessed many twists and turns, with both rivals being in need of interchangeably implementing antagonistic measures. In other words, the only thing that counts when determining the forced nature of a given line will always be whether it includes a series of only moves or not. Your score: One point for reaching 18.Qg4!, the second one if you made it to 19...d4!. The third point is only awarded is you got all the way to 21.Bg5!. }  

