{ Material is equal in this position, but the game is certainly far from over. Black's [h6]-pawn is on the verge of falling, which would lead to the creation of a potentially dangerous pair of connected passed pawns for White on the kingside. At the same time though, White cannot start celebrating yet as he first needs to make sure that after such a capture, the resulting queenless endgame is indeed going to be winning for him. Finally, Black's sole [c3]-passer cannot be underestimated either. } 
44.Qh3 $3 { I never claimed that in this type of an exercise the very first move is not going to be the mistake already! To my defense I can moreover say that I sincerely hope that you smelled a rat here, in a sense that starting the analyzed sequence with a tempting capture must have simply made your spidey senses tingle. The deeper point behind this mysterious move can however only be understood after a closer inspection of the course of the puzzle. } ( 44.Qxh6+? { looks like the type of move which cannot be possibly wrong, especially as no other reasonable alternative was apparently available to begin with. Still, it turns out that after the further } 44...Qh7 45.Qxh7+ Kxh7 { Black would have not only managed to stall White's passers safely along the light squares, but would have also been just is in time to promote his [c3]-pawn, thus transposing into an easily holdable endgame after } 46.Kg3 c2 47.Kf3 Nc5! 48.Ke2 Nb3 49.f7 Kg7 50.g6 c1=Q 51.Bxc1 Nxc1+ 52.Ke3 Nb3 53.Ke4 Nc5+ 54.Kxe5 Nd7+ 55.Kf5 Nf8= { . } ) Nf8  { This knight-retreat was absolutely necessary here based on tactical grounds as something like } ( 44...Kg8 { would have been met by the sneaky } 45.g6! { , decoying the black queen away from the defense of said piece, when after the further } 45...Qxg6 46.Qxd7 Qxf6 47.Qc8+ Kg7 48.Qxc3 +- { converting this endgame into a full point by White would have been trivial. } ) 
45.Qxh6+  Qh7  ( { An attempt to keep the queens on board would not have helped Black either, e.g. } 45...Kg8 46.Qh4 Qc7 47.Qe4 { when the most aggressive } 47...c2 { would have been countered by } 48.g6 { with Black ending up once again completely dismantled after } 48...Qd6 49.Qc4+ Qe6 50.Qxe6+ Nxe6 51.Bh6 +- { . } ) 
46.Qxh7+  Kxh7  
47.Kg3  { The idea behind White's 44th move is now officially revealed. The opponent's knight had been sent back towards [f8], meaning that Black is not in time anymore to support the promotion of his [c3]-pawn. Under these circumstances, the only plan left for him would be to try to establish some sort of a fortress along the light squares, but these efforts are going to be in vain. White will namely take down [c3] first, afterwards getting interested in the remaining black pawn on [e5]. Once the second one falls, the material advantage of three pawns is going to suffice for a win for sure. } 
{ Sample line: } Ne6  
48.Kf3  Kg6  
49.Ke2  e4  { Delayed does not mean denied - the white monarch can simply take the longer route towards [c3]. } 
50.Kd1  Nc7  
51.Kc2  Nd5  
52.Kb3  Kf7  
53.Bc1  Ke6  
54.Kc4 $18 { It's high time for Black to resign already in view of a zugzwang. All in all, the only way to solve this puzzle lied in so-called 'retrograde thinking', that is the analysis of a long line until its very end, and then - upon reaching an unsatisfactory result - going backwards on a move by move basis in search of an improvement along the way. This exercise featured an extreme case of application of such a method as you were required to come back all the way back to square one in pursuit of the truth... Your score: Two points belong to you if you have considered 44.Qh3!! in conjunction with the [g5]-[g6] trick. Add yourself the third point if you comprehended the need to push the black knight back to [f8] this way. }  

