{ White to play. } 
33.--   

[Event "Calculate Like a Beast: The Moranda Method"]
[White "2.2) No Pain = No Gain: Puzzle Set #2"]
[Black "Neef, M. vs. Gazik, Viktor, Charlotte 2022"]
[Result ""]
[FEN "3Rnrk1/R3p1b1/6QB/2p5/6n1/1PN3PP/5PK1/4q3 w - - 0 33"]

{ After barely surviving the early middlegame stage, Black should consider himself extremely fortunate to have achieved this very position. Despite being nominally a pawn down } { (rook and two pawns in exchange for two light pieces) } { , with 32...Ng4! he had just created a murderous mating threat on [f2]. To make things even worse for White, standard defensive methods like elimination of the attacker or reinforcing the king's position are clearly doomed to fail in this specific situation. This circumstance might suggest the need to use some more extreme means just to be able to prolong the lifespan of his monarch, but the basic question remains - how to achieve it before Damocles' sword falls? } 
33.h4 $3 { Yet another striking example of an invisible move - ignoring the threat of the opponent! It turns out that we forgot about another defensive resource to which we are forced to resort to in particular in hopeless moments: simply hitting the road with our king as far as possible away from danger. For this to be possible at all, however, White needed to create a bolt hole for his king on [h3] in the first place, which square shall furthermore constitute the first step on the ladder towards his salvation. By comparison, capturing on [g4] with the pawn would have granted also him the [h3]-pawn, simultaneously however exposing him to a corridor mate on the very next move. The text move has been the first choice of barely any student of mine, notwithstanding their playing strength 1500-2700. The reason for this might be purely psychological in nature - humans find it so difficult to go about a perceived danger, that the urge to avert it represents a clear priority in their decision-making process. In order to avoid instances in which the quality of our calculation might be adversely affected by such a phenomenon, we should learn to recognize not only the threat itself, but to measure their real gravity as well. It may happen from time to time that the said threat is only going to look dangerous, or maybe as in this exercise, that there is nothing we can do about it, forcing us to seek alternative antidotes to the problem. } ( { In the game, White played } 33.Qxg7+? { , quickly going down for the count after the further } 33...Nxg7 34.Rxf8+ Kxf8 { , although things would have remained interesting had he now decided in favor of } 35.Bxg7+ Kxg7 36.hxg4 Qxc3 37.Rxe7+ Kf6 38.Re3 { , seeking to establish an apparently fortress-like position. Without the inclusion of the queenside pawns, this would have been a clear draw, even if for some strange reason White was supposed to part with both of his g-pawns. Still, the presence of an additional black pawn on [c5] seems to change this assessment and it is quite instructive to follow the manner in which White is going to get outplayed here. } 38...Qc2 { Black should keep an eye on the [b3] and [f2]-pawns of White for starters. Now the second step of the plan commences - eliminating the [g4]-pawn. This is going to be child's play, however, as the white rook should optimally stick to the safe haven square [e3] most of the time. } 39.Rf3+ Kg5 40.Re3 Kxg4 { Now comes stage three - infiltrating the [h3]-square by the black king. Once this happens, in view of mating threats along the first rank or [g2], White will have no other choice but to surrender his second g-pawn by means of [g3]-[g4]+. } 41.Kg1 Kh3 42.g4+ Kxg4 { The most curious part of the conversion process begins. Black needs to transfer his king behind enemy lines. For this to take place, he will need to first cross the e-file and then the third rank via [a3]. All this is possible thanks to some queen-sacrifice ideas for the white rook on [e3] or [g3], leading routinely to a winning pawn endgame for Black. } 43.Kf1 Kf4 44.Kg2 Qd2 45.Rg3 Ke4 46.Rh3 Kd5 47.Rg3 Kc6 48.Rh3 Kb5 49.Rg3 Kb4 50.Re3 Ka3 51.Rg3 Qd5+ { A truly nasty check for White. On top of the king-invasion being underway, king-retreats to the first rank might be losing for yet another reason, that is the immediate ...[Qdxb3], followed by a winning pawn-race to the respective promotion-squares. } 52.Kh2 Kb2 53.Rh3 Kc2 54.Re3 Kd2 55.Kg3 Qg5+ 56.Kf3 Qg6 { The position now starts reeking with a zugzwang as the third rank becomes to short for the rook and the white king needs to keep on protecting [f2]. } 57.Re2+ Kc3 58.Re3+ Kc2 59.Ke2 Qh5+ { and now with White having been forced to touch his [f2]-pawn, the rook loses its footing and things escalate quickly: } 60.f3 Qd5 61.Kf1 Qd1+ 62.Kf2 Qd4 -+ { . } ) Qxf2+  { Not better than the alternative 33...[Rxf2]+, but surely much scarier once you see how deep will the white king need to make it into Black's camp before it is time for both sides to call it a day. } ( 33...Rxf2+ { is surprisingly nothing more than a draw for Black after the further } 34.Kh3 Qh1+ 35.Kxg4 Qf3+ 36.Kh3 Qh1+= { . } ) ( { On the other hand, } 33...Nxf2?? { would amount to proof that Black did not enjoy the luxury of taking on [f2] with any piece whatsoever, as here after } 34.Bf4! { he would be able to continue blasting checks for a couple of moves more, without ever reaching the desired target. For example } 34...Qh1+ 35.Kxf2 Qh2+ 36.Ke3 Qg1+ 37.Kd2 Qg2+ 38.Ne2 +- { . } ) 
34.Kh3  Qf1+  
35.Kxg4  Nf6+  
36.Kg5  Nh7+  
37.Kg4  Nf6+  
38.Kg5  Qc1+  
39.Kf5  Qc2+  
40.Kg5  Qc1+ $10 { Only now does it become clear that Black cannot make any further progress here no staircase motif for the black queen because of the white rook standing on [d8]!, thus yielding the draw by perpetual check a fair result. Your score: Two points for noticing that 33.h4!! is a feasible candidate move at all, the additional pawn going your way only after making sure however that it indeed works tactically. You may award yourself a point if you tried to hold that endgame instead, and maybe even a second one if you simultaneously understood why is it lost at the end of the day. }  

