{ Black to play. } --   

[Event "Calculate Like a Beast: The Moranda Method"]
[White "2.2) No Pain = No Gain: Puzzle Set #2"]
[Black "Alsina Leal, D. vs. Donchenko, Alexander, Formentera 2023"]
[Result ""]
[FEN "5k1r/2q1bppp/p3p3/2n1P2P/3r1B2/5Q2/1PBN1PP1/4R1K1 b - - 0 24"]

{ Black has been under pressure for most of the game since White applied a thematic exchange sacrifice in the Sicilian Moscow. As a result, despite being up on material, Donchenko is still a little bit away from consolidation. His king is namely deprived of castling rights, on top of that his rook remains stuck on [h8] for the time being. Without solving those two problems first, there is no way Black will be able to convert his material edge as - at least from the dynamic point of view - his forces would have continued being dynamically outnumbered. } g5 $1 { Only this slightly exotic move would have allowed Black to deal with both of the aforementioned issues at the same time. Compared however to the alternatives listed below, it is highly-tactical in nature, meaning that its viability is going to depend largely on accurate calculation. } ( { In the game, Black sought activity with } 24...Qd8?! 25.Be3 Nd3 { , eventually managing to outplay his slightly lower-rated opponent, but if White kept his cool here, he would have been able to put up some stiffer resistance, e.g. } 26.Ra1 Nxe5 27.Qg3 Rd5 28.Bb3 Rxd2 29.Bxd2 Qxd2 30.Qxe5= { when the additional pawn would have surely not sufficed for a win here for Black, at the very least due to the drawish tendencies related to the presence of opposite-colored bishops on the board. } ) ( { Interestingly enough, even the theoretically helpful trade of queens would not have been enough to claim a decisive edge for Black in this position, as demonstrated by the following sample line: } 24...Qb7?! 25.Be3 Qxf3 26.Nxf3 Rb4 27.Rc1! Ke8 28.Bd1 Nd7 29.Rc7 Kd8 30.Ra7 ⇆ { , when the activity of the white forces would have most probably once again prevented Black from cashing in on his additional exchange. } ) 
25.Be3  ( 25.hxg6 hxg6 26.Bxg6 { should have been seen by Black already upon embarking on the journey starting with 24...[g5]!, otherwise this whole story would have just constituted losing speculation. Fortunately for him, he can defend in a tactically-clever way with the elegant } 26...f5!! { , after which White's possible passivity would have led to further material losses in view of ...[Kf8]-[g7], while } 27.exf6 Qxf4 28.fxe7+ Kxe7 -+ { would have just exacerbated the process by coercing White into multiple unwanted exchanges. } ) Nd3 $1 { A necessary follow-up, one which for the second consecutive time would need to be verified by means of calculation in advance. } ( 25...Rd5?! { would have been a logical alternative to the text, especially if you considered [Qf3]-[a8]+ to be a nuisance, although now after something like } 26.Ra1 Qxe5 27.Nc4 Qc7 28.g4= ∞ { it would have been extremely difficult for Black to make progress due to the [a6]-pawn constantly hanging and ...[Kf8]-[g7] always falling short to the intermediate [h5]-[h6]+!. } ) 
26.Bxd4  Nxe1  
27.Qa8+  Kg7  ( { The first instinct of any strategist in such a situation would be to get rid of queens after } 27...Qd8?? { , but here this solution would have been counter-productive as after } 28.Qxd8+ Bxd8 29.Be4 Ba5 30.Nc4 Bb4 31.h6 +- { the fate of Black's knight trapped on [e1] would have been sealed as - due to the strength of White's [h6]-pawn - he would not be able to involve into play in order to have it saved. } ) 
28.h6+  Kxh6  
29.Qxh8  Nxc2  
30.Bc3  { Just when it seemed as if White was able to steer up things thanks to his active queen wreaking havoc behind enemy lines, Black could eradicate any signs of counterplay based on the cold-blooded } Qd8 $1 { when after the further } 
31.Qxd8  Bxd8 $19 { The arising endgame would have been hopeless for White on account of Black's additional pawn and the more active king, as well as eventually also the exposure of the white [e5]-pawn to forthcoming attacks. The analyzed exercise highlights an important feature of converting a material advantage. Most players tend to believe that this is a mostly technical procedure, which relies predominantly on a series of correct positional decisions like exchanges or killing off counterplay. It cannot be forgotten, however, that in such situations calculation still plays a crucial role. Our opponents will frequently be 'wiggling', that is: trying to complicate matters as an attempt to avoid getting outplayed in the long haul. More importantly however, converting a material edge can sometimes be simplified by means of so-called 'small tactics', which - if carried out successfully - tend to quite significantly shorten the whole process. Whatever is tactical in nature needs to be confirmed precisely by calculation, though. And things can get particularly tricky in cases in which those 'small tactics' represent actually the only feasible path towards victory... Your score: One point goes to you for at least considering 24...g5!. The second one belongs to you if you simultaneously understood why 25.hxg6 hxg6 26.Bxg6 was not dangerous for Black after all. Finally, you may award yourself the third point if you saw the main line of the puzzle all the way to its end. }  

