[FEN "r1q1kb1r/pp3ppp/2n5/1N1p4/Q4B2/2P5/PP3PPP/RN4K1 b kq - 0 15"]

{ Black landed in this mess after misplaying the Moscow Sicilian rather badly. Sensing blood, his opponent has just sacrificed an exchange on [d5], followed by a threat of its recapture thanks to a knight-fork on [c7]. Success in this regard would have allowed him to restore the material balance, thus leaving the opponent defenseless against White's ongoing central initiative. Under these circumstances, Black has only two options left to explore - generating counterplay with 15...Qg4 or trying to damage White's coordination with 15...g5. Which one do you pick? } g5 $1 { It was absolutely essential to put White's dark-squared bishop under pressure at once. The justification behind this little operation is that the opponent might be able to snatch our rook on [a8] now, but without the support of the said bishop his knight will not make it alive out of the corner. } ( { Instead, the game saw Black err with } 15...Qg4? { , a mistake which, however, went unpunished as White apparently did not comprehend that after the slightly counter-intuitive } 16.Nc7+ Kd7 17.Nxd5! {((Instead, 17.Nxa8? would have jeopardized White to 17...Bd6 , pushing him into an unfavorable endgame once multiple exchange on [f4] take place after 18.g3 ))} 17...Bd6 18.h3! Qe6 19.Na3 +- {(his attacking initiative would have progressed without any delay. The white [d5]-knight is taboo on account of a skewer along the d-file, but ignoring its presence would have not been acceptable either. With White about to bring his rook to [d1] and the other knight to [b5]/[c4], Black's end would have been near already. I suspect that Black went for 15...Qg4? in the game exactly with the assumption that White will simply consume his [a8]-rook immediately thereafter. A similar belief must have been strong with White as well, who responded with the inferior 16.h3?!, apparently being himself concerned by the backlash following after [Nc7xa8]. In other words, both players seemed to be programmed towards considering exclusively the mentioned materialistic approach, without paying that much or any attention to the capture on [d5] instead. This, on the other hand, would have affected the tree of variations needed to be calculated from two alternatives into a matrix of four. All this really emphasizes the importance of the relationship between conceptual thinking when calculating as even general considerations regarding the nature of the position heavily influence the type, length and finally number of the lines required for analysis in the given situation.)} ) 
16.Nc7+  { There is no turning back now for White, although it still remains an open question whether he is going to use this knight to claim more material, or maybe to expose the black monarch more by eliminating Black's [d5]-pawn somewhere along the way. } ( { By comparison, } 16.Bxg5? { would have not only given up on White's most dangerous threat for now, but also allowed Black to pursue ideas of his own right thereafter: } 16...a6 17.Nd4 Rg8! 18.Be3 Qg4 19.g3 O-O-O { ∓ } { , when White's initiative would have been a matter of the past already because of the pin along the fourth rank, significantly slowing down any previous play of his. } ) Kd8 $7 
17.Bg3  ( 17.Nxa8? { would have been much too premature due to } 17...gxf4 18.Qxf4 Be7 { with White temporarily running out of possibilities to pressurize his opponent. In practice, converting this position into a full point is going to be anything but easy, but objectively speaking, after something like } 19.Na3 Qxa8 20.Rd1 Qc8 21.Nb5 Qd7 22.Qxf7 Kc8! 23.Rxd5 Rf8! 24.Rxd7 Rxf7 ∓ { Black would be enjoying some winning chances based on seeking targets among White's queenside pawns. } ) f5 $1 { This was the real point behind 15...g5!. One way or another, the days of White's dark-squared bishop are numbered. Judging by the sample lines provided below, the only thing White can decide about here is whether he wants keep on fighting with some risk involved, though, or settle for a draw by repetition. } 
18.Nxa8  ( 18.Nxd5 f4 19.Na3 fxg3 20.Rd1 { looked scary for a second there, but keeping his cool with } 20...Bxa3! 21.Nb6+ Kc7 22.Nxc8 Bc5! 23.hxg3 Rf8 ⇆ { would have granted Black a fighting chance for even something more than a draw. The [f2]-pawn of White looks rather sensitive, with the presence of an additional pair of rooks apparently giving the black pieces a bigger boost than the white queen. } ) f4  
19.Bxf4  gxf4  
20.Qxf4  Be7  
21.Na3  Qxa8  
22.Rd1  Bxa3  
23.Rxd5+  Kc8  
24.Qf5+  Kb8 $10 { Taking on [a3] would still have been fine, simultaneously however allowing Black to keep on playing for a win once the king of his is successfully transferred towards [a7]. Because of this, personally I would rather stick to a drawing barrage of checks if I were White. Your score: One point for choosing 15...g5!, the second one for following up with 17...f5! afterwards. Last but not least, you may give yourself the third one if you were purposefully branching between Nc7xa8 and Nc7xd5 both after 15...Qg4? and 15...g5!. }  

