[FEN "6k1/2N2pp1/1p5p/p2PP3/2p2p1P/P2b1P2/1q4P1/4Q1K1 b - - 0 34"]
c3 $2 
{ This position arose on the board as a result of an Advance French. White started losing the thread somewhere between the middlegame and the endgame, leading to a position a pawn down and apparently featuring the easiest of winning plans on top of that, which is pushing the c4-pawn all the way to promotion-square. What could be simpler than that? Sadly for Black, however, 34...c3? right away throws the win out of the window. Much simpler was 34...Qd4+ 35.Kh2 Bf5 36.e6 fxe6 37.dxe6 Qe3 -+ , when after a possible trade of queens on [e3] White would have admittedly won a piece by promoting his e-pawn, but would have not been capable of stopping Black's [c3]-passer at the same time. } 
35.e6  { As White is unable to stop Black's passed pawn on the queenside, he needs to resort to counterplay instead. And if any of the white central pawns is supposed to be pushed, only the movement of his [e5]-pawn is going to grant him a sufficient amount thereof. } fxe6  ( 35...c2 36.exf7+ Kh7 { would have been smart as the double-queen endgame must be a win for Black here thanks to his material advantage, but unfortunately for him there is the exquisite } 37.f8=N+! Kg8 38.Qe8 c1=Q+ 39.Kh2 { , when Black could keep on going for a short while, but at the end of the day would not be able to avoid a draw based on the [Nf8]-[g6]+ motif, e.g. } 39...Qxg2+ 40.Kxg2 Qf1+ 41.Kh2 Qf2+ 42.Kh1 Qxf3+ 43.Kh2 Qg3+ 44.Kh1 Qxh4+ 45.Kg1 Qg4+ 46.Kh2 Qh4+ 47.Kg1= { with a threefold repetition probably representing Black's smartest choice here. } ) 
36.Qe5 $3 { Most probably missed by both of the players in time-trouble. In order to understand the merits of the text move, the white player would first need to determine the deficiencies of the tempting 36. [Qxe6]+, and then only try to figure out a suitable modification of his own follow-up. } ( { Instead, the game saw } 36.dxe6? c2 37.e7 c1=Q 38.e8=Q+ Kh7 -+ { with a winning position all the same, which Black still somehow managed to squander in zeitnot... } ) ( { White should have wrestled with the temptation of going for the straightforward } 36.Qxe6+? { , as the position arising after } 36...Kh7 37.Qe5 {(with [Nc7]-[e6] and therefore a mating threat on [g7] to come would have granted him enough counterplay to hold the draw, was it not for)} 37...Qd2! { , threatening an exchange of queens on [e3] once again. White could certainly evade here with } 38.Kh2 { , but then after } 38...Qf2! { Black would not be giving White the time to capture on [c3] due to the ...[Bd3]-[f1] mating threat. After the further } 39.Ne6 Qxh4+ 40.Kg1 Qf6 -+ { all of a sudden it would turn out that Black managed to consolidate effectively as the [c3]-pawn survives and White cannot create any serious counter-threats anymore. } ) Qd2  
37.Kh2  { As in the case of 36.Qxe6+? analyzed above, here evading a trade of queens would have also been essential. This time, however, the position would have been significantly different. } Qf2  
38.Nxe6  Qxh4+  
39.Kg1 $10 ( 39. Kg1 { It would have been necessary to hand over the c3-pawn after something like } 39...Qe7 { , as acting accordingly with } ) Qf6 $2 { would have jeopardized Black to } 
40.Qb8+ $1 { when after the forced } Kf7  
41.Qf8+  Kg6  
42.Nxf4+  Kf5  
43.Qxf6+  Kxf6  
44.Nxd3 $18 { White would have been a piece up and would therefore also be winning. So what was the difficult part of this exercise? On one hand, surely the fact that the saving 36.Qe5!! intermezzo represented a move by which White stopped literally one square before helping himself to a pawn with a check. For me however personally, it was the hardest to understand that the difference between the text move and the game continuation 36.Qxe6+? lied in a relatively small detail 40.Qb8+! revealed only many moves later, with the line leading to it in the first place not being entirely trivial either... Your score: Three points for spotting 36.Qe5!! and figuring out its justification. Award yourself a singular point if you saw nothing better than the line beginning with 36.Qxe6+? and ending after 40...Qf6. }  

