Qc6 $1 { The strongest approach. Apart from exposing the white [a2]-pawn to the dangerous rook-battery, Black may from now on start contemplating the merits behind the possible arrival of his queen to [c3]. } ( { By comparison, } 31...Qxa2+?! { would have significantly prolonged the white king's lifespan as after the further } 32.Qxa2 Rxa2 33.e5 dxe5 34.Nc1 R2a6 35.Rxe5 Rxf6 36.Rd3 Rf4 ∓ { Black's hopes of winning this position would have to start revolving exclusively around the idea of technically converting his material advantage. Still, this could prove to be an arduous task in practice because of White being able to generate counterplay along the seventh rank any moment now. } ) 
{ Black has been under pressure for most of the game, but now it is his time to shine. Despite being a pawn down, all of his pieces are focused on attacking the opponent's king. White, on the other hand, cannot benefit from the additional [f6]-pawn as long as it remains blocked by the black bishop on [f7]. Taking all this into consideration, Black takes over the initiative from now on. } 
32.Rd2  { A perfectly understandable response - White anticipates a nasty strike on [a2], so before this happens she scrambles to establish additional defenses around her [c2]-pawn. } ( 32.Nd4 Qe8 33.e5 Rxa2 34.e6 Rxb2+ 35.Kxb2 { has been suggested by one of my students as a desperate attempt to turn the tables in an otherwise already lost position, but Black's material edge is so considerable here that he could even afford to proceed with the calm } 35...Kg8 -+ { , as now taking the bishop would have simultaneously freed the black queen as well. } ) Qc3 $2 { Unbelievably, this logical follow-up could have cost Black half a point in the tournament table. } ( { Instead, Ponomariov could have already cashed in on his attacking initiative by means of the materialistic } 32...Rxa2 33.Qxa2 Rxa2 34.Kxa2 Qa4+ 35.Kb2 Qa3+ 36.Kb1 Bxb3 37.cxb3 Qxb3+ 38.Rb2 { , just to all of a sudden start acting in a much more 'altruistic' manner with } 38...Qd3+! 39.Ka1 Kg8! 40.f4 Qg3 -+ { , thus transposing into a winning queen versus two rooks endgame. This evaluation stems mainly from the fact that White would have now been unable to coordinate her rooks while at the same time protecting both the king and her pawns from queen checks. Once White's zombie pawns on [f4], [f6] and [h6] start dropping, what would have proven to be fatal for her would be the necessity to stop the opponent's passed pawns from promoting on top of that. } ) 
33.Qxc3 $7 { This capture looks admittedly suicidal, as the newly arriving black pawn to [c3] will begin contributing to a variety of mating threats. Still, chess is not ski jumping so that any points where to be awarded for style. Also, calculation - as a purely empirical method of determining the best move in the position - is completely independent from questions of beauty or taste. If the given continuation works, even if it looks ugly, it can be played. Too many a times players rely rather on appearances when judging their position, even if the amount of time left on the clock as well as the nature of the situation allows/suggests to verify it deeper. } ( { In the game, White preferred } 33.Red1? { , when after } 33...Rxa2 34.Qxa2 Rxa2 35.Kxa2 Qxf6 36.Rd3 Kg8 37.Kb1 Qf4 38.Na5 Qxh6 -+ { the presence of an additional pair of minor pieces on the board did not seem to substantially alter the assessment mentioned in the previous note. What is more, I can imagine the black bishop together with the queen becoming more of nuisance for the rival than the two rooks and knight team as the bishop can now contribute both to some threats along the [g8]-[a2] diagonal as well as support the [h7]-pawn on its path forward. } ) bxc3  
34.Rd4 $1 ( { Literally every single student of mine instinctively explored } 34.Rd3?? { as their primary candidate move in this position. The reason for this is simple - nobody wants to tolerate such a thorn in their eye as the black [c3]-pawn for long. Still, this would have been losing in view of the invasion of the black rook along the a-file, resulting in corridor mate or heavy material losses for White: } 34...Bxb3 35.cxb3 Rxa2 36.Rxc3 {(otherwise ...[Ra2]-[b2]+ followed by ...[Ra8]-[a1]# comes)} 36...Ra1+ 37.Kc2 Rxe1 -+ { . } ) Rxa2  ( { This time } 34...Bxb3 35.cxb3 Rxa2 { would not have worked just as well in view of } 36.Ra4! { , which might be clearly visible at this point of time, but was certainly far less obvious before capturing on [c3] or even just a move earlier. After this, the game would have escalated towards absolute equality, e.g. } 36...R2xa4 37.bxa4 Kg8 38.e5 Kf7 39.exd6 cxd6 40.Re7+ Kxf6 41.Rxh7 Rxa4 42.Rd7= { . } ) 
35.Re3 $1 Bxb3  
36.Rxc3 $7 { The tactical justification behind the daring 33.Qxc3 is now revealed - in return for the piece, White would have averted mate and gained strong counterplay after the capture on [c7], together with a couple of pawns as a consolation prize. } 
{ Now after the trickiest } c5 $5 
37.Rxd6  Ra1+  
38.Kb2  c4  { in order to save her skin, White would still need to spot } 
39.Rd8+ $1 Rxd8  
40.Kxa1  { but then after something like } Ba4  
41.Rxc4  Bd7 $7 
42.Rd4  Kg8  
43.e5  Kf7  
44.f4 $10 { Thanks to the strength of her connected passed pawns on the kingside, White would have been totally out of danger already. Your score: Award yourself one point for not rejecting 33.Qxc3 on general grounds. The two remaining points belong to you if you managed to discover 34.Rd4! followed 35.Re3 as well as their concrete justification. }  

