{ In this position stemming from the Classical Nimzo, White has just played 14.e5, clearly aiming to snatch one of the black knights. Black's situation admittedly looks dire because of this, so no wonder that his response in the game was dictated predominantly by this very circumstance. } Nb8 $3 { Quite surprisingly, from this apparently problematic situation Black was able to transpose straight into a much better position, had he resorted to this far-from-obvious idea. By playing like this, Black would be daring his rival to help himself to the rook on [a8], just to have it trapped immediately afterwards. } ( { Instead, Gadimbayli went for } 14...Qa5+?! 15.b4 Qd5 { , intending to consolidate his position tactically. Now after the further } 16.Rc1 Qxc6 17.Rxc6 Nd5 18.Ke2 b5 19.Bd2 N7b6 20.Ra1= { the position remained more or less balanced, as every try of Black to install his knight on [c4] is going to be countered by [a3]-[a4], softening up its base on [b5]. } ) ( { Some of my students suggested an alternative solution which appeared to be very much in the spirit of the text move, that is } 14...Nd5?! 15.Qxd7 c6 { , with the justification that the [c6]-pawn cannot be captured yet as its disappearance would have opened the floodgates for the black rooks right into White's camp. Still, after the precise } 16.a4! { , trying to evacuate the queen via [c6] to [b5] soon enough, the game would have much probably escalated into a draw after something like } 16...h6 17.Bd2 Qd3 18.Qxc6 Rac8 19.Qb5 Rc4= {(when Black would have stayed safe on account of the perpetual check motif with ... [Qd3]-[e4]-[c2]b1+. But why was 14...Nb8!! so hard to spot? On one hand, barely anybody would have decided to voluntarily part with a whole rook for the sake of saving just a troubled knight. The main reason for this, however, was in my opinion that setting traps in chess requires a completely different mindset than making other types of moves. First of all, the idea to trap a piece of the opponent needs to come to our minds in the first place. Afterwards, one needs to ensure that the whole operation is actually worth it, comparing the possible material investment during such a procedure to the probability of it working out at the end of the day. Finally, the whole concept needs to be falsified, meaning that it needs to be tested for its viability by confronting it with various, even the most obscure refutation attempts. As opposed to regular calculation or analysis, falsification implies the necessity to mentally take the other side of the whole affair taking place on the board, which can prove to be difficult to achieve psychologically for some.)} ) 
15.Qxa8 $6 { Not White's best response in objective terms, but certainly the one which allows us to demonstrate the full extent of Black's idea. } ( { The engine claims that } 15.Qc2 Nd5 16.Rc1 h6 17.Bd2 Rc8 ∓ { would have constituted the least of evils for White, but personally I would not be feeling overjoyed here. His king is stuck in the center while the position is on the verge of opening up by means of ...[c7]-[c5]. On top of this, White's dark-squared bishop looks strategically-castrated as it is literally surrounded by its own pawns, without being able to do anything about Black's omnipotent [d5]-knight either. } ) Nd5  { Despite being a whole rook up, White is unable to save his queen in the long run. It is going to take some time, but eventually Black is going to get his hands on her for sure. He will almost universally begin with ...[c7]-[c6], making ...[Nd5]-[c7] possible no escape towards [e4] anymore!. White can put up some resistance here, with the sample lines explaining how Black should deal with them on a concrete basis. } 
16.Rc1  ( 16.b4 h6 17.Be3 b5! { All of a sudden it turns out that ...[Nb8]-[c6] represents a decent winning option as well. } 18.Rc1 c6 -+ { when the silicon beast says that only } 19.a4 { would have helped the white queen survive for longer, although then the simple } 19...bxa4 {(would have now added yet another problem passed pawn to the long list of nuisances White would be facing.)} ) c6  
17.Rxc6  
{ and just when it seemed that the white queen was about to be saved eventually, Black could reply with } Nd7 $1 
18.Qxf8+  Nxf8  
19.Rc2  h6  
20.Be3  Ng6  
21.g3  Qd3  
22.Rd2  Qc4 $19 { The position would still have been winning for Black. White may in fact be whole two pawns up, but at the same time he would have been virtually paralyzed as his pieces are unable to restore coordination. In the meantime, Black is going to transfer his knight from [g6] to [f5], followed by tightening the screw on the queenside by means of a pawn-march. In view of the above, White's position would have gradually become indefensible. Your score: Finding 14...Nb8!! grants you two points, with the third one going your way if you additionally figured out how the white queen is going to be caught afterwards. Either of the alternatives 14...Qa5?! and 14...Nd5?! brings you merely one point. }  

