{ This was one of the messiest games I have seen in the 2023. Black's nothing less than crazy opening play 1. [d4] [Nf6] 2. [c4] [a6] 3. [Nc3] [b5]?!? 4. [cxb5] [axb5] 5. [Nxb5] e5?!? was just the beginning as what came afterwards was an impressive } { (albeit not always correct) } { display of romantic, attacking chess. Encounters like these usually require not only a decent level of calculation skills, but also the ability to navigate logically through the maze of variations. And how about you - would you be able to handle things here? } Be5 $3 { This would have been Black's only saving resource. There was no point in trying to defend the [c6]-pawn - vacating the [h2]-square for a possible rook-invasion felt much more convincing here. } ( { Instead, the game saw } 24...Rg8? { , which was then swiftly countered by the spectacular } 25.Bg6! { , with the bishop being taboo no matter how Black would have tried to capture it. Sadly for Kanyamarala, after the further } 25...O-O-O { she failed to convert her edge with the relatively simple } 26.Bf5+ Rd7 27.Qe3 +- { , thus allowing the win to slip right past her fingers. } ) ( { One student of mine claimed that Black can hold after } 24...Rb8? 25.Qe3 Be5 26.Bxc6+ Kf8 27.Bh6+ Ke7 { , with the tide apparently about to turn in White's direction due to the incoming ...[Rb8]-[g8]+. Fortunately however for White, he would now be able to gain an essential tempo thanks to } 28.Rd6 +- { when Black would not have had enough time to attack the white king and defend both of his hanging bishops at the same time. } ) ( { Finally, the anticipatory } 24...Kf8? { would have failed to do the trick either in view of } 25.Bh6+ Rxh6 26.Rxh6 Qe5 27.Bg6! Qg5+ 28.Kh1! Qf4 { , with the easiest path towards the win leading via simplification after } 29.Qxf7+! Qxf7 30.Bxf7 Be5 31.Bh5 Bxb2 32.Rg1 +- { . } ) 
25.Bxc6+  ( 25.Rxc6?? { would have revealed another fine tactical point behind retreating the dark-squared bishop to [d4] in the first place, namely that now after } 25...Rh2+ 26.Kg1 Bd4+ 27.Be3 Qg3+ 28.Bg2 Qxg2 { White would be unexpectedly getting mated herself. } ) Kf8  
26.Bh6+  ( { Alternatively, White could have gone for } 26.Rxf7+ Qxf7 27.Qxf7+ Kxf7 28.Bxa8 Rxa8 29.Re1 { , but this option would have represented merely a bailout attempt, as now after } 29...Bxb2 30.Rxe2 Bxa3= { the additional [c5]-pawn would have surely not sufficed for a win for White anyhow. } ) Rxh6  ( { The white dark-squared bishop needed to be eliminated at all cost. Not because it creates such dangerous threats, but rather just because instead } 26...Kg8? 27.Rg6+! Kh7 { would have allowed White to finish the opponent off in the loveliest of styles, that is with } 28.Be4!! fxg6 29.Rh1 { . The black king would have proven to be helpless against the discovered attack along the h-file, e.g. } 29...Rae8 30.Bf8+ Bh2 31.Bd6 +- { . } ) 
27.Rxh6  Ra6 $1 { This aesthetically pleasing rook-lift along the sixth rank constitutes one of the cornerstones of Black's saving idea. All of a sudden, White's light-squared bishop would have become a hostage, thus reducing the opponent's attacking potential to zero. } ( 27...Rb8? 28.Qh3 Rxb2 29.Kh1 Bh5 { felt quite compelling for Black in the beginning, but even here White's guardian angel would have remained on duty: } 30.Bg2 □ Rxg2 31.Qxg2 Bxa1 32.Qa8+ Ke7 33.Qe4+ Kf8 34.Rxh5 +- { , when the material edge of three pawns would have been more than enough to claim a win in such an endgame. } ) 
28.Qd5  ( 28.Kf2 { would have led to a much shakier version of a draw for White, even if after the further } 28...Rxc6 29.Rxc6 Qxc6 30.Kxe2 Qg2+ 31.Kd3 { it would have still been Black who would need to pick his checks wisely: } 31...Qg6+ □ 32.Kc4 Qe4+ □ 33.Kb5 Qb7+ 34.Ka4 Qa6+ 35.Kb4 Qb7+= { with a perpetual check. } ) Bb5 $1 { Without this pinning motif Black's play so far would have been absolutely pointless. The threat of ...[Bb5xc6] resulting in a skewer along the [h1]-[a8] diagonal cannot be ignored. } 
{ Now White would have been well-advised to agree a draw by threefold repetition: } 
29.Bxb5  Rxh6  
30.Rd1  Kg7  
31.Bd3  Rh2+  
32.Kf3  Rh3+ $10 { When White's two surplus pawns would have been meaningless, as her monarch is unable to evade checks along the dark squares successfully. This example highlights a problem arising in similar situations, that is how to handle positions which are so chaotic that it is impossible to evaluate them properly, yet alone understand why a given variation should even be calculated. Such cases are rare, but do certainly happen in practice from time to time. What I suggest then is to gather as much intel about the position as possible, even if these would be just scraps of information, e.g. a threat of the rival we need to watch out for, or maybe at least a sign of any typical motif available in similar structures. When formed together, the general type of information may help us understand what should be our overall goal in the given situation, while more concrete threats of the opponent might translate to candidate moves needed to be considered at different stages of the variation tree. Your score: You receive one point already for at least considering 24...Be5! as a candidate move. The second and third one may be added as well after reaching 27...Ra6! and 28...Bb5! in your calculations, respectively. Add only one point to your stack if you wrongly believed that 24...Rb8? holds. }  

