{ 47...Ka7 or 47...Ka8? } --   

[Event "Calculate Like a Beast: The Moranda Method"]
[White "2.3) No Pain = No Gain: Puzzle Set #3"]
[Black "Abasov, Nijat vs. Andersen, Mad, Poland 2023"]
[Result ""]
[FEN "1k6/4n3/1p1Q3p/pP1P2p1/P2R2P1/5B2/2r2PK1/4q3 b - - 0 47"]

{ Black has been under pressure throughout almost the entire game, but stubborn defense allowed him to escape into a tactical, but objectively equal endgame. The reason for this is that both of the players seems to be equally close to ending each other, with the potential discovered attack after [d5]-[d6]+ looking as menacingly as Black's capture on [f2]. Now, in order to maintain the favorable status quo, Black just needed to pick the correct square to go away to with his king. And which one do you prefer here - [a7] or [a8]? } Ka8 $1 { Voluntarily walking into the aforementioned discovered-attack motif would have surely required nerves of steel. It is quite instructive to learn however why the escape towards the apparently much safer dark square [a7] failed utterly. } ( { Literally everyone and their mother would have decided in favor of the game continuation } 47...Ka7? { , but what Andersen must have missed or underestimated here was } 48.Rd2!! { , dynamically winning a tempo to defend his [f2]-pawn, but most importantly forcing Black to decide how to have the [d2]-rook captured. Taking with the rook would have been just hopeless as it exposes Black to [Qd6]-[c7]+ followed by [d5]-[d6]+, allowing White eventually to recapture the rook with even more pawns on top of that. Because of this, Black now went for } 48...Qxd2 { , only to get stunned by } 49.Qxe7+ Kb8 50.Qf8+ Ka7 51.Be4! { with the usual suspect on [f2] being all of a sudden completely out of danger, and White simultaneously threatening to open up the long light diagonal in order to once again try and land mate on [a8]. It is nonetheless quite instructive to witness how White would be using the so-called staircase motif in order to cash in the full point against Black's most tenacious defensive try: } 51...Rc4 52.Qg7+ Kb8 53.Qh8+ Ka7 54.Qh7+ Kb8 55.Qxh6 { White can afford to consume the rival's [h6]-pawn as his [e4]-bishop is taboo on account of a possible double-attack from [h7] later on. Still, now after something like } 55...Qd4 56.Bf3 Rc2 57.Qf8+ Ka7 58.Qf7+ Kb8 59.Qg8+ Rc8 60.Qxg5 Rc2 61.Qg8+ Rc8 62.Qg6 +- { , he would have been winning thanks to his material advantage as well as the inability of the black pieces to generate any further threats against the white monarch. What was most interesting for me however about this moment was that, at least judging by the time stamps, Black played 47...Ka7? almost off-hand, while still having around 14 minutes left on the clock. In other words, this must have been a purely instinctive decision of his to avoid light squares by definition. With plenty of time left on the clock though and this possibly also being a moment in which White is going to throw in the towel and agree a draw in the absence of anything convincing, Black should have spent maybe a minute or two more here. There is namely a huge probability that this additional amount of time would have allowed him discover 48.Rd2!!, thus leading to a more in-depth analysis of the arising position. Time-management plays a crucial role within the calculation process, especially as nobody wants to be in the right regarding the evaluation of a given line, but not be left any time more on the clock to be able to prove it. Sometimes, however, we rely on intuition when it was exactly accurate calculation that needed to be asked its opinion. Being able to recognize such moments and distinguish between those two approaches is not less crucial in chess than the ability to calculate accurately as such. } ) 
48.Qg3  ( { This time } 48.Rd2 { would not have worked half as well compared to the previous note, as now after } 48...Qxd2 { White would have no longer been in a position to play [d5]-[d6] with a check. This does not mean, however, that he would have been immediately doomed. Objectively speaking, the situation would have remained balanced, with it actually still being Black who would need to stay a bit careful: } 49.Qxb6 Nxd5 50.Be4 Rc4 51.Qd8+ Ka7 52.b6+ Ka6! 53.Bxd5 Rxg4+ 54.Kh3 Rh4+ 55.Kg2 Rg4+= { with a draw by threefold repetition arising on the board. } ) Ng6  { Quite unexpectedly, a major backlash is coming in White's direction here. The inclusion of Black's knight into play via [h4] or possibly even via [f4] under certain circumstances would have surely caused headaches to the white monarch as his safety would have this way become severely compromised. } 
49.Kh3 $5 { The most testing idea for White, as we shall see in a moment from now. } ( { By comparison, } 49.d6+ Ka7 50.d7 { would have been the far more tempting approach of White, but fortunately for Black, he would have been able to defend thanks to the foxy } 50...Nh4+ 51.Kh3 Nxf3 52.Qxf3 Rc3! { , when after the precise } 53.Kg2! { White would not have been able to avert the barrage of checks coming from the black queen, e.g. } 53...Rxf3 54.Kxf3 Qc3+ 55.Ke4 Qe1+ 56.Kd3 Qd1+ 57.Ke4 Qxg4+ 58.Ke3 Qe6+ 59.Kd2 Qa2+= { . } ) Nf4+  ( { But not } 49...Nh4?? { , attempting to transpose into the variation quoted before, as now after } 50.d6+ Ka7 { White could have switched plans with } 51.Rd1 +- { , taking away from the opponent any double-attack motifs from [f1] in advance. } ) 
50.Rxf4  gxf4  
51.d6+  Ka7  
52.Qxf4  Qf1+  
53.Bg2  Qxf2  { when it would this time be White who would need to resort to the only } 
54.Bf3 $1 Qf1+  
55.Kh4  Qf2+  
56.Kh3 $10 { in order to secure a draw due to a perpetual check. Your score: You receive one point for electing 47...Ka8! and the remaining two ones for neutralizing 49.d6+ and 49.Kh3!? respectively. No points awarded if you intuitively favored 47...Ka7? or just missed 48.Rd2!! as a response thereto. }  

