{ Evaluate 40...a3. } --   

[Event "Calculate Like a Beast: The Moranda Method"]
[White "2.3) No Pain = No Gain: Puzzle Set #3"]
[Black "Sindarov, Javokhir vs. Horvath, Dominik, Sharjah 2024"]
[Result ""]
[FEN "7R/k1p2p2/2n4P/5b2/pp5P/3r1PB1/1P6/5BK1 b - - 0 40"]

{ After a rather shady treatment of the opening stage 1.e4 [e5] 2.Nf3 [Nf6] 3.d3?!, White found himself in need of entering an extremely double-edged pawn-race happening on opposite wings. Black, on the other hand, is currently about to make it out of the time-trouble zone by playing his 40th move. Despite his rook hanging on [d3] at the moment, it does in fact look appetizing to just ignore the threat and push the [a4]-pawn all the way to promotion-square. And what do you think? } a3 $1 { 'Passed pawns should be pushed!' - I can hear you exclaim. But after reaching this point of the course you must have already gotten used to the idea that no points are being awarded for general assumptions here - it is only the calculated variations and material proof of their viability that matter. } ( { Instead, in the game Black blew it by going for } 40...Rd1? { , when after the further } 41.h7 Bxh7 42.Rxh7 a3 43.bxa3 bxa3 44.Rxf7 a2 45.Rxc7+ Kb6 46.Rxc6+= { a draw has been agreed as White is going to follow up with [Bg3]-[e5], taking down the black pawn once it reaches [a1], with a transposition into a drawn endgame. } ) 
41.bxa3  { Just for the record, the immediate capture on [d3] would have changed nothing, merely transposing into a position analyzed below after 42.Bxd3. } b3 $1 ( { By comparison, } 41...bxa3?? {(would have been a terrible mistake on one hand because it increases the probability that White's dark-squared bishop is going to make it in time to stop Black's passed pawn before it reaches [a1] e.g. thanks to the [Bg3]-[e1]-[c3], once the [d3]-rook gets eliminated, but also in view of the more concrete)} 42.Bxd3 a2 43.Bf2+ Kb7 44.Ba6+! { , luring the black king right into a skewer along the a-file after the further } 44...Kxa6 45.Ra8+ Kb7 46.Rxa2 +- { . } ) 
42.h7  ( { Alternatively, } 42.Bxd3 Bxd3 43.Re8 b2 44.Re1 { would have not been particularly helpful for White either, provided merely that Black would have remembered about defending his [c7]-pawn first after something like } 44...Kb6 -+ { before calmly promoting his [b2]-pawn into a queen. } ) Bxh7  ( { In such a tactical position Black could not have possibly allowed White to promote to a queen first with } 42...b2?? { as now after } 43.Ra8+! Kb7 44.h8=Q b1=Q 45.Ra4 +- { White would have been also the first one to strike thanks to the deadly [Qh8]-[a8]+ idea. } ) 
43.Rxh7  Rd5 $1 { The last finesse required from Black before claiming the full point. It was imperative to prevent White from reintroducing his rook into play along the fifth rank, as confirmed by the lines quoted below. } ( { During an analysis session, one of my students pointed out that Black could have achieved the very same goal by means of } 43...Rxf3? 44.Bf2+ Kb7 45.Rh5! f5! { As we started investigating this position even deeper, we reached the preliminary conclusion that White might be even unexpectedly winning this after the further } 46.Bg2 b2 47.Bxf3 b1=Q+ 48.Kg2 { , as Black's king seemed to be in dire straits on account of the awful pin along the [h1]-[c6] diagonal. Now imagine our surprise when the engine informed us that Black still holds this thanks to } 48...Qa1!! { , aiming to meet [Rh5]-[h6] with the ...[Qa1]-[g7]+ double-attack. If this is not yet enough for you, after the second best } 49.Rxf5 { Black would have been in a position to conclusively unpin his knight with } 49...Ka6= { , as now } 50.Bxc6 { would have resulted in an instant draw by stalemate after } 50...Qh1+! 51.Kxh1= { Now, how do you like that?! } ) ( { Interestingly enough, } 43...Rd1?? { would have somehow proven to be a 'cursed' move within this exercise, as afterwards White would have once again been all of a sudden winning after } 44.Bf2+ Ka8 45.Rh8+ Nb8 46.Rh5! { with a timely comeback of the white rook taking place. Now, Black does not necessarily lose the pawn for nothing here, as } 46...b2 47.Ra5+ { can be answered by the apparently sly } 47...Na6!? { - but even so, the position arising on the board after the further } 48.Rb5 b1=Q 49.Rxb1 Rxb1 50.h5 +- { would have still needed to be assessed as winning for White based on the distant [h5]-passer supported by the powerful bishop-pair. As a sidenote, this is also exactly the line which represented the justification behind 43...Rd5! in the main variation. } ) 
44.Rh6  b2  
45.Rxc6  b1=Q  
46.Rxc7+  Kb6 $19 { With White unable to defend, among others, against Black establishing a battery along the first rank. Although you might have gotten the impression that I battered the reputation of solving studies as a chess training method, I cannot possibly deny their positive effect on the development of a player when used properly and in moderation, though!. This puzzle may not be a study per se as the starting position stems from the game analysis instead of having been constructed, but meets all the requirements of a study-like exercise. It stems namely from a practical game and is as challenging as it is instructive. It requires a lot of branching and punishes any deviation from the main line swiftly. On top of that, it improves the understanding of such factors as coordination of pieces and the geometry of the board as it forces us to get the most out of our pieces in view of limited material available. So just in case you wondered what I meant when indicating in the introduction to this course that you should only be using studies which meet 'some basic working criteria', the nature of this very puzzle might serve as a decent point of reference for you in the future. Your score: The very first point goes your way for understanding that 40...a3! is indeed the way to go for Black. The second and third point belong to you once you have reached 41...b3! and 43...Rd5!, respectively, in your calculations. }  

