{ Find the mistake in the following continuation. } 
40.Rd5+  Ke4  
41.Qb4+  Kf3  
42.Bd1+  Kg3  
43.Rd3+  Ne3  
44.Qc3  Re4  
45.Qe1+  Kf4  
46.Qh4+  Ng4  
47.Rf3+  Ke5  
48.Qg3+  Kd5 $132  

[Event "Calculate Like a Beast: The Moranda Method"]
[White "2.3) No Pain = No Gain: Puzzle Set #3"]
[Black "Gurel, Ediz vs. Martirosyan, Haik, Sharjah 2024"]
[Result ""]
[FEN "8/p2RQp2/1q2p3/1P2k2p/5rn1/1B6/K7/8 w - - 0 40"]

40.Rd5+  
{ White is three pawns down here but in possession of decent counter-attacking chances due to the exposed king of the opponent. As however a backlash already seems to be hanging in the air in particular ideas like ... [Rf4]-[f2]+ and/or ...[Qb6]-[a5]+, he needs to start blasting now. } Ke4  
41.Qb4+  Kf3  
42.Bd1+ $2 { But this trivially-looking move turns out to be the culprit here. } ( { With the benefit of hindsight, we will have now understood that instead of the text move, White should have started off with } 42.Rd3+ Ne3 43.Bd1+ Kg3 { , thus proceeding to transpose straight back towards the game with } 44.Qc3 { . One student of mine wanted to explore the alternative } {44.Qe1+ Rf2+ , when the slightly counter-intuitive 45.Kb1! (would have still yielded White's position savable why on Earth would walking straight into 45...[Qxb5]+ work though?!, but not the apparently much more logical) 45.Ka1?? in view of the stunning 45...Kg2!! 46.Rxe3 Qd4+ 47.Rc3 h4 -+ . Quite unexpectedly, White would have ended up paralyzed, and therefore close to defenseless against the simple push of the black [h4]-pawn all the way to promotion-square. } ) Kg2 $1 { After checking out the time consumption of both players, I would risk the statement that none of them considered this move as a possible candidate. } ( { In the game, Black quickly decided to stick to the rook by means of } 42...Kg3? { when after the more or less forced } 43.Rd3+ Ne3 44.Qc3 Re4 45.Qe1+ Kf4 46.Qh4+ Ng4 47.Rf3+ Ke5 48.Qg3+ Kd5 ⇆ { White's position would have remained perfectly defensible in view of the black monarch still being stuck in the center. Interestingly enough, the engine now claims that even } 49.Ra3 { , allowing a theoretically unfavorable for White trade of queens on [f2], would have sufficed for a draw as well. } ) 
43.Rd2+  ( 43.Qxf4 { would have allowed White to win a pawn after the further } 43...exd5 44.Bxg4 hxg4 45.Qxg4+ Kf2 46.Qf4+ Ke2 -+ {(, but the endgame as such would need to be evaluated as lost for him. The reason for this is that no matter what happens next, Black always stays two pawns up. A similar queen endgame with the additional [f7]- or [d5]-pawn may theoretically present White with excellent drawing chances make sure to study theoretical endgames thoroughly kids!, these dissipate however completely if you place one more black pawn on [a7] in this situation.)} ) Rf2  
44.Bxg4  Qe3 $1 { Assuming that this is exactly the move Black missed during the game from afar which is however highly improbable taking into consideration the time-stamps online, a possible rejection of 42...Kg2! by the second player upfront would have become totally relatable. Before recapturing on [g4], Black namely needed to use this intermezzo in order to ensure that he will be able to guard his king against a queen-check from [g4]. } ( { Quite obviously, instead the most natural } 44...hxg4 { would have led to a draw by threefold repetition after } 45.Qxg4+ Kf1 46.Qd1+ Kg2 47.Qg4+= { . } ) 
45.Rb2  hxg4  
46.Qxg4+  Qg3  
47.Qe4+  Qf3 $19 { With a winning position for Black as White is slowly running out of checks while still being two whole pawns down. This puzzle featured a plethora of occasions for us to go wrong. First of all, the stem line looked pretty convincing as it consisted mostly of checks and forced replies. Secondly and more importantly however, the 42...Kg2! improvement represented a move which would apparently not only be letting go of the [f4]-rook, but also exposing the black king to multiple follow-up threats as well. Even afterwards, Black would still need to correctly assess the arising queen endgame as winning for him and spot the necessary 44...Qe3, too. This only confirms one truth about life - which also appears to be perfectly applicable to chess at the same time - expressed by Chuck Palahniuk in his novel and later also movie titled 'Fight Club', which is that 'on a long enough timeline, the survival rate for everyone drops to zero'... Your score: You receive the first point for finding 42...Kg2! and another one for throwing in 44...Qe3! later along the way. You may give yourself a third and final point if you managed to figure out that any potential checks and threats of White after the above improvement is played were just ghosts. }  

