{ After a rather double-edged middlegame stage, White managed to transpose into the following multi-piece endgame with an additional pawn in store. This circumstance alone should not however suffice for a win here alone, especially as Black's forces make a much more active impression. In particular, ...[Rc8]-[c2] looks pretty dangerous as literally nobody likes to ever see their second rank being invaded by the major pieces of the opponent. With the window opportunity about to close in a second from now after [Nb3]-[c5], the decision regarding Black's next move seems fairly obvious... But is it? } Bf5 $3 { An extremely difficult move to play in view of how tempting the previously discussed alternative was. What is more, the logic behind the text can only be fully comprehended once the reasons why other moves of Black fail are revealed. } ( { It turns out that the game continuation } 31...Rc2? 32.Qa1 h5 { would have only helped White consolidate his position, had he gone for the most accurate } 33.Qf1! { . After something like } 33...a6 34.Nf3 Ne4 { White would have been able to solve almost all of his problems thanks to the dynamic } 35.Ne5! Qg5 36.f4! Qf6 37.Nc5 +- {(, with the black forces getting gradually but also quickly! pushed back. A move like 31...Rc2? belongs to the type of resources players universally call 'impossible to be wrong'. In view of this, I can imagine many of them not being able to withstand the urge to play it, even if they simultaneously saw that this way they would be only accelerating the necessary transfer of White's queen towards the kingside.)} ) ( { Apart from that, Black could have also tried } 31...Qg4? { , but then after something like } 32.Nc5 Qd1+ 33.Nf1 Ne4 34.Qe2 Qxd4 35.Qg4 Rg8 36.Qf4 +- { , despite having have recovered a pawn, Black's position would have become critical, this time due to a plethora of dangers awaiting Black along the [a1]-[h8] diagonal, especially should [Nc5]-[e6] and [f2]-[f3] come one day. } ) 
32.Nf3  { Objectively speaking, this is White's best option, even if closing the c-file with 32.Nc5?! would have been once again very enticing, this time however on a mirror-like basis. } ( { Instead, } 32.Nc5? { would have highlighted the big point behind 31...Bf5!!, which is that now after } 32...Rg8! { all of a sudden, White would have been jeopardized to various attacking ideas along the g-file, in particular ...Ng5-[h3]+. For example } 33.Qc3 Nh3+! 34.Rxh3 Bxh3 35.Qxh3 Qxd2 36.Qf3 Qc1+ 37.Kh2 Qh6+ 38.Kg1 Rf8 ∓ { , when the mere fact that White cannot help himself to the black [d5]-pawn would have already indicated how much his situation had worsened in the meantime. } ) ( { As a possible antidote to the above, White might resort to the prophylactic } 32.Rg3?! { , which would have backfired, however, in view of the 'notorious' } 32...Rc2 { . After something like } 33.Qa1 Rxd2 34.Nxd2 Qxd2 35.Re3 Ne4 36.Qe1 Qxd4 37.f3 Nd6 38.Qf2 Qf6 ↑ { , in order to survive this, White would need to find } 39.Qg3! { , intending an exchange of queens, and pray that the two black light pieces are not going to overpower his rook in the ensuing endgame. } ) Nh3+ $1 { With all of Black's forces locked and loaded for a kingside attack, even sacrificing the knight on [h3] right away would have led to potentially lethal consequences for White. } 
33.gxh3  Bxh3  { White should probably try to bail out towards safety with } 
34.Ne5  Rg8+  
35.Rg3  Rxg3+  
36.fxg3  Qxg3+  
37.Kh1  { even if after the further } Bf5 $1 
38.Nd2  Qh3+  
39.Kg1  Qg3+  
40.Kf1  Qh2 $10 { Black would have still been in a position to force out a draw by means of a perpetual check in case of need. Only now does it become clear to us why 31...Bf5!! was such a hard move to play in a practical game. Black needed to withstand the temptation to go for the forcing 31...Rc2? in lieu of the apparently slow alternative. The text move was just meant to increase the spectrum of possibilities of Black, without conclusively focusing on a pre-specified idea only. Depending on the circumstances, Black would have been ready to switch to a kingside attack or 'consequently' infiltrate via [c2], should the conditions for such an operation improve. Especially in the modern age of instant gratification as well as generally short human attention spans We do like to binge play those nasty bullet games, don't we?, such abilities should be absolutely cultivated and made use of in chess. Your score: One point for spotting 31...Bf5!!, and two more points for figuring out when to bring the rook to [g8], and when to have it transferred to [c2] nonetheless. But if you insisted on playing 31...Rc2? despite not having a convincing follow-up, you should be happy that there are no minus points deducted in this course... }  

