29.Rxb7 $2 ( { Instead, the calm and prophylactic } 29.Qc2!! { would have caused Black a lot of disquiet. Ruling out the ...[Bh5xf3] idea in advance would have neither tarnished White's halo, nor reduced his level of domination in this game as after something like } 29...Qg5 30.Rxb7 Bxf3 31.Qf2! Bh5 32.Bc4 +- { Black would have been on the edge of precipice because of [Bc4xe6] already seriously coming into consideration. } ) ( { When giving this puzzle a try myself, I initially wanted to execute } 29.Bf2? {(with a very similar point to 29. [Qc2]!! taking the [g3]-square under control first for safety reasons, but missed)} 29...Nb8! {(backward knight moves!, when after the further)} 30.Qc7 Ra1+ 31.Bf1 Qxc7 32.Rxc7 Nc6! 33.Rxb7 Rb1= { Black would be getting his pawn back, therefore coming ultra-close to equality. I certainly appreciated the strong outpost on [d4] for my dark-squared bishop from the beginning, but who would have thought that controlling the [a1]-square will prove to be the decisive factor here eventually... } ) 
{ A couple of moves ago, White decided in favor of an enterprising pawn-sacrifice on the queenside, leading to a position in which Black would be actually not that far away from full paralysis. His pieces are either stuck on the edge of the board knight on [a6] or simply restricted by his opponent's forces bishop on [h5]. At first glance, the situation looks ripe for a capture on [b7], after which Black would be on the verge of losing said knight as well. In the absence of anything more forcing than that, Sarana did not hesitate any longer and played 29.Rxb7. } Bxf3 $1 { It turns out that 29.Rxb7? lets the rival off the hook as Black was now able to generate tactical counterplay literally across the whole board. Judging by the time-stamps on the live broadcast, White spent about 12 minutes analyzing this move, so we cannot at all say that it was impulsive or not well-thought. Black certainly cannot afford to waste any more time by remaining passive. } 
30.Qf1  { A pin along the f-file combined with a double-attack against Black's knight and [f7]-pawn at the end of many lines - what could possibly go wrong here? } ( 30.Bxa6 { would have been met by } 30...Qd2! 31.Bf2 Qg5 32.g3 Rd8! { , when after the most precise } 33.Qc2! Rd1+ 34.Bf1 Qh5! 35.Rb8+ Kh7 36.e5+ g6= { it is White who would most probably need to seek a draw one way or another, as otherwise things could very ugly once ...[Qh5xh3] comes. } ) Qxe4  
31.Rd7  
{ Just when it seemed Black was about to be tactically outplayed in this exchange of dynamic blows, he uncorked } Nc7 $1 { My rough guess is that this is the move White had missed from afar when contemplating the consequences of 29.Rxb7?. Until this moment of time he has been playing almost off-hand, only to spend another ten minutes or so on his response to this move. The knight is taboo because of the white [d4]-bishop falling, while other options introduce merely some unorthodox material balances, but no real edge for White. } 
32.Bd3  ( 32.gxf3 Qg6+ 33.Kh1 Nxb5 34.Qxb5 Qb1+ 35.Bg1 Qxb3 ⇆ { and White may be able to win Black's [f7]-pawn in more than one way, but will never be in a position to block the movement of his opponent's [b4]-passer without jeopardizing his king to perpetual check ideas. } ) Qc6 $1 { Those 2700-rated guys really have some mad skills, we gotta give them that! } 
{ Now play might continue } 
33.Rxc7  Qxc7  
34.Qxf3  Qc1+  
35.Bf1  Rd8  
36.Qe3  Qd1 $132 { If things start getting heated up, Black may want to consider sacrificing an exchange for the sake of taking down White's [b3]-pawn, once again preventing White from benefiting from his additional piece. As much as I have been constantly reminding you about the necessity to always consider at least two moves during calculation, I wanted to use this example as an opportunity to explain the concept of something that I personally call 'random tactical shootout'. This is a variable which some of my students implemented into their own calculation process right before the stage when they evaluate the position and, based on that, create a list of candidate moves. What they claim is that if the starting position features a move which stands out as being much very tactical/forcing, they tend to analyze it as deep as possible in the first place, without checking other lines simultaneously too. The point behind this approach is to verify the soundness of the most tempting continuation. If it wins, they play it without too much hesitation. If it fails to achieve the desired goal though, they come back straight to square one to evaluate the situation from scratch, but richer in knowledge in terms of many motifs and ideas they may have discovered along the way. For example, in this case it made plenty of sense to explore the consequences of 29.Rxb7? before anything else, switching our attention to other, much less forcing alternatives only later on. Although I do not feel entirely bought on this concept I am not a huge fan of cutting corners by purposefully omitting moves which at times might prove to be even stronger than the primary choice, I cannot help but admit this may work out in practice from time to time. Just make sure to exercise caution and use common sense while doing so. Your score: Two points are going your way if you correctly assessed the consequences of 29.Rxb7? all the way to 32...Qc6!. The third and final point is awarded only upon understanding the merits behind 29.Qc2!! instead. If you calculated 29.Rxb7? till the end but saw nothing better, add a singular point to your stack for the effort. }  

