{ This position has arisen as a result of the 6.Be3 Sicilian Najdorf. Somewhere between the opening and middlegame stage, Black missed a fairly standard trickshot and needed to start defending desperately from that point on. Compared to how hopeless his situation was previously, the diagram position actually presents him with realistic chances for survival though. The reason for this is that two white pieces are being forked right now. There are two paths he can take at this point of time - 35.Qd5 [Nxd7] 36.Rf7 } { (aiming to recapture material soon enough) } { or 35.Rf8+ [Ka7] 36.Qe3 } { (not giving up on material in the first place) } { . Which of the suggested solutions is the correct one in your opinion? } 
35.Rf8+  ( { In the game, Cheparinov went for } 35.Qd5? Nxd7 36.Rf7 Nb6 { , but must have understood somewhere along the way that the position arising after the further } 37.Rxc7 Nxd5 38.Rc5 Bxg3 39.Rxd5 b5 40.Kc2 Kb7= { is a theoretical draw as per Dvoretsky's Endgame Manual. The white king cannot activate here due to one of his pawns always being threatened by the black bishop. However, even if he moved the [b2]-pawn to the safer square [a2], as long as Black would have kept the enemy king away from [c8], the assessment would have remained unchanged. Most probably because of this, he deviated with } {37.Qf3 . And although he eventually even managed to score a win in a rather amusing manner, this fact did not influence the theoretically drawn evaluation of the positions appearing on the board after 35.Qd5?. } ) Ka7  
36.Qe3 $1 { So far, so good - the white bishop is taboo in view of the pin along the [g1]-[a7] diagonal. But Black has not exhausted all of his defensive resources yet - I hope you did not think it would be that easy, did you? } Qd6 $1 { The most challenging approach - Black renews the double-attack, albeit in a different shape and form. Except for the rook, White should seriously consider the safety of his bishop too as ...[Be5]-[d4] neutralizing the aforementioned pin would have started looking unpleasant already. } ( { In lieu of the text move, Black could have snatched a pawn with } 36...Qb6? 37.Bf5 Bxb2 { , but then after something like } 38.Bc2 Ba3 39.g4 Qe6 40.Qxe6 Nxe6 41.Rf5 +- { resignation would have not been entirely premature as the white [g4]-pawn would have been chaperoned all the way towards promotion-square in no time. } ) 
37.Rf7 $1 { I can imagine my own hand trembling when executing this move on the board in view of the slightly scary 37...[Qg6]+. Fortunately for White however, neither this nor any other moves would have won material for Black on the spot. } ( { Alternatively, White could have also explored the possibility of transposing into an endgame a pawn up after } 37.Bh3? Qxf8 38.Qxe5 { , with hopes of making use of the additional passed g-pawn in the long haul. Still, after the most convincing } 38...Qf2 39.Ka2 Nd3 40.Qc3 Nxb2! 41.Qxb2 Qxg3= { , Black should be able to save this game confidently by just keeping an eye on the [b7]-pawn, while at the same time avoiding the exchange of queens at all cost. } ) Bd4  ( { Instead, } 37...Qd1+ 38.Ka2 Bd4 { does not work as after the further } 39.Rf1! Qxf1 40.Qxd4 Qf8 41.g4 Ka8 42.Bf5 +- { Black's knight would have been badly dominated by the white bishop, leaving him helpless against the progress of White's kingside passer. } ) ( { At the same time, the endgame arising after } 37...Bxg3 38.Rf5 Qd3+ 39.Qxd3 Nxd3 40.Rf3 Ne5 41.Rxg3 Nxd7 42.Kc2 +- { would have been much more interesting to analyze, even if objectively speaking still winning for White. His doubled pawns make things harder for us, as for the sake of evaluating this position we would have not been able to refer exactly to Dvoretsky's work. Then again, a similar endgame featuring one pawn each only on b2/b7 is believed to be winning for the stronger side, so under these circumstances, White could simply activate his pieces to the maximum before carrying out an exchange of pawns on [b5]. } ) 
38.Qf4 $1 { Absolutely necessary if White does not want to lose his bishop for free or allow dangerous counterplay against his king should his queen recklessly decide to abandon the queenside. } Be5  ( { Apart from the text move, Black could have also tried the much more tactical } 38...Qg6+ 39.Rf5 Nxb3 40.Ba4 Nd2+ 41.Kc2 Bg7 { when his [d2]-knight would have been untouchable, at least without the inclusion of } 42.Qf2+! Kb8 43.Kxd2 +- { making the said piece edible once again. } ) 
39.Qc4 $1 { This strong consolidating idea needed to be seen from afar when deciding upon playing 35.Rf8+. No dangers can be posed to White's king anymore, and although his bishop seems to be there for the taking, White would have still managed to stay on top. } Qb6  ( { The most tempting } 39...Nxd7 { would have now been met by } 40.Rxd7! Qxd7 41.Qc5+ b6 42.Qxe5 +- { , with the queen endgame being winning for White as well. There is simply not much Black can do about the opponent's g-pawn turning into a queen one day on [g8]. Curiously enough, in this scenario the slightly damaged white structure on the queenside would have proven to be extremely helpful for the purpose of securing the king a safe haven against checks on [a2]. } ) 
40.b4  Nxd7  
41.Rxd7  Bxg3  
42.Rd5 $18 { When White would have yet again obtained a winning position after [b4]-[b5] to come. The pawn cannot be ignored, but a double-capture on [b5] to follow would have led to a win, with White squeezing his rival after the king arrives to the fifth rank together with the last pawn. You might be wondering why this example has been included in this course at all. First of all, contrary to common belief among especially my junior students, achieving the endgame stage does not discharge you from the duty to keep on calculating variations. Secondly and more importantly, however, this way I wanted to highlight an important tendency, that is the interconnectedness of calculation and theoretical endgame knowledge. What I mean by this is that the closer the game gets towards the endgame, the more important for one's calculation skills does it become to have solid theoretical endgame foundations. The reason for this is that every line you calculate needs to end in an evaluation. During the middlegame stage, such an assessment is going to be to a considerable degree general in nature, while in the endgame assessing the given position should be much more concrete as it can and should! take into consideration one's positive knowledge about the given endgame situation. This is particularly noticeable in computer chess - it is just amazing how much stronger do engines become towards the endgame if they have access to tablebases. The same applies to humans, however - knowing theoretical endgames very well is going to benefit your calculation skills in the endgame immensely. Without them however, you will not be able to evaluate many of the arising endgames conclusively, frequently leaving you feeling like a babe lost in the woods... Your score: You receive one point for working out the winning line all the way to 39.Qc4!. The second point belongs to you if you knew how to win at least three theoretical endgames arising along the way. Finally, add yourself the third and final point if you knew why 35.Qd5? was not the way to go. }  

