[Result ""]
[FEN "r1q2k2/1b1nb1p1/p2p1nB1/4p1N1/P3P2r/1Pp1BQ1P/2P2P2/R3K1R1 b Q - 0 18"]

{ Every single time I get myself into a similar mess as Black in the Sicilian Najdorf, I try to console myself with the words that 'at least I am not a London player...'. But seriously, Black misplayed the opening stage by allowing White to hit him hard on [f7] by the [Ng5] & [Bc4] duo. Right now, as he is scrambling to restore coordination, he already needs to deal with a lot of pressure coming from White's side, in particular the knight invasion via [e6]. Under these circumstances, 18...Nc5 makes perfect sense, but are you sure that executing this move is going to mark the end of the whole story? } Nc5 $1 { Maybe not the most spectacular of moves we have ever seen during our respective chess careers, but surely one which was absolutely necessary in this specific situation - [Ng5]-[e6]+ simply had to be prevented at all cost. Not all heroes wear capes, after all. } ( { Instead, in the game Lukas tried to defend with the rather elaborate } 18...Kg8? 19.Qg3 Rh5 { , hoping to bribe his opponent with an exchange to give up on the attack entirely. Sadly for him, van Batenburg was ruthless, and after the further } 20.Bf5! Rxg5 21.Qxg5 Qf8 22.Qg6 +- { did not let the win slip past his fingers anymore. } ) 
19.Qg3  { This move looks slightly counter-intuitive compared to White's other candidates discussed below, but the point behind it is clear - quickly organizing a battery along the g-file followed by a powerful strike on [g7]. } ( 19.Bxc5 { , played with the idea of continuing with the murderous [Bg6]-[f5] and [Ng5]-[e6]+ combo, would have been met by the prophylactic } 19...Rf4! { intermezzo, aiming to be ready to eliminate the white bishop whenever it arrives to [f5]. Now the engine says that the position remains equal after something like } 20.Qd3 dxc5 21.f3 c4! 22.Qxc3 Ne8! 23.Qxe5 Bxg5 24.Qxg5 Qc7 ⇆ { , but from the practical standpoint all three results would have certainly still been possible. } ) ( { Trying to carry out a similar idea but via an inverted move order with } 19.Bf5?! { , with the justification that the [f4]-square would have temporarily still been covered by White's dark-squared bishop, would have nonetheless failed to a slightly modified version of the same counter, that is: } 19...Rf4! 20.Bxf4 Qxf5! { . The endgame arising after the further } 21.exf5 Bxf3 22.Bxe5 dxe5 23.Nxf3 Re8 ⩱ { would have been slightly more pleasant for Black, in my opinion. Castling long would have been a no-no in view of ...Nc5-e4, so White's rooks would need to stay disconnected for a bit more. Simultaneously, taking on [e5] would have not affected the situation too much either as the knights supported by a rook would have still looked a tad stronger compared to the white pair of rooks. } ) Bxe4 $1 ( { Admittedly, removing the rook from the exposed h4-square with } 19...Rxe4? { appeared to be the most natural continuation. In such a case however, a crazy example of a time-stop in the middlegame after } 20.O-O-O !! { would have proven to be fatal for Black at the end of the day. The reason for this is that despite being a full piece up here, he is unable to consolidate his king's position or generate any effective counterplay against the white counterpart. The machine says that the best he can obtain here is a position that is still lost after } 20...Ne8 21.Nh7+ Kg8 22.Bxe8 Rg4 23.Qxg4 Qxg4 24.Rxg4 Rxe8 25.Bxc5 dxc5 26.Ng5 +- { , when the classical bishop-pair would have not granted him enough compensation for the missing material. } ) 
20.Bxe4  ( { Alternatively, White could have also tested his opponent here with the much more devilish } 20.Bf7!? { , thus creating a mating idea on [g7] after a knight-capture on [e4]. In the absence of any other defensive resource, Black would now need to resort to tactical means, that is decoying the said knight with } 20...Rxh3! { . After the further } 21.Nxh3 Kxf7 22.Bxc5 Ke8 23.Bxd6! Bxd6 24.Qxg7 Qf5 25.Ng5 Bd5 { , just when it seemed that Black was about to consolidate his position successfully, White could uncork the unbelievable } 26.Rh1 ⇆ { threatening back-rank mate, but most importantly exposing the black monarch to a plethora of further checks should Black dare to take that juicy rook on [h1]. } ) ( { The most tempting } 20.Qxh4? { would, however, have been utterly wrong on account of } 20...Bxg6 21.Bxc5 dxc5 22.Qc4 Ke8! 23.Ne6 Bf7 24.Nxg7+ Kf8 25.Qxc3 c4 -+ { , with Black already taking over the game notwithstanding his slight material deficit. } ) Rxe4  
21.Nxe4  Ncxe4  ( { But certainly not } 21...Nfxe4?? { , as after the further } 22.Qxg7+ Ke8 23.Qg6+ Kd7 24.b4 +- { the lack of stability of Black's knights would have cost him material. } ) 
22.Qxg7+  Ke8  
23.O-O-O  Kd7  { Now White would need to consider himself truly lucky to be able to force out a draw by repetition. } 
24.Qf7 $1 Qf8  
25.Qc4  Qc8  
26.Qf7 $10 { Black should also most probably accept the repetition, unless he would happen to have an idea of how to deal with the invasion of the white rook via [g7] coming right up. In the context of this exercise, I wanted to draw your attention to the existence of so-called one-sided forced lines. As the name suggests, those are variations within which only one of the players has to keep on finding the only moves all the time in order to survive. This has consequences for the construction of the variation tree, as we as the defending side are going to be presented with a series of choices between two moves one of them usually being refutable of the spot, while the vast majority of calculation is going to pertain to the whole spectrum of candidate moves our opponents are allowed to play as of every single turn. This, on the other hand, shall influence the length of the calculated lines, with our moves needed to be calculated far ahead as our life depends on their viability, while the resources of the opponent being treated in a more concise fashion, just as to make sure that none of them critically endangers our position. If drawn on paper, such a variation tree of variations is going to look more like a 'bush', which might sound funny to some, but is incidentally also a professional name of this phenomenon, which I had seen already being mentioned in the past in a couple of older Soviet/Russian chess books. Your score: You receive the first point for at least considering all of the three options of White as of move 19. You can give yourself the second one if you chose wisely between 19...Rxe4? and 19...Bxe4!. Finally, add the third point to your stack if you once again recognized all three critical alternatives of White as of move 20. }  

