Rc7  ( { White's distant e7-passer deserves a priority treatment here for sure. The above statement is nicely confirmed by the following line which starts with Black attacking the wrong white piece instead: } 32...Rc5? 33.Nd7 Rc6 34.g5 { when Black would already need to bail with } 34...f5 35.gxf6 Rd6 { but even so, the position arising after something like } 36.Rxd6 Nxd6 37.Ne5 Ba4 38.Kf1 Nf7 39.Ng4 +- { would have been utterly hopeless for him as the white king is slowly going to approach and consume that poor black fellow stuck on [b1]. } ) 
{ White is a pawn down here two pawns in return for a knight, but does not seem to be complaining about his fate too much yet. The deadly [Rd4]-[d8] is being threatened already, while Black's [b1]-knight as well as the [c2]-bishop seem to be out of play. That being said, Black could not afford to stay passive for any longer, and thus played 32...Rc7. } 
33.Rd8  ( { Another good move for White would have been } 33.Bg5 { , even if it would have allowed the black knight to escape from [b1] after the further } 33...f6 34.Rd8 Ba4 35.Bxf6 Nd2 { . Still, with material balance having been restored, White would have remained perfectly fine here as well, e.g. } 36.f3 Nc4 37.b3 Nxe5 38.bxa4 Nxf3+ 39.Kf2 Kf7 40.Rxe8 Kxe8 41.Kxf3= { , when the elimination White's [a4]-pawn would have changed nothing as regards the still drawn evaluation of this endgame. White's [e7]-pawn is simply too strong. } ) Rxe7  ( { By comparison, } 33...Ba4 { would have not allowed Black to release his king effectively as after the further } 34.g5 Rxe7 35.Ng4 Kh8 36.Nf6 Re2 { White could now start blackmailing him with } 37.Ra8! Bc6 38.Rc8 Re6 39.Rd8= { , proving that the whole situation is actually a Mexican stand-off as Black could do nothing to neutralize White's interchangeable rook-attacks. } ) 
34.Nd7  f5  { Erecting a bolthole for the king on [f7] costs Black the [h7]-pawn, but this was a price that he correctly did not hesitate to pay. } 
35.Nf6+  Kf7  
36.Nxh7  g5 $1 { I can imagine that this move came as a surprise for White during the game. By giving up yet another pawn, Black has just vacated the [g6]-square and is now ready to play ...[Kf7]-[g6] with a double-attack against the rival's light pieces. } 
37.f4 $1 { Once you focus on the reason for White's demise in the game, it becomes much easier to locate the improvement. By playing like this, White would have given his king more air to breathe, but also increased the chances of survival of his light pieces on the kingside by creating a useful outpost on [g5]. } ( { In the game, White apparently missed that after } 37.Bxg5? Re1+ 38.Kh2 f4! 39.Rd7+ Kg8 { he is not in time to evacuate his knight via [f6] with } 40.Nf6+ { . After the further } 40...Nxf6 41.Bxf6 Be4 -+ { he would be getting mated in seven moves just because his king cannot be evacuated via [g3] anymore. In particular, I believe that White must have only considered the greedy but inferior } {38...fxg4? , not comprehending from afar that his own king could possibly get into such trouble at the same time. One can argue that 38...f4! was such an invisible resource that barely anybody would have been able to predict it from afar. Maybe so, but the truth is also that it was already 36...g5! which should have raised White's eyebrows. Every single time your opponent plays something unexpected or outright strange, exercise additional caution. It might be that your rival's last move is just a mistake, any divergence from the standard should be checked with due diligence, without naturally the need to immediately become paranoid about it. } ) fxg4  ( 37...Kg6 { would have changed nothing in view of } 38.Bxg5 Rxh7 39.Rxe8 Rxh3 40.Re6+ Kg7 41.gxf5 Bxf5 42.Rd6= { , when Black would have still been very far away from releasing his entrapped knight, yet alone taking down White's [b2]-pawn on the queenside. } ) 
38.Nxg5+  Kg6  
39.Bf8 $1 { An essential, although relatively simple intermezzo. } Re1+  
40.Kf2  gxh3  
41.Nxh3  Rh1  
42.Ng5  Nf6  
43.Be7 $10 { The game can certainly carry on, although personally I do not believe that Black can effectively come closer with this pieces towards the white monarch. Your score: This time it is simple: three whole points for finding 37.f4!. }  

