[FEN "5r1k/4Nqpp/R3R3/2pP2r1/2n2pP1/P4P2/5Q2/6K1 b - - 0 46"]

{ The following position is so imbalanced that, at least at first glance, it is quite hard to comprehend what is happening out there at all. White's pieces managed to invade Black's camp, but the white king cannot really feel secure either, especially should the black knight come closer towards him. White may certainly consider pushing his passed [d5]-pawn one day, but for the moment this is disallowed as it would have left his rook on [e6] hanging. At the same time, Black might want to push ...[h7]-[h5] one day, but now this would have been met by [Ne7]-[g6]+ or even [Qf2]-[h4]. Actually, the latter is already being threatened now, so in the absence of any idea of his own, Black might want to prioritize this... But how? } Qh5 $3 { A truly remarkable resource! By playing like this, Black would have effectively prevented the annoying transfer of the white queen to [h4], while simultaneously keeping White on his toes in view of potential sacrificial ideas on [g4]. Such moves are extremely easy to miss in a practical game, as literally nobody enjoys putting their pieces en prise - I guess that years of teaching players that a material advantage suffices for a win most of the time is to blame. Even if we spot an idea like this and consider playing it, just like gravity pulls objects towards the ground, our subsconsciousness shall keep on trying to convince us that this is simply not the way to go. The only antidote to this 'primal fear' is to gain exposure to as many situations like this as possible in your own practice, this way consistently remaining oneself that material in chess is certainly not everything. } ( { Instead, in the game Bjerre went for the multi-purpose move } 46...Ne5? { , on one hand preventing [Qf2]-[h4] due to a fork from [f3], on the other however threatening to expose the white monarch after a possible strike on [g4]. In view of the above, White responded with the equally-prophylactic } 47.Kf1! { , when after the further } 47...Nxg4 48.fxg4 Rxg4 { he could have finished the opponent off with the elaborate } 49.Re5!! f3 50.Rf5 Qxe7 51.Qxf3 +- { . It might seem initially as if White had sacrificed his knight for nothing. Upon closer inspection, though, it turns out that Black would be experiencing difficulties along the back-rank, while at the same time losing his [g4]-rook. } ) ( { By comparison, } 46...Ne3? { would have exposed Black to the already thematic } 47.Qh4 { , when after the further } 47...Rxd5 48.Nxd5 Nxd5 49.Qg5 Qb7 { , just when it seemed that Black was about to obtain counterplay against the white king, White could seal the deal with the elegant } 50.Rab6! Nxb6 51.Re7 +- { , leaving the rival no other choice but to give up his queen in return for prolonging the fight for a little bit. } ) ( { Finally, the counter-attacking } 46...Rb8? { would have been to no avail either as after something like } 47.Nc6 Rb1+ 48.Re1 Rxe1+ 49.Qxe1 h5 50.Nd8! Qf5 51.Ne6 +- { . White would have manage to reduce Black's attacking potential on the kingside before anything hatches. } ) 
47.Qh2  { The most logical and challenging approach of White. By offering an exchange of queens, White would be trying to transpose into a favorable endgame. } ( { At first, } 47.Qg2 Qh4 48.Qh2 { looked even more straightforward from the point of view of ensuring the aforementioned trade, but now unexpectedly after } 48...Ne5! 49.Qxh4 Nxf3+ 50.Kf2 Nxh4 51.d6 h5 ⇆ { White would not be in a position to focus on supporting his passed [d6]-pawn completely as he would need to constantly reckon with progress being made against his own king by Black on the kingside. } ) ( { Just as a sidenote, } 47.d6?? { would have made some sense as the black queen is not touching the white [e6]-rook anymore, but fails tactically to } 47...Rxg4+! 48.fxg4 Qxg4+ 49.Kh2 Qxe6 -+ { . It's always interesting to see how strong positional ideas have the curious tendency of consistently working from the tactical standpoint. } ) g6 $3 { Simply unbelievable! White left Black no choice but to accept the exchange of queens, but how is it going to happen specifically would have still been up to him. Helping himself to the queen on [h5] right now would endanger White's [g4]-pawn after ...[g6xh5]. What cannot be underestimated either is the fact that the text move also improves the safety of Black's king, who will not have to worry about any mating ideas along the eighth rank anymore. } ( { But not } 47...Qxh2+? { , as after the further } 48.Kxh2 g6 49.Ra4! Ne5 50.Kg2 Kg7 51.Re4 ± { it would have been tough for Black to hold as he would need to deal with two white passers simultaneously. } ) 
48.Qc2  Qh3 $10 { White should most probably agree a draw by means of a threefold repetition as the attempt to snatch material with } 
49.Qc3+ $4 Ne5  
50.Rxe5  { would have backfired to } Rxg4+ $19 { Your score: You receive two points for finding 46...Qh5!!. Another two points go your way if you were able to neutralize both 47.Qh2 and 47.Qg2 successfully. Finally, the fifth point belongs to you if you understood why instead 46...Ne5? was bound to fail. }  

