{ White to play. } 
40.--   

[Event "Calculate Like a Beast: The Moranda Method"]
[White "3.1) Train Insane Or Remain The Same: Puzzle Set #1"]
[Black "Mendonca, Leon Luke vs. Pranav, V., Formentera 2023"]
[Result ""]
[FEN "r5k1/1pq2p1n/2p2P2/4R1P1/1P1p2P1/p2P2Q1/6B1/6K1 w - - 0 40"]

{ White has been enjoying the upper hand for the most part of this encounter. His advantage finally started taking concrete shape and size when he decided to go after his rival's king. In the heat of the battle, however, he unnecessarily allowed Black to create a distant passed pawn along the a-file, which looks pretty much unstoppable already. Under these circumstances, what is the only move which grants White a chance to maintain the equilibrium? } 
40.Qh2 $1 { In the absence of any effective defensive tries, White should have resorted to attacking ideas instead. Now, if Black does nothing, after [Qh2]-[h6] White is going to be landing mate on [g7] very shortly from now. Although not always bullet-proof as a thinking method within the calculation process, general considerations pertaining to the nature of the position do tend to help us prioritize certain types of candidate moves over others. This way of approaching things also happens to be one of the cornerstones of the so-called 'conceptual calculation', which I have been a huge advocate for years now. The reasoning behind it is simple - calculating specific moves and simultaneously discussing the ideas behind them gives the whole process an additional layer of understanding. From my experience I can attest that students who started calculating variations like this, instead of shooting out pure series of moves like from an AK-47, began spotting the resources of the opponent better. Also, their intuitive choice of solutions during calculation improved heavily, as they frequently seemed to know why are they considering concrete moves in the first place and how to recognize whether they are at all going in the right direction when calculating a longer line. } ( { Instead, the game saw } 40.Re8+? Rxe8 41.Qxc7 { snatching the queen, but after the further } 41...a2 42.g6 a1=Q+ 43.Kh2 Ng5 44.Qf4 Qe1 45.Qxg5 Qe5+ 46.Qxe5 Rxe5 -+ { Black emerged an exchange up and was therefore also winning. Interestingly enough, he started playing rather passively, allowing White to push his kingside pawns far ahead, eventually also scoring the full point. } ) ( { While trying to improve upon the above variation, some of my students figured out that after } 40.Bd5? cxd5 41.Re8+ Rxe8 42.Qxc7 a2 43.Qc1 { Black would not be able to promote his [a2]-pawn, at least not right away. Still, the position arising after the further } 43...Ra8 44.Qa1 Nxg5 -+ { would have remained hopeless for White, as his queen would have been unable to move, with his king not capable of handling the situation alone, especially when confronted with his black counterpart supported by an additional knight. } ) ( { Finally, } 40.g6? { would have been to no avail either, as after the accurate } 40...a2! 41.gxh7+ Kh8! 42.Re8+ Rxe8 43.Qxc7 a1=Q+ 44.Kh2 Qe1 -+ { Black would be getting his hands on the white monarch in no time based on the ...[Qe1]-[h4]+ & ...[Re8]-[e1]+ motif. } ) Qxe5 $1 { Fortunately for Black, however, the game may carry on thanks to a timely counter. For the sake of neutralizing White's mating idea as well as ensuring a safe passage for his [a3]-passer, Black should sacrifice his queen in yet another manner. } ( { Obviously, } 40...a2?? { would have not been enough for Black in view of } 41.Qh6 a1=Q+ 42.Bf1 { when he could have still given a few more checks, but with a tiny degree of care, White would have easily evaded them all: } 42...Qxf1+ 43.Kxf1 Ra1+ 44.Kg2 Ra2+ 45.Kf3 +- { , but obviously not } {45.Kh3?? , for reasons which I will let you work out on your own... } ) 
41.Qxe5  a2  
42.g6 $1 { It is not over until White says it is over, though! Black can and will certainly promote his pawn to a queen now, but the question stays the same: will he able to avoid any of White's perpetual check ideas on the kingside? } a1=Q+  
43.Kh2  Nxf6  { Eliminating White's [f6]-pawn followed by defending [f7] from afar by his queen is Black's best try for a win here. Thanks to this approach, Black would come close to ruling out any kind of checks of the opponent in the long run. } ( { By comparison, } 43...fxg6 { would not have granted the black monarch the same level of security, as after the further } 44.Qe7 Nxf6 45.Qxf6 Qa2 46.Qxg6+ Kf8 47.Qd6+ Kf7 48.Qf4+= { he would not have been in a position to avoid the perpetual check, provided that White continuously chooses the right squares for his queen to keep on giving checks from. } ) ( { The same applies to } 43...Qc1 { , as then after the further } 44.gxf7+ Kh8 45.Qe8+! Nf8 46.Qxa8 Qf4+ 47.Kh3 Qe3+ 48.Kh4 Qe1+= { it would be actually Black who would need to resort to constant checks in order to stay within the drawing zone. } ) 
44.Qxf6  Qa2  
{ Just when it seemed that White was about to face the music due to him running out of checks, he could have uncorked the completely unexpected } 
45.b5 $3 { Allowing himself to miraculously escape with a draw. } cxb5  
46.gxf7+  Qxf7  
47.Qxd4  { This would have allowed us to admire the point behind 45.b5!! in full blaze. This inconspicuous pawn-lever was supposed to deflect Black's [c6]-pawn in order to make the [Bg2]-[d5] follow-up possible. } 
{ In view of the black king now being exposed to attacks along the light squares too, after something like } Qg7  
48.Qd5+  Kh8  
49.Qxb5 $10 { Black would most probably need to eventually agree a draw on account of a direct transposition into an equal queenless endgame. Your score: You obtain the first two points for choosing 40.Qh2!. Give yourself another point if you made it all the way to 44.Qxf6 Qa2, but the remaining two ones can only be awarded if you had seen and understood 45.b5!! from afar. }  

