{ 25.Kf2 or 25.Kh1? } 
25.--   

[Event "Calculate Like a Beast: The Moranda Method"]
[White "3.1) Train Insane Or Remain The Same: Puzzle Set #1"]
[Black "Macias Pino, Diego vs. Sosa, T., Menorca 2024"]
[Result ""]
[FEN "1k1r1r2/1pp3pp/p5q1/2Qp1N2/PP6/1Np2n1P/2B3P1/R1B3K1 w - - 0 25"]

{ This ultra-sharp position as arisen out of one of the calmest versions of the Giuoco Piano available. The modern tendency, however, is for even strategic openings in nature to be analyzed so deeply by theoreticians that they sooner or later start escalating into a regular bloodbath. In any case, here White is nominally a pawn up } { (three light pieces in return for a rook accompanied by three pawns) } { , but as the situation is otherwise highly-imbalanced, we are not supposed to focus on material too much for the time being. Instead, White has a clear-cut problem to solve immediately - where to run away with his king from the knight-check? Two reasonable retreats come into consideration, both of which have their respective pros and cons though. And which one would you prefer? } 
25.Kh1 $1 { By playing like this, White put his king to the safest square available on the board h1, but at the same time allows ...[Nf3]-[e1] with a double-threat of mate on [g2] as well as a capture on [c2]. Still, under these specific circumstances, this was White's best bet. } ( { The alternative } 25.Kf2? { would have prevented the aforementioned knight-fork from [e1], but also exposed the white monarch to an attack along the f-file after } 25...Nh4! 26.g4 Rxf5+! {(( but not the hasty 26...Nxf5? in view of the intermediate 27.Bf4 +- gaining an invaluable tempo before recapturing on [f5]. Once this happens, the white king would have not been in danger anymore thanks to the temporarily closed f-file.))} 27.Bxf5 Nxf5 { , when taking on [f5] would have led to a draw by perpetual check, but other options would have not represented an improvement for White either. Sample lines: } 28.Qxc3 {(28.Bf4 Nd6 29.Kg1 Qd3 30.Be3 d4! 31.Qxd4 Ne4 32.Qa7+ Kc8 33.Re1 Qd6= and Black would have once again saved himself thanks to the queen invasion to [g3]. )} 28...Rf8!! { Absolutely essential! Returning with the knight to [d6] first would have allowed White to secure enough time to reorganize his forces, while here after something like } 29.Qf3 Rf6! 30.gxf5 Rxf5 31.Bf4 Qf7 32.Ke3 Qe7+ 33.Kd3 Qxb4 34.Bxc7+ Kxc7 35.Qxf5 Qxb3+ 36.Kd2 Qb4+= { , if White wanted to avoid a draw by perpetual check, he would need to part with his rook, which is obviously a deal-breaker for him. } ) Ne1  
26.Bg5 $1 { A lovely intermezzo! This way, White would have gained a valuable tempo to prevent mate on [g2] and create a threat of his own in the meantime. } ( { Instead, the game saw } 26.g4?? Nxc2 27.Bf4 Nxa1 28.Bxc7+ Ka8 29.Bxd8 Rxd8 30.Nxa1 d4 -+ { , which was clearly not half-as-good for White as now he was no longer able to stop Black's connected passed pawns in the center. } ) Nxc2  ( 26...Rde8 {(looked very smart at first avoiding an attack and defending the knight at the same time, but then after the classy)} 27.Rxe1! Rxe1+ 28.Kh2 Rxf5 29.Bf4! { the best Black could get out of this situation would have been a strategically-lost endgame after } 29...Qf7 30.Bxf5 Rf1 31.Bg3 Rxf5 32.Qxc3 +- { where, despite his nominal material edge, he would have been defenseless against White's forces, especially once [Nb3]-[d4] together with [b4]-[b5]-[b6] arrive. } ) 
27.Bxd8  Rxd8  
28.Rc1 $1 { The very last finesse, the point behind which is going to be revealed in a moment from now. } ( { The logical looking } 28.Rf1?! { would have made White's conversion process significantly more difficult in view of } 28...d4! 29.Nbxd4 Nxd4 30.Nxd4 Qd6! 31.Qxd6 Rxd6 32.Nc2 Rd3 ⩲ { . The knight versus two pawns kind of endgame would have become inevitable. The engine insists that White stays a little better here, but personally I am not convinced. In particular, the immediate exchange of rooks would not be advisable for White as the black king then arrives to the queenside faster than his white counterpart. Admittedly, the white knight is a decent blockader here, but is barely capable of simultaneously hitting Black's [c3]-passer. In my opinion, with good technique and a wee bit of luck, Black should be able to hold this in a sovereign manner. } ) Qxf5  
29.Qxc3  { There you go - who would have thought that the black knight will get trapped in the middle of the board! Technically speaking, it can certainly run away to [a3], but this is going to cost Black an arm and a leg once White hits [c7]. Personally, I believe that anticipating this very strike from afar was the most difficult part of the puzzle as the opportunity to attack Black along the c-file was never really on the agenda except for now. Black can certainly still wiggle, but the result has already been decided, as proven by the following sample line. } Re8  
30.Rxc2  Qf4  
31.Nc5 $1 Re3  
32.Qd2  d4  
33.Kg1 $1 Qg3  
34.Rc1 $18 { After neutralizing all of Black's tricks, White would now be ready to start turning his edge into a full point. This puzzle was extremely hard to solve since it involved a number of special intermediate moves, which I personally call 'time-stop inducing' ones 27.Bf4!, 28...Rf8!! and 28.Rc1! in the main line, to name a few. What I mean by this is that intermezzos in general do not alter the forced nature of a given variation - they just steer the pace thereof in another direction. Time-stop inducing intermediate moves are, however, different, as they make the impression of substantially slowing down the course of events happening on the board. Such situations can be particularly unpleasant for any player, as they have the capacity to perplex us into thinking that calculation may be of lesser importance in the concrete situation already, as the position appears to have stabilized, subconsciously convincing us to begin relying on general considerations more. Your score: Three points are going your way if you predicted the whole 25.Kh1! [Ne1] 26.Bg5! [Nxc2] 27.Bxd8 [Rxd8] 28.Rc1! sequence. The other two points are awarded for understanding what was wrong about 25.Kf2? instead, in particular for spotting 26...[Rxf5]+! followed by 28... Rf8!!. }  

