{ Evaluate 33.Ng6+. } 
33.--   

[Event "Calculate Like a Beast: The Moranda Method"]
[White "3.1) Train Insane Or Remain The Same: Puzzle Set #1"]
[Black "Hari, Madhavan vs. Neelotpal, D., Nashik Rapid 2024"]
[Result ""]
[FEN "2q2r1k/6pp/p2b4/3pN3/b2B4/4R2P/3Q1PP1/6K1 w - - 0 33"]

33.Ng6+ $2 { As pretty and forcing as it might look, the move 33.Ng6+ could have thrown away the win if Black had handled things in a more resolute manner. Due to its beauty and forced nature, not many players would have been able to resist the temptation to go for it after a merely short inspection. In critical moments like these however, in which achieving a win depends mostly on the ability to find the best move or even a series thereof!, we need to constantly remind ourselves of the fact that there is no proven correlation between the forced nature of a given move and its strength. In other words, the fact that a specific move looks forced does not simultaneously mean that it is also strong. Instead, it just testifies that it might be easier for us to figure out its consequences as more only moves may follow directly afterwards. } ( { Just for the record, } 33.Rg3 Qb7 34.Qh6!! { would have been objectively speaking much stronger than the text. White's queen is obviously taboo due to a beautiful double-mate, while the position arising after the more or less forced } 34...Bxe5 35.Bxe5 Rf7 36.Qd6 Qe7 37.Qxd5 { would have not been a walk in the park for Black either, even despite material equality. The reason for this would have been White's nagging pressure against [g7], about which Black could do very little without owning a dark-squared bishop himself. The following sample line illustrates nicely what kind of troubles his king would be subjected to in the long haul: } 37...h6 38.Bc3 Qd7 39.Qa8+ { Obviously, the trade of queens would have made Black's life significantly less problematic. } 39...Kh7 40.Qe4+ { White could have naturally snatched the pawn somewhere along the way too, but this little passer is so irrelevant here that it can just as well be ignored for the time being. } 40...Kg8 41.Kh2! { Securing the king to a maximum before going all in on the attack. } 41...Re7 42.Qc4+ Kh7 43.Bf6! Rf7 44.Ba1 { It never hurts to optimize the placement of the other pieces as well before taking active action. By moving the bishop even more backwards towards [a1], White would be introducing the additional option of creating a diagonal battery with his queen in front on [c3] one day. } 44...Re7 45.f4 ± { and with the white f-pawn rushing all the way ahead towards [f6], Black's situation would have only now started seriously deteriorating. } ) 
{ Despite being a pawn down here, White's forces look perfectly prepared for a possible kingside attack. The bishop occupying the long dark diagonal as well as the possibility of including his major pieces into play immediately do suggest the existence of some final solution for White. However, would 33. [Ng6]+ be the correct way for White to begin with? } hxg6  
34.Re7 $1 { The immediate tactical point behind White's 33rd move - Black's [g7]-pawn is begging for help, but only now has the threat of [Qd2]-[h6]+ become a real menace. With two elements of his position being attacked so fiercely, Black has no other choice but to pick the lesser evil, that is to prevent the more dangerous one, while minimizing the damage after the other one materializes. } Rf4 $1 { The only response allowing Black to stay alive. Compared to the alternative which happened in the game, here the rook not only interferes along the [c1]-[h6] diagonal, but also escapes from trouble on [f8], should White decide to capture on [g7] with his bishop. } ( { Instead, in the game Black collapsed almost instantly with } 34...Bf4? { , when after the further } 35.Bxg7+ Kg8 36.Qxd5+ Kh7 37.Bxf8+ Kh8 38.Qd4+ +- { he had to resign in view of incoming mate. } ) 
35.Rxg7  { This move leaves the black monarch in a state of stasis. If Black does nothing, he will eventually succumb to a deadly discovered attack. } ( { By comparison, this time } 35.Bxg7+ Kh7 { would have been far less murderous for Black, despite the need to temporarily walk into a discovery along the seventh rank. White can certainly keep trying with } 36.Bb2+ Bxe7 37.Qxf4 Qf5 38.Qd4 { , but Black should be able to hold this position with ease, e. g. } 38...Qb1+ 39.Kh2 Bd6+ 40.g3 Bxg3+! 41.fxg3 Qc2+ 42.Kg1 Qd1+ 43.Qxd1 Bxd1= { with a draw on account of the presence of opposite-colored bishops on the board. } ) Qc4 $1 { There are a couple of things the defender can do upon noticing a threat. Bringing in reinforcements or - in extreme cases - running away from danger are two strong candidate ideas, but here Black had no other choice but to attack the attacker itself. Fortunately for White, the game is far from over yet... } 
36.Bf6 $1 { I hope that you have seen this little tricky bastard of a move from afar! It turns out that the bishop cannot be taken for now, meaning that Black will need to stay on his toes while dealing with the thematic discovery. } ( { The 'safer' } 36.Bb2 { would have allowed Black to take advantage of an interference-based defense with } 36...d4! { , when after something like } 37.Rxg6 Qd5 38.g3 Bc6 39.f3! Qxf3 40.gxf4 Qh1+ 41.Kf2 Qf3+ 42.Ke1 Qh1+= { a draw by threefold repetition would have been a fair result for both sides. } ) g5 $1 { By far the most difficult move to find in this exercise. By resorting to this move, Black argues that he has not yet given up on the idea of taking down White's sniper-bishop on [f6], since now helping himself to it would have not resulted in mate on [h6]. What is more, only by playing like this could he have made sure that he is not going to lose to some unexpected coordination-based motifs afterwards. } ( { The vast majority of my students preferred } 36...Bf8? { , claiming that after the further } 37.Rc7+ Rxf6 38.Rxc4 dxc4 39.Qd4 Be7 { the sizeable material edge would have allowed Black to consolidate his forces effectively. Sadly for them, White now has } 40.Qe5! Bd8 41.Qb8 Rf8 42.Qb4 +- { , snatching Black's bishop as well as consecutively also his queenside pawns based on a nasty geometrical motif. } ) 
37.Rc7+  Rxf6  
38.Rxc4  dxc4  
39.Qxg5  Re6 $10 { As incredibly as it may sound, White would not be even able to steal the [c4]-pawn away from Black right away because of the mating threat along the back-rank. Having this in mind, the best practical solution for White would be to force out a draw by perpetual check at the very first opportunity. Your score: For every single move from among 34.Re7, 34...Rf4!, 35...Qc4!, 36.Bf6! and finally 36...g5!, you receive one point. }  

