{ In this game stemming from the Classical Slav, at a certain point of time, Black managed to crush through White's defenses on the kingside. Material may still be equal here, but various ways of executing the discovered check along the third rank definitely keep White on her toes. In the absence however of any reasonable alternative, White decided to simply keep on going as if nothing serious was really happening yet. } 
1.Nd3  Nf1+  { For a second there, 33...Ne2+ 34.Ke4 f5+ 35.gxf5 gxf5+ 36.Kxf5 Rxe3 (, aiming to take down the white [d3]-knight or the [e1]-rook should the aforementioned piece run away somewhere, looked scary, but fortunately for White, here she would have had) 37.Reb1! b6 38.Nc5= at her disposal, when White's queenside counter-attack would not have given Black the time to organize any mating threats on the kingside. } 
2.Ke4  Nd2+  
3.Ke5  Nf3+  { Black snatches a rook with this knight-fork, but as in life, in chess there is usually no free lunch. Now the white king invades and starts teaming up with his other pieces in order to create dangerous counterplay against the black monarch. } 
4.Kd6  Nxe1  
5.Nc5  Ka8 $1 { Black should have resorted to the anticipatory move 37...Ka8! } { Alternatively, 37...Rxe3? would have been met by 38.Rxb7+ Ka8 39.Rb3! The most precise response. Apart from preparing [Nc5]-[a6] followed by [Rb3]-[b8]#, this move also makes sure that Black will not be able to sacrifice her rook for our knight by means of ...[Re3xc3]xc7, should we decide in favor of the [Na6]-[c7]+ mating threat instead. 39...Rh8 40.Na6 Rc8 41.Nc7+ Rxc7 42.Kxc7 Re7+ 43.Kc8 Re8+ 44.Kc7= with a most probable draw by threefold repetition. } { The logical 37...Rh8? was played in the game, intending to include the rook into the defense along the eighth rank. However, it turns out to be the mistake. Play continued 38.Rxb7+ Ka8 (( But not 38...Kc8?? as after the further 39.Kc6! Rc2 40.Rb3! Black would not be able to dismantle White's mating net without sustaining heavy material losses, e.g. 40...Rd8 41.Na6 Rxc3+ 42.Rxc3 Nf3 43.d6 +- )) 39.Rb1! An essential gain of tempo on the black knight. This way White gains a crucial amount of additional time required to give [Nc5]-[a6] an additional boost. 39...Rd8+ and it's quite unbelievable that Black cannot capitalize on such a huge material edge, but sample lines are pretty convincing: 40.Ke7 Rc8 (40...Rhh8 41.Na6! Rhe8+ 42.Kxf7 Rf8+ 43.Kg7 Rg8+ 44.Kf7 Rgf8+= and after a couple more checks, both players decided to call it a day. ) ( Funnily enough, the greedy 40...Rxd5 would have been to no avail either in view of 41.Na6 Re5+ 42.Kd7 Rd2+ 43.Kc6 Re6+ 44.Kc7 Re8 and now the calm 45.Kc6= would have renewed the [Na6]-[c7]# threat, basically forcing Black to once again repeat moves. ) 41.Na6 f5 42.d6 Rh7+ A fancy inclusion of the other rook into play as well, but now after the most forcing 43.Ke6 Nd3 44.d7 Rc6+ 45.Kd5 Rxd7+ 46.Kxc6 Ne5+ 47.Kc5 when Black should probably not hesitate before agreeing a draw based on the ...[Nd3]-[e5]-[d3]+ mechanism.As I have never spoken to Black in person, I can only speculate about the root cause of her mistake as of move 37. If I ever to guess however, I would say that as of move 33 she recognized the sequence of tactical motifs, leading to her winning a rook. What she was clearly less than prepared for though was the sudden surge in activity of the white forces. Maybe she did not anticipate it at all, or maybe she did but underestimated its strength - we cannot know this for sure. What is however certain is that, especially in sharp positions, we cannot afford to resort to tactical pattern recognition without calculating the consequences thereof at the same time. In other words, even if you manage to spot a typical tactical motif and all signs on the sky seem to indicate that it works, remember to make sure that it is indeed viable by simultaneously verifying it based on calculation. } 
6.Rxb7  Nd3 $1 
7.Kc7 $1 Rb2 $1 { White is not in time to land mate. } 
{ Admittedly, after the further } 
8.Rxb2  Nxb2  
9.d6  Nc4  
10.d7  { White's [d7]-pawn would have been promoted, but the knight endgame to follow after } Rh8  
11.d8=Q+  Rxd8  
12.Kxd8  f5  { Would have certainly been winning for Black based on the strength of her passed g-pawn. If I were to guess, the main difficulty of this exercise was all about making use of the ...Ne1-d3 motif not just for the purpose of deflecting the strong white knight from [c5], but rather to dynamically support simplification ideas via ...Rh2-b2. Your score: You receive three points for recognizing the need to deviate from the main line with 37...Ka8!. The two remaining points are going your way for finding the consecutive 38...Nd3! and 39...Rb2!, respectively. }  

