{ Throughout the game, White has been very pushy on the kingside, which was already reflected by her choice of variation against Black's KID the Averbakh line with 5.Be2 [O-O] 6.h4!?. Also in this position, despite the opponent's apparent control over the e-file as well as the central dark-squares, she has been sparing no efforts to destabilize the position of the black king, here specifically by undermining the [g6]-pawn. Taking into consideration that White may want to carry on with the undermining [f3]-[f4] in the near future as well, Black's most instinctive reaction would be 25...g5, but would this move have in fact helped Black solve all of her problems? } Qd8 $3 { Nope, there was a far superior solution available for Black instead. This mysterious move carries a strong anticipatory counter with it, should White naturally dare to capture on [g6] right away. } ( { In the game, Black collapsed without putting up any resistance after } 25...Rxe1? { . The only alternative to the text move which I see would be therefore the thematic } {25...g5? , attempting to prevent or at least slow down [f3]-[f4] for as long as possible. In reality, however, after the further 26.f4 Rxe1+ 27.Rxe1 Rxe1+ 28.Bxe1 , the [f4]-pawn would have remained taboo on account of the murderous [Qc2]-[g2]+, while the prolonged presence of the said pawn on the board would have allowed the white queen to infiltrate the e-file after something like 28...Qd8 29.Qe2 +- when the [Qe2]-[e6]+ threat would have proven to be decisive. } ) 
26.f4  ( 26.fxg6? f5! { would have illustrated the idea behind 25...Qd8!! perfectly. All of a sudden, there would not be much she could do to prevent ...[Qd8]-[h4] from happening, with serious mating threats to start materializing on the kingside. She could certainly try to minimize the damage by means of } 27.Bc3 { , but after something like } 27...Bxc3 28.gxh7+ Kh8 29.Qxc3 Qg5 -+ {(the exposed position of the white king in conjunction with the strategic domination of Black over the center would have eventually decided the outcome of the game. 25...Qd8!! was hard to find for more than one reason. First of all, trying to find an antidote to White's attack on [g6] certainly absorbed most of Savina's attention. Secondly, such a long maneuver as ...[Qb6]-[d8]-[h4] takes plenty of time three tempi together with ...[f6]-[f5], to be precise, during which lots of bad things can potentially happen to us. Finally, a certain level of strategic prowess also needed to be demonstrated by Black here who needed to be much more sensitive to the fact that - with her queen being completely out of play on the queenside - she is not going to be in a position to defend her kingside effectively.)} ) Rxe1+  
27.Rxe1  Rxe1+  
28.Bxe1  Qf8 $1 { This move also had to be seen from afar, as otherwise White would have nonetheless retained an advantage based on the already discussed invasion ideas along the e-file. In this case however, White would have never been in time for this because of the murderous counterplay to come via [f4] or along the h-file. } 
29.Qh2  ( 29.fxg6 Qxh6 30.gxh7+ Kh8= ∞ { would have highlighted yet another scenario in which White's temporary material edge would have not meant so much after all compared to the size of the backlash coming her way after the further ...[Qh6xf4]+ and ...[Nd7]-[e5]. } ) g5 $1 { Instead of passively defending the hanging [a5]-pawn, it would have been the wisest to secure an excellent outpost for the knight on [e5] right away. } 
30.Bxa5  Be3  
31.fxg5  Bxg5  
32.Bc3  Qe7  
33.Qe2  Ne5  { Despite being a pawn down, Black would not have been in danger anymore. The reason for this would not be the ability to recapture a pawn on [h6], but rather the fact that White should better not tolerate Black's omnipotent knight on [e5] for too long. Then again, chopping it with her dark-squared would have led almost instantaneously to a draw because of the resulting transposition into an endgame featuring opposite-colored bishops. Your score: Three points belong to you for spotting 25...Qd8!! together with the idea behind it. Add two more points to your collection for following up with 28...Qf8!, while at the same time understanding why Black would be out of danger afterwards. }  

