{ This highly-imbalanced position has arisen out of a rather timid version of the London System. This sounds like a pleonasm, I know... Anyway, Black has just managed to infiltrate the opponent's camp with his queen and intends to inflict even more damage by taking down the [g2]-pawn. At the same time however, it is more or less self-evident that White had prepared for this in advance by building up the infrastructure of a powerful discovered check along the [a2]-[g8] diagonal. Under these circumstances, what should Black do about this in your opinion? } a5 $3 { This could have been one piece of a nasty surprise for White indeed! Instead of going after White's kingside or evading a dangerous discovery in advance, Black's best plan was to rip the white queenside open and this way expose the rival's king. If you did not see this idea during the solving process, you really have nothing to worry about from the point of view of your calculation technique. There are some candidate moves which exceed the boundaries of evaluation done by any reasonable player orientated around the general needs of the position, with their viability based solely on such an approach working tactically thanks to some specific features of the position. In other words - in extreme cases, even the most thorough assessment of the position might not necessarily steer you towards the best candidate move available in the given position. True, such resources can be explained rather confidently with the benefit of hindsight or once somebody points them out to you, but there is no way someone would be able to figure them out at the board, especially when having have to ignore more straightforward plans available on the board upfront. } ( { Instead, in the game Black went for the apparently active } 20...Rac8? { , only to succumb to } 21.Nd6 Kh8 22.Rd2! Qe3 23.Re1 Qb6 24.Nxc8 Rxc8 25.Qe6 Rf8 26.Nxg5 +- { when he could have already surrendered with a clear conscience as the [g5]-knight is taboo here in view of corridor mating ideas along the h-file. } ) ( 20...Qxg2? { looked tempting at first as a move which attacks two pieces of White at the same time, but after the further } 21.Ncxe5! { it quickly turns out that the [h1]-rook would have been taboo on account of the [Rd5]-[d1]+ discovery. Now the engine claims that the best Black would have had here would be the obviously hopeless } 21...Rf7 22.Nxf7 Qxh1+ 23.Rd1 Qh5 24.Nxh6+ Kh7 25.Nf7 +- { . } ) ( { Finally, } 20...Kh8? { would have failed similarly to the game continuation. After something like } 21.Rd2! Qe4 22.Nd6 Qe3 23.Re1 Qb6 24.Nxg5! Nd8 25.Nf3 Nc6 26.Nh4 Ne7 27.Qe6 +- { Black would have not been able to avoid heavy material losses on the kingside. } ) 
21.bxa5  ( { By comparison, } 21.Nxa5?! { would have allowed Black to consolidate provisionally with } 21...Kh8 { when White would have no longer been in a position to continue his kingside onslaught with the standard [Nf3xg5] because of the double attack from [e2]. The machine suggests that even after the most accurate } 22.Rd2! Nxa5 23.bxa5 Qe3 24.Re1 Qc5 25.Nxg5! Qxa5 26.Ne6 Rfc8 ↑ { he would still need to consider himself lucky if he managed to maintain the equilibrium here due to the nagging pressure exerted by Black against the queenside position of his monarch. } ) ( 21.Rd2? { , on the other hand, would have been plain wrong however due to } 21...a4! 22.Rxe2 axb3 23.Kb1 e4 -+ { , when White's queenside structure would be abruptly falling apart. } ) b5 $3 { A true bolt from the blue sky! The [b5]-pawn can now be taken in three different ways, each and every single one of them suffering from serious deficiencies though. } 
22.axb6  { Paradoxically, the move which exposes White to immediate counterplay along the a-file would have simultaneously been his most promising choice. } ( 22.Rxb5?! Kh8 { would have been quite similar in a certain way to consequences of 21.Nxa5?! analyzed previously, as now White would need to go to great lengths in order to minimize the damage. Sample line: } 23.Re1! Qf2 24.Qc2 Qxg3 25.Re3! Qf4! 26.Qe2 Rac8 27.g3! Qg4 28.Nfd2 Qxe2 29.Rxe2 f4 ⩱ { , when Black's connected kingside pawns would have surely looked stronger than the separated white ones on the queenside. } ) ( { However, } 22.Qxb5? { would have already been a mistake of a more severe gravity, as after the unexpected } 22...Nxa5! 23.Nxa5 Qxg2 { White would have been struggling to defend against consecutive material losses. Despite the fact that White would still be able to keep the additional piece after a display of impossible coordination with } 24.Qc4 Kh8 25.Rf1 Rac8 26.Rd2 Qh3 27.Ng1 { , after the simple } 27...Rxc4 28.Nxh3 Rxc3+ 29.Rc2 Rxg3 30.Ng1 e4 -+ { the rolling black kingside pawns could not be stopped anymore. } ) Qxa2  
23.Qxa2  Rxa2  
24.Nfd2  e4  
25.Rc5  Nb8  
26.Kb1  Ra4 $132 { The machine claims that the position is just a tiny bit more pleasant for White, but personally I would not be so sure of that during an over-the-board game. I simply do not see White's [b6]-pawn promoting in the near future here, while his pieces are still trying to establish some optimal coordination. Your score: You receive three whole points for seeing 20...a5!! as well as the remaining two if you decided to follow-up with 21...b5!! as well. }  

