{ Black has been subjected to immense pressure almost throughout the whole encounter. At a certain point of time, things became so unbearable for him already that he decided to gamble with an exchange-sacrifice, just to get any play of his own. In the game, it paid off handsomely, as White chickened out with 28.Qe3?. In lieu of that move, however, White had two superior alternatives available - 28.Rh2 } { (claiming the h-file) } { and 28.Rd6 } { (sticking to the d-file for a change) } { . Which of the aforementioned resources do you believe to be better, especially from the standpoint of Black's incoming attacking initiative along the dark squares on the queenside? } 
28.Rd6 $1 { The basic principle of calculation when defending is that our candidate moves as well as the ideas they are supposed to pursue depend heavily on the threats the opponent is generating against us on the board. This, on the other hand, also means that when comparing one candidate move of ours with another, we should first and foremost compare how efficiently do they fare against the threats in discussion. } ( { Instead, } 28.Rh2?! { would have exposed the white king to some nasty attacking ideas after } 28...Nc3+ 29.Kb2 Qa5 { , when } 30.a4 { would have jeopardized him to } 30...Qb4 { , followed by a knight-capture on [a4], while } {30.a3 would have been met by 30...Nb5! . After the further 31.a4 □ Bc3+ 32.Kc1 □ c4 ⇆ (White would have never been in a position to enjoy his extra exchange in view of the raging attack of his opponent on the queenside. What is even worse for him, a runaway with his king towards the kingside does not appear to be on the agenda either due to the ...[Nb5]-[d4]+ forking idea once the white monarch tries to evacuate via [e2].)} ) Nc3+  
29.Kb2  Nb5 $1 { It turns out that Black's 'standard' approach seen in the previous note does not seem to work anymore, so a change of plans would have been required here. Now, ...[Be1]-[c3]+ is the threat, so White needs to act accordingly. } ( { This time, } 29...Qa5? 30.a4 Qb4 { would have not been as effective as it was after 28.Rh2?!, as now White could respond with the elegant } 31.Bc6 +- { , both defending the [a4]-pawn and threatening mate under use of the [d7]-square, should Black decide to help himself to the sacrificed bishop. } ) 
30.Rd3  { The only reasonable response from White taking into consideration the idea behind Black's last move. Still, this would have invited } c4 $1 { leading us straight into a situation after } 
31.bxc4  Qxc4  { in which the pawn-shield in front of the white monarch would have been severely compromised. Fortunately for White, here he would have had the stunning } 
32.Bh7 $3 { The point behind this move is both peculiar and understandable at the same time. Understandable, since now all of a sudden the murderous [Qf3xb7]+ would have been threatened. Peculiar, as we only begin to appreciate this idea once we start comprehending that the said bishop on [e4] would be hanging the moment White would be forced to eliminate any of the black pieces arriving to [c3] in the near future. } ( { For the purpose of preventing the unpleasant check from b4, White could have also researched the natural-looking } 32.a3?! { . This move fails to do the trick however as after } 32...Nc3 33.Rxc3 Bxc3+ 34.Qxc3 Qxe4 { the best he could get would be an equal queen endgame after something like } 35.Qc7+ Ke8 36.Qb8+ Kd7 37.Qxa7 Qxf4 38.Qxb7+ Kd8= ) Ke8 $1 { The best practical try for Black would be to use anticipation - taking on [b7] without a check would have left the white king in great danger. } ( { It is now being revealed that White did not need to care about the b4-check, as now after } 32...Qb4+? 33.Rb3 Qd4+ 34.c3 Qd2+ 35.Bc2 +- { White would have avoided all of the checks, thus also conclusively consolidating his position. } ) 
33.Qe3  { The first step towards fixing his position - White needs to gradually rule out all the checks. } ( { Interestingly enough, on this occasion resorting to } 33.Rb3?! { would have failed to achieve the desired goal due to } 33...Qd4+ 34.c3 Nxc3 35.Rxc3 g6= { . White is unable to benefit from his additional bishop because of the inability to untangle from the pin on [c3], while at the same time securing his monarch against further checks. } ) Bb4  
34.Rb3  g6  
35.f5  Na3  
36.Rxa3  Bxa3+  
37.Kxa3  gxf5  
38.gxf5  Qxc2  
39.Qd4  { White should be capable of releasing his stranded light-squared bishop eventually, although the process as such is going to be nothing less than pain-staking. The following sample line demonstrates some of the difficulties White might be facing along the way towards victory: } Qc1+  
