51.Kc3  Qe1+  
52.Kc4 $18 { Black might have captured yet another pawn in the process, but with White's [a3]-pawn far away from being traded off, I can imagine him being able to convert this position into a full point at the end of the day. Before I let you move on and check your score, I wanted to draw your attention to a certain rule which needs to be known and applied by anybody who wants to learn how to calculate chess variations well. In this context, I wish to mention a phenomenon which I have observed among many of my students which I personally call the 'Construction worker mentality'. What I namely quite often hear about from my even non-chess playing friends is that the renovation crew they hired to refurbish their house had messed up the work as they did not care about it at all, at least not as much as they would if it was their own home that they would be trying to improve. And in case you were wondering, I hereby would like to explain how this applies to chess calculation: you are supposed to pay as much attention to the candidate moves of the opponent as you do to the candidate moves of your own. Way too often do we namely religiously analyze our own ideas during the game, while at the same time not trying hard enough to find the most testing response to them on behalf of the opponent, or even outright assuming that if a counter-solution like this exists, they are surely not going to find it. For the sake of your own results, please do not do this. Be thorough and objective in your pursuit of the truth during calculation and you will barely ever experience the unpleasant sensation of cold sweat running down your back upon facing a surprise answer from your rival. Your score: You obtain one point for choosing 28.Rd6! as well as seeing 31.Bc6!!, respectively. The remaining three points belong to you only if you have spotted 32.Bh7!! in the main line too. }  

