{ Black was close to dismantling his opponent completely when emerging out of our 'favorite' opening } { (yes, you guessed it, it was yet another London System!) } { . Regrettably for him, in the meantime White managed to regroup his major pieces towards the kingside, and before Black even noticed it, he was the one who was getting bullied on that part of the board. White is admittedly a pawn down for the moment, but the [Rg6xh6] threat forces his rival to treat things seriously here. So now comes the question - would 40...Rxc3, aiming to damage the coordination between the white rooks, now represent a viable option for Black? } Rxc3 $1 { In view of what happened in the game after the alternative 40...Kh7?, this move simply had to be played, even if opportunistically, so that we know that we are at least suffering in return for some material, as opposed to the far more toothless game continuation. } ( { Instead, the game saw } 40...Kh7? 41.Qe5 Rcc7 42.Rxe6 axb4 43.cxb4 +- { when, despite the material equality, Black's position was slowly becoming critical already. What happened next was that White invaded the opponent's camp along the eighth rank and lured his king closer towards certain death in close proximity of the center. } ) 
41.Rxh6  ( { Alternatively, } 41.Rxg7+ Rxg7 42.Rxc3 axb4 43.Qb8+ Kh7 44.Rc8 { could have also been tested by White as an attacking attempt, but Black would have still been in a position to defend resolutely. For example } 44...Qd1+ 45.Kh2 Qxd4 46.Rh8+ Kg6 47.Qe8+ Kg5 48.Qxe6 Qf4+ 49.Kg1 Qc1+= { with a draw by threefold repetition. The illustrated drawing mechanism needs to be remembered, as it is going to constitute the cornerstone of Black's saving operation. } ) Qd1+  
42.Kh2  Rxg3  { So far everything has been pretty much straightforward, but now an unexpected turn of events takes place. } 
43.Rxe6 $1 { The key position of the whole exercise. In lieu of simply recapturing the rook, White could have helped himself to the [e6]-pawn. Said pawn is, however, merely a foot-soldier - what White would be threatening out here in reality is the [Qf4]-[b8]-[e8] maneuver, establishing a deadly mating threat with [Qe8]-[g6]+ and [Re6]-[e8]# immediately afterwards. } ( { The vast majority of my students only considered the logical } 43.Qxg3 { here, but then after something like } 43...axb4 44.Qb8+ Kf7 45.Rh8 Qxd4 { , after exhausting some more tricky possibilities, they would be universally reaching a dead end in their calculations, e.g. } 46.f4 Kf6 47.Rf8+ Ke7 48.Rg8 Kf6= { . } ) Rc3 $1 { The only resource allowing Black to save the game. However, the point behind it shall only be revealed a couple of moves later. } ( { The greedy } 43...Rxg2+? { would have not increased Black's chances of survival. After something like } 44.Kxg2 Qh5 45.bxa5 Rb7 46.Rxa6 Rb3 47.h4 +- { White would be winning for yet another reason: material superiority in a technical endgame. } ) ( { The above applies similarly to } 43...axb4? { , with the caveat that here White would have started out with bunkering up his king after } 44.fxg3! { , only to carry on hunting down the black monarch creatively with } 44...Qh5 45.Qb8+ Kh7 46.Re8 Qf7 47.Rh8+ Kg6 48.h4! Qe6 49.Qxb4 +- { when material might once again be equal, but the difference in safety levels of both of the kings would have proven decisive. } ) 
44.Qb8+  ( 44.Re8+ Kf7 45.Qe5 Rc6 46.Rh8! { with a queen invasion looming from [e8] looked scary for a moment, but luckily for Black, after the further } 46...Re7 47.Qxf5+ Rf6 48.Qxd5+ Ree6 49.Qd7+ Re7 50.Qd5+= { White would have had nothing more than a draw by perpetual check available. } ) Kh7  
45.Qe8  { Just when it seemed that White was about to conclusively seal his mating net, Black could uncork a straight up unbelievable move. } Rc8 $3 { An extremely difficult move to find, especially if you take into consideration that another rook of Black was already hanging out there on [d7]. The point behind 45...Rc8!! is to increase control over the eighth rank should White decide to go for mate with [Qe8]-[g6]+ immediately, or at least deflect the white queen away from [c8], this way gaining enough time for our 'standard' defensive mechanism to materialize again. } ( { But not the comparable } 45...Rd8? { , as then after something like } 46.Qxd8 Qxd4 47.Qg5 Rc8 48.Qxf5+ Kh8 49.Re5 Qc4 50.bxa5 +- {(Black's situation would have once again become hopeless. Sadly for Black, his only chance for counterplay pushing the [d5]-pawn would have allowed a murderous counter-attack with [Re5]-[e4]-[h4]+.)} ) 
{ Sample lines after 45...Rc8!! confirm that Black would have been perfectly fine here no matter what: } 
46.Qxd7  ( 46.Qxc8 Qxd4 47.Qe8 Qf4+ 48.Kg1 { and after the most precise } 48...Rd8= { White's attack would have been nullified. Other moves are also possible, but this one is particularly elegant as it would have simultaneously deprived White of the interference-based defense with [Re6]-[e1] in case of any random back-rank checks. } ) Rf8 $1 { The final accuracy! Black could not have allowed White to rebuild his mating infrastructure with [Qd7]-[f7]. } 
47.Qd6  Qxd4  
48.bxa5  { Black could force a transposition into a slightly shady, although essentially drawn queen endgame after } Rf6  
49.Rxf6  gxf6  
50.Qxa6  Qxf2  
51.Qb7+  Kg6  
52.Qxd5  Qf4+  
53.g3  Qa4 $10 { White could still keep on trying, but as long as Black keeps an eye on the white [a5]-pawn and remembers about potential perpetual check motifs, nothing bad should happen to him here really. Your score: You receive the first point for figuring out Black's basic defensive mechanism after 40...Rxc3!. The second point goes your way if you managed to determine that 42.Rxe6! was also a candidate move worth looking at. Finally, the remaining three points belong to you for locating 45...Rc8!!, obviously together with the idea standing behind it. }  

