36.Qf2  
{ White managed to collect a pawn thanks to some exquisite dynamic play in a Sveshnikov Sicilian and was already well on the right path towards converting it into a full point. However, taking into consideration the rapid time control as well Black's attempts to try and stir up things, she still needed to demonstrate a decent level of accuracy in order to achieve the desired goal. } { White's first move makes perfect sense - offering a trade of queens and taking away ...[Re8]-[e2] from the rival. } { But surely not 36.d6?? , because of 36...Re2 37.Qd5 Qf2+ 38.Kh2 Qh4+ 39.Kg1 Qf2+= with an immediate draw. } Qg4  { In situations like these, it is usually a good idea for the defender to keep the stronger side constantly absorbed with some tiny issues here and there. } ( 36...Qc3 { would have changed nothing, with the most elegant way to finish Black off being } 37.Ne7! Qb4 38.Re1! Qxb5 39.Re6 +- { , when Black would have not been able to do much against ideas like [Re6xh6]+ in the long run. } ) 
37.Qf3  { Yet another good consolidating move. Now White enjoys a short pause from checks and can therefore start thinking about progressing with her [d5]-pawn. } ( 37.Rd4 Qg5 38.c4 Qc1+ 39.Kh2 Re1 40.Re4 +- { would have been winning all the same, with White's last move being a particularly pretty tactical simplification measure for sure. } ) Qc4  
38.d6  { Passed pawns should be pushed - there was no reason to refrain from sticking to this principle here either. } ( { Even } 38.Qd3?! Qc5+ 39.Kh1 Bc8 40.Rf1 Qd6 41.c4 +- { would still have been winning for White, although admittedly her job would have gotten far more complicated afterwards without the possibility to push the [d5]-passer easily anymore. Still, when calculating lines for the purpose of ensuring a smooth conversion of an advantage, it is certainly a great idea to have some alternative winning plans up one's sleeve. And conversely, when embarking on a certain forced line, it always makes sense to have some 'safety exits' along the way, that is checkpoints allowing you to steer the game towards a draw in case the position ceases to be as attractive as it was before. } ) Qc5+  
39.Kh1 $2 { This natural-looking retreat turns out to be the bad apple. By playing like that, White would be ruling out any checks for the time being, but also falling victim to a quite invisible counter in the center. } ( { In case you wondered, the optically-similar } 39.Kh2? { would have represented no improvement either due to } 39...Qxb5 40.d7 Rd8! { with a cunning situation arising on the board. The pin along the [f3]-[a8] diagonal is not absolute, in the sense that after } 41.Nxd8 Bxf3 {(, instead of recapturing on [f3] and even possibly losing as a result!, she could have continued with)} 42.Nf7+ Kh7 43.Rd2!? { when the promotion to a queen on [d8] could no longer be prevented. Still, even here Black could have still minimized the damage thanks to } 43...Qh5+ 44.Kg1 Bxg2! { , when after the more or less forced } 45.d8=Q Qh1+ 46.Kf2 Qf1+ 47.Ke3 Qf3+ 48.Kd4 Qe4+ 49.Kc3 Qc6+! 50.Kb2 Qb5+ 51.Kc1 Qf1+ 52.Rd1 Qxf7 ⇆ { Black would have been out of danger as White cannot take down the opponent's [b6]-pawn without jeopardizing his king to yet another barrage of checks. } ) ( { As anti-climatic as it might appear to be, White could have just parried the threat against her king with } 39.Qf2 Qh5 { , followed by a useful centralization of her queen to } 40.Qd4 { . Black would have had no other choice but to accept defeat at the end of the day, by heading straight into a lost endgame after } 40...Qxb5 41.d7 Bxc6 42.dxe8=Q+ Bxe8 43.Re1 +- { . } ) Re4 $3 { Sometimes it is truly darkest under the candlestick! Black would this way be creating a corridor-mate threat with ...[Re4]-[h4]+. The rook, however, simultaneously remains taboo on account of a nasty geometrical motif with ...[Qc5]-[h5xd1]xd6, collecting not only the [d1]-rook, but also White's biggest asset on [d6] in one go. This move was extremely difficult to find, as nobody likes to actively put their pieces en prise, especially since the rook was 'by default' designed to guard the eighth rank here. } ( { Instead, the game saw } 39...Qxb5? 40.d7 { when } 40...Bxc6 { was just hopeless. However, it is interesting to see what would have happened if Black tested } {40...Rd8 , which is a motif already known from one of the previous notes: 41.Nxd8 Bxf3 42.Nf7+ Kh7 43.gxf3 Qe2 , and now the only move allowing White to convert this position into a full point would have been 44.Rd3! , since the white king would eventually be able to find salvage from checks coming from the black queen deep behind enemy lines. For example: 44...Qf1+ 45.Kh2 Qe2+ 46.Kg3 Qe1+ 47.Kf4 Qh4+ 48.Ke3 Qe1+ 49.Kd4 Qb4+ 50.Kd5 Qb5+ 51.Ke6 Qc4+ 52.Rd5 Qc6+ 53.Rd6 Qc4+ 54.Ke7 Qe2+ 55.Kf8 +- . } ) 
40.g4  { White's most testing approach - taking away [h5] from the black queen and [h4] from the black rook. } ( 40.Qxe4 Qh5+ 41.Kg1 Qxd1+ 42.Kf2 Qxd6= { would have perfectly illustrated the domino-effect White would be subjected to after helping herself to Black's [e4]-rook. } ) ( { Instead, trying to cover the h-file would have not been that more effective either in view of the threefold repetition blackmail by Black with } 40.Qh3 Re3! 41.Qg4 Re4! 42.Qh3 Re3= { , when White should have most probably once again been content with a draw. } ) Bxc6  
41.bxc6  Qxc6  
42.Qg2  
{ Just when it seemed White would have been able to eliminate any checks before pushing her [d6]-pawn all the way to promotion-square, Black could uncork the unexpected } Qe8 $1 
43.d7  Re1+  
44.Rxe1  Qxe1+  
45.Kh2  Qh4+  
46.Kg1  Qe1+ $10 { A draw by perpetual check would not have been avoidable anymore. Your score: Three points for finding 39...Re4!!. One point more for neutralizing each of the white responses 40.g4 and 40.Qh3, respectively. }  

