28.--   

[Event "Calculate Like a Beast: The Moranda Method"]
[White "3.3) Train Insane Or Remain The Same: Puzzle Set #3"]
[Black "Trybus, Daniel vs. Dobrovolsky, L, Bohumin 2020"]
[Result ""]
[FEN "3q1r1k/1r3ppp/2p1Pn2/8/p7/3B3P/P1P1Q1P1/B4R1K w - - 0 28"]

{ White has just sacrificed one of his rooks for the black bishop on [f6], this way obtaining an archetypical attacking position from the Open Sicilian. Despite his material inferiority, all of his pieces seem perfectly prepared for a final, devastating kingside attack. The only dilemma apparently left to be decided upon are the specific means utilized for the purpose of hurting the black king Rf1xf6, [Ba1xf6], [e6]-[e7], together with the timing/order in which they are supposed to happen. In the game, my student simply started blasting, but it turns out that the whole situation demanded a much subtler approach... } 
28.Ba6 $3 { This is surely one of the most difficult moves to be found in the entire course. There are a couple of reasons for this. First of all, a vast majority of players would have fallen victim to the so-called 'Einstellung effect', manifesting itself in this case by the fact that launching an attack on the kingside looked so attractive that barely anybody would have considered any alternative plan instead, especially as the only winning idea involves moving one of the prime attacking pieces of White all the way in the exactly opposite direction. Secondly, and most importantly however, in my opinion the text move could have only been found by means of deep retrograde thinking, that is testing the most tempting attacking options, recognizing that they do not work, figuring out their flaws, and finally improving upon them by removing the obstacle hindering their efficacy in the first place. } ( { In the game, White went for } 28.Rxf6?? gxf6 29.e7 { , only to get himself countered by } 29...Rb1+ 30.Kh2 Qd6+ { . After the most stubborn } 31.g3 { Black would be able to respond with the dynamic } 31...Rg8 { , disallowing the promotion to a queen on [e8] in view of imminent mate on [g3]. In view of the above, White would now need to resort to } 32.Bxf6+ Qxf6 33.e8=Q Rxe8 34.Qxe8+ Kg7 -+ { , but due to the missing exchange as well as the exposure of his monarch, he would have been pretty much busted. The first conclusion after the analysis of this line is clear: was it not for the intermediate 29...[Rb1]+, White would have been winning on the spot. } ) ( { Instead, } 28.e7? Rxe7 29.Bxf6 gxf6 30.Qh5 { would have been to no avail either, this time however because of the interference-based defense after } 30...f5! 31.Qxf5 f6 ∓ { with the thematic mating threat on [h7] being confidently averted. Another important remark comes to mind here: the black rook standing along the seventh rank saved Black's skin for the second time already. } ) ( { Finally, } 28.Bxf6? gxf6 29.Bxh7! { might not be as terrible for White as the previously checked alternatives, but after the simple } 29...fxe6 30.Bd3 Qd6 ⩱ {(White's attack would be running on fumes already since the opponent's king could always hide on [g8] and shield himself with a rook on [g7] in case of need. So what do the above lines have in common? They all featured attempts of White which would backfire due to the essential role of the black [b7]-rook, notwithstanding whether it was all about some counterplay along the b-file, or maybe defensive ideas along the seventh rank instead. What it might mean at the end of the day is that this very piece constitutes the mastermind of Black's play in this position. And if there is one thing that I have learned from watching Hollywood movies about aliens pardon the stretched logical segue here! is that there is no winning against them without taking down their command center first. If you begin reasoning like this, you will almost immediately understand that before commencing any active action on the kingside, White was supposed to disturb the coordination of that very rook first...)} ) 
{ Now after the text move staying along the b-file does not come into consideration on account of the [e6]-[e7] fork, while } Ra7  { would have only now made the following sequence work. } 
29.Rxf6 $1 gxf6  
30.e7 $1 { Soon a technically winning endgame will arise on the board. } Qxe7  
31.Qxe7  Rxe7  
32.Bxf6+  Kg8  
33.Bxe7  Re8  
34.Bb4 $18 { Apart from all the discourse regarding this specific example, another interesting tendency manifested itself in the thinking process of many of my students during the solving process. Upon determining that an interesting idea fails to do the trick in the given position, human players tend to throw the said resource out of the window and never come back to it again. In reality, however, it is better to keep it at the back of one's head over the course of the next couple of moves as it might happen that under changed circumstances, the mentioned idea is going to start working again a couple of moves later. Your score: You receive three points for discovering 28.Ba6!! and two more for being able to logically explain the merits behind it. }  

