{ A scenario familiar from various types of Open Sicilians has arisen on the board. White has sacrificed a pawn in the process, and is now about to begin capitalizing on his investment. There are at least a couple of ideas available for him at this point of time. Those range from active piece-play to pawn-levers aiming to rip Black's kingside setup open. And which of these do you like the most? } 
31.f6 $1 { A good start for sure. Such a pawn-push is usually played with the idea of vacating the [f5]-square for another piece of ours. Here, however, White should have something slightly different in mind... } ( { Instead, the game saw } 31.Ne6?! { , which could have been dynamically countered by } 31...Qxg1 32.Nxg7+ Kf8 33.Rdxg1 Nf2! 34.Rh2 Re2! { . After the most precise } 35.Rgg2 Bxf3 36.Rxf2 Rxf2 37.Rxf2 Kxg7 38.Rxf3 f6 39.Rg3= { taking into consideration the proximity of the black monarch towards the center, it would be White who would need to stay careful in order to maintain the equilibrium. } ) ( { One of my students recommended } 31.g6?! { here. We started analyzing it together deeply and initially concluded that after something like } 31...h6 32.gxf7+ Kxf7 33.Ne6 Qxg1 34.Rhxg1 g5 35.Ne5+ Kf6 36.Ng4+ Kxf5 37.Nf8! { Black would be facing troubles in view of a possible skewer along the f-file. Imagine our surprise when we decided to check this position with the engine and discovered that this would have still been perfectly equal, provided that Black resorted to the impossible } 37...Ng3! 38.Rxg3 Kf4!! 39.Rgg1 Rxf8 40.Rdf1+ Bf3 41.Nh2 Ke3! 42.Re1+ Kf2= { . It turns out that White would need to agree a draw by repetition, as otherwise he even risks losing the arising endgame based on the strength of Black's connected passed pawns on the kingside. } ) gxf6  
32.Qg4 $1 { An elegant inclusion of the white queen into play. Now it becomes clear that White's previous move was exactly supposed to allow this piece to infiltrate Black's camp along the [h3]-[c8] diagonal. } ( { As much as the text move would have enabled the thematic arrival of the white knight to f5 with } 32.Nf5?! { , this approach would have allowed a trade of queens, severely reducing White's attacking potential on the spot. After the further } 32...Qxg1 33.Rhxg1 Rd7 34.Rde1 Kd8 35.gxf6 Nd6 36.Nxd6 Rxd6 37.Ne5 Bd5= { Black would have managed to hold his own thanks to the possibility to return the additional pawn at a certain point of time in case of need. } ) Nf2  { Black has no other choice here but to go down the rabbit hole dug by White. } ( 32...Qc7 33.Nf5 b3!? { looked like an interesting attempt to gain some counterplay at first, but after the accurate } 34.axb3 □ cxb3 35.Rh2! Rd7 36.Rxd7 Qxd7 37.Re2 +- { White would be taking over the fight once again due to the nasty pin along the e-file. } ) ( { On the other hand, } 32...Kf8 33.Nf5 Nf2 { would be losing outright to the beautiful } 34.gxf6! Nxg4 35.fxe7+ Ke8 36.Rhe1 +- { with Black forced to sacrifice plenty of material in exchange for the privilege of not getting mated immediately. } ) 
33.Qc8+  Qd8  
34.Qxa6 $3 { It would have been ultra-hard from the psychological point of view to willingly go for this type of a position from afar. The reason for this is that the human brain is going to start protesting that it does not make sense to go after pawns while so much material of our own keeps on hanging in the meantime. However, this small 'pause in the attack' would have been necessary in order to create some additional threats which were again not that obvious from the start. } Nxd1  ( 34...Bxf3 { would be losing even faster on account of } 35.Qb5+! Rd7 36.Rhe1+ Be4 37.Nf5 +- { when Black would have ended badly tangled, especially since White's [d1]-rook would have remained taboo. } ) 
35.Qa4+ $1 { The point behind White's previous play. Now Black is facing a choice - self-pinning with ...[Re7]-[d7] which would additionally expose his king to attacks along the e-file, or running away to [f8] with his king, resulting in [gxf6] in conjunction with [Qa4xb4]+, when the hunt would have continued. } Rd7  ( 35...Kf8 { would have faltered to } 36.gxf6 Bxf3 37.fxe7+ Qxe7 38.Nxf3 Qe3+ 39.Kb1 Qxf3 { when White would be recapturing material with a huge surplus after } 40.Qxb4+ Ke8 41.Re1+ Ne3 42.Qb8+ +- { . } ) 
36.Re1+ $1 Ne3 $5 { A nice practical try. The white rook on [e1] would have found it comparably easier to harass the black king along the g-file in the near future compared to its placement on [e3]. Concrete lines prove that this is not an issue for White, but in some situations the white [f3]-knight could potentially represent an obstacle. } ( 36...Kf8 { does no change anything due to } 37.Qxb4+ Kg7 38.gxf6+ Kxf6 39.Qd2!! Rg8 40.Qf4+ Kg7 41.Ne5 +- { . The black king is unable to defend against the white queen-and-knight gang anymore. } ) 
37.Rxe3+  Kf8  
38.Qxb4+  Kg8  ( 38...Kg7 { would be losing all the same in view of } 39.Nf5+ Kg6 40.N3h4+! Kxg5 41.Rg3+ Kh5 42.Qe1 +- { with the white knights literally wrapping themselves around the black monarch on the kingside. } ) 
39.Qe1 $3 { Yet another lovely transfer of the white queen towards the kingside. As [Re3]-[e8]+ is now being threatened, Black would have no other choice but to respond with } Kf8  { but then after something like } 
40.gxf6  Bxf3  
41.Qb4+ $1 Kg8  
42.Rxf3 $18 { In light of [Rf3]-[g3]+ coming right up, resignation would have not been premature at all. Your score: You receive the first three points for spotting 31.f6! followed by 32.Qg4!. The last two points are going your way once you have located 34.Qxa6!!, intending to continue with some nasty checks from [a4] or [b5]. }  

