{ Black handled the Meran Defense in an aggressive, sacrificial manner. As a reward, he was able to complicate matters for his opponent quite considerably. In this specific position, White is facing yet another tough choice - to grab the bull by the horns with 23.Qxd5, or to just protect his king with 23.Bd4 instead. And what would you do? } 
23.Qxd5 $1 { White should have picked up the gauntlet, even if the type of play arising afterwards would have resembled walking with his king through a minefield. } ( { In the game, White chickened out with } 23.Bd4? { , when Black could have more or less conclusively achieved equality after } 23...Bxd4 24.Qxd4 Qxe6 25.h3 h5! { . White would now really need to seek an exchange of queens, just to make sure that the rival's counterplay on the kingside does not transform into something more serious, e.g. } 26.Rd1 hxg4 27.Qxd5 Qxd5 28.Rxd5 Rc1 29.e6 Kf8! 30.hxg4 Ke7= {(, when any further attempts to play for a win by White would have to involve the sacrifice of his [f1]-bishop, that is a circumstance which would have however considerably increased the level of risk already. Sometimes the decision to choose between one move or another does not namely take the shape of an elimination or comparison problem. Instead, we are supposed to analyze the most ambitious albeit risky! continuation above anything else. Should this line fail to meet our objectives, we may then choose the safer option without the need for too much further analysis and thus, spending even more time on the clock!, as we will have known by this point of time already that there is nothing better available in that position anymore.)} ) Bxe3+  
24.Kh1 $1 { The first examination question required answering - by no means does White want to allow a rook-check coming from [c2]. } ( { The provocative } 24.Kg2? { , inviting } 24...Rc2+ { , could be theoretically justified by the fact that after the further } 25.Kf3 { White would be netting in a whole piece outright. Still, after } 25...Nd2+ 26.Kxe3 Qg5+ 27.Kd3 Qg6+= { he would not have been able to benefit from the additional material, as Black would have managed to save his skin thanks to a threefold repetition in the meantime. } ) Nf2+  
25.Kg2 $7 { No doubts regarding the correct way of proceeding - stepping into a discovery on [g1] would have been so bad that it does not even deserve to be quoted within a separate variation. } Rc2  { Yet another critical moment has just arrived. Black's rook infiltration along the second rank is a tad less dangerous since it does not take place with an immediate check. As they say, however, sometimes the threat is stronger than its execution. With a nasty discovery about to be carried out by Black in a moment from now, White needs to respond in an uncompromising manner. } ( { As an alternative to the main move, Black could have also ventured } 25...Nxg4 26.Qd7 Rc2+ { , when White would need to literally waltz around the dangers awaiting him on his path towards consolidation: } 27.Kg3! Bf2+ 28.Kf4! Be3+ 29.Ke4! Nf2+ 30.Kf3 +- { with Black eventually running out of checks, therefore also losing in view of his [e3]-bishop hanging, not to mention the material inferiority. } ) 
26.Ba3 $3 { An eye for an eye, or maybe should I say, a threat for a threat! The bishop is clearly taboo because of the [e6]-[e7]+ counter, whereas starting blasting as Black is surely going to be less of a pleasant experience with his own pieces hanging in the meantime too. } ( { One of my students got so fired up during the solving process that he even wanted to voluntarily go on a crusade with his king after } 26.Kf3? { here, which I quickly brushed off based on the very same drawing ideas seen earlier. Imagine how surprised we were to see that Black could even keep on playing for a win here himself after } 26...h5! {(a Kingdom for a bolthole like that one!)} 27.gxh5 Rd2 { , and now, in order to minimize the damage, White would need to go for } 28.Ba3! Qe8 29.Qxd2! Bxd2 30.Kxf2 b5 ⩱ { when he would also need to give up his [e6]-pawn on top of that, still retaining some chances of holding his position together though. } ) Nxg4+  
27.Kg3 $1 { Sticking to [g3] would have been best for White at this point, even if common sense would suggest that on dark squares the king is going to be exposed to more checks than along the light ones. } ( 27.Kf3? { would have once again jeopardized the white king to a perpetual check motif after } 27...Nxh2+ 28.Kxe3 Qg5+ 29.Kd4 Qg1+ 30.Kd3 Qg6+ { , when he would already need to assume a certain degree of risk if he wanted to carry on fighting. For example } 31.Qe4 Rd2+ 32.Kxd2 Qxe4 33.Rd1! Qd5+ 34.Kc1 Qxd1+ 35.Kxd1 Nxf1 ⇆ { and it cannot be said for sure from afar that the doubled e-pawns of White are going to be as strong as Black's connected passers on the kingside. } ) Bf2+ $1 { This way Black would be tempting the white monarch to help himself to the knight, which is obviously untouchable however. Instead, he needs to pick another square to escape to. } 
28.Kf3 $1 ( { But not } 28.Kf4?? { , in consideration of the rather anti-climatic refutation } 28...Ne3! 29.Bxe7 Nxd5+ 30.Ke4 Nxe7 -+ { . When the game revolves around momentous things like king safety, it is super easy to forget about far simpler winning methods. } ) Bc5 $1 { Upon witnessing that his attack was slowly going nowhere, Black would have been advised to deviate back towards a more defensive try. } ( { Under changed circumstances, } 28...Nxh2+?! { would have now been far less efficient, as after the further } 29.Ke4 Qh4+ 30.Kd3 { the white king would be finding refuge on the queenside. If you would like to claim that you are holier-than-thou in terms of chess calculation, figuring out immediately afterwards that } 30...Rc8 { would be now losing to } 31.e7+ Kh8 32.Qe4! Qg3+ 33.Ke2 Rc2+! 34.Kd1 +- { would still have been called for. } ) 
29.Kxg4  h5+ $1 { and now after the more or less forced } 
30.Kh3  Bxa3  
31.Rd1  Rc3+  
32.Bd3  Rc5  
33.Qd6  Ra5 $1 
34.Qb8+  Qf8  
35.Qxf8+  Kxf8  
36.Rf1+ $16 { Black would be landing in a difficult endgame. True, the presence of opposite-colored bishops on the board may increase drawish tendencies quite substantially, but the rook-invasion along the seventh rank propelled by a strong passed [e6]-pawn would have certainly forced Black to tread carefully here. Your score: You receive one point each for finding the moves 23.Qxd5!, 24.Kh1!, 26.Ba3!!, 27.Kg3! and 28.Kf3!, respectively. Taking the easy way out with 23.Bd4?, just because it looked safer than the alternative, grants you a round zero, Chicken Little... }  

