{ This position has arisen out of an 'ancient' 7.Na3!? line of the Classical Catalan. As a result thereof, Carlsen first sacrificed a pawn for the sake of opening up the position for his bishop-pair, and then followed up with an even larger material investment: an exchange in return for discoordinating his opponent's forces. This example has been featured in a couple of sources already, but - at least to my knowledge - none of them dug deeper into the consequences of the move 18...Reb8 here. And what do you think about it? } Reb8 $5 { This resource certainly deserved attention as an attempt to restore coordination by over-defending the [b7]-pawn and vacating the [e8]-square for the queen in case of need for an immediate evacuation. } ( { In the game, Giri went for the less appealing } 18...Nd5? { , when after the further } 19.Rb1 Qa5 { Carlsen could have obtained a winning position with } 20.Be4! g6 21.Bxd5 exd5 22.Rxb7 +- { . There would have been no room left for Black's wiggling anymore due to the severely weakened position of the black monarch, with the 20.Be4! intermezzo serving the purpose of provoking some more damage with ...[g7]-[g6] in the process. } ) ( { Just for the record, however, Black had a far more straightforward equalizing opportunity up his sleeve: } 18...Nb6! { , when after the more or less forced } 19.Bxb7 Nc4! 20.Bxa8 Nxa3 21.Qxc7 Rxa8 22.Qxf7+ Kh8 23.Rc1 Qe8= { it would be White who would need to stay a little bit more careful to maintain the equilibrium, as he would be slowly running out of momentum in view of the incoming queen exchange. } ) 
19.Rb1  Qe8  
20.Bxb7  { The first key position of the puzzle. Seeing this far would not have yielded you any points - it is only recognizing Black's most promising reaction to White's last move which would have allowed you to start scoring. } Nc3 $1 { Surprise, surprise - this is actually a move! For a second there, White is going to enjoy a solid material edge, but in chess - as in life - there is no free lunch... } ( { Black could also try } 20...Nd5? { , hoping for massive exchanges once White recaptures the exchange on [a8]. In lieu of that, however, he could go for } 21.Bc6! { , when after something like } 21...Rxb1+ 22.Qxb1 Qb8 23.Qd3! Nab6 24.e4! Nf6 25.Be7 +- { his king would have started feeling the heat, being slowly swarmed by the entirety of White's forced on the kingside. } ) 
21.Qxc3  { Certainly White's most logical follow-up is to eliminate said piece at once. } ( { In particular, an attempt to ignore the said knight would have led to some practical issues for White after } 21.Rb3?! Rxb7! 22.Rxb7 Nb5 23.Bb2 Qc8 24.Qc6 Ne8 25.Nc4 Qd8 ⇆ { , as he would constantly worry about the fate of his rook trapped on [b7]. } ) Ra7  { The idea behind 20...Nc3! is now officially revealed - White's [b7]-bishop is now pinned, as his remaining rook ceased to be defended once White captured with his queen on [c3]. } 
22.Qf3 $1 { Again, White's most ambitious try. If Black does nothing, defending with [Bb7]-[e4] would have become a respectable consolidating resource for the opponent. } ( { As opposed to the text move, } 22.Qxc7 { could only be scary for White in view of } 22...Nd5 { , when he would need to go for the double-edged } 23.Bc5! Nxc7 24.Bxa7 f6 25.Bxb8 Qxb8 26.Nd7 Qd8 27.Bc6 Qe7= { in order to make sure that he stays within the drawing zone. } ) ( { Interestingly enough, White could also resort to the highly-original } 22.Be4!? { , when after something like } 22...Nxe4 23.Rxb8 Qxb8 24.Qc2 Qb7 25.Nc6 Nf6 26.Nxa7 Qxa7= { White may enjoy the better structure, but Black may be actually in possession of the slightly superior light piece as the white bishop clearly lacks targets in the opponent's camp for the time being. } ) c6 $1 { It is not over until Black says it is over! By using interference, Black could have cut the communication between the white pieces, thus simultaneously sealing the fate of the [b7]-bishop. This is not the end of the line though. As a matter of fact, we are still far away from it! } ( { This time } 22...Nd5? { would have been countered by } 23.Bc6! Rxb1+ 24.Kg2 { . All of a sudden, despite being two whole exchanges up, Black would be facing the music on account of the weakness of his monarch, e.g. } 24...Qd8 25.Qxf7+ Kh8 26.Qxe6 +- { with no defense against the smothered mate [Ne5]-[f7]+ motif. } ) 
23.Nxc6  ( { One student of mine suggested } 23.Qb3 { as an alternative, with the justification that after the further } 23...Raxb7 24.Qxb7 Rxb7 25.Rxb7 { Black would have found it hard to defend against [Ba3]-[d6] & [Rb7]-[b8] or even the more direct [Rb7xf7]-[f8]+. Fortunately for him, there is a saving idea available for Black here, which combines both an evacuation of the queen from the eighth rank and generating counterplay on top of that: } 25...c5!! {(opening up the [e8]-[a4] diagonal for the queen just in case)} 26.dxc5 Qd8! {(double attack motifs against the white king and rook start appearing on [d5])} 27.c6 Qd1+ 28.Kg2 Qd5+ 29.f3 h5 {(creating a bolthole for the king on [h7])} 30.Bb2 { , and just when it seemed there was no defense against [Rb7]-[b8]+ followed by the pawn promotion, Black could uncork } 30...Ng4!! 31.Rb8+ Kh7 32.c7 Qc5! 33.fxg4 Qxc7 34.Rb3 hxg4 ⇆ { with a perfectly balanced endgame arising on the board. Black's material inferiority would cancel itself out with the need for the white pieces to spend a couple of tempi on additional consolidation efforts. } ) Raxb7  ( { By comparison, } 23...Rbxb7? { would have been much worse for Black because of } 24.Rc1! Ra8 25.Ne7+ Rxe7 26.Bxe7 Nd5 27.Ba3 ± { . The presence of the additional pair of rooks would have made all the difference, as will be explained in more detail in the last note to this exercise. } ) 
24.Rc1  { This quiet move looks really smart if you take into consideration that every retreat of the black rook from [b8] would have now resulted in a recapture of the exchange by White. } 
{ Still, after the most accurate } Rc8  
25.Ne7+  Rxe7  
26.Rxc8  Qxc8  
27.Bxe7  Qc1+ $1 { there you go, in the absence of rooks, the black queen is able to invade White's camp along the c-file } 
28.Kg2  Nd5  
29.Bd6  Qc2 $10 { Despite being a pawn up, White would not be really in a position to claim any tangible edge. The reason for this is that in order to make progress in this situation, he would need to evict Black's knight from [d5] by means of [e2]-[e4] first. However, this pawn push backfires strategically in view of ...[Nd5]-[f6], forcing White to theoretically push the same pawn further to [e5]. In such a case, Black's knight would be coming back to [d5] immediately, thus becoming a strong 'eternal' piece. This puzzle was particularly difficult since it did not feature a forced line intertwined by intermediate moves, but a series of many intermezzos in a row instead. Despite the quoted lines still making a forced impression, in reality calculating such a position would have been hell on Earth since it would be hard for anybody to catch the rhythm of calculation with our own intermezzos being interrupted by many more consecutive intermezzos of the opponent. Whenever faced with a choice or noticing a branching moment, we are obliged to slow down in order to be able to accurately evaluate the new circumstances, but with the course of events changing so rapidly as of every single move, this would have at least taken its toll on our time remaining on the clock... Your score: You obtain one point for each of the moves 20...Nc3!, 22.Qf3!, 22...c6! and 24.Rc1 you detected while calculating the forced line arising after 18...Reb8!?. You may award yourself the final point if you managed to minimize White's damage in the ensuing endgame, and, as a result, correctly evaluated it as balanced. }  

