{ Black sacrificed his [d6]-pawn in the Kalashnikov Sicilian for the sake of entrapping the white knight on that square for a longer period of time. His creative approach is about to start paying dividends, especially if you consider the shaky position of his opponent's forces as well as potential tactical threats like, among others, ...[d4]-[d3]. } 
17.Bb5  { As his queenside structure was the verge of collapse already, White had no other choice but to try and stir up things tactically. In particular, the removal of Black's [d7]-knight could potentially enable a knight-fork on [b6]. } axb4  { Black accepts the challenge, as he probably believes that his bishops are going to turn out to be superior against the rival's knights in the skirmish which is about to come. } ( { By comparison, } 17...bxc5 18.bxc5 Nxc5 19.Nxc5 Qxd6 20.Nxe6 Qxe6 21.Bd3= { would have been a much simpler way of achieving equality. The position arising afterwards would have remained perfectly equal, as Black does not seem to be able to successfully lift White's blockade on [d3] anyhow. Still, I guess that this did not appeal to Black so much as he was still playing for a win at this point of time. } ) 
18.Bxd7  Qxd7  ( { But not the greedy } 18...Bxd7? { in view of } 19.Nxb6 Ra3 20.Qb1 +- { , with Black dropping [b4] without obtaining any tangible counterplay in return for the already two missing pawns. } ) 
19.Nxb6  Qc6  { Surely the safest square for the queen, if you take into consideration how many consecutive moves White would need to make in order to have the queen attacked there with either of the knights. } 
20.Nxa8  Qxc5 $2 { This ambitious move proves to be the rotten egg. By playing like this, Black tried to intimidate his opponent with the perspective of actually losing both knights at the end of the day. However, this ploy could have backfired very badly, had White spotted a tactical, simplification-based counter. } ( { Much better would have been the straightforward } 20...Rxa8 { , when after something like } 21.f4 Qxc5 22.e5 d3+ 23.Kh1 Ra3 ⇆ { the strong [d3]-passer would have fully compensated for the missing exchange. White's [a1]-rook remains miserable, while his knight appears to be rather unstable on [d6]. What is more, the white [a2]-pawn might fall one day as well, giving rise to yet another black passed pawn on the queenside. } ) 
21.Ne8 $3 { An extraordinary concept indeed! Even if there was not much he could do about the incoming loss of material, White could have at least chosen the manner in which he wants to have it surrendered. The big point behind this move is going to be revealed shortly. } ( { Instead, the game saw } 21.e5? Bxe5 22.Ne4 Qa5 23.Rc1 Qxa8 24.Nc5 Qxa2= { , with White being advised to deprive his opponent of the bishop-pair immediately, followed by establishing a blockade around the squares [b3] and [d3], respectively. However, as none of the queenside pawns of Black are about to fall, this Mexican stand-off would need to be considered as a prelude to a future draw. } ) Rxe8  
22.Rc1  Qd6  
23.Nc7  { There you go - thanks to the aforementioned knight sacrifice, White gained enough time to not only have the other one evacuated safely from [a8], but also to strip Black of his dangerous bishop-pair while doing so. What is more, compared to the variation quoted in the previous note, here White would have been fortunate enough to additionally keep his [a2]-pawn, forcing Black to rely exclusively on his [d4]-pawn as an element of his possible compensation. } 
{ Then again, after the further } Rd8  
24.Nxe6  Qxe6  
25.Qb3 $1 Qxb3  
26.axb3  d3  { Black would be unexpectedly landing in a lost endgame more or less by force, as White would have now been in possession of a convincing plan of converting his material edge. } 
{ A sample line goes: } 
27.Rc5 $1 { Forcing out a trade of rooks, thus neutralizing the danger of Black promoting his [d3]-pawn. } d2  
28.Rd5  Rxd5  
29.exd5  Bc3  
30.g3  { Preparing to include the king into play, with the intention of discharging his rook from defensive duties. } Kf8  
31.f4  Ke7  
32.Kf2  Kd6  
33.Ke2  Kxd5  
34.Rf3  { Activating the rook. The optimal square for it shall be [d7], from where it would not only be targeting the black kingside pawns, but keeping an eye on the [d2]-passer as well. If evicted therefrom, the rook should head for [b7] instead, pinning the black bishop to the defense of his [b4]-pawn on many occasions. } Ke4  
35.Rd3  Kf5  
36.Rd7  Ke6  
37.Rb7  h5  
38.h3  { Preparing the [g3]-[g4]-[g5] pawn-push, aiming to put the black king into zugzwang based on the necessity to keep on defending [f7] at all times. } Kf6  
39.g4  hxg4  
40.hxg4  Ke6  
41.g5  { and now } f6  { would have been met by } 
42.Rb6+  Kf5  
43.gxf6  Bxf6  
44.Rxb4 $18 { Winning the [b4]-pawn, and therefore also eventually the game itself. The difficulty of this puzzle stems largely from the fact that a tactical resource such as 21.Ne8!! served a strategic goal, that is: transposing into a technically winning endgame. As much as topics like 'tactics at the service of strategy' or vice versa have been known to chess literature for many decades, the execution of such ideas in over-the-board games remains rather difficult for the practical player. The reason for this is that it is very easy to fall prey to the bias that tactical play especially when backed by concrete calculation simply must lead to a similarly tactical type of a result. Examples like these serve as a universal reminder that strategy and tactics are more intertwined than one might initially believe. Your score: Three points for spotting 21.Ne8!! and understanding the idea behind it. Add two more points to your stack if you also figured out the winning plan beginning with 25.Qb3! afterwards. }  

