{ Black is a pawn up but is certainly not enjoying himself at all from the tactical standpoint. His knight is about to go down, while the threat of [Rb7xf7] makes an even more disturbing impression. True, the vast majority of White's forces are far away from the kingside, which may give rise to some potential counter-attacking ideas against the enemy king. Once the white rook arrives to [f7] however, the queen plus rook and bishop trio operating along the [a7]-[f7]-[f2] triangle might actually be enough to safeguard their king against any dangers. With his position looking absolutely critical, Black needed to apply equally drastic measures in order to be able to survive. } Nd7 $3 { 'Wait, what? Why?' was more or less how my students would usually react when I shared the correct solution to this puzzle with them. And I do not blame them at all, as once I found this one during the analysis of the encounter in discussion, I was collecting my jaw from the floor as well! } ( { In the game, Black went for the admittedly tempting } 32...h4? { , when after } 33.Rxf7 hxg3+ 34.fxg3 Rc2+ 35.Bf2 Qc3 { Black's counter-attack was over, with White capable of obtaining a winning position after the precise } 36.Rf4 +- { . Black would be facing defeat in view of the incoming [Rf4]-[h4]+, with the preventive ...[g6]-[g5] weakening the position of his king even more. } ) ( { Saving the knight after } 32...Nxc6? 33.dxc6 h4 { would have been to no avail either, in view of the comparable } 34.Rxf7 hxg3+ 35.fxg3 Rc2+ 36.Bf2 Rxf2+ 37.Qxf2 Qxc6 38.Qf6 +- { . } ) ( { Finally, staying passive with } 32...Kg8? { would have been just as hopeless due to } 33.Rxb8+ Kh7 34.Rb7 Kg8 35.Be8 +- { . White breaks his rival's resistance thanks to the inclusion of his light-squared bishop into play via [e8]. Some will say that the last line was completely unnecessary and should therefore have not even be considered in the first place. Still, the reason why it is analyzed at this point of time shall be revealed soon... } ) 
33.Rxd7  { Just renewing the threat of eliminating [f7]. Why would Black's position be any better now than a move before? Even a keen eye is going to tell us that the only difference between those two situations is the missing knight of Black on [b8]. Well, just see for yourself... } ( 33.Bxd7 { would have led to a draw by means of a perpetual check after the further } 33...h4 34.gxh4 Be5+ 35.Kg1 Qd1+ 36.Kg2 Qxd5+ 37.Kg1 Qd1+= { . The first point 32...Nd7!! is now officially disclosed - with the white bishop standing in the way, Black would not need to worry about the devastating [Rb7xf7] anymore. } ) Kg8 $3 { As unbelievable as it might look, Black is now cruising towards a draw safely despite being almost a full bishop down. It turns out that the fact that White is unable to increase the pressure against [f7] right away effectively strips him of any winning chances. } 
34.Re7  { A multi-purpose move. By playing like this, White would be trying to re-introduce his light-squared bishop into play, but at the same time also anticipating the arrival of Black's dark-squared counterpart towards [e5] too. } ( { The alternative way of getting closer towards Black's f7-pawn with } 34.Qb8+ Kh7 35.Qe8 { would have remained unimpressive because of } 35...h4 36.gxh4 Rc1! 37.Bxc1 Qxf2+ 38.Kh1 Qf1+ { . Black hash a draw by threefold repetition available right away, although I would have personally continued with } 39.Kh2 Be5+ 40.Qxe5 dxe5 41.Bg5 Qf2+ 42.Kh1 e4 ⇆ { , when White would probably need to sacrifice one the bishops eventually for the sake of stopping Black's central passers. } ) ( 34.h4 { would have killed off the ...[h5]-[h4] pawn-lever, but after } 34...Be5! { , in view of ideas like ...[Rc3]-[c2] and/or ...[Be5xg3]+, White would need to stay on his toes here: } 35.Rd8+ □ Kh7 36.Bf4! Bg7! 37.Be8 Ra3! { and due to the inability to defend against ...[Ra3]-[a1], White would now be advised to seek a draw by threefold repetition after } 38.Bxf7 Rxa7 39.Bg8+ Kh8 40.Be6+ Kh7= { . } ) h4  
35.gxh4  { It might seem as if the capture on [h4] was harmless for White as his king is no longer exposed to the deadly checking idea from [e5], but Black has a strong follow-up up his sleeve. } ( 35.Re8+ Kh7 36.Ba4 { , anticipating ...[Rc3]-[c2]+ after the capture on [g3], certainly made a lot of sense here, but then after } 36...hxg3+ 37.fxg3 Be5 38.Bf4 Qe2+ 39.Kg1 Qe1+= { Black would once again be achieving salvation, or maybe even more should White now resort to the careless } 40.Kg2?? { on account of } 40...Rxg3+! 41.Kh2 Rxh3+! 42.Kxh3 Qf1+ 43.Kg3 Qxf4+ 44.Kg2 Bd4 -+ { with White forced to sacrifice his queen just to avoid immediate mate. } ) Rb3 $3 { All of a sudden, the rook invasion along the first rank starts looking murderous for White. This can certainly be stopped by a rook move like [Re7]-[b7], but that would, on the other hand, allow Black to renew the ...[Bg7]-[e5]+ motif. } ( { But not } 35...Rd3? { , as after } 36.Be8 Be5+ 37.Rxe5 dxe5 38.Ba4! { the black rook would not have been able to create any mating threats from [d1] anymore, with things going from bad to worse for him after the inconspicuous } 38...Qxd5 39.h5!! gxh5 40.Bc2 +- { with the fine point behind 39.h5!! only now to be seen in full blaze. Should the black rook move away at once, this would have led to a mating net being eventually erected around the black monarch after something like [Qa7]-[b8]+ followed by the thematic [Be3]-[h6]+!. } ) 
36.Re8+  { Transferring the rook for defensive purposes towards [b8] amounts to pulling the emergency brake by White, but by this point of time this would have actually been the most reasonable decision for him already. } ( { The thematic } 36.Be8 { would have now been met by } 36...Be5+ 37.Rxe5 dxe5 { . Just when it seemed that White was about to decide the outcome of this game in this favor after } 38.d6 Rb1 39.Bxf7+ Qxf7 40.d7 { , Black could save his skin thanks to } 40...Qd5= { when, instead of promoting his [d7]-pawn, White should actually switch to defending his king. } ) Kh7  
37.Rb8  Be5+  
38.Kg1  Rxb8  
39.Qxb8  Qxh3  
40.Qf8  Bh2+  
41.Kh1  Be5+ $10 { With yet another version of a draw by threefold repetition emerging on the board. The star-move 32...Nd7!! was so difficult to comprehend as it featured a phenomenon which I personally call a 'time-stop'. This is defined as a situation in which one of the players obtains a significant material advantage and seems to have all the time in the world to convert it, but is unable to do so because of the extremely good coordination/activity of the opponent's forces. The human mind is capable of understanding the concept of a sacrifice for immediate gains, in some cases even a sacrifice for long-term positional compensation. However, both of those cases feature instant gratification in return for the investment, or the feeling of control over the situation over an extended period of time, respectively. In this exercise none of the above applied though, with the specific properties of the position allowing Black to save a draw to be determined by means of precise calculation. Your score: You receive three points for figuring out 32...Nd7!!, together with the two coordination-based ideas behind it. The remaining two points are going your way if you managed to effectively deal with any two of White's attempts to continue playing for a win mentioned as of move 34, respectively. }  

