{ Black is a pawn down } { (he's an exchange down in return for a pawn) } { , but cannot complain too much about his current situation from the positional standpoint. With all of his pieces ready to go after the white king, he does not even feel his material inferiority yet. On top of that, White's forces make a rather chaotic impression. His queen is out of play and the rooks look disconnected, and therefore also vulnerable to attacks like ...[Qc8]-[c5]. Now - paraphrasing a former U.S. President - the question remains to be answered: all this sounds good, but does it also work? } Rf8 $3 { Certainly the most counter-intuitive move in the entire course! Who in their right mind would have retreated such a lovely rook operating along the d-file back towards the [f8]-square? Before you burn me on the stake for recommending such a heretical move, please let me explain first what sort of devilish logic one was supposed to abide by in order to reach such a sacrilegious conclusion. } ( { For starters, it would be worth mentioning that the most straightforward } 38...Qc5?? { , targeting both rooks at the same time - although tactically desirable for Black - would have been badly countered by } 39.Re8+ Rxe8 40.Qxe8+ Kh7 41.Rxf3! Nxf3 42.Qe4+ +- { , with Black losing a whole knight to a double-attack at the end of the day. } ) ( { The moment one understands that Black is a slave to the Re7-e8+ threat for the time being, } 38...Kf8?! { , played additionally in order to take advantage of the rather cramped placement of the white rook on [e7], begins to come into consideration. Fortunately for White, after the razor-sharp } 39.Qb4! Nc6 40.Rxf7+! Kxf7 41.Rxf3+ Kg8 42.Qe1= { he would have been able to hold his own as the black knight would not necessarily be such a strong piece here compared to the white bishop. } ) ( { Another way of taking the sting out of the aforementioned idea of White would have been } 38...Kh7?! { , but here things would have taken an even more straightforward path after } 39.Rxf7! Nxf7 40.Rxf3 Ne5 41.Qc2+ Kg8 42.Rf4= { with White once again in a position to hold everything together. } ) ( { Finally, instead of preventing the check on e8, Black could have also created an additional bolthole with } 38...g6?! { , but even so, after something like } 39.Bg2 Qc5 40.Re8+ Kg7 41.Rxd8 Qxf2 42.Qd1 { White would have been capable of defending resolutely against any further attacking attempts of the opponent, e.g. } 42...Qh4!? 43.Bxf3 Nxf3 44.Kg2= { . The best Black could get from this position would be a draw by threefold repetition. Now things have become a little more comprehensive already, right? The evaluation led us to believe that Black wanted to include his queen into play with attacking intentions. However, once we started checking all reasonable moves which could possibly help him achieve this goal, they were all being answered by the same old Re7-[e8]+ motif. Out of all the preparatory moves enabling the said reorganization of forces, it is only the apparently illogical 38...Rf8!! which would pass the test with flying colors, as will be confirmed by the analysis provided below. Before we move on towards that analysis, I wanted to mention one thing: the 'logic' telling us that [Re7]-[e8]+ needed to be neutralized in the first place was a different type of 'logic' compared to the one which eventually steered our attention towards the powerful text move. In other words, depending on the stage of the calculation process, you should exercise a varying degree of diligence. When evaluating the position initially, general considerations will most often be sufficient, but during the comparison/elimination part taking place afterwards, you will frequently need to be much more concrete than that. } ) 
39.Qc2  { After recognizing Black's idea, White would do well by organizing his defenses in the meantime. } ( { Knowing Black's response to the text move, White could have also resorted to the prophylactic } 39.Rg2 { , but this approach would have been to no avail either in view of } 39...Qd8! { . After something like } 40.Rb7 Nh4 41.Rg3 Nf5 42.Rg1 Ne3 43.Rb2 Qf6 -+ { White would eventually be forced to surrender material due to the inability to deal effectively with the black tactical trio consisting of a queen supported by two knights. } ) Qg4  
40.Bg2  ( { This time } 40.Rg2 { would have already been too late because of } 40...Qh4 41.Rb7 Ne1 -+ { , with Black not only obtaining a material edge, but also carrying on with his attacking ideas thanks to the tactical superiority of the knight over the bishop. } ) Qh4 $1 { This position needed to be seen by Black from afar when embarking upon 38...Rf8!!. White has no other choice but to surrender the additional exchange. No matter how he influences the material balance on the board, he will still end up struggling to survive. } 
{ Sample lines: } 
41.Bxf3  Qxe7  
42.Qe2  ( 42.c5? { would only be exacerbating White's demise on account of } 42...Nxf3 43.Rxf3 Qe1+ 44.Kg2 Rd8 45.Qf5 Rd2+ 46.Kh3 Qe8 -+ { , when the white [c5]-passer would be going nowhere while Black consolidates his position with ...[g7]-[g6] and/or ...[Rd2]-[d5]. } ) Kh8  
43.Bd5  f6  
44.Qe3  Qd6  
45.Qc3  Rb8  
46.Rf1  Qc5 $19 { With White getting outplayed slowly, but inevitably based on the dark-squared blockade stretching across the board. As Black, I would start off by pushing my [a6]-pawn all the way to [a4], for the sake of enabling a rook invasion via [b3] soon thereafter. Your score: Four points are going your way for logically recognizing the need for 38...Rf8!! in the starting position. The final, fifth point belongs to you if you aimed to transfer the queen towards the kingside immediately afterwards to create mating threats against the white monarch. }  

