{ At first glance, this position stemming from the Catalan Opening appears to be perfectly equal. The structure is symmetrical and the level of activity of both of the players looks pretty much comparable. However, a closer inspection of the situation reveals that there are some practical difficulties awaiting Black soon from now. On one hand, White can go for 19.b4 here, when the [b4]-pawn would have been taboo due to the incoming knight-fork on [c6]. On the other hand, however, she could also resort to the disruptive 19.Bc6, with the bishop being just as untouchable in view of Black's back-rank weakness. With both options looking similarly attractive, a decision needs to be made - which of the analyzed moves works and which does not? } 
19.Bc6 $1 { When faced with a comparison-based problem within the calculation process, I always advise my students not only to try and understand why are they playing a certain move, but also what is the reason for rejecting the other one. It is namely often the case that those two ideas may be interconnected in a sense that the superior alternative manages to specifically overcome the flaw of the inferior one. However, without comprehending the nature of the faulty line, it might at times not be possible to appreciate the advantage behind the best move. } ( { In fact, } 19.b4!? { was featured in the game and even led to a swift and convincing victory for White, since her rival panicked and immediately faltered with the losing } 19...e5? { . As much as opening up the a-file with } {19...axb4 looked suspicious, since after the recapture on [b4] White would have gained an additional method of hurting Black with the [Rd1]-[a1] skewer, this move was absolutely necessary, especially because the more restrained 19...Nd7 would have failed to 20.bxa5 bxa5 21.Nxe6! . The tricky defensive resource 21...Re8!? could be met by the unbelievable 22.Qa7 +- , claiming plenty of material and simultaneously invading the seventh rank on top of that. Please note however the importance of the inconspicuous intermezzo 20.bxa5 bxa5 in the quoted variation. Without it, White would be neither in a position to create a double-attack against Black's [g8]-king and [a6]-bishop after a possible capture on [e6], nor shift the queen of hers in the most surprising manner towards [a7], evading a nasty defensive pin along the e-file. Instead, after the further 19...axb4 20.axb4 Black would need to demonstrate excellent tactical vision before being able to spot 20...Bb7!! . The justification is that after the more or less forced 21.bxc5 Bxg2 22.Kxg2 bxc5 23.Nb3 Rxd1 24.Rxd1 c4 White would not be able to secure her material edge, in particular with the 25.Rc1 pin being met by 25...Qa8+! with a draw to be agreed after 26.f3 cxb3 27.Rxc8+ Qxc8 28.Qxb3= . All those lines are surely very pretty, but now you are probably wondering - how are they supposed to help me discover the reason why 19.Bc6 could be a potential improvement over 19.b4? Well, sometimes two seemingly distinct candidate move may escalate into the same type of a position, with the hardest part about such a situation being not really the need for calculation itself, but rather the fact that it may take several more moves to reach the position in which the comparison process is supposed to happen. } ) Qc4  { The queen is admittedly going to feel a little stuck on [c4] here, but with [b2]-[b3] not being a threat yet and White's bishop even beginning to hang on [c6] at times, at least optically speaking Black should not be concerned at the moment. But now comes the game-changer... } ( { The interference-based defense with } 19...b5?! { would have certainly deserved attention, was it not for } 20.b3! Qxa3 21.Nc2 Qxb3 22.Qxc5 +- { , netting in the knight on [c5] in return for the irrelevant queenside pawns. } ) 
20.Bf3 $3 { The rabbit is now officially pulled out of the basket, even if the magician's trick cannot yet be fully observed by the spectators. In order to figure out the idea behind it, we will now need to compare it with the continuation which saved Black after the immediate 19.b4!?. } Bb7  ( 20...Qa4 { would have been similarly met by } 21.b4! { . After the further } 21...axb4 22.axb4 Bb7 23.bxc5 Bxf3 24.exf3 bxc5 25.Nb3 Rxd1 26.Rxd1 c4 27.Rc1 +- { , at the end of the line, Black would have not had the intermediate ...[Qa4]-[a8]+ at his disposal anymore - an absolutely insane example of the use of comparison during calculation! } ) ( { Instead, } 20...a4 { would have ruled out any movements of the white [b2]-pawn whatsoever. Sadly for Black, now after something like } 21.Nc6 Rxd1 22.Rxd1 Re8 23.Rd6 +- { she would still be losing the game, this time in the long run, based on White's general domination over the position. } ) 
21.b3  Qa6  
22.b4 $1 { Now White gains a winning edge in a more prosaic manner already. She would be able to establish a knight-presence on [c6] in a moment from now, thus also effectively paralyzing the opponent's forces. } 
{ The lines given below are by no means exclusive - I have chosen the ones which I consider to be most representative for illustrating White's winning plan: } Bxf3  
23.Qxf3  Na4  ( 23...axb4 24.axb4 Qa4 { would have been all bark and no bite too, on account of } 25.bxc5 Rxd4 26.Qb7! Qxd1 27.Qxc8+ Rd8 28.Rxd1 Rxc8 29.cxb6 +- { with a completely winning rook endgame for White thanks to the strength of her passed [b6]-pawn. } ) 
24.Nc6  Rxd1  
25.Rxd1  Qb7  { and now } 
26.Rc1 $1 { would have given rise to a few murderous tactical motifs like [Nc6]-[e7]+, or something even more devious like } Kf8  
27.Rc4  axb4  
28.Nd8 $3 Qxf3  
29.Rxc8 $18 { With Black forced to surrender her queen for the sake of averting immediate mate after [Nd8]-[c6]+. Your score: You receive three points for discovering why 19.b4!? does not work at the end of the day, with the remaining two points going your way after spotting 20.Bf3!! as well as understanding the merits behind it. }  

