29.g4 $1 ( { The primary reason making this position so interesting to calculate is the long lifespan of the black knight on e2. For example, after the immediate } 29.Rxe2?! { , Black could respond with } 29...Rd7! { when after the more or less forced } 30.Qe1 Bd3 31.Kg1 Bxe2 32.Qxe2 Qa1+ 33.Bf1 Rd1 34.Kg2 Kg7 ⇆ { White would be struggling trying to re-establish coordination, yet alone to obtain anything more out of this materially-balanced position. } ) 
{ This position has arisen on the board during an analysis session with a student of mine. Black played the opening and middlegame stage so ambitiously that not only did he manage to grab a pawn in the process, but also that his knight made it all the way to [e2]. One might argue whether this last circumstance makes it an asset or a liability, but one thing is for sure - with our attention drawn by the engine towards the surprising 29.g4, we immediately became aware of the need for Black to demonstrate some great calculation skills in order to be able to maintain the balance. The decoy 29.g4! represents White's most promising try in this situation, especially if you consider the reason why Black's appetizing knight standing deep behind enemy lines cannot be eliminated yet. } Rd7 $1 { The initial response of Black stays the same, albeit this time Black would be trying to 'force-feed' White with his knight instead of punishing him for its early capture. } 
30.Qe3  { The placement of the white queen on [e3] makes perfect sense here as - apart from keeping an eye on Black's entangled knight - this way White would be also anticipating the arrival of the black bishop to [d3] any moment now. } ( { The alternative } 30.Qh6?! { would have been comparably worse than the text, since after the further } 30...Bd3 31.Ne5 Nf4+ 32.Kg1 { Black would now be in a position to defend his [f4]-knight while running away with his rook from danger with } 32...Rd4 { . Then again, the line could continue with attempts of White to nonetheless disturb the coordination of Black by means of } 33.Nf3 { with the culmination point arising after } 33...Ne2+! 34.Kh1 {(the black knight and rook were taboo in view of the eventual ...Qa8-[a1]+)} 34...Rd7 35.Qe3 Bxc4 36.Bf1 { . Just when it seemed that White would be finally able to consume the opponent's knight, Black could uncork the unexpected } 36...Qd8! { , with the justification that after the further } 37.Bxe2 Re7 38.Bxc4 Rxe3 39.Rxe3 b5 ⇆ { White would not be able to target the black monarch right away in view of the necessity to keep an eye on the potentially dangerous march of Black's queenside pawns. } ) Bd3  
31.Ne5  Nf4+  ( { Maybe this is going to be a little anti-climatic from my side to reveal an idea which is going to help Black save his skin later on, but here the tricky } 31...Re7? { , aiming to tactically prevent the capture of his knight on [e2] after } 32.Qxd3 Rxe5 { , would have failed to the calm and collected } 33.Qd2 +- { with the black knight unable to escape anywhere in the meantime. } ) 
32.Kg1  Ne2+  
33.Kh1  { All this has been forced so far. With almost all of his pieces hanging, Black simply had to resort to some back-and-forth jumping with his knight. But now the moment of truth has arrived - how to continue defending here successfully? } Re7 $1 { A lovely geometrical motif! It turns out that those repeated knight checks were supposed to push the white monarch towards the corner, this way once again ruling out a double capture of pieces on [d3] and [e2] due to the back-rank check coming from the black queen. } 
34.Qc5 $1 { A lovely counter! By playing like this, White would be unpinning his queen, thus actualizing the threat of capturing on [d3] with his knight. This idea is even more so unpleasant as Black's rook simultaneously lands under attack as well. Finally, White's passivity here could have also allowed a nasty double-attack via ...[Qa8]-[a5] here at times. } ( { An attempt to lure Black's bishop towards c4 for the purpose of organizing a similar double-attack from c5 with } 34.Rd1? { would have actually backfired after } 34...Nc3! 35.Rg1 Qb8! { . White would need to defend the knight with } 36.f4 { in order to avoid a hopeless endgame arising on the board once the black queen chops on [e5]. Now Black could answer } 36...Ne2 37.Re1 f6! { , when the engine claims that after the unbelievably pretty sequence of } 38.Qc5! Qf8! 39.Bd5+! cxd5 40.Qxd5+ Kh8 41.Nxd3 Nxf4! 42.Qf3 Nxd3 43.Rxe7 Qxe7 44.Qxd3 Kg7 ∓ { White would be fighting for his life in a queen endgame a pawn down. I do encourage you to examine the line quoted above closer without an engine running in the background, trying to figure out any possible alternatives together with their refutations emerging along the way. } ) Qf8 $3 { This might be the deepest anticipatory idea featured in this entire course! We typically associate anticipation with the ability to predict the future turn of events on the board for the purpose of getting ready for it in advance. In reality, anticipation also has a tactical side to it, in the sense that we need to use it when determining whether a threat of the opponent is really as dangerous as it looks like. } 
35.Bf3 $1 { Resisting the immediate capture on was to be avoided for a reason. Additionally, it might seem as if the text move added more fuel to the fire by targeting yet another piece of Black simultaneously. } ( 35.Nxd3 { would have been met by } 35...Ng3+! 36.hxg3 Rxe1+ { , when due to to the inability to recapture on [e1], White would need to agree to a double-edged endgame after } 37.Kh2 Qxc5 38.Nxc5 b5 39.cxb5 cxb5= { . Although the engine insists that the chances of both players remain roughly equal, I would definitely prefer Black from the purely practical standpoint. White's light pieces would find it rather difficult to maintain a blockade over the black [b5]-passer due to the absence of any solid outposts on the queenside. } ) Qd8 $3 { Clearly the second most difficult move of this entire puzzle. Black does not seem to care for the moment that his knight is under attack, since he will always be able to recapture material afterwards on a tactical basis. What is even more, the consecutive captures on [d3] and [e7] would have actually led to immediate mate on [f3], taking advantage of the arrival of the white bishop to that very square just a second earlier. } ( { Instead, } 35...Nf4? { would have just been losing because of } 36.Qd4! g5 37.Nxd3 Nxd3 38.Rxe7 Nxf2+ 39.Qxf2 Qxe7 40.Qd4 +- { . } ) 
36.Qxe7 $1 { The most ambitious continuation for White. If he only managed to collect the bishop and knight without allowing any counterplay, he would have enjoyed serious winning chances in the ensuing endgame. } ( 36.Bxe2 Bxe2 37.Rxe2 f6= { is exactly Black's basic defensive idea, with the additional twist that any tricks related to the seemingly clever } 38.Nxc6?? { would have resulted in defeat after } 38...Qd1+ 39.Kg2 Rxe2 -+ { . } ) Qxe7  
37.Nxd3  { Fortunately for Black, thanks to } h5 $1 { he would have been able to significantly reduce the number of pawns on the board, when after something like } 
38.gxh5  Qh4  
39.Bxe2  Qxc4  
40.hxg6  Qd5+  
41.f3  fxg6 $132 { White's advantage would have remained barely symbolic, in particular when you take into consideration the amount of care he would need to exercise when battling the opponent's active queen and passed queenside pawns at the same time while trying to convert his tiny edge into a full point. Puzzles like this one remind me of a question many of my students ask right at the start of their calculation training: how to recognize the optimal moment to stop calculating? Obviously, there is no universal answer to this conundrum, at least not a predetermined number of moves after which ending the lines would have been always allowed. In general, we should try to calculate as far as we can, but we should not stretch it either as the risk of missing something increases dramatically around move four. Then again, if the position remains forced in nature, you are advised to continue analyzing it until it ceases to be so. Usually, reaching yet another branching moment is going to be indicative of the need to stop calculating and evaluating the situation arising on the board. What is important to remember, though, is that in such a moment your evaluation efforts should be typically intuitive in nature, as trying to make them more concrete by calculating even further beyond would have put you in a vicious logical circle... Your score: You receive one point for each of the moves 29...Rd7!, 33...Re7!, 34. Qc5!, 34...Qf8!! and 35...Qd8!! which you managed to locate along the way, respectively. }  

