{ In this game, which started as a rather positional 6.Be3 [e5] 7.Nf3 Najdorf, things all of a sudden started getting very tactical once the [e4] and [e6]-pawns of both of the sides were dynamically exchanged for one another. But this was only supposed to be the very beginning of the complications... } 
18.Nxe5 $1 { A very strong resource indeed. The knight is taboo, but what makes things even uglier for Black is the first element of a smothered mate construction with [Ne5]-[f7]+. } Nxf2 $1 { The white knight was taboo, so in the absence of any other defensive ideas, the only method of carrying on by Black was to launch a respective counter-attack. } ( 18...dxe5? { would have been clearly wrong in view of } 19.Rd7 Qxc2 20.Qxe7 Rg8 21.Qxe5 Nf6 22.Rxb7 +- { , with two pawns constituting a sufficient winning edge already. } ) ( { By comparison, } 18...Bg5?! { would have represented a small improvement over the previous line, as then after something like } 19.Bxg5 Nxg5 20.Qxd6 Qxd6 21.Rxd6 Rxc2 22.Nd3 Ne4 23.Re6 ± { White would be one pawn up less. Still, as the capture on [f2] would now be out of the question due to a pin along the f-file, Black would need to face the music by retreating the knight, thus being forced to endure a long and possibly also unsuccessful defense. } ) 
19.Rd4 $1 { The logic behind this move is admittedly very deep. As highlighted by the lines quoted below, capturing Black's [f2]-knight would have simultaneously allowed the opponent to help himself to the white counterpart on [e5], while escaping towards [d2] instead of [d4] with the rook would have enabled Black to have the same knight evacuated via [e4]. There is also one more beneficial feature of bringing the rook to the fourth rank, which will only be revealed in a moment from now. } ( { After } 19.Bxf2?! dxe5 { , the thematic invasion of the rook via [d7] would have failed to achieve the desired goal as the black queen coming to [c2] would be generating an attack against White's [f2]-bishop too quickly. Instead, after something like } 20.c3 Bc5 21.Bxc5 Qxc5+ 22.Kh1 Rce8 ↑ { , it would be White who would need to remain precise in order to maintain the balance in view of the potential of Black's [e5]-passer. } ) ( { Some would say that White should be better after something like } 19.Rd2 Ne4 20.Ng6+ hxg6 21.Qxe4 Kh7 22.c3 Rce8 ⇆ { , at least based on his superior structure. However, pawns are just one of many elements influencing the evaluation of the position in the game of chess, with activity along the open e- and f-files granting Black sufficient counterplay in return in this case. } ) Rf6 $1 ( { As Black was not in a position to prevent Ne5-f7+ anymore, he correctly decided to attack the queen of White in the interim. Thanks to this rook lift, he simultaneously made sure that White is not getting anything more than a draw by threefold repetition, provided that he executes the discussed idea in its purest form at once. Just as a sidenote, at first glance it might seem as if } 19...Bf6?? { was a much better idea, but then White would have responded with } 20.Rc4! { , which was exactly the hidden additional point behind his previous move. } ) ( { Funnily enough, } 19...dxe5? { would be again letting Black down after } 20.Rd7 { since the queen taking on [c2] now would for a change not be creating any subsequent threat on [f2] anymore. Black's best practical try here would have been } 20...Bc5 21.Rxc7 Bxe3 { , for the purpose of making use of a discovered check soon enough. However, objectively speaking White would be able to defend in a sovereign manner here thanks to } 22.Rf7! Ne4+ 23.Kh1 Nf2+ 24.Rxf2! Bxf2 25.Qxe5 Rxc2 26.b4 +- { , with just a little bit more technique required from him before netting in the full point. } ) 
20.Nf7+  Kg8  
21.Nh6+  Kh8  
22.Qd5 $1 { Very brave for sure! White could have agreed a draw at will, but the text move keeps the game going by renewing the [Nh6]-[f7]+ threat. One could argue that this way of handling the position would actually lead to a loss of the very same knight on [h6], but White had seen everything from afar. } Rcf8 $1 { A powerful multi-purpose move. This way, apart from preventing the knight-check from [f7] for good, Black would be also over-defending his stranded [f2]-knight. } ( { In the game Black lost his cool and chose } 22...gxh6? 23.Bxf2 Bf8 { , but this solution left his king badly exposed along the [a1]-[h8] diagonal. Now White could have sealed the deal with } 24.Rd3! { , when after the further } 24...Bg7 25.Rc3 Qd7 26.Rxc8+ Qxc8 27.Bd4 Re6 28.Rf1 +- { Black would have been defenseless against various infiltration ideas of White, with the f-file being the easiest one for him to take advantage of. } ) 
23.Rd2  { The final test of viability of White's position. We already know that he cannot afford to expose his king to a bishop attack from [d4], which seems to be excluding the possibility of eliminating White's [h6]-knight upfront... or does it? } Rxh6 $1 { This move has not been on our radar so far, and understandably so, since the underlying idea behind it has only been made possible as of Black's last move. } ( { Instead, } 23...Qc6?! 24.Qxc6 bxc6 25.Rxf2 Rxf2 26.Bxf2 gxh6 { seemed to lead to a defensible version of the position discussed in the previous note as owing to the absence of queens on the board, the safety of the black monarch would have not been critically important here anymore. Still, the endgame arising after the further } 27.Ra3! Rb8 28.Re3 Bf8 29.b3 ± { would have been ultra-hard, if not outright impossible for Black to hold. His compromised structure is one thing here, but what I like even less from his perspective would be the fact that every single white piece would have appeared to be better than every single black counterpart. } ) 
24.Bxh6  Ng4  { Now everything becomes clear. Black is temporarily an exchange down, but recaptures it fairly easily thanks to the unavoidable ...[Qc7]-[b6]+ counter. } 
25.Bg5  Bxg5  
26.Qxg5  Qb6+  
27.Kh1  Nf2+  
28.Rxf2  Qxf2  
29.h3  Qxc2  
30.Qe7  Qc8  
31.Qxd6  h6 $10 { It is high time for both rivals to call it a day. Your score: No room for discussion left here - you either see 23...[Rxh6]! 24.Bxh6 Ng4 and get five points or you do not. }  

