{ As indicated in the introduction, please find below a summary of the rules and principles governing the calculation process discussed in this course. These shall be organized according to themes as well as the level of their complexity, taking the form of a respective Q&A. 0. General Remarks What is calculation? Calculation in chess is defined as the ability to predict a sequence of best moves available in the given position. As opposed to other skills like, for example, anticipation, calculation tends to be extremely concrete and takes the form of variations instead of just general predictions regarding the future course of the game. Is calculation more important than strategy? Depending on the situation, calculation may serve a superordinate or a subordinate role as regards other thinking methods in chess. To speak more precisely, calculation usually overrides general assumptions, typical for strategic play. On the other hand, in some cases calculation shall serve merely as a more scientific way of confirming our knowledge-based views on the current position. Can all positions in chess be solved purely by means of calculation? In your own tournament practice you will inevitably stumble upon positions which can only be solved by means of brute force calculation. Then again, they are statistically speaking pretty scarce, with the most standard case of using calculation featuring yourself just calculating short lines here and there as the game progresses, for the purpose of making sure that your strategic ideas are not flawed tactically. What is intuition and in which cases should I use it instead of calculation? Intuition is defined as the sum of one’s previous experience and knowledge, and takes the form of a whisper in the back of their head suggesting ideas which ‘look good’ in the given position. The better player you become, the more accurate feedback will you be receiving from this type of an advisor. There are some positions in chess which barely require any calculation whatsoever, and as such should be played exactly purely intuitively. Such cases include positional endgames on reduced material without any tactical potential left in them, but also certain closed middlegames in which abilities like piece coordination or long-term planning happen to be much more necessary. Does the above mean then that I can stop calculating altogether upon reaching a strategically-winning position? Even in apparently calm, winning positions we cannot afford to stop calculating and hope that reasonably-looking strategic resources are going to guide us safely past the finish line. In particular, from the psychological point of view, players whose position is objectively winning tend to relax prematurely, assuming that the game is going to 'win itself'. Their opponents however, upon feeling that the noose is tightening around their necks, become extremely motivated to save the game and because of this focus twice as much than before. They start calculating literally all possible lines in search of a singular path towards salvation. In other words, even the biggest of advantages can be squandered when a reckless player faces an inferior, but highly-driven one… Does calculation and intuition exclude one another? Those two skills should optimally go hand in hand supplementing each other. Resorting to intuition exclusively will usually be a good idea only when running out of time on the clock, or maybe while already being deep within a calculated line and therefore unable to carry on checking it any further beyond due to your own human limitations. How far ahead should I calculate? The human mind is unable to calculate accurately multiple branches within a given variation beyond a certain limit } { (typically 4-5 moves) } { . For this reason, I personally try to calculate 2-3 moves ahead at all times, going deeper into a certain position only upon determining that I am dealing with a so-called ‘critical’ one. Then again, if the position remains forced in nature, I typically continue analyzing it until it ceases to be so. Usually, reaching yet another branching moment is going to be indicative of the need to stop calculating and start evaluating the situation arising on the board already. Is calculation in the middlegame any different to calculation in the endgame? Apart from some differences as regards the typical construction variation trees depending on the stage of the game, there is also the interconnectedness of calculation and theoretical endgame knowledge to be mentioned in this context. What I mean by this is that the closer the game gets towards the endgame, the more important for one's calculation skills does it become to have solid theoretical endgame foundations. The reason for this is that every line you calculate needs to end in an evaluation. During the middlegame stage, such an assessment is going to be to a considerable degree general in nature, while in the endgame assessing the given position should be much more concrete as it can } { (and should!) } { take into consideration one's positive knowledge about the given endgame situation. This is particularly noticeable in computer chess - it is just amazing how much stronger do engines become towards the endgame if they have access to tablebases. The same applies to humans however - knowing theoretical endgames very well is going to benefit your calculation skills in the endgame immensely. Without them however, you will not be able to evaluate many of the arising endgames conclusively, frequently leaving you feeling like a babe lost in the woods... How does a typical calculation process look like? A typical calculation process in chess is going to be constructed as follows: 1. Evaluate the position 2. Create a list of candidate moves 3. Eliminate unsuitable candidate moves and/or compare them with one another 4. Calculation of the consequences of a given } { (forced) } { line 5. Check whether the given line features any notable intermediate moves 6. Evaluate the final position and play the move Is the above construction binding? In other words, can I have it modified in accordance with my own needs? Most variations you calculate will look like this, although I can certainly imagine some deviations in this regard dictated by the specific nature of the position. For example, if there is only one reasonable candidate move available from the start, there will be no room left for any elimination/comparison whatsoever. With regard to some possible modifications of the above though, some of my students have mentioned that they in general abide by the discussed construction, but somewhere between the evaluation of the position and the creation of a list of candidate moves, they utilize an additional step which I personally call a ‘random tactical shootout’. What they namely claim is that if the starting position features a move which stands out as being much very tactical/forcing, they tend to analyze it as deeply as possible in the first place, without checking other lines simultaneously too. The point behind this approach is to verify the soundness of the most tempting continuation. If it wins, they play it without too much hesitation. If it fails to achieve the desired goal though, they come back straight to square one to evaluate the situation from scratch, but richer in knowledge in terms of many motifs and ideas they may have discovered along the way. Although I do not feel entirely bought on this concept } { (I am not a huge fan of cutting corners by purposefully omitting moves which at times might prove to be even stronger than the primary choice) } { , I cannot help but admit that this may work out in practice from time to time. Please just make sure to exercise caution and use common sense while doing so. Is it OK if I sometimes come back within the calculation process towards one of the previous steps? Chess calculation is actually rarely linear in nature. Most coaches will namely tell you that you need to first evaluate the position. Then comes the time to elect some candidate moves which correspond with the needs of the position. Afterwards, you should use elimination and/or comparison to choose the one which is best, and finally have its viability confirmed by means of calculating its consequences before you execute it. All this is indeed correct, provided however that your initial list of candidate moves indeed includes the best move from the start. Otherwise, you will make it all the way to the end of the process, but leave empty-handed as there is always going to be something wrong about each of the primary candidates. What should you do then? My suggested approach is to gather all the knowledge on the position you have accumulated so far, come back to the candidate move selection process and look for additional alternatives which serve the desired purpose, but do not suffer from the same defects. There is a chance that the position still features a strong idea - it is just that you did not see or appreciate it initially as you were still in the dark to a certain extent regarding the evaluation of the position, and only figuring out why other moves fail to deliver would have allowed you to understand what you should have been really looking for from the start. Will solving tactical puzzles help me get better at calculation? I have repeatedly heard coaches advise their students to solve plenty of tactical puzzles if they wanted to improve their calculation skills. As much as excellent tactical vision is an absolute must when calculating lines, I do need to put a hard stop here already by remarking that calculation absolutely does not equal tactics in chess. Tactics namely infers the ability to recognize certain patterns comprised of moves or short sequences thereof, leading to a more or less immediate gain of material or danger for the enemy king. In practice, tactics is often correlated with calculation in a sense that the calculated line might feature a tactical shot somewhere along the way, but there are also so many lines which we calculate in every game of ours which have absolutely nothing to do with tactics. What are studies and how can they improve my calculation skills? Studies are puzzles artificially constructed by their composers for the purpose of accentuating a particularly beautiful motif or sequence of moves. These are by definition supposed to be aesthetically pleasing, with the final point behind the puzzle representing the solver’s reward for their efforts. As much as I have nothing against using these as one of many elements of your own training regimen, if I were you, I would be definitely making sure that they meet some basic working criteria in the first place. First of all, those studies need to be as practice-oriented as possible, which means that the featured positions could have theoretically arisen in an over-the-board game. Secondly, make sure that they are cherry-picked by a coach or friend, and that their main theme varies between different elements or stages of the calculation process. Finally, be aware of the effects of the dopamine rush solving excessive numbers of studies brings upon your chess mind. In particular, you would prefer to avoid a situation in which you go to a tournament after an intense training session and subconsciously start looking for pretty hidden motifs in otherwise innocuous positions, just because you have been fed with such puzzles all the time right before the event. I. Vision & Perceptivity What is vision? Vision means the ability to spot a move or an idea which deserves attention. As opposed to ‘tactical vision’, which is the ability to recognize tactical patterns, pure vision is to be understood in a much broader manner, that is as a skill allowing us to find a candidate for a strong move, notwithstanding the nature of the reasoning behind it. What is ‘global vision’? ‘Global vision’ is the ability to search for possibilities across the whole board, even if the main action is taking place exclusively on one part thereof. It is namely a trademark of world-class players that they stay vigilant and sensitive to moves at which the position does not even hint at the slightest. By definition, global vision will also be largely independent from standard tactical pattern recognition, which circumstance only makes things harder for the untrained eye. What is perceptivity? Perceptivity means the capacity to discover resources which many other chess players would describe as ‘invisible moves’, that is moves that for some reason tend to escape the attention of human players. It entails the ability to withstand certain limitations of our way thinking pertaining to optics. Why do some players struggle at finding backward knight moves? Backward knight-moves are a very specific subtype of invisible moves. There are many examples among chess classics featuring a similar motif and these always raise the eyebrows of commentators. It is quite difficult for me to indicate the root cause of this phenomenon, but if I were to guess, I would say that it goes against human nature to retreat one's pieces. The knight is however a very special, tactical piece, which can almost always bounce back towards a more active outpost in case of need. What are ‘collinear moves’ and why do they tend to escape our attention sometimes? Collinear moves tend to escape the attention of human players as with major pieces of both sides standing on one file, rank or diagonal, the brain tricks us into thinking that mutual exchanges constitute the only available possibility. And, obviously, the deeper into the calculated line a move like this is supposed to happen, the less visible it is from afar. Why do we find it so difficult to willingly ignore a threat of the opponent? The reason for this might be purely psychological in nature - humans find it namely so difficult to go about a perceived danger that the urge to avert it represents a clear priority in their decision-making process. In order to avoid instances in which the quality of our calculation might be adversely affected by such a phenomenon, we should learn to recognize not only the threat itself, but to measure their real gravity as well. It may happen from time to time that the said threat is only going to look dangerous, without escalating into anything really serious. What keeps me away from putting my pieces en prise at times? Nobody enjoys putting their pieces en prise - I guess that years of teaching players that a material advantage suffices for a win most of the time is to blame here. Even if we spot an idea like this and consider playing it, just like gravity pulls objects towards the ground, our subconsciousness shall keep on trying to convince us that this is simply not the way to go. The only antidote to this 'primal fear' is to gain exposure to as many situations like this as possible in your own practice, this way consistently reminding oneself that material in chess is certainly not everything. How to become good at setting traps? Setting traps in chess requires a completely different mindset than making other types of moves. First of all, the idea to trap a piece of the opponent needs to come to our mind at all. Afterwards, one needs to ensure that the whole operation is actually worth it, comparing the possible material investment during such a procedure to the probability of it working out at the end of the day. Finally, the whole concept needs to be falsified, meaning that it needs to be tested for its viability by confronting it with various, even the most obscure refutation attempts. As opposed to regular calculation or analysis, falsification implies the necessity to mentally take the other side of the whole affair happening on the board, which can prove to be difficult to achieve psychologically for some. What are ‘time-stops’ in chess? A ‘time-stop’ in chess is defined as a situation in which one of the players obtains a significant material advantage and seems to have all the time in the world to convert it, but is unable to do so because of the extremely strong coordination/activity of the opponent's forces. The human mind is namely capable of understanding the concept of a sacrifice for immediate gains, in some cases even a sacrifice for long-term positional compensation. Both of those cases feature however instant gratification in return for the investment, or the feeling of control over the situation over an extended period of time, respectively, while time-stops are – at least at first glance – far more ephemeral in this regard. II. Evaluation & Candidate Moves When am I supposed to evaluate the position within the calculation process? The first thing to be done when emerging out of the opening stage or just seeing a previously unknown puzzle is evaluating the position, that is determining who is better depending on the entirety of elements present on the board. Based on that, a plan is usually formulated, indicating not only the suggested further course of action for yourself, but also what you should be watching out for when it goes for the rival’s intentions. Please bear in mind however that evaluation always comes back to us like a boomerang towards the end of our calculation process too. What is the ‘candidate moves method’ all about? Once we have already figured out the evaluation of the position, now is the time to create a list of fitting candidate moves. The search for candidates itself will be usually reliant on the so-called ‘CCTV method’ popularized by Judit Polgar, that is considering checks, captures and threats in the first place. One must not forget however that, on a conceptual level, there always needs to be a logical link present between the evaluation of the position and the elected candidate moves in the sense that the considered candidate moves should aim to bring into fruition the goals set forth by the evaluation process. How many candidate moves should I consider as of every turn? It depends. Sometimes the position will require just one winning move } { (e.g. while landing mate) } { , and on other occasions } { (e.g. in non-forcing positions) } { you will need to check even up to 3-4 of those. In any case however, the habit of always taking into account at least two candidate moves/ideas is priceless as it helps us avoid so-called 'tunnel vision', that is the situation in which we are so enchanted with a given move so much that our chess mind literally refuses to acknowledge the existence of any alternatives. Will evaluation always help me find the best candidate moves available in the position? There are some candidate moves which exceed the boundaries of evaluation done by any reasonable player orientated around the general needs of the position, with their viability based solely on such an approach working tactically thanks to some specific features of the position. In other words - in extreme cases, even the most thorough assessment of the position might not necessarily steer you towards the best candidate move available in the given position. True, such resources can be explained rather confidently with the benefit of hindsight } { (or once somebody points them out to you) } { , but there is no way anyone would be able to figure them out at the board, especially when having have to ignore more straightforward plans available upfront. How to handle positions which are too chaotic to evaluate them properly? Such cases are rare, but do certainly happen in practice from time to time. What I suggest then is to gather as much intel about the position as possible, even if these would be just scraps of information, e.g. a threat of the rival we need to watch out for, or maybe at least a sign of any typical motif available in similar structures. When formed together, the general type of information may help us understand what should be our overall goal in the given situation, while more concrete threats of the opponent might translate to candidate moves needed to be considered at different stages of the variation tree. What to do if my primary candidate move does not work immediately? Upon determining that an interesting idea fails to do the trick in the given position, human players tend to throw the said resource out of the window and never come back to it again. In reality however, it is better to keep it at the back of one's head over the course of the next couple of moves as it might happen that under changed circumstances, the said idea is going to start working again a couple of moves later. While calculating variations, can I afford to cut some slack on the candidate moves of my opponent? You are supposed to pay as much attention to the candidate moves of the opponent as you do to the candidate moves of your own. Way too often do we namely religiously analyze our own ideas during the game, while at the same time not trying hard enough to find the most testing response to them on behalf of the opponent though, or even outright assuming that if a counter-solution like this exists, they are surely not going to find it. For the sake of your own results, please do not do this. Be thorough and objective in your pursuit of the truth during calculation and you will barely ever experience the unpleasant sensation of cold sweat running down your back upon facing a surprise answer from your rival. How to react when my opponent plays a move which I have not expected before? Every single time your opponent plays something unexpected or outright strange, exercise additional caution. It might be that your rival's last move is just a mistake, any divergence from the standard should be checked with due diligence, without naturally the need to immediately become paranoid about it. III. Elimination & Comparison What is ‘elimination’ in chess? Once we have established the list of candidate moves, then comes the time to separate the wheat from the chaff, that is to reduce their number accordingly } { (usually to two) } { . The first thing to be done in this respect is typically elimination, that is rejecting any candidate move which fails tactically already at first glance, or maybe just does not correspond with the needs of the position after all. How is ‘comparison’ different from the above? As opposed to elimination, comparison proves to be helpful when dealing with two } { (rarely more) } { moves that look similar to one another or aim to achieve the same goal in the given position. The difference between such alternatives shall often times be minuscule so we will need to remember to compare them with one another very strictly, and not just based on our optical preference or gut feeling. Is it always beneficial to choose candidate moves which reduce the number of the opponent’s answers? Sometimes the comparison process related to two or more similarly-looking moves shall namely pertain strictly to determining the most accurate course of action. Just to make things clear though - in such a case, we are not looking for the move order which is as forcing as possible } { (cuts the majority of intermediate moves) } { , but rather one that does not allow the opponent to throw in a spanner into our works anywhere along the way. If a move A solves two problems of mine, while move B only one, should I always give preference to the former? Especially in sharp positions, the comparison process cannot rely on general assumptions like 'Which of the moves solves more problems of mine?', but should rather be based on precise calculation of the consequences of the respective alternatives as well as the prioritization of problems occurring on the board. In such situations, generalizations may serve a supplementary role in helping us choose only when specific lines do not provide concrete proof for superiority of one idea over the other. It might also be namely that the less universal move would be solving a singular, but critical problem, with the former dealing solely with some tiny nuisances. I like move A very much, but have not checked move B yet. Can I simply skip the latter altogether? When faced with a comparison-based problem within the calculation process, I always advise my students not only to try and understand why are they playing a certain move, but also what is the reason for rejecting the other one. It is namely often the case that those two ideas may be interconnected in a sense that the superior alternative manages to specifically overcome the flaw of the inferior one. Without however comprehending the nature of the faulty line, it might at times not be possible to appreciate the advantage behind the best move. Am I allowed to reject moves on the spot if they look ugly appearance-wise? Chess is not ski jumping so that any points where to be awarded for style. Also, calculation - as a very concrete method of determining the best move in the position - is completely independent from questions of beauty or taste. If the given continuation works, even if it looks ugly, it can be played. Too many a times players rely rather on appearances when judging their position, even if the amount of time left on the clock as well as the nature of the situation allows/suggests to verify it deeper. Move A looks much safer than move B? Is it a good idea to simply rely on the former then? It is a very popular mistake for players confronted with a problem of choice to automatically } { (that is, with no or just scarce calculation) } { prefer the option which looks less risky. Such an approach should be however rejected as one that has very little to do with an empirical science such as chess calculation. Instead, it would be a better approach to examine the riskier } { (albeit also more ambitious) } { move first. If it turns out to be unsound at the end of the day, in the worst-case-scenario you may almost always revert back to move A and treat it as an escape exit. IV. Forced Lines & Intermediate Moves What are ‘forced’ lines? Once we have already decided which of the candidate moves appeals to us the most, it is time to start calculating its consequences. Some of such variations can take the form of ‘forced’ ones, meaning that they will constitute longer sequences of only moves. Naturally, forced lines } { (or lines containing forced moves) } { are typically to be analyzed first within our calculation process since it is relatively easier to reach their respective end than in case of non-forcing ones. What does ‘only’ mean in the context of forced lines? First of all, a given move may be the only legal follow-up available in the position, e.g. running away with your king from a double-check. Additionally, we speak about only moves in cases in which it is just obvious to us that there is only one idea available in the position, e.g. eliminating an attacking piece of the opponent a second before it lands mate. Finally, in the game of chess we also distinguish forced lines comprised of only moves, with their 'only' nature not being obvious at all without diving deep into the analysis of the situation. In other words, the difficulty level of calculating a forced line consisting of only legal moves is going to be drastically different from calculating the same type of a line built of only, but at the same time optically non-forcing moves. Do forced lines require both of the players to find only moves as of every single turn? In chess, there is something that I call ‘one-sided forced lines’, which are variations within which only one of the players has to keep on finding the only moves all the time in order to survive. This has consequences for the construction of the variation tree as we } { (as the defending side) } { are going to be presented with a series of choices between two moves } { (one of them usually being refutable of the spot) } { , while the vast majority of calculation is going to pertain to the whole spectrum of candidate moves our opponents are allowed to play as of every single turn. This, on the other hand, shall influence the length of the calculated lines, with our moves needed to be calculated far ahead } { (as our life depends on their viability) } { , while the resources of the opponent being treated in a more concise fashion, just as to make sure that none of them critically endangers our position. My engine always seems to suggest forced lines during the analysis. Does this mean that they are always best? There is no proven correlation between the forced nature of a given move and its strength. In other words, the fact that a specific move looks forced does not simultaneously mean that it is also strong. Instead, it just testifies that it might be easier for us to figure out its consequences as more only moves may follow directly afterwards. What are ‘intermediate’ moves? Sometimes a forced line gets ‘interrupted’ by a more or less unexpected move which either changes the nature of the variation back to a more general } { (non-forced) } { one, or just leaves us no other choice but to do some additional branching at this point of time – we call them ‘intermediate moves’. These tend to be rather unpleasant as, on one hand, they disturb the natural flow of the calculated line, and on the other, introduce a certain degree of chaos into our calculation process, especially if we had not anticipated their emergence from the very beginning. Do intermediate moves rule out the forced nature of a given line? In general, intermezzos do not alter the forced nature of a given variation - they just steer the pace thereof in another direction. So-called ‘time-stop inducing’ intermediate moves are however different as they make the impression of substantially slowing down the course of events happening on the board. Such situations can be particularly unpleasant for any player, as they have the capacity to perplex us into thinking that calculation may be of lesser importance in the concrete situation already, as the position appears to have stabilized, subconsciously convincing us to begin relying on general considerations more. } 
1.--   